window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"title":"Appearance and themes","href":"administration/appearance","headings":["Themes","Switch your own theme","Set the theme for everybody","Settings &rsaquo; Appearance, every option","The sign-in page","The resizable sidebar","Your own console options","Tips"],"text":"Change how Buildspawn looks for you and your team: the light or dark theme, shared icon colours and how build pages and consoles are shown. There are two places to change the look: Page. Who. What it changes. Settings › Appearance. Administrators. The default theme for everybody, shared icon colours and server-wide display options. Account menu › Appearance. Every user. Your own theme and console options. They win over the server defaults, for you only. Themes. Buildspawn has three themes: Theme. What it looks like. Default. The light theme: light pages with a dark side panel. Dark. The dark theme: dark everywhere. Easier on the eyes in a dark room. Dark (System). Follows the operating system's light or dark setting, and switches when it does. Settings › Appearance in the light theme. The same page in the dark theme. Switch your own theme. The quickest way: click the sun / moon switch in the top bar. Your choice is saved with your account, so it follows you to other computers. Or choose it on your own Appearance page: Click the account menu (the round button with your initial) and pick Appearance. Click a theme card: Default , Dark or Dark (System). Click Save. Your own Appearance page. Set the theme for everybody. Open Settings › Appearance. Under Themes , click the theme new users should get. Leave Allow theme selection for each user on so people can still pick their own, or switch it off to use one theme for everybody. Click Save. Settings &rsaquo; Appearance, every option. The page is called Appearance and says Configure the look and feel of Buildspawn. A Find plugins link at the top lists plugins that add more looks. Everything on the page applies to the whole server, in this order: Section and field. What it does. Themes : Default , Dark , Dark (System). The theme people get until they pick their own. One card is selected. Allow theme selection for each user. On: every user can pick a theme on their own Appearance page or with the switch in the top bar. Off: everybody gets the theme chosen above. Icon colour favourites ( Shared with everyone ). A table with the columns Name , Colour and Shown on light / on dark. These colours appear in the Favourites row of the icon colour swatch of every build configuration and project, for every user. Only administrators can change them. Click Add favourite to add a row, give it a name and pick a colour. Up to 24. Removing one does not change icons already coloured with it, because the colour is stored on the icon. Colour check under the favourites. A colour that is too light for the light page, or too dark for the dark side panel, is shown adjusted there. The two columns show the colour on light and on dark, and a line under the table says which colour is used where, for example that a dark green is shown a little lighter in the project tree and on the dark theme. Pipeline Stages : Show pipeline stages on job page , Show stage names by default , Show stage durations by default. Three switches for the stage view of build configurations that run in stages. Each can be left on its default or set here for everybody. Pipeline Graph : Show pipeline graph on build page. Shows the graph of stages on the build page. Prism syntax highlighting : Theme. The colour scheme for code shown in the browser, such as scripts. The default, PRISM , follows light and dark mode. Console URL Provider. Which view opens when someone clicks a build console link. Providers higher in the list win. If none can show the link, the default is used. Click Add to add a provider and Delete to remove one. Users can override this on their own page. Click Save or Apply at the bottom. Apply saves and keeps you on the page. The sign-in page. The sign-in page (from release 1.7) has the logo on the left and the form on the right, with faint branch lines behind. It follows the theme: light, dark, or your system's setting when the default theme is in use. The resizable sidebar. From release 1.9 you can change the width of the lef"},
{"title":"Authorization","href":"administration/authorization","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","The strategies","Permission cards","The two special cards","Ambiguous names","Project-based security","Inheritance strategy","Entra ID Matrix-based security","Every permission","Overall","Credentials","Agent","Build configuration","Run","View, SCM and Perforce","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Authorization decides what each person may see and do, once the security realm has said who they are. When to use it. You set the authorization strategy when you set up the server, and again when you want to give a team its own rights. For a studio with several teams, choose Project-based security and use roles. For a small server, Matrix-based security is enough. If people sign in with Entra ID, choose Entra ID Matrix-based security. How to set it up. Open Settings › Security. You need Administer. Find the section Authorization and open the drop-down. Pick a strategy (see the next section). For a matrix strategy, click Add user or Add group , type the name, and tick permissions on the card (see Permission cards ). Make sure your own user or group has Administer. Then click Save. If you save a matrix without Administer for yourself, you lose access to Settings. The recovery steps are on Security realms. The Authorization section: the strategy drop-down, the filter and the permission cards. The strategies. Strategy. What it does. Use it when. Anyone can do anything. No checks. Everyone, even people who are not signed in, has full control. A private test on one computer, or a trusted network where people only need a profile. Legacy mode. The old behaviour. Whoever has the role admin has full control. Everyone else, including anonymous visitors, can only read. You need to keep the behaviour of a very old setup. Prefer a matrix. Logged-in users can do anything. Every signed-in user has full control. A visitor who is not signed in only reads. The checkbox Allow anonymous read access says whether they may read at all. It is on by default. A warning says to use it only if every signed-in user is fully trusted. A small, trusted team that wants to know who did what. Matrix-based security. One list of permission cards for the whole server. Each card is one user or one group. You want exact control, and the same rights on every project. Project-based security. The server list, plus a list on each project and a Roles tab. Rights given on a project apply to everything inside it. Several teams, each with its own project. The right choice for most studios. Entra ID Matrix-based security. A matrix that finds users and groups in your Entra ID directory. See Entra ID sign-in. The security realm is Entra ID. Old Entra ID Matrix-based security (do not use). An earlier form of the Entra ID matrix, kept so that old settings load. It is not recommended. Never for a new setup. If you have it, switch to Entra ID Matrix-based security and add the cards again. Changing the strategy does not delete users. It only changes who may do what. Permission cards. The matrix strategies show one card for each user or group. A card is a bar with the name, a short summary of what is allowed, three small buttons, and an arrow. Click the bar to open the card and see every permission, grouped by area. Cards in the matrix. Click the bar of a card to open it. Part of the card. What it does. Name. The user or group. A group has two small people in its icon, a user has one. Summary. A one-line list of what is ticked. No permissions granted shows when nothing is. Grant all. Ticks every permission for this card. Remove all. Clears every permission on this card. Bin. Removes the card. The built-in cards Anonymous and Authenticated Users have no bin. Open card. The permissions are grouped: Overall , Credentials , Agent , Build configuration , Run , View , SCM and Perforce. Hover over the help icon beside a permission to read what it allows. implied. A permission can be granted by another one. For example Administer grants all others. The implied ones show as ticked and say (implied). You can not clear them without clearing the one that grants them. Above the cards: Filter users and groups hides cards that do not match what you type. The funnel button, Filter by permission , shows only the cards that hold the permissions you pick. Reset clears it. Add user and Add group make a "},
{"title":"Backups and upgrades","href":"administration/backups","headings":["Where your data is","Back up","What you can leave out","Prepare for shutdown, and reload","Restore","Upgrade","Windows setup","Windows portable zip","Linux packages","Linux and macOS tar.gz","The war file","After an upgrade","Tips"],"text":"Keep your data safe and move to new versions of Buildspawn without losing anything. Everything Buildspawn knows is in one folder, the home (also called the data folder): projects, build configurations, build history, users, credentials, settings and plugins you added. The program files are separate and can be replaced at any time. Back up the home folder and you can always get back. Where your data is. Download. Home (back this up). Settings file. Logs. Windows setup. C:\\ProgramData\\Buildspawn\\data. C:\\ProgramData\\Buildspawn\\buildspawn.cfg. C:\\ProgramData\\Buildspawn\\logs. Windows portable zip. data\\ in the Buildspawn folder. buildspawn.cfg next to Buildspawn.exe. logs\\. Linux.deb and.rpm packages. /var/lib/buildspawn. /etc/default/buildspawn (.deb) or /etc/sysconfig/buildspawn (.rpm). /var/log/buildspawn. Linux and macOS tar.gz. data/ in the Buildspawn folder. buildspawn.conf in the folder. logs/. The same folder also holds secrets\\ (with initialAdminPassword on a new server), trust\\ (your trusted certificates) and users\\. The full list is in Inside the data folder. The settings file can move the home somewhere else. Check the data or BUILDSPAWN_HOME key, or see Settings › System › Home directory. The keys are listed in Settings files. Back up. Stop Buildspawn, or click Prepare for Shutdown on the Settings page so no build is writing files. A copy taken while builds run can be incomplete. Copy the whole home folder to a safe place, for example another disk or a network share. Copy the settings file too ( buildspawn.cfg or buildspawn.conf ). Start Buildspawn again. Example on Linux with a package install: sudo systemctl stop buildspawn sudo tar -czf /backup/buildspawn-$(date +%F).tar.gz -C /var/lib buildspawn /etc/default/buildspawn sudo systemctl start buildspawn. Example on Windows, in a command prompt opened as administrator: cd \"C:\\Program Files\\Buildspawn\" buildspawn-service stop robocopy C:\\ProgramData\\Buildspawn D:\\Backup\\Buildspawn /MIR /XD logs buildspawn-service start. What you can leave out. The home folder can be large because of build history and artifacts. If space is short, these are safe to skip: workspace/ : working copies of the sources. Builds check them out again. war/ : unpacked again when Buildspawn starts. The logs folder. Always keep the secrets/ folder and the secret.key files in the home folder together with the rest. Without them, Buildspawn cannot read the saved passwords and credentials. Prepare for shutdown, and reload. Two buttons on the Settings page help around a backup or an upgrade. Prepare for Shutdown stops new builds from starting so the system can be shut down safely. Running builds finish. You can type a Reason : it is shown to everybody in a banner at the top of every page until you click Cancel Shutdown. Use it before a backup, a restart or an upgrade. Click Update shutdown preparation to change the reason. Reload Configuration from Disk throws away everything the server holds in memory and reads it all again from the data folder. Use it after you changed files in the data folder by hand, or put back part of a backup, when you cannot restart the server. A restart is safer after a full restore. Restore. Install the same version of Buildspawn, or a newer one. Stop Buildspawn. Replace the home folder with your backup, and put back the settings file. On Linux with a package or service, give the files back to the service user: sudo chown -R buildspawn:buildspawn /var/lib/buildspawn. Start Buildspawn and sign in with your usual account. Try a restore once on a spare machine. A backup you have never restored is only a hope. Upgrade. An upgrade replaces the program and keeps your home folder and settings. Back up first, then follow the steps for your download. Windows setup. Download the new buildspawn-<version>-setup.exe and run it. The setup stops the service, replaces the program files, keeps your settings and data, and starts the service again. For a silent upgrade, run setup.exe /S. setup."},
{"title":"Dashboard","href":"administration/dashboard","headings":["Dashboard settings","Start with the suggested widgets","Change the dashboard","Add a widget","Widget types","Related pages","Tips"],"text":"Choose what the dashboard shows: the builds, failures, queue, agents and numbers your team should see first. Click Dashboard in the top bar to open it. The dashboard is a grid of widgets. An administrator arranges it, and everybody sees the same dashboard. Each widget only shows what the viewer is allowed to see. The full list of projects and build configurations is on the Projects page, not here. Use the dashboard for the few things that matter most. Dashboard settings. You need the System administrator role to change the dashboard. Other users see the dashboard but no Edit dashboard button. Start with the suggested widgets. When the dashboard is empty, you see Arrange the dashboard with two buttons: Click Start with suggested widgets. Buildspawn adds a useful set: metric cards, builds per day, the build queue, executor status, recent builds and failing builds. Move, resize or remove widgets as you like (see below). Click Save. Edit mode. Each widget has a bar with buttons to move, resize, set options and remove it. Change the dashboard. Click Edit dashboard. The title changes to Edit dashboard. Change the widgets (see the table below). Click Save to publish the dashboard to everybody, or Cancel to throw your changes away. Each widget has a bar with these buttons: Button. What it does. Drag handle (dots). Drag the widget to a new place. ‹ ›. Move earlier or later in the grid. Narrower / Wider. Change the width. The grid has 12 columns; a widget is 2 to 12 wide. Shorter / Taller. Change the height, from 1 to 6 rows. Widget options. Change the title and the widget's own options. ×. Remove the widget. The size is shown at the top right of each widget, for example 8 × 2 (8 columns wide, 2 rows high). Add a widget. In edit mode, click Add widget. Pick a widget from the list. Open its Widget options to set it up, then click Save on the dashboard. The seven widget types. Widget types. Widget. What it shows. Options. Metric cards. Four cards: failing configurations, running builds, builds waiting in the queue and available executors. Each card links to its list. Title. Builds per day. A bar chart of builds per day, stacked by result, with the success rate, average duration and average queue time. Title, period: last 24 hours, 7, 14 or 30 days. Recent builds. The latest builds across all build configurations, with result, changes, start time and duration. Title, how many to show. Build queue. How many builds wait, the oldest wait, why each build waits, and the waits over the last hour. Title. Build executor status. Online, busy and offline agents, and idle executors. Title, list offline agents. Needs attention. Everything that is failing, failing first. Title, include unstable builds, how many to show. Watched builds. The build configurations you choose, with their status, recent history and running progress. Title, the build configurations (type to find one, then order them). A dashboard holds up to 30 widgets, and a Watched builds widget up to 50 build configurations. Related pages. Settings overview : every administrator page in one list. Queue and Agents page : the full lists behind the queue and executor widgets. Load Statistics : long-term charts of executors and queue length. Tips. Leave a widget's title empty to use its own name. A user who may not see a build configuration in a Watched builds widget does not see it at all, so one dashboard works for the whole studio. Widgets refresh by themselves. If the server cannot be reached, a widget keeps the last data and says Showing last known data. Put a Watched builds widget with your release build configurations at the top, so everybody sees at once whether the game builds."},
{"title":"Entra ID sign-in","href":"administration/entra-id","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Part 1: Register Buildspawn in the Entra admin centre","Part 2: Fill in the Security Realm form","Part 3: Choose the Entra ID matrix and add yourself","Every field of the realm form","The Entra ID Matrix-based security grid","Matrix cards in projects","Profile photos","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Let people sign in to Buildspawn with their Microsoft work account, and give rights to people and groups from your Entra ID directory. Entra ID is Microsoft’s cloud directory (it used to be called Azure Active Directory or Azure AD). With the Entra ID security realm, Buildspawn sends people to the Microsoft sign-in page and gets back who they are and which groups they belong to. You never create users in Buildspawn. When to use it. Use it when your studio already manages accounts in Microsoft 365 or Azure. People use one password, your IT team can switch an account off in one place, and you give permissions to the same groups you already have. To do all of it in one go, follow Sign in with Entra ID. How to set it up. There are three parts: register Buildspawn in Entra ID, fill in the realm form, and choose the Entra ID matrix for permissions. Part 1: Register Buildspawn in the Entra admin centre. You need an Entra ID role that may register applications (for example Application Administrator ). Ask your IT team if you do not have one. Before you start, open Settings › System and check that Buildspawn URL is the real address people use, for example https://build.studio.example/. See System settings. Open the Entra admin centre ( entra.microsoft.com ) and sign in. Go to Identity › Applications › App registrations and click New registration. Type a Name , for example Buildspawn. Under Supported account types choose Accounts in this organizational directory only. Under Redirect URI choose the platform Web and type your Buildspawn address followed by /securityRealm/finishLogin , for example https://build.studio.example/securityRealm/finishLogin. It must match exactly, including https and the last part. Click Register. On the Overview page, copy two values: Application (client) ID and Directory (tenant) ID. You will paste them into Buildspawn. Open Certificates & secrets › Client secrets › New client secret. Type a description, choose how long it lasts, and click Add. Copy the secret Value now. Entra ID shows it only once. Open API permissions › Add a permission › Microsoft Graph › Application permissions. Add User.Read.All and Group.Read.All. Also add People.Read if your tenant offers it for applications. Then click Grant admin consent. Buildspawn uses these to read a person’s groups, to search people and groups in the matrix, and to fetch profile photos. Skip step 9 only if you cannot get admin consent. Then tick Disable graph integration in the realm form. People can still sign in, but group search and photos do not work, and the matrix asks you to type names. See below. The Entra ID realm form. Do not save until the values are right. Part 2: Fill in the Security Realm form. Open Settings › Security. In Security Realm choose Entra ID. Paste the Client ID. Under Authentication Type keep Client Secret and paste the secret into Secret. Paste the Tenant (the directory ID). In Test user principal name or object id type your own work account, for example dana.k@studio.example. Click Verify configuration. Wait for a green message. See Problems and fixes below if you get an error. Do not save yet. Go to Part 3 first, so that you are an administrator after the switch. Part 3: Choose the Entra ID matrix and add yourself. Still on Settings › Security , open Authorization and choose Entra ID Matrix-based security. In Add from Entra ID start typing your name. Pick yourself in the list and click Add. A card for you appears. Open your card and tick Administer. It ticks the other boxes as implied. Add a card for the group of everybody who should see Buildspawn (for example Studio staff ) and tick Read under Overall. Without it people sign in and see nothing. Click Save. Open a private window and sign in with your work account. If you save without an Administer card for a person who can really sign in with Entra ID, you are locked out. The recovery steps are on Security realms. Every field of the realm form. Field. What it does. Default. Example. "},
{"title":"Global connections and Vault","href":"administration/global-connections","headings":["Global connections","Vault","Add a Vault connection","Use a Vault secret in a build","Perforce roots","Related pages","Tips"],"text":"Connections set on the root project are shared by every project. Set up a service once, such as HashiCorp Vault or a Perforce server, and every build configuration can use it. The root project sits above all your projects. What you set there is passed down to every project and build configuration on the server. To open it, click Settings › Root project , or Root project in any breadcrumb. The root project's Build settings tab has a menu on the left: Item. What it holds. Build settings. Build steps, parameters, post-build actions and build wrappers that every build configuration on the server gets. Templates. Templates every project can use. See Templates. Connections. Connections to outside services, such as HashiCorp Vault. Perforce roots. Perforce server connections that many build configurations reuse. Credentials. User names, passwords, keys and tokens that builds and connections use. Global connections. A connection is a link to an outside service, with its address and sign-in details. Every build configuration in the project where it is defined, and in all projects below, can pick it by its connection ID. A connection on the root project is therefore global. Root project › Build settings › Connections. Connections from a parent project show in the list of a child project too, but you change them where they are defined. Secrets in a connection are stored encrypted and never shown again. To change a connection you need permission to configure the project that holds it. Vault. HashiCorp Vault keeps secrets such as signing keys, console SDK passwords and store tokens in one safe place. With a Vault connection, a build reads the secret from Vault at the moment it runs. The secret is never saved in Buildspawn. Add a Vault connection. Open Root project › Build settings › Connections and click Add connection. For Connection type , choose HashiCorp Vault. Type a Display name , for example Studio Vault. Buildspawn makes the Connection ID from it. Jobs refer to the connection by this ID, so don't change it later. Type the Vault URL , for example https://vault.example.com:8200. If you use Vault Enterprise namespaces, type the Vault namespace. Otherwise leave it empty. Choose how Buildspawn signs in to Vault under Authentication (see the table below) and fill in its fields. Click Test connection. When the test passes, click Save. Add a HashiCorp Vault connection. Authentication. Fields. Use it when. AppRole. Endpoint path (where AppRole is mounted in Vault, approle by default), Role ID and Secret ID. The usual choice for a server. Your Vault administrator gives you the role ID and secret ID. LDAP. Endpoint path (where LDAP is mounted in Vault), User name and Password. Your Vault signs in with your company directory. GCP IAM. Role , an optional Service account , the Credentials (the agent's own identity from the metadata server, or a pasted service account key in JSON) and the Endpoint path ( gcp by default). Your agents run on Google Cloud. Each agent then signs in with its own Google identity. The secret ID and passwords are stored encrypted and never shown again. Use a Vault secret in a build. Once the connection exists, a build configuration reads secrets through a parameter that points into Vault. How to add one is in Secrets and Vault references. Perforce roots. A Perforce root is a connection to a Helix Core server with a workspace mapping, defined once and reused by many build configurations. Editing a root changes every build configuration that uses it. Settings › Perforce roots. The Used in column shows how many build configurations use each root. Open Settings › Perforce roots and click Create root. Give it a name, pick the server credential, and set the workspace mapping or stream. Click Save. The root can now be picked in any build configuration's Version control settings. The full Perforce setup is in Perforce. The Helix Swarm button sets up the code review server. Related pages. Management pages › Root project. Credenti"},
{"title":"Install on Linux and macOS","href":"administration/linux-macos-install","headings":["When to use which download","Install a package (Linux)","Where the package puts things","Everyday commands","Upgrade and remove","Install from a folder (tar.gz, Linux and macOS)","macOS: unsigned and not notarized","Run it as a service","Ports, access and the firewall","Upgrade a folder install","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Install the Buildspawn server on Linux with a package or a folder, or on a Mac with a folder, and run it as a service. Every download includes its own Java, so nothing else needs installing. For the short version, see Install the server. This page covers everything in the packages: the files they put on your computer, how to start and stop the server, ports, the home folder, and how to upgrade and remove it. When to use which download. Download. Best for. Service.deb package (Debian, Ubuntu). A Linux server that runs all the time. Set up for you: runs as the system user buildspawn , starts at boot.rpm package (RHEL, Rocky, Fedora). The same on an RPM-based Linux. The same. tar.gz for Linux (x64 and aarch64). Any glibc Linux, a try-out, or a server in a folder you choose. Optional: install-service.sh. tar.gz for macOS (Apple silicon and Intel). A build Mac. Optional: a LaunchAgent, install-service.sh. The packages are for x86-64. You need a Linux with glibc (most distributions; not Alpine). Install fontconfig and a font so that charts can be drawn. The macOS download for your Mac's chip is named -macos-aarch64 (Apple silicon) or -macos-x64 (Intel). Install a package (Linux). Download the package and its.sha256 file. Check the download: sha256sum -c buildspawn_<version>_amd64.deb.sha256. Install it. # Debian, Ubuntu sudo apt install./buildspawn_<version>_amd64.deb # RHEL, Rocky, Fedora sudo dnf install./buildspawn-<version>.x86_64.rpm. The package starts the service. Open http://<this machine>:8080/. Get the initial admin password: sudo buildspawn admin-password. Type it on the setup page and follow the steps. See Your first start. Where the package puts things. Path. What it is. /opt/buildspawn. The program: bin/buildspawn , app/buildspawn.war , runtime/ (Java). /usr/bin/buildspawn. A link, so you can type buildspawn. /etc/default/buildspawn (.deb) or /etc/sysconfig/buildspawn (.rpm). The settings file. See buildspawn.conf. /var/lib/buildspawn. The home: projects, build history, settings. Back this up. /var/log/buildspawn. gc.log and heap dumps. The server log is in the journal. Builds run as the system user buildspawn , so the tools and files that builds need must be readable by that user. Everyday commands. sudo systemctl status buildspawn # or: buildspawn status sudo buildspawn restart # or: sudo systemctl restart buildspawn buildspawn logs # the same as: journalctl -u buildspawn -f buildspawn check # checks the settings and Java sudo buildspawn set port=8090 --apply. Upgrade and remove. Install the new package over the old one with the same command you used to install. The upgrade stops the service, replaces the program, keeps the settings file and the home, and starts the service again. See Backups and upgrades. Goal. Command. Remove the program. Keeps the settings, the home, the logs and the buildspawn user. sudo apt remove buildspawn or sudo dnf remove buildspawn. Also remove the settings file (Debian, Ubuntu). sudo apt purge buildspawn. Delete the data as well. This cannot be undone. sudo BUILDSPAWN_PURGE_DATA=yes apt purge buildspawn (Debian, Ubuntu). Install from a folder (tar.gz, Linux and macOS). The tar.gz holds one folder that is portable. The settings ( buildspawn.conf ), the home ( data/ ) and the logs ( logs/ ) stay inside it, so you can move or copy the whole folder. Download the tar.gz for your system and check its.sha256 file. Unpack it, for example into /opt : tar -xzf buildspawn-<version>-linux-x64.tar.gz -C /opt cd /opt/buildspawn-<version>-linux-x64. On a Mac, clear the quarantine flag first (see below). Run bin/buildspawn. On a first run it opens the launch flow , then the live monitor. Press b to open the web page. To run the server in the terminal without the interface: bin/buildspawn run. Ctrl + C stops it. macOS: unsigned and not notarized. The Mac download is not signed with an Apple developer ID and is not notarized. When a browser downloads it, macOS marks it as quarantined, and Gatekeeper blocks"},
{"title":"Management pages","href":"administration/management-pages","headings":["How to open any of them","Reload Configuration from Disk","When to use it","How to use it","Problems and fixes","System Information","Logs","How to read the log","How to add a recorder","Problems and fixes","Load Statistics","Buildspawn CLI","Script Console","Problems and fixes","Nodes","About Buildspawn","Manage Old Data","Prepare for Shutdown","Root project","Credentials","Credential Providers","Perforce roots","BuildPatch Tool","Upload a package","See the agents","Problems and fixes","Plugins pages","Installed plugins","Available plugins","Download progress","Advanced settings","Related pages"],"text":"One section for each of the smaller pages under Manage: reload, system information, logs, load statistics, the command line client, the script console, nodes, old data, shutdown, the root project, credentials, Perforce roots, the BuildPatch Tool and the plugin pages. When to use it: when you look after the server and need to find out what a Settings page does, what it shows, and what its buttons change. The big pages have their own guide pages, listed in the Settings overview. How to open any of them. Click Manage in the left rail. The Settings page lists every page as a card, or as a row in the list. Click the page you want, or use the search box at the top of Settings. Use the breadcrumb at the top to go back to Manage. Every Settings page shows the list of pages on its left. Page. Address. Reload Configuration from Disk. /reload. System Information. /systemInfo. Logs. /log. Load Statistics. /load-statistics. Buildspawn CLI. /cli. Script Console. /script. Nodes. /computer. About Buildspawn. /about. Manage Old Data. /administrativeMonitor/OldData/manage. Prepare for Shutdown. /prepareShutdown. Root project. /rootProject. Credentials. /credentials. Credential Providers. /configureCredentials. Perforce roots. /perforce-roots. BuildPatch Tool. /buildpatchtool. Plugins pages. /pluginManager/. Reload Configuration from Disk. Throws away everything the server holds in memory and loads all settings, build configurations and build records again from the data folder. It does not restart the server. When to use it. Only after you changed files in the data folder by hand, for example a config.xml of a build configuration. Normal changes through the web pages never need it. How to use it. Open Manage › Reload Configuration from Disk. A page asks you to confirm. Click Retry using POST (the button is the confirmation). Wait. The server shows Starting Buildspawn and your browser reloads when it is ready. Anything not saved to disk is lost. Running builds keep running, but the page is not available for a short time. Do it when no one is editing settings. Problems and fixes. Settings you changed on disk do not show: check the file is valid. A broken file is skipped and an entry appears under Manage Old Data. System Information. Runtime details of the server, to help you find problems. The header shows the Buildspawn version, the Java version and the operating system. Part. What it shows. System Properties. The Java system properties, as Name and Value. Values that look like secrets are masked. Click Show values to see the masked ones. Examples: the data folder, the Java home, the file encoding. Environment Variables. The environment of the server process, with secrets masked in the same way. Plugins. Every plugin with Name , Version and Enabled. Memory Usage. A chart of the Java heap: Used and Total. Choose a Timespan of Short , Medium or Long. Thread Dumps. A link to the thread dumps of the server and of each agent. An offline agent shows Offline · not available. Settings › System Information. When you ask for help, copy the version line and the plugin list from this page. Logs. Log recorders collect log messages from the server code, at the levels you pick. Use them to find out why something in the server fails, for example a version control poll or an agent connection. How to read the log. Open Manage › Logs. Under Log Recorders , the first row is All Buildspawn Logs : every logger at level info and above. Click View log or Open full log. The newest line is first. Columns are Time , Level , Logger and Message. How to add a recorder. Click Add recorder. Type a name, for example Perforce polling , and confirm. On the recorder's page, add one or more loggers and pick a level for each (for example the logger of the Perforce plugin at FINE ). Click Save. Then reproduce the problem and open View log. Each recorder row has Configure , View log and, in More actions , Delete. Log levels shows the levels set now. Messages are kept in memory. A resta"},
{"title":"Plugins and updates","href":"administration/plugins","headings":["Installed plugins","Add a plugin from the update site","Add a plugin from a file","Watch the download","Updates","Tips"],"text":"See which features are installed, turn them on or off, and add more with plugins. A plugin adds a feature to Buildspawn, such as a build step, a version control system or a way to publish files. Buildspawn ships with all its plugins inside the download, already tested together. You don't need to install anything to start. The plugin pages are under Settings › Plugins. Each page, with its fields, is also described in Management pages. Page. What it does. Installed plugins. Lists every plugin with its version. Turn plugins on or off, or remove them. Available plugins. Lists plugins from the update site that are not installed yet. Download progress. Shows plugins that are being downloaded and installed. Advanced settings. Install a plugin from a file, and choose the update site. Installed plugins. Open Settings › Installed plugins. Type in Search installed plugins to find one by name. Installed plugins, filtered by name. Each row shows: Column. What it shows. Name. The plugin, its version and what it does. Health. A score for how well the plugin is kept up. Enabled. The switch turns the plugin on or off. A greyed switch means other plugins need this one, so it cannot be turned off. Uninstall. The bin removes the plugin. Turning a plugin off or removing it takes effect after a restart. Build configurations that use its steps or settings stop working, so check first. Add a plugin from the update site. Open Settings › Available plugins. Click Check now to refresh the list. Search for the plugin and tick it. Click Install , or Install after restart to finish it on the next restart. Add a plugin from a file. Open Settings › Advanced settings. Under Deploy Plugin , choose the plugin File on your computer, or type a URL. Click Deploy , then restart Buildspawn. Watch the download. After you click Install , the Download progress page opens. Each plugin shows its state until it is done. When all are done you can use the new plugins at once, or restart if one asks for it. See Download progress. Updates. Buildspawn ships its plugins and does not update them one by one. A new Buildspawn version brings new versions of the plugins, tested with the rest. To update plugins, upgrade Buildspawn. A file for a plugin that is already installed is refused. To replace a bundled plugin on purpose, tick Replace the installed plugin under Deploy Plugin. The Update Site field in Advanced settings sets where Available plugins comes from. Plugins you add yourself are kept in the data folder, so they survive an upgrade. After an upgrade, check that they still work with the new version. Tips. Add as few extra plugins as you can. Every plugin is code that runs inside your server. Try a new plugin on a test server first. Back up the data folder before you add or remove plugins. See Backups and upgrades. Plugins can add fields to the System page. See System settings. For a proxy that sits between the server and the update site, set the HTTP Proxy Configuration."},
{"title":"Roles","href":"administration/roles","headings":["When to use it","The built-in roles","The Roles page","Make a custom role","Give a role on a project","What the Roles tab shows","Rules and safeguards","The “Roles” note on the Security page","From the terminal","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"A role is a named set of permissions, such as Developer , that you give to a user or a group on a project in one step. When to use it. Use roles when you do not want to tick single permissions on a card. You give a role on a project, and it applies to that project and everything inside it. Give a role at Root and it applies to the whole server. Roles work only with the authorization Project-based security. See Authorization. A role is a shortcut. When you assign it, Buildspawn ticks the matching permissions in the permission grid. The grid still decides what people can do, and you can change any box there. The built-in roles. Built-in roles are fixed. You cannot change or delete them. Role. What it allows. Where. Project viewer. See the project, its build configurations and builds, and download artifacts. Any project. Developer. Project viewer, plus run and stop builds, read settings, use workspaces, edit build descriptions and tag sources. Any project. Project administrator. Developer, plus configure, create, move and delete build configurations and subprojects, manage views, and assign roles here. Below Root, also the project’s credentials. Any project. Build settings administrator. Change the steps, publishers, wrappers and parameters this project passes on. These run in every build configuration below it, with those build configurations’ credentials. Any project. Agent manager. Add, configure, connect, disconnect, build on and delete agents, and move them between pools. Nothing on projects. Creating or editing a pool stays with the system administrator. Root only. System administrator. Everything on the server. Root only. The Roles page. Settings › Roles shows the table of built-in and custom roles ( Role , What it allows , Kind , Actions ) and the form New custom role. Only administrators can open it. If Project-based security is off, a yellow note says Roles can’t be assigned because project-based security isn’t in use. You can still prepare custom roles. Settings › Roles: the built-in roles and the form to make your own. Make a custom role. Open Settings › Roles. Under New custom role type a Name , for example Release manager. Type a Description. It is shown beside the role wherever it can be given. Under Permissions tick what the role holds. Each group ( Overall , Credentials , Agent , Build configuration , Run , View , SCM , Perforce ) has Tick all and Clear. The box Find a permission filters the list. The card This role shows the name and n of m permissions ticked. Check it. Click Save role. It now appears on every project’s Roles tab. Field. What it does. Limit. Example. Name. The name people see when they assign the role. 1 to 80 characters, not used by another role. Release manager. Description. One line shown next to the role. Up to 500 characters. Runs and publishes release builds. Permissions. The permissions the role holds. At least one. Administer cannot be chosen: use System administrator. Build, Cancel, Workspace, Read. Below Root, only the project permissions of a role apply. Whoever gives a role must hold every permission in it, unless they are a system administrator. A role that holds exactly the same as another role is refused: This holds exactly what the role … holds. Use that role. To edit or delete a custom role, use the buttons in its row. Saving or deleting a role changes only future assignments. People who already have it keep their permissions. Give a role on a project. Open the project (or the Root project for the whole server) and click the Roles tab. Click Assign a role. Choose Type : User or Group. Type the User ID or group name. A group must exist first (see Users and groups ). Choose the Role. Click Assign. The person or group appears in the table Assigned on this project (or Assigned at Root ). The Roles tab of a project. What the Roles tab shows. Part. What it shows. Assigned here. Columns User or group , Role , Grants here and Actions ( Remove ). A row that fits no role shows Custom or A"},
{"title":"Running the server","href":"administration/running-the-server","headings":["Pick a package","First start","The initial admin password","Ports","Arguments","Memory","Where the logs are","One data folder, one server","Pages in this section","The Windows app","Install on Linux and macOS","The server terminal program","Backups and upgrades","Settings files","Trusted certificates"],"text":"The short guide to choosing a download, starting the server for the first time, and knowing where its port, memory, logs and data are. Pick a package. Your computer. Download. Details. Windows, a build server that stays on. buildspawn-<version>-setup.exe : installs the program and, if you want, the Windows service. The Windows app. Windows, a try-out or a USB stick. The portable.zip : unpack and open Buildspawn.exe. The Windows app. Debian or Ubuntu. The.deb package. Install on Linux and macOS. RHEL, Rocky or Fedora. The.rpm package. Install on Linux and macOS. Any other Linux, or a folder of your choice. The Linux tar.gz (x64 or aarch64). Install on Linux and macOS. A Mac. The macOS tar.gz (Apple silicon or Intel). Install on Linux and macOS. Your own Java, or a container. The.war file. Backups and upgrades. Every package except the war includes its own Java. There is a start page in the download folder, START-buildspawn-<version>.html , that shows the steps for each system. First start. Install and start as described for your system. On Windows, open the app and press Start on Run here , or install the service. On Linux and macOS run buildspawn (or bin/buildspawn ), which opens the launch flow. Open http://localhost:8080/ in a browser. From another computer use the server's name or address instead of localhost. A setup page asks for the initial admin password. Get it as described below and type it in. Follow the setup page. See Your first start. The initial admin password. On a new data folder the server makes a one-time password for the user admin. It is written to secrets\\initialAdminPassword inside the data folder, and deleted when you finish the setup page. Where you run Buildspawn. How to read it. Windows app. The Run here tab shows it under First start: initial admin password , with Copy. Terminal program. The launch flow shows it ( v shows, c copies). Later run buildspawn admin-password (add --copy to use the clipboard). On a Linux package use sudo. Any system. Open the file in the data folder. Ports. Port. Use. 8080 (default). The web page, the API and the agents. Change it in the settings file ( port on Windows, BUILDSPAWN_PORT on Linux and macOS), in the Windows app's Settings tab, or with buildspawn set port=. The server listens on all network interfaces by default in the settings file. The launch flow suggests This PC only for a try-out. Agents and other people need all. On Windows the setup and the launch flow can add a firewall rule named Buildspawn for the port. On Linux and macOS, open the port yourself. Arguments. Extra arguments go in the arguments setting ( BUILDSPAWN_ARGUMENTS on Linux and macOS), or in Extra arguments in the Windows app. They are added to the command that starts the server. Two you may need: Argument. What it does. --sessionTimeout=480. How long a signed-in browser stays active without use, in minutes. 480 is eight hours. --prefix=/buildspawn. Serves Buildspawn under a path, for example http://server:8080/buildspawn/. Use it when a reverse proxy publishes the server below a path. Set the same path in the proxy. Restart the server after a change. Memory. The memory setting is the Java heap, used as both the starting and the largest size. When it is empty (Linux, macOS) or not changed (Windows) the server takes a quarter of the computer's memory, between 2 GB and 16 GB. Builds run in their own processes, so they do not use this memory. Raise it when the server is slow with many projects or long histories. Change it in the Windows app's Settings tab, the launch flow, or buildspawn set memory=8g --apply. See Settings files for the Java options. Where the logs are. System. Server log. Memory log and heap dumps. Windows, installed. C:\\ProgramData\\Buildspawn\\logs\\buildspawn.log. The same folder. Windows, portable. logs\\buildspawn.log. logs\\. Linux packages. The journal: journalctl -u buildspawn -f. /var/log/buildspawn. Linux and macOS folder. The terminal, or logs/buildspawn.log for a backgroun"},
{"title":"SAML sign-in","href":"administration/saml","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","SP Configuration (read-only, to copy into the IdP)","IDP configuration","User Profile Configuration","Advanced Configurations","Custom attributes","Buttons at the bottom","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Let people sign in through your company’s single sign-on (SSO) with the security realm miniOrange SAML 2.0. SAML 2.0 is a standard way for a company sign-in system (the identity provider , or IdP, for example Okta, Keycloak or Active Directory Federation Services) to tell another application (the service provider , or SP, which is Buildspawn here) who a person is. Buildspawn never sees the password. When to use it. Use it when your company has an identity provider that speaks SAML 2.0 and you do not use Entra ID directly. If you use Entra ID, the Entra ID realm is simpler and also gives you groups and a people picker. How to set it up. Open Settings › Security. In Security Realm choose miniOrange SAML 2.0. The form only says to click Apply and configure the realm from the SAML page. Click Apply. Open Settings › miniOrange SAML SSO. The page has SP Configuration at the top and your identity provider settings below. Copy the values of SP Entity ID , Audience URI , ACS URL and SP Logout URL into the application you create in your identity provider. You can also use Service Provider Metadata (an XML file) and Download SP Certificate to set up the IdP, if it accepts files. In your identity provider, make sure it sends the user name (and, if you want it, the e-mail) in the sign-in answer. Note the names of the attributes it uses. Back in Buildspawn, under IDP configuration choose one of three ways: I will enter metadata url , I will enter metadata file path or I will do manual configuration. Fill in the fields (see the table). Click Validate metadata Url or Validate metadata File to check them, and the form fills the rest. Under User Profile Configuration say which attribute holds the user name and the e-mail. Click Test Configuration. A window opens, signs you in at your IdP and shows the attributes it received. Check the user name. Make sure the authorization gives Administer to the user name you will get from the IdP. Then click Save. The SAML single sign-on page. The server must be reachable by the address in the ACS URL , and your IdP must accept it. If you reach Buildspawn behind a proxy, set the real address in Buildspawn URL on System settings , so that these URLs show the public address. Every field. SP Configuration (read-only, to copy into the IdP). Field. What it is. SP Entity ID. The name of Buildspawn as a service provider. It is the base address of the server, for example https://build.studio.example. Audience URI. Same value. The IdP puts it in the answer so Buildspawn knows the answer is meant for it. ACS URL. The address where the IdP sends the sign-in answer: https://build.studio.example/securityRealm/moSamlAuth. SP Logout URL. The address that signs a person out: …/securityRealm/logout. Service Provider Metadata and Download SP Certificate. Links to the SP settings as an XML file and as a certificate, built from your last saved settings. IDP configuration. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Enter metadata url. The address of the IdP’s metadata file. Buildspawn reads the entity ID, sign-in address and certificate from it. Use Validate metadata Url to check it. Empty. https://idp.studio.example/metadata. IDP Metadata. The path of a metadata file on the server, when you downloaded it from the IdP. Use Validate metadata File to check it. Empty. C:\\buildspawn\\idp-metadata.xml. IDP Entity ID / Issuer. The name the IdP gives itself in the answer. From metadata. https://idp.studio.example/. Single Sign On URL. The IdP address that Buildspawn sends people to. From metadata. https://idp.studio.example/sso. Single Logout URL. Greyed out in this edition. —. —. Name ID Format. How the IdP names a person: unspecified , emailAddress , persistent or transient. Match what the IdP is set to send. unspecified. emailAddress. IDP Signing Certificate. The certificate the IdP signs its answers with, in text (PEM) form. Buildspawn rejects answers that are not signed with it. Paste only the public certificate. From metadata. -----BEG"},
{"title":"Security realms","href":"administration/security-realms","headings":["When to use it","The five realms","How to set it up","Switch safely","If you are locked out","Every field","Buildspawn’s own user database","Delegate to servlet container","Entra ID and miniOrange SAML 2.0","None","Sign-in option for every realm","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"A security realm decides where the list of users comes from and how people prove who they are when they sign in. When to use it. Pick the realm once, when you set up the server, and change it only when your company changes how it manages accounts. The realm answers \"who are you?\". A second choice, the authorization strategy , answers \"what may you do?\". The two are set on the same page and work together. The five realms. Realm. Where users come from. Use it when. Buildspawn’s own user database. A list kept by the server itself. You create the users, and they sign in with a user name and password. A small team, or a first test. No other system is needed. See Users and groups. Delegate to servlet container. The web server that runs Buildspawn (a servlet container such as Tomcat) signs people in. You run Buildspawn as a web application inside such a container that already handles sign-in. The standard server and the Windows and Linux packages do not use a container, so you will not need it. Entra ID. Your company's Microsoft Entra ID directory. People sign in with their work account. Your company uses Microsoft 365 or Azure. See Entra ID sign-in. miniOrange SAML 2.0. Any identity provider that speaks SAML 2.0, for example Okta, Keycloak or Active Directory Federation Services. Your company uses single sign-on with SAML. See SAML sign-in. None. Nobody signs in. Every visitor is the anonymous user. Only a throw-away test on one computer. Anyone who can reach the address uses the server. How to set it up. Open Settings › Security. You need the Administer permission. Find the section Security Realm. The page also has a Summary card on the right. It says in one line who can sign in and what decides what they may do. Open the drop-down and pick a realm. The fields for that realm appear below it. Fill in the fields (see below), then click Save. The Security page. The Security Realm section holds the realm drop-down; the Summary card says who can sign in. Do not lock yourself out. Before you save a new realm, make sure the authorization grants Administer to a user that exists in the new realm. A user called dana in your own database is not the same person as dana@studio.example in Entra ID. If nobody can administer after the change, you cannot open Settings. Switch safely. Keep the current browser tab open and signed in. Open a second private window for the test. Set up the new realm and its authorization so that your future sign-in name has Administer. For Entra ID, add yourself in the Entra ID matrix before you save. Click Apply , or Save , and sign in from the private window. Close the first tab only when the private window works. If you are locked out. You can turn security off on the server, then set it up again: Stop the server. Open config.xml in the Buildspawn home folder with a text editor. Change <useSecurity>true</useSecurity> to false. Start the server. Anyone can now open it. Go to Settings › Security and fix the realm and authorization straight away. Copy the home folder first. See Backups and upgrades. Every field. The page Settings › Security has these fields next to the realm. The other sections of the page are in Security settings. Buildspawn’s own user database. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Allow users to sign up. Puts a Sign up link at the top right of the sign-in page, so people can make their own account. When it is off, an administrator creates every account. Do not give much power to Authenticated Users while this is on, because anyone who can reach the server can create an account. Off. Off. Password Complexity Rule. The rule a new password must pass. Choose None or Basic password complexity. It applies when a password is set or changed. None. Basic password complexity. Minimum password length (Basic only). The shortest password allowed. 0 means no minimum. 8. 12. Require at least one uppercase letter (A-Z). The password must hold a capital letter. Off. On. Require at least one lowercase letter (a-z). T"},
{"title":"Security settings","href":"administration/security-settings","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every section","Authentication","Security Realm","Authorization","Markup Formatter","Agents","Git notifyCommit access tokens","Roles","Prism syntax highlighting","Personal machines","Git Hooks","Hidden security warnings","API Token","Content Security Policy","Git Host Key Verification Configuration","Sandbox Configuration","Sections that appear only sometimes","Other security pages","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"One page, Settings › Security , holds the sign-in, permission, agent and protection settings of the whole server. When to use it. Open it when you set up the server, when you change how people sign in, and when you want to tighten the server (API tokens, Git hooks, content policy). You need Administer to change it. People with System read can look at it but not save. How to set it up. Click Settings in the top bar, then Security. Use the list of sections on the left ( Jump to section ) to move down the page. Change the settings you need (below). Click Save , or Apply to stay on the page. The bar with both buttons stays at the bottom of the window. The top of Settings › Security, with the list of sections on the left. The card Summary on the right says in one line who can sign in and what decides what they may do. For example: Users of the built-in user list of this server sign in; Project-based security decides what they may do. It names the realm and the strategy. Every section. Authentication. Field. What it does. Default. Disable “Keep me signed in”. Removes the checkbox from the sign-in page. Everyone signs in again when the browser session ends. Off. Security Realm. Where users come from. The fields depend on the choice. See Security realms , Entra ID sign-in and SAML sign-in. With Buildspawn’s own user database you also see Allow users to sign up and Password Complexity Rule. Authorization. What people may do. The permission cards and the strategies are explained on Authorization. Markup Formatter. Field. What it does. Default. Markup Formatter. Decides how descriptions of projects, build configurations, views, builds and users are shown. Plain text shows them as written, with line breaks kept. Safe HTML allows a safe set of HTML tags. Plain text. Descriptions can be set by people you do not expect: any signed-in user can set their own, and build steps can set a build’s description. A formatter that shows HTML as it is would allow cross-site scripting (a page that runs someone’s script in your browser). Keep Plain text unless you trust everyone who can write descriptions. Agents. Field. What it does. Default. Example. TCP port for inbound agents. The port that agents which connect to the server use. Disable turns it off, which is best if you use no inbound agents. Random lets the server choose a port at start, which never clashes with other programs. Fixed takes a port number, so you can open exactly that port in your firewall. Disable. Fixed, 50000. If the server was started with a system property that sets the port, the field is replaced by a line like enforced to 50000 on startup through system property. See Agent settings for the agent protocols. Git notifyCommit access tokens. Tokens that protect the address Git servers call to tell Buildspawn about a new commit ( notifyCommit ). Every such call must carry a valid token in the token query parameter. Part. What it does. Current access tokens. The list. It says There are no access tokens yet. when empty. Revoke removes one. Generate. Makes a new token from a name. It is shown only once, so copy it. Cancel closes the box. Roles. A note that explains how the Roles tab relates to the permission cards, with a link Open the roles of Root. See Roles. Prism syntax highlighting. Field. What it does. Default. Permitted Source Code Directories. Some tools copy source files next to build results (coverage, warnings) so the page can show them. Files outside the build’s workspace are hidden unless their folder is listed here. Give an Absolute path of permitted directory on the agent, and click Add. Delete removes a row. Empty. Show source code only to people with the Workspace permission. Only people who may browse a workspace can see these source files. Off. Personal machines. Field. What it does. Default. Allow personal machines. People can connect their own computer and run their own builds on it. Who may add one is set by the UsePersonal permission under Agent. Off. Offline ma"},
{"title":"The server terminal program","href":"administration/server-terminal","headings":["When to use it","Two modes: the interface and single commands","The launch flow","The start screen: presets","The five steps","The live monitor","Read only, and the token","The Service view","The Settings view","Quitting","Look and feel","The text twins","Watching another server","Start, stop and the service","Trusted certificates: buildspawn certs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"buildspawn is a program for the command line that sets up, starts, watches and changes your Buildspawn server on Linux, macOS and Windows. It is in every server download: bin/buildspawn on Linux and macOS, bin\\buildspawn.exe on Windows. The Linux packages and the Windows setup also put it on the PATH, so you type just buildspawn. A short introduction is in Get started: the terminal program. The table of every command, flag and key is in Terminal program commands. This page explains how the program behaves. When to use it. Use it on a server you reach over SSH, on a Mac or Linux box without a desktop, or when you want to script checks. It manages the same service, settings file and folders as the other tools, so it never disagrees with them. On Windows it works next to the Windows app. Two modes: the interface and single commands. Type buildspawn with no command to open the full-screen interface. It opens the launch flow on a first run, and the live monitor after that. A first run means: no settings file, no service installed and no server running. The interface opens only in a real terminal. In a scheduled task, a pipe or with --no-tui , the program prints the status as text instead. A command such as buildspawn status does one job and exits. Buildspawn 1.9.3 · ● Serving http://127.0.0.1:8080 · up 23s LIVE 1 Overview 2 Builds 3 Queue 4 Agents 5 Logs 6 Service 7 Settings ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── ┌─ Server ───────────────┐┌─ Builds running ───────┐┌─ Queue ────────────────┐ │ ● Running up 23s ││ 0 of 2 executors ││ 0 waiting · old 0:00 │ │ 2.589-buildspawn.21 ││ ░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░ 0% ││ ▁ 30 m │ └────────────────────────┘└────────────────────────┘└────────────────────────┘ ┌─ JVM heap ─────────────┐┌─ CPU ──────────────────┐┌─ Disk free · home ─────┐ │ 1.7 / 15.0 GB ││ 0 % ││ 913 GB free of 1006 GB │ └────────────────────────┘└────────────────────────┘└────────────────────────┘. The launch flow. The launch flow sets Buildspawn up in five steps. Nothing is changed until you press Start on the last step. At any time: Enter goes on, Esc goes back, Tab moves between fields, Space ticks a box, ? is help and q quits. The start screen: presets. If you have saved presets they are listed first, each with its home, port and how it runs, plus three more choices: Set up from the beginning , Use an existing home folder… (pick a folder that already holds Buildspawn data) and Open the monitor only. Press e , d or n to edit, delete or rename the selected preset. A preset keeps the home, port, who can connect, memory and the run options, so you can set up a second server the same way. Preset names may use letters, digits, space, dot, dash and underscore, up to 40 characters. The file is presets.json in your user configuration folder. See Where presets are saved. buildspawn launch --preset build-box --yes buildspawn preset list buildspawn preset save build-box buildspawn preset delete build-box. --preset skips the start screen. --yes accepts every default. The five steps. Step. What you choose. 1. Home. Where does Buildspawn keep its data? Type a folder. Tab completes the path, the arrow keys pick a suggestion, and b browses folders ( s uses the folder you are in). The program checks the folder as you type. The folder is made when you press Start. Suggestions include the default for a service and the homes of your presets. 2. Port & memory. The Port (checked for use as you type), Who can connect : This PC only (127.0.0.1, recommended) or The network (0.0.0.0, opens it to other computers), and Java memory (max) : 2g, 4g, 8g, 16g or a number you type. A bar shows the choice against the computer's memory. Automatic is a quarter of the computer, between 2 and 16 GB. 3. Run as. A service (a systemd service on Linux, a LaunchAgent on macOS, a Windows service on Windows), marked recommended, or Portable , which runs in this window, stops when you close it, needs no administrator rights and suits a tr"},
{"title":"Settings overview","href":"administration/settings-overview","headings":["How to open the Settings pages","Every Settings page","Settings that live on other pages","System settings","Management pages","Tools","Security settings","Appearance","Backups and upgrades","Tips","Related pages"],"text":"The Manage page lists every settings page of the server. This page tells you what each one is and where it is described in this guide. When to use it: when you know what you want to change but not which page to open, or when you want to see everything an administrator can set. How to open the Settings pages. Click Manage in the left rail. Find the page in the list. You only see the pages your permissions allow. Click it. Use the breadcrumb to come back to Manage. The list of Settings pages on the left of every Settings page. The pages that appear depend on the plugins that are installed and on your permissions. A person who only has the System read permission sees many pages read-only. Every Settings page. Page. Address. What it is. Where it is described. System. /configure. Server-wide settings: server address, quiet period, naming rule, defaults, and the settings of the bundled plugins. System settings. Security. /configureSecurity. Who may sign in and what they may do, and the protection settings. Security settings , Security realms , Authorization. Credentials. /credentials. The stores of sign-in details that builds use. Management pages › Credentials. Credential Providers. /configureCredentials. Which credential sources and kinds are allowed. Management pages › Credential Providers. Trusted certificates. /trustedCertificates. Certificates the server and agents trust. Trusted certificates. Users. /securityRealm/. Create, change and remove user accounts of the built-in user database. Users. Groups. /groups. Groups of users, for giving permissions to many people at once. Users. Roles. /roles. Roles that bundle permissions, assigned per project. Roles. Tools. /configureTools. Where the build tools are, and how agents get them. Tools. Plugins. /pluginManager. The group of plugin pages below. Plugins and updates. Available plugins. /pluginManager/available. Plugins from the update site that are not installed yet. Management pages › Available plugins. Installed plugins. /pluginManager/installed. Every plugin, with switches to turn them on or off. Management pages › Installed plugins. Download progress. /pluginManager/downloads/. Progress of plugin downloads. Management pages › Download progress. Advanced settings. /pluginManager/advanced. Install a plugin from a file and choose the update site. Management pages › Advanced settings. Nodes. /computer. The machines that run builds. Management pages › Nodes , Agents page , Agent settings. Clouds. /cloud. Cloud services that add agents when needed. Clouds. Agent registration. /agentRegistration. How new agents sign up with the server. Agent settings. Agent Telemetry. /agentTelemetrySettings. CPU, memory and disk figures that agents report. Agent telemetry. Root project. /rootProject. Connections, parameters and templates that every project inherits. Management pages › Root project , Global connections and Vault. Perforce roots. /perforce-roots. Saved Perforce server connections that build configurations reuse. Management pages › Perforce roots , Perforce roots. BuildPatch Tool. /buildpatchtool. Upload the Epic BuildPatch Tool once and send it to agents. Management pages › BuildPatch Tool. Appearance. /appearance. Theme, icon colours and other looks for the whole server. Appearance. In-process Script Approval. /scriptApproval. Approve Pipeline and Groovy scripts that need more rights than the sandbox allows. Security settings. SAML single sign-on. /MoPluginConfigView. Settings for sign-in with a SAML identity provider. Security realms. Reload Configuration from Disk. /reload. Load everything again from the data folder. Management pages › Reload Configuration from Disk. System Information. /systemInfo. Versions, properties, plugins, memory and thread dumps of the server. Management pages › System Information. Logs. /log. The server log and log recorders. Management pages › Logs. Load Statistics. /load-statistics. Charts of executors and queue length. Management pages › Load Statistics."},
{"title":"System settings","href":"administration/system-settings","headings":["How to open it","General","Restrict build configuration naming","Views tab bar","Buildspawn Location","Serve resource files from another domain","Global properties","Visual editor","Pipeline Speed / Durability","Usage Statistics","Project Health Metrics","HTTP Proxy Configuration","Global Build Time Out","Build timeout step","Timestamper","Perforce operations","Agent environment variables","Build console","Fingerprints","Administrative monitors","Global Build Discarders","Build steps","Default notification URL","Filter build step environment variables","Global Trusted and Untrusted Pipeline Libraries","Git","Perforce (P4)","Shell","E-mail Notification","Publish over CIFS, FTP and SSH","Publish over CIFS","Publish over FTP","SSH servers","SCM Polling","Where every other global setting lives","What you see when it works","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"The System page holds the server-wide settings: the server address, how builds are queued, limits and defaults, and the settings of the bundled plugins. When to use it: when you set up a new server, when builds queue too fast or too slowly, when the server sits behind a proxy, or when you want a default (such as a build timeout or a build discarder) to apply to every build configuration. How to open it. Click Manage in the left rail, then System. The address is /configure. You need the Manage permission to change the settings. People with the System read permission see the page read-only. Use the On this page index on the left to jump to a section. Change what you need. The Save and Apply buttons stay at the bottom of the window. Apply saves and stays on the page. Save saves and leaves it. The System page. The Save and Apply buttons stay at the bottom of the window. Each field has a small ? button. Click it to read the help for that field. Some sections only appear when they have something to set. For example, the Fingerprint storage list only shows when more than one storage type is installed. General. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Home directory. Shows the folder where Buildspawn keeps all its data: configuration, build results and plugins. You cannot change it here. Use the copy button to copy the path. See Backups and upgrades. Set when the server starts. D:\\Buildspawn\\data. System Message. A message shown at the top of the main page for every user. Write plain text, or HTML if your markup setting allows it. Preview shows how it looks. Empty. Perforce is down for maintenance until 18:00. Computer Retention Check Interval. How often Buildspawn checks whether an agent should be disconnected by its availability rule. Whole number, 1 to 60. Details are in Agent settings. 60. 30. Quiet period. Seconds that a newly triggered build waits in the queue before it starts. When several commits arrive close together, they go into one build instead of many. A new trigger while a build waits does not restart the wait. A build configuration can set its own quiet period. 5. 60 for a game repository with many small submits. SCM checkout retry count. How many times Buildspawn tries a version control checkout again when it fails, for example because the Perforce server is busy. 0 (no retry). 2. Default view. The view that opens first on the Projects page (see Views ). A list of all your views. It only shows when you have more than one view. The first view, all. Release jobs. Restrict build configuration naming. Tick Restrict build configuration naming to force a rule on the names of new build configurations. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Strategy (Naming Strategy). Pick Default (any name is allowed) or Pattern (the name must match a regular expression). Default. Pattern. Name Pattern. A regular expression that every name must match.*. (Win64|PS5|Switch)-[A-Za-z0-9-]+. Description. The message people see when a name does not match. Explain the rule in plain words. Empty. Start with the platform: Win64-, PS5- or Switch-. force existing. Also applies the rule to build configurations that already exist. They must match the next time someone saves them. Off. Off. Views tab bar. See Views for views and tabs. Two settings choose which tab bar shows your views, and which shows My Views. A plugin can offer its own tab bar. When only the built-in bar is installed, there is nothing to choose and the setting does not appear. Buildspawn Location. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Buildspawn URL. The address people use to open the server. Buildspawn puts it in links in e-mails and notifications, and agents use it for some links. Buildspawn cannot find out this address reliably by itself, so set it. Empty (a warning is shown). https://build.skyforge.example/. System Admin e-mail address. The From address of e-mails the server sends. You can add a display name. Empty. Buildspawn Server <builds@skyforge.example>. If a reverse proxy serv"},
{"title":"Tools","href":"administration/tools","headings":["What the Tools page shows","Add a tool","Install a version from an archive","Use the copy installed on the agents","Custom tools","Install tools on agents","The tool types","Automatic installers","Each tool","Custom archive","Git","JDK","JGit","JGit with Apache HTTP client","MSBuild","MSTest","Maven","Unity Editor","VSTest","Tool Locations on agents","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Tips"],"text":"Tell Buildspawn where to find the tools your builds use, such as Git, Maven, MSBuild or the Unity Editor, and let it copy them to your agents. A tool is a program a build step runs. You can use the copy that is already installed on each agent, or upload a version to the server once. Buildspawn then copies it to each agent the first time a build needs it. This way every agent builds with the same version. Settings › Tools. Each tool type has its own section with its versions. What the Tools page shows. Open Settings › Tools. The page has one section for each tool type that is set up, for example Git , Maven and VSTest. Each section has a table: Column. What it shows. Version. The name of the version. Default is the one steps use when they don't pick one. Usages. How many build steps use this version, or Not used. Default. Marks the default version of this tool type. Source. Found on agents when the agents' own copy is used, or the archive you uploaded. Actions. Edit the version, or remove it. Further down: Custom tools holds your own tools, uploaded as archives. See Custom tools. More tool types lists the types with nothing set up yet, such as JDK, MSBuild, MSTest and Unity Editor. Click Set up to add one. The Jump to list on the right takes you to each section. Add a tool. Install a version from an archive. Click Install tool at the top, or Install version in the tool's section. Pick the Tool type and click Next. The list holds the types that are not installed yet; choose Custom archive to upload your own tool. Click Choose file and pick a.zip or.tar.gz file with a bin folder inside. Check the Version. Buildspawn reads it from the file name; change it if needed. Tick Set as default if steps that pick Default should run this version. Click Install. The first step of Install tool: choose the type. Use the copy installed on the agents. If the tool is already installed on your agents, you don't need to upload it: In the tool's section, click Edit locations. Add a version, give it a name and type the folder where the tool lives on the agents, for example C:\\Program Files\\Unity\\Hub\\Editor\\2022.3.40f1. Click Save at the bottom of the page. Some tool types have extra settings in their section. For example, Maven lets you pick the default settings provider and global settings provider that every Maven step uses. Custom tools. For any other program, such as a console SDK tool or a shader compiler: Pack it as.zip ,.tar or.tar.gz. The file name becomes the tool ID and the folder name on the agents, so name it well, for example shadercomp-2.4.zip. In Custom tools , click Upload archive and pick the file. Use the tool in a step through its path, %tool.<id>%. Install tools on agents. You never copy tools to agents by hand. This is what happens: A build step picks a tool version, or uses %tool.<id>%. The first time a build on an agent needs that version, Buildspawn copies it from the server to the agent. The agent keeps the copy for the next builds, until you remove the version on the Tools page. Use the path in a script step like this: \"%tool.shadercomp-2.4%\\bin\\shadercomp.exe\" --platform win64 Shaders\\. The first build on a new agent takes longer, because it downloads the tool. Later builds use the copy on the agent. The tool types. Buildspawn has ten tool types. A tool type needs a Name that build steps pick, and the place where the program lives. Types that run on agents only store the location. The table says what each is for. Tool type. Used by. Fields. Custom archive. Any step, through %tool.<id>%. Uploaded archive. Git. Git version control, Git steps. Name, Path to Git executable. JDK. Java and Maven steps. Name, JAVA_HOME. JGit. Git version control without a Git program. None. JGit with Apache HTTP client. Git over HTTP on Windows servers. None. MSBuild. MSBuild step. Name, Path to MSBuild Installation, Default parameters. MSTest. MSTest step. Name, Path to MSTest, Default parameters, Omit NoIsolation. Maven. Maven step. Name, MAVEN_"},
{"title":"Trusted certificates","href":"administration/trusted-certificates","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","The page","Added to this server","The Add certificates dialog","Windows certificate store","Agents","The expiry notice","Where the files are","Other places to manage certificates","The terminal command","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Add your company’s root certificates, so that the server and its agents trust your internal Perforce, Git, proxy and artifact servers. When a server uses HTTPS or TLS, it shows a certificate that proves who it is. A root CA (certificate authority) is the company that signs such certificates. Java, which runs Buildspawn, knows the public root CAs. It does not know your own company’s root CA, so it refuses to connect to a server signed by it, with errors such as PKIX path building failed. This page fixes that. Buildspawn never changes the cacerts file of the Java it ships with, because an upgrade would replace it. It keeps its own folder of certificates instead. When to use it. Use it when a build or a plugin fails to reach an internal server because the certificate is not trusted: a Perforce server over SSL, a Git server, a package feed, or a proxy that re-signs traffic. To do the whole job with an example, follow Trust your company’s root CA. How to set it up. Ask your IT team for the root certificate (a.crt ,.cer ,.pem ,.der or.p7b file) and for its SHA-256 fingerprint (a code like 3F:9A:C1:07:… ). Open Settings › Security › Trusted certificates ( /manage/trustedCertificates/ ). Only administrators see it. Click Add certificates. Paste the certificate text, or click Choose file… and pick the file. Each certificate found appears as a card. Compare its SHA-256 with the one from your IT team. Untick any you do not want. Click Add 1 certificate (or Add n certificates ). Click Restart the server in the yellow bar, at a time that suits you. Java reads trusted certificates only when it starts. The Trusted certificates page. The page shows Restart needed as long as the certificates on disk differ from what the running server loaded. The page. Added to this server. A table of the certificates you added, with a count such as 3 · plus 117 built into Java. Until you add one it says No certificates added yet. The server trusts the certificates built into Java. Column or button. What it shows or does. Name. The subject of the certificate (its common name). Issued by. Who signed it. Expires. The last day it is valid. An Expired badge shows if the date has passed. SHA-256. The fingerprint, shortened. A copy button copies the whole thing. Added on , Added by. When and by whom. Add certificates. Opens the add dialog. Remove. Tick one or more rows, then click it. It asks Remove … ? The server stops trusting it after the next restart. View. Tick one row to see all its details. The Add certificates dialog. You can paste text that has one or more -----BEGIN CERTIFICATE----- blocks (other text around them is ignored), or choose a file of these types:pem ,.crt ,.cer ,.der and.p7b (also.p7c ). Text (Base64) and binary files both work. A file that holds a whole chain adds every certificate in it. The dialog closes only with Cancel , Esc or the add button. Nothing is added until you press the button. Each certificate found shows its name, who issued it, its dates and its full SHA-256 fingerprint, with a reminder to compare it with your IT team. It also gets badges: Badge. Meaning. What happens. Root CA. A certificate authority that signed itself. Ticked, can be added. Intermediate CA. A certificate authority signed by another one. Ticked, can be added. Not a CA certificate. The certificate of one server, not of an authority. A warning explains that you trust a single server certificate, and that it must be replaced when the server’s certificate changes. Can be added after the warning. Expired. The valid-until date has passed. Not ticked by default. You can still tick it. Already trusted, skipped. It is already in your added list or is built into Java. Never added twice. Messages you may see: 1 certificate found or n certificates found ; No certificate found. Paste text that starts with -----BEGIN CERTIFICATE----- or choose a certificate file. ; and This is a private key. Buildspawn only needs the public certificate; the key was not kept. Buildspawn ref"},
{"title":"User settings","href":"administration/user-settings","headings":["When to use it","How to open it","Every setting","Account","Security","Appearance","Preferences","Feature preview","Picture from Entra ID","My machines","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Every person has their own settings page for their name, password, API tokens, theme, time zone and more. When to use it. Use it to set up your own account, or to help someone else (an administrator can open any user’s settings from Settings › Users ). The settings belong to one person and do not change anything for other people. How to open it. Click your initial (the round button) at the right of the top bar. Choose one of the pages: Account , Security , Appearance or Preferences. Your profile page (your name) has My machines too. Change what you need and click Save. A user's Account page. Page. What it holds. Account. Full name, description, credentials, e-mail, notification URL, Perforce e-mail, SAML attributes, time zone. Security. Password, API tokens, session termination. Appearance. Theme, console links, console line times. Preferences. Start view and search. Feature preview. Switches for trial features. The page is hidden when no trial feature exists. My machines. Your own computers that can run your builds. See Personal agents. Every setting. Account. Setting. What it does. Default. Example. Full name. How other people see you. Use your real name, so people recognise you instead of your ID. The user ID. Dana Kim. Description. A short text on your profile page, with a Preview. It follows the markup formatter on Security settings. Empty. Build engineer, Unreal and Perforce. Credentials. Credentials that only you can use. They are not available to anyone else. The section opens your own credentials store. None. A personal Perforce password. Email. Where build e-mails for you are sent. Empty. dana.k@studio.example. Notification URL. Chooses which page the links in notifications and in other tools open: the default page, or another provider’s page. Default. Default. Perforce User Property › P4 Email. The e-mail address from your Perforce user. It is read-only: it is defined in the Perforce user spec. From Perforce. —. Saml Custom Attributes. Extra values your company’s sign-in sent about you, shown as label : value. Read-only. They are set up on SAML sign-in. Shown only for SAML users. None. Department : Art. Time zone. The zone used to show dates and times to you, instead of the server’s zone. The server’s zone. Europe/Lisbon. Security. Setting. What it does. Password and Confirm Password. Change your password. Leave both boxes as they are to keep it. Only for people of Buildspawn’s own user database. The password rule from Security realms applies. API Token. Tokens that let scripts and tools sign in as you. Add new token takes a Name and an Expiration (7, 30, 60, 90 days, Custom or No Expiration ). Current token(s) shows name, expiry, created and last used. The full guide is API tokens. Session Termination. The button Terminate All Sessions signs you out of every browser and device, and asks you to sign in again. It confirms first, and then says Operation success. All sessions were terminated. Use it when a computer is lost. At a glance. A side card: number of API tokens, last use and whether a password is set. Appearance. Setting. What it does. Default. Themes. How pages look for you: Default (light), Dark , or Dark (System) , which follows your computer’s setting. Your choice wins over the administrator’s default. See Appearance and themes. The administrator’s choice. Console URL Provider. Which page opens for build console links. Views listed higher win over those below. Use Add to add a provider and Delete to remove one. Your list is used instead of the one the administrator set. Default, then Pipeline Graph View. Console line times › Line times. How the console output times each line: Time of day , Time since the build started or Off. The Time control of the console page changes it too. The administrator’s choice. Preferences. Setting. What it does. Default. Default Views Tab Bar › My Views › Default View. The view you see first on the dashboard, and the view selected when you open your private views. The server’s "},
{"title":"Users and permissions","href":"administration/users-and-permissions","headings":["Users","Create a user","Change or delete a user","Groups","Create a group","Project roles","Give a role on a project","Make your own role","Permissions","Tips","Go deeper","Security realms","Entra ID sign-in","SAML sign-in","Authorization","Roles","Users and groups","User settings","Security settings","Trusted certificates"],"text":"Control who can sign in to Buildspawn and what each person can see and change. Buildspawn controls access with four things that work together: Thing. What it is. Where. Users. The people who can sign in. Settings › Users. Groups. A named set of users, so you can give rights to many people at once. Settings › Groups. Roles. A named set of permissions, such as Developer. Settings › Roles , and the Roles tab of each project. Permissions. The single rights, such as Job: Build. Roles are made of them. Settings › Security. You need the System administrator role (the Overall: Administer permission) to change any of these. Users. Settings › Users lists everybody who can sign in, with their groups. Your own row is marked You. The Users page. The buttons on the right edit or delete a user. Create a user. Open Settings › Users and click Create User. Type a Username. People sign in with it, and it cannot be changed later. Type the Password twice, the Full name and the Email. Click Create User. Back in the list, click Add to group on the new row to put the user in a group. The Create User form. A new user can sign in at once, but sees nothing until you give them a role or permissions. See Project roles below. Change or delete a user. Click the settings button on a row to change the user's name, email or password. Click the red bin to delete the user. You cannot delete yourself. Each person can also change their own password, theme and API tokens from the account menu (the round button with their initial). See API tokens. Groups. A group collects users, and other groups, so you can give a role to everybody in it at once. When someone joins the team, you add them to the group and they get the right access straight away. The group All users is built in. It means every signed-in user. You cannot rename, empty or delete it. Where a permission card says Authenticated Users , it means this group. Create a group. Open Settings › Groups and click Create group. Type a Name , for example Artists. Buildspawn makes the ID from it. You can change the ID now, but not later, because roles and permissions refer to it. Add a Description if it helps. Optional: pick a Parent group. Members of this group then also belong to the parent. Click Create group. Then add members from the group's row or from the Users page. Create a group. The list behind shows the members, subgroups and roles of each group. Give roles to groups, not to single users. It is much easier to see who can do what, and to change it later. Project roles. A role is a named set of permissions. You give a role to a user or group on a project, and it applies to that project and everything inside it: its subprojects and build configurations. Give a role at the Root project and it applies to the whole server. Buildspawn has six built-in roles: Role. What it allows. Where you can give it. Project viewer. See the project, its build configurations and builds, and download artifacts. Any project. Developer. Everything a viewer can do, plus run and stop builds, read settings, use workspaces, edit build descriptions and tag sources. Any project. Project administrator. Everything a developer can do, plus configure, create, move and delete build configurations and subprojects, manage views, and give roles on this project. Any project. Build settings administrator. Change the steps, actions and parameters a project passes down to every build configuration below it. Any project. Agent manager. Add, configure, connect, disconnect and delete agents, and move them between pools. Root only. System administrator. Everything on the server. Root only. Settings › Roles: the built-in roles, and the form to make your own. Give a role on a project. Open the project (or Root project for the whole server) and click the Roles tab. Click Assign a role. Choose User or Group , and type the user ID or group name. Pick the role and click Assign , then Save. The Roles tab also shows the roles inherited from above and Your roles"},
{"title":"Users and groups","href":"administration/users","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Create a user","The Users list","Change a password","End all sessions of a user","Groups","Create a group","The Groups list","The page of one group","From the terminal","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Create the people who can sign in, put them in groups, and give a whole group a role or permission at once. When to use it. Use these pages when people sign in with Buildspawn’s own user database (see Security realms ). With Entra ID or SAML, people and groups come from your company, so you do not create users here. The page Users then shows only people who signed in before, and the groups that Buildspawn keeps are not used for them. You need Administer to create, change or delete users and groups. How to set it up. Create a user. Open Settings › Users and click Create User. Fill in the form (see the table below). Click Create User. Back in the list, click Add to group on the new row and tick the groups. Every user is already in All users. The Create User form. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Username. What the person types to sign in. It is the user ID. Use letters, digits, dashes and underscores. It cannot be changed later. Empty. ht3-artist. Password. The first password. It must pass the password rule on Settings › Security. Empty. (a long password). Confirm password. The same password again. Empty. —. Full name. The name other people see. Empty. Alex Artist. Email. Where build e-mails for the person are sent. Empty. alex@studio.example. A new user can sign in at once, but sees nothing until you give them permissions or a role. The Users list. Settings › Users lists the people who can sign in, with columns User ID , Name and Groups. Your own row is marked You. The text above the list links to the longer list of every user Buildspawn has seen, including people who only made a commit. The Users list. The icons on the right open and delete a user. Button. What it does. Configure. Opens the settings of that user: name, e-mail, password and the other user settings. Delete. Asks Delete Buildspawn user ‘…’? and removes the account. You cannot delete yourself. Add to group. Opens the list of groups to tick. They are in All users already. Change a password. Open Settings › Users and click Configure on the user. People can also open their own page from the account menu and choose Security. Type the new password in Password and again in Confirm Password. Leave both boxes as they are to keep the old password. Click Save. End all sessions of a user. On the Security page of a user, Session Termination has the button Terminate All Sessions. It signs the user out of every browser and device, and disconnects computers that use their sessions. They must sign in again. Use it when a laptop is lost. It does not remove API tokens. See User settings. Groups. A group collects users and other groups. You give a role or permission to the group, and everyone in it has it. When someone joins the team you add them to the group and they get the right access straight away. Groups work in the permission cards and on the Roles tab of every project. Groups are kept only by Buildspawn’s own user database. The group All users (ID authenticated ) is built in. It means every signed-in user. You cannot rename, empty, delete or add it to another group. Where a card says Authenticated Users , it means this group. Create a group. Open Settings › Groups and click Create group. Type a Name , for example Artists. The ID is made from it. Optional: choose a Parent group. Click Create group. The Groups list. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Name. Shown in lists and pickers. You can change it at any time. Empty. Artists. ID. What roles and permissions point to. Letters, digits, dots, dashes and underscores. It cannot change later. Made from the name. artists. Description. A line about the group. Empty. The art team. Parent group. Optional. Members of this group then also belong to the parent. No parent group. Studio staff. The Groups list. The columns are Name , ID , Description , Members , Subgroups , Roles and Actions. Use Filter by name or ID to find one. The page of one group. Click a group to open its page. A list of sections on the side shows: Section."},
{"title":"The Windows app","href":"administration/windows-launcher","headings":["When to use it","Run here","The tray icon","Settings","Advanced","The buttons","Windows service","The LocalService account","If Buildspawn stops soon after it starts","Certificates","About","Setup: the installer","The wizard","Silent install and the switches","The portable zip","The service commands","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Buildspawn.exe is the window you use to start, stop and set up the Buildspawn server on a Windows computer. Every Windows download of the server includes the app. It has five tabs: Run here , Settings , Windows service , Certificates and About. Open it from the Start menu ( Buildspawn ) or run Buildspawn.exe from the program folder. A short walk-through is in Get started: the Windows app. This page lists every control. The app is for the server. The small program for a build agent is described in The agent tray app. Buildspawn runs either inside the app or as the Windows service, on one data folder, never both at once. The app checks this and tells you which one is running. When to use it. Use the app to try Buildspawn out, to change its settings with a form instead of a text file, to install the Windows service, and to add your company's root certificates. On a server that nobody signs in to, install the service once and then you rarely open the app. Run here. This tab starts Buildspawn inside the app, for this signed-in user. It stops when you stop it or close the app (see the tray icon below). Control. What it does. Status and a coloured label. Shows Stopped , Starting here , Running here (with the address, the process number and how long it has run), Stopping or Failed to start. It also shows Service running or Service stopping when the Windows service owns the data folder. Settings. The settings file this copy uses. Start. Starts Buildspawn here. If you have unsaved changes on the Settings tab it asks whether to save them first. It refuses, and says why, when the settings have a problem or when the service already runs on the same data folder. Stop. Stops the server and waits until it has really ended. Open in browser. Opens the web interface at the address and port from the settings. Open logs folder. Opens the folder with buildspawn.log. The live log. The server's output as it starts and runs. The status turns to Running when the server says it is fully up. First start: initial admin password and Copy. On the first start the server makes a one-time password for the user admin. It is shown here until you finish the setup page in the browser. Copy puts it on the clipboard. The tray icon. When you close the window while Buildspawn runs here, a question appears: Buildspawn is still running here. Stop it, or keep it running with an icon in the notification area? The buttons are Keep running in the tray , Stop and close and Cancel. The tray icon (the notification area at the bottom right) has a menu: Menu item. What it does. Open Buildspawn. Shows the app window again. Open in browser. Opens the web interface. Stop. Stops the server. Exit. Closes the app. Hover over the icon to see the state. If the server stops by itself, a balloon tells you why. Settings. The form writes buildspawn.cfg. The Windows service and the terminal program read the same file. See Settings files for every key and the placeholders. Field. What it does. Default. Port. The web port. Next to it the app says whether the port is already used, for example In use by Buildspawn running here or In use by the Buildspawn service. 8080. Listen on. This computer only or Network (all interfaces). Choose the network when agents and other people must reach the server. Network. Memory. A slider from 2 GB up to 32 GB (never more than the computer has, minus 1 GB). The label beside it shows of N GB in this machine. This is the Java heap. A quarter of the computer's memory, between 2 and 16 GB. Data folder (with Browse… ). Hint: Jobs, build history and settings. The home of the server. C:\\ProgramData\\Buildspawn\\data (installed) or data in the program folder (portable). Logs folder (with Browse… ). Hint: buildspawn.log, gc.log and heap dumps. C:\\ProgramData\\Buildspawn\\logs or logs in the program folder. Advanced. Field. What it does. Java options. A multi-line box with the Java options. {data} , {logs} and {install} (the program folder) stand for those folders. Folders na"},
{"title":"Connect an agent","href":"agents/agent-connection","headings":["When to use it","How the connection works","WebSocket or TCP","The settings file","Start-up options","Registration token","Command-line agent","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"An agent connects to the server from its own machine. This page explains how that connection works, the settings file that controls it, and the options for WebSocket, TCP, proxies and Java. When to use it. Read this page when an agent will not connect, when it must pass a company proxy or firewall, when you need to give the agent more memory, or when you want to know what is in conf/buildspawn-agent.properties. How the connection works. The agent calls the server. This is the inbound agent ( Launch agent by connecting it to the controller ). The server never needs to reach the agent, so the agent can sit behind a router or a company firewall. It only needs to reach the server's address. The agent starts with the server address and one of two ways to prove who it is: a secret (an agent made with New Node ), or a registration request that an administrator approves (see Install an agent ). After approval, the secret is written into the settings file for you. The connection is a WebSocket by default. A WebSocket is a normal web connection on the same address and port as the Buildspawn pages. No extra port is needed. If the connection drops, the agent tries again by itself. The tray app and the terminal program show the reason in their Connection log. WebSocket or TCP. WebSocket. TCP. How. Uses the server's web address and port. A separate port on the server for agents ( Settings › Security , TCP port for inbound agents ). Default. Yes. No. Used only when you turn WebSocket off. Firewalls and proxies. Works wherever the web pages work, if the proxy allows WebSocket. Needs the extra port to be open. Turn it off with. —. webSocket=false in the settings file, or leave out -webSocket on the command line. When the server has the TCP port turned off, an agent that asks for TCP switches to WebSocket by itself. To forbid that switch, set webSocketFallback=false in the settings file, or add -noWebSocketFallback to a command-line agent. Then an agent that cannot use TCP simply fails, and says why. The agent page on the server, Connect the agent , shows a notice when the TCP port is off ( The TCP port is disabled so TCP agents may not be connected. You may still use WebSocket agents. ) and the command with -webSocket in it. See Security settings. The settings file. Every agent folder holds conf/buildspawn-agent.properties. It is a plain text file with one key=value per line. Lines that start with # are comments. Keep it private: it holds the agent's secret. The install scripts lock it to the service account. Key. What it does. Default. Example. serverUrl. Address of the server. Required. It must start with http:// or https://. A closing / is added if missing. empty (the zip for one agent fills it in). https://buildspawn.studio.example/. name. The agent's name. The machine's host name. win-build-04. registrationToken. Token that the server may require for self-registration. See below. empty. k3J9x…. workDir. The agent's work folder. A relative path starts at the agent folder. work. D:\\agent-work. secret. The agent's secret. Written for you after approval, or by the zip made for one agent. Do not share it. empty. (never type it into a ticket). javaOptions. Extra options for Java, such as memory. Read at every start of run-agent. A service uses them after its next start; on Windows (and in the Linux and macOS service definitions) run install-service again after you change it. empty. -Xmx4g. proxy. Connect through a web proxy: host:port or http://user:pass@host:port. The port defaults to 8080. none. proxy.studio.example:3128. webSocket. true or false. false uses the plain TCP connection. true. false. webSocketFallback. false stops the automatic switch to WebSocket when the TCP port is off (the same as -noWebSocketFallback ). on. false. The agent also writes two keys of its own while it waits for approval, registrationKey and registrationId. Leave them alone. The tray app and the terminal program edit serverUrl , name , workDir , javaOptions , pro"},
{"title":"Builds at a time (executors)","href":"agents/agent-executors","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","How to choose a number","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"An executor is one slot on an agent that runs one build. The number of executors is the number of builds that the agent runs at the same time. When to use it. Set it when you add an agent, and change it when you see builds waiting while agents are half idle, or when builds slow each other down because the machine is overloaded. How to set it up. Open Agents , click the agent, then Configure. In General , set Number of executors. Click Save. The change applies at once. Running builds are not stopped. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Number of executors. The most builds that run on this agent at the same time. An agent needs at least 1. A good first value is the number of CPU cores. A higher value raises the total work done, because one build may wait for the disk while another uses the CPU, but each build takes longer. 1. 2. Other places that show or set it: Place. What it does. Agents list. The Status column says how many executors are free. The header counts busy executors of all executors. An opened row lists each busy executor with its build. Pool page and Pools tab. Show the executors of each pool. My machines. The owner of a personal machine sets Builds at a time there. Default 1. Tray app , terminal program. Show Builds at a time read only. Set it on the server. Agent's Load Statistics. A chart of busy and total executors over time. The built-in node. Set its executors to 0 so that it runs no builds itself. It still runs the light \"flyweight\" jobs, such as the top-level part of a pipeline. How to choose a number. Devkits and consoles: 1. The devkit can run one thing at a time. Unreal Engine or Unity builds: usually 1 or 2. Each build uses many cores and a lot of memory, and two builds of the same project need two workspaces on disk. Small tests and scripts: up to the number of cores. Personal machine: 1, so builds do not slow your own work. Every executor needs its own workspace folder, so more executors also use more disk. For a game project of 200 GB, 2 executors need about 400 GB. A worked example. The agent win-build-02 has 16 cores, 64 GB of memory and a 2 TB disk. It runs the studio's quick Unit tests build configuration and the heavy Windows Client build. Set Number of executors to 2. Click Save. Start a client build and a unit-test build together. What you see when it runs. On the Agents page, win-build-02 shows Building and 0 free while both run. With one running, it shows 1 free and the pool still counts the agent as available. The third build waits in the queue with the reason that all executors are busy, until one is free. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Builds are very slow since you raised the number. The machine is overloaded. Lower it, and check CPU and memory in Telemetry. The disk fills up. Each executor has its own workspace. Lower the number or clean workspaces (the tray app has Work folders ). The form refuses 0 for an agent. An agent needs 1 or more. To stop builds, use Mark offline. Only the built-in node may have 0. Two builds of one build configuration cannot run at once. The build configuration may have Execute concurrent builds off. That is a setting of the build configuration, not of the agent. Related pages. Agent settings. The Agents page. Pools. Personal agents."},
{"title":"Labels and agent requirements","href":"agents/agent-labels-requirements","headings":["When to use it","Labels","Label expressions","Agent requirements","How to set it up","Conditions","Rules that come from build steps","Compatible agents","Agent parameters","Custom parameters","Exposed environment variables","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Labels and requirements decide which agent may run which build. A label is a tag you put on an agent. A requirement is a rule on a build configuration that an agent must meet, such as \"Windows, at least 32 GB of memory, in the Unreal pool\". When to use it. Use a label when one word is enough: windows , ps5 , gpu. Use a requirement when the rule is about a value that the agent reports, such as its operating system version, its free disk space, its name or its pool. Requirements need no tagging by hand. The agents report the values themselves. Labels. A label is a word, or several words separated by spaces, in the agent's Labels field (see Agent settings ). Example: windows unreal-5.4 gpu gives the agent three labels. A label can have any characters but spaces. Avoid ! & | < > ( ) , because label expressions use them. Every agent also has a label that is its own name. The built-in node has the label built-in. Labels need not be about the operating system. They can say what is installed: vs2022 , docker. Label expressions. A build configuration can ask for agents with a label expression. The expression is true for an agent when its labels match. Write. Meaning. Example. a. The agent has label a. windows. a && b. Both labels. windows && gpu. a || b. Either label. ps5 || xbox. !a. The agent does not have a. !devkit. ( … ). Group parts. windows && (gpu || cpu-only). a -> b. If it has a , it must have b. gpu -> vulkan. a <-> b. It has both or neither. ps5 <-> devkit. A wrong expression is refused with That is not a label expression: …. An empty expression lets any agent run the build configuration. Click a label on an agent's page to see its label page: which agents have it and which build configurations use it. Agent requirements. Requirements are rules on parameters. Every build configuration has a page for them. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings. Open Agent Requirements. (Reading it needs permission to read the build configuration; changing it needs Configure.). Click Add to add a rule. Pick a parameter (the field offers all the parameters that agents report, and you can type your own name), a condition , and a value. Save the row. The page shows N of M agents can run this configuration and updates as you change the rules. To change the label expression, click Edit label expression on the row of kind label. The old Labels field shows as one such row. Agent Requirements, with the compatible agents listed below. A build runs only on an agent whose parameters satisfy every row. A build configuration has at most 100 rules. A rule that is already there is refused ( This requirement is already there ). Conditions. Condition. True when the agent's value…. exists. is there (the agent has the parameter). does not exist. is not there. equals , does not equal. is the same as, or different from, the value. more than , no more than , less than , no less than. compares as numbers. The value must be a number ( This condition compares numbers, and this is not one ). starts with , ends with , contains , does not contain. compares text. matches , does not match. matches a regular expression, for example WIN-BUILD-0[1-4]. version more than , version no more than , version less than , version no less than. compares as version numbers, so 10.0.9 is less than 10.0.10. Except for exists and does not exist , a condition needs a value ( This condition needs a value ). Rules that come from build steps. Some build steps need a program on the agent's search path: for example a.NET step needs dotnet , a Docker step needs docker. These steps add rules by themselves. They show on the page as rows marked From a step (and inherited from a template when they come from one). You cannot delete them, because the step needs them. To remove one, change or disable the step. If the tool is on an agent in an unusual place, click Ignore on the row. Builds of the build configuration may then start on an agent without it, and fail there. If no"},
{"title":"Agent settings","href":"agents/agent-settings","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","General","Launch","Availability","Node Properties","Properties that are not on the form","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"The Configure page of an agent holds everything that you can set for one machine: its name, how many builds it runs, its labels, how it connects, when it is online and a list of extra properties. When to use it. Use it when you add an agent with New Node , and later when you want to change its labels, its work folder, its number of builds, its schedule, or tell Buildspawn where a tool lives on that machine. How to set it up. Open Agents and click the agent's name. (To create one, click New Node , type a name, choose Permanent Agent and click Create ; the same form opens.). Open the agent's Configure page. From the Agents list you can also use the row menu entry Settings of an opened row. Fill in the fields described below. Click Save. Apply saves and stays on the page. The page has a summary card at the top (name, executors, labels, launch method and availability) and four sections: General , Launch , Availability and Node Properties. You need the Configure permission on the agent. The agent Configure page: General, Launch and Availability. Every field. General. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Name. The name that identifies the agent on this server. It need not match the host name. These characters are not allowed: ? * / \\ % ! @ # $ ^ & | < > [ ] : ; What you typed in New Node. win-build-04. Description. Free text. Plain text with a preview. empty. Rack 3, RTX 4080, Unreal 5.4 builds. Number of executors. How many builds may run on this agent at the same time. At least 1. See Builds at a time. 1. 2. Remote root directory. A folder on the agent machine that Buildspawn uses for workspaces and tools. Use a full path. It must be local to the agent. If you use a relative path, it starts from the folder where the agent runs. Keep it where a reboot does not delete it, so that workspaces and tools stay cached. empty. D:\\buildspawn-agent\\work. Labels. Words, separated by spaces, that tag the agent. Build configurations pick agents by label. Avoid ! & | < > ( ) in a label, because label expressions use them. See Labels and agent requirements. empty. windows unreal-5.4 gpu. Usage. Use this node as much as possible : any build that the pool and labels allow may run here. Only run build configurations with label expressions matching this node : reserves the agent for build configurations that ask for one of its labels. Use this node as much as possible. Reserved, for a devkit host. Launch. Field. What it does. Launch method. How the agent starts and connects. Two choices, described next. Launch agent by connecting it to the controller. The inbound agent. The agent machine opens the connection to the server. The server does not need to reach the agent, so it works behind a firewall or home router. This is what the downloads and the New Node zip use. The agent page shows Connect the agent with the commands, the download for this node and the secret. See Connect an agent. The launcher has no field of its own in the current version. Give the options to the agent itself (for example webSocket=false in its settings file). If an old agent still has launcher properties, a note says setting them is deprecated. Launch agent via execution of command on the controller. The server runs a command that starts the agent on the other machine, for example over SSH. The command's input and output carry the connection. Field. What it does. Example. Launch command. The command that the server runs on itself. It must start java -jar agent.jar on the target machine and keep its standard input and output connected. ssh build@mac-mini-02 java -jar /Users/build/agent.jar. Use the inbound agent when you can. The command launcher needs the server to reach the machine, and the command runs with the server's rights. Availability. Controls when Buildspawn starts and stops this agent. The field Availability offers three choices. Choice. What it does. Fields. Keep this agent online as much as possible. The agent stays online. If it drops (a network fault, for examp"},
{"title":"Agent terminal program","href":"agents/agent-terminal","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Options","The keys","The eight views","1 Overview","2 Builds","3 Connection log","4 Work folders","5 Service","6 Settings","7 Agent log","8 Certificates","The certs command","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"The agent terminal program, buildspawn-agent-tui , shows what an agent is doing in a console window. It works on Windows, Linux and macOS, over a remote shell too, and it lets you pause the agent, read logs, clean work folders and change settings. When to use it. Use it on a build machine that you reach over SSH or remote desktop, on a machine with no desktop, or when you prefer the keyboard. On Windows it shows the same information as the tray app. Like the tray app, it talks to the agent on the same machine, so it keeps working when the server cannot be reached. How to set it up. It is already in every agent download, in the bin folder of the agent folder. Nothing to install. Open a terminal on the agent machine. On a service machine, run it as the account that runs the agent, or as an administrator or with sudo , so that it may control the agent. Go to the agent folder and start the program: # Linux and macOS cd /opt/buildspawn-agent./bin/buildspawn-agent-tui # Windows cd C:\\buildspawn-agent bin\\buildspawn-agent-tui.exe. Press 1 to 8 to change view. Press ? for help and q to leave. Leaving the program does not stop the agent. The screen says Terminal closed. The agent is still running. Run buildspawn-agent-tui to come back. Options. Option. What it does. Default. --home FOLDER. The agent folder to control. The folder above the program's own bin folder. --view N. Open on view 1 to 8. 1. --once. Draw one frame as plain text and exit. Good for scripts, logs and support tickets. off. --cols N. With --once : the width of the frame. The window width. --endpoint FILE. Path of the agent's local connection file. <work folder>/control/endpoint.json. --version. Print the program version. The program needs a real terminal. With output sent to a file or a pipe, use --once. buildspawn-agent-tui --once --view 3 --cols 100. The keys. Key. What it does. 1 to 8. Switch view. p. Pause or resume. The running build finishes, and no new build starts while paused. r. Reconnect to the server now. If the agent is not running, look for it again. b. Open this agent's page in the browser. ? Help with every key. q. Quit this screen (the agent keeps running). Asks Quit or Stay. Ctrl + C. Quit at once. Esc. Close a dialog or cancel an edit. The screen looks again every 2 seconds. When the agent cannot be reached, the data is greyed out and marked last known. The eight views. 1 Overview. Current build : job and number, step, elapsed time, a progress bar and the last log lines. No build is running. when idle. This machine : CPU, RAM and free disk of the work folder, with bars that turn yellow and red. Agent version, Java version and Builds at a time. On a personal machine: Personal agent and the owner. 2 Builds. The current build, Waiting for this agent (n) with the reason (not on a personal machine) and the recent builds with result, duration and time. Key. What it does. ↑ ↓. Pick a build. Enter. Open the build's log in the browser. x. Stop the running build, after you confirm. s. Personal machines only: copy a share link for the selected build. On a personal machine the view says Only your builds are shown. Other people can't see them unless you share a link. 3 Connection log. Every connect, disconnect and reconnect with the reason in plain words and a summary such as 3 drops today, longest offline 4 min. No events yet. when empty. 4 Work folders. The size of each build configuration's folder, and the total. c cleans the selected folder and a cleans all of them, each after a confirmation. A folder that a build uses cannot be cleaned. 5 Service. Shows the service that keeps the agent running: a Windows service, buildspawn-agent with systemd on Linux, or com.buildspawn.agent with launchd on macOS. It shows whether it is installed, running, its start type and account. Keys: s start, x stop, t restart, i install, u remove. Each asks to confirm. Stop, restart and remove warn that A running build will be stopped. Install and remove run the scripts described in"},
{"title":"The Agents page","href":"agents/agents-page","headings":["The page at a glance","The summary strip","The list","Filters, search and sorting","Expanded rows","The row menu","Messages and Undo","Move to pool","Show connection log","Personal machines","Open one agent","Tips","Related pages"],"text":"Agents are the machines that run your builds. The Agents page lists them all in one live table, with their health, their current builds and quick actions. Click Agents in the top bar. A red number on it means some agents are offline. The Agents tab: the summary strip, the filter bar and the live list. The page at a glance. The header counts agents, online and offline agents, and busy executors (an executor is one slot that runs one build at a time; an agent can have several). The buttons on the right: Refresh , Configure monitors (see Telemetry ), Install agents (see Install an agent ) and New Node. The tabs : Agents, Pools (see The Pool Scheduler ), Clouds (see Clouds ) and Load Statistics. An Unauthorized tab appears only when agents are waiting for approval. In the top bar, a red number on Agents counts the offline agents. The side panel lists your pools. See Pools. The summary strip. Six cards sit above the list: Card. What it counts. Total agents. All agents, and how many send telemetry. Building. Agents running at least one build, with their share of all agents. Idle. Online agents with nothing to do. Offline. Agents that are not connected, and how many of them were marked offline by hand. Average CPU and Average memory. Across all online agents, with a small chart of the last refreshes. The list. Each row is one agent. The coloured bar on the left of a row shows its worst value: yellow for a warning, red for critical. Column. What it shows. Agent. The name, the operating system and the pool, with the pool's icon. Click the name to open the agent, or the pool to open the pool. Status. Idle, Building or Offline, and how many executors are free. \"Marked offline\" means someone took it offline by hand. Current build. The build it runs, with its number and time so far. For an offline agent, the reason. CPU , Memory , Disk , Network , GPU. Live values from telemetry , with small bars and charts. Pressure. One number for how loaded the agent is, from 0 to 100. It weighs CPU 35%, memory 30%, disk used 15%, disk input/output 10% and GPU 10%. Freshness. How old the latest sample is: Fresh, Stale (no sample for a while) or Offline. The colours come from the thresholds under Configure monitors. They only colour the page; they never stop or fail a build. On the screenshots in this guide the disk shows Critical on every agent, because the demo machine's D: drive is nearly full. The list refreshes by itself every 5 seconds ( Live · 5 s at the bottom). Below it you can choose how many Rows to show per page and move between pages. An agent that is running a build shows it in the Current build column. Filters, search and sorting. Use the bar above the list to find agents: All / Online / Offline chips, each with a count. Pool : one pool or all pools. Platform : Windows, Linux, macOS and so on. Search agents : type part of a name. Warnings only : show only agents with a warning or critical value. Offline agents count as warnings. Sort : the default, Offline first , puts offline agents at the top and then sorts by pressure. The arrow button flips the order. Under the Offline filter the list shows each agent's launch method instead of its live values. Your filters are kept in the page address. Bookmark it, or send it to a colleague, and the list opens with the same filters. The old address for disconnected agents opens the Offline filter. Expanded rows. Click the arrow at the start of a row to open it. An open row. The busy executor's build is at the top. The open row shows: Each busy executor's build : the executor number, the build with a link, a progress bar against the usual duration, the time so far and the estimate (for example 25m 56s of ~4m ), the current step, and × to stop the build. Idle executors are not listed. Details : CPU cores, memory and swap, disk read and write, network in and out, telemetry freshness and uptime, the agent's pool, executors and labels, and its connection. Filesystems : every drive with how full it is. GPU("},
{"title":"Clouds","href":"agents/clouds","headings":["When to use it","What this version includes","How to open the page","The page without a cloud","How to add a cloud","A cloud's own pages","Clouds and the Agents page","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"A cloud is a source of agents that Buildspawn creates when builds wait and removes when they are done, for example virtual machines or containers. The Clouds tab lists the clouds you have set up. When to use it. Use a cloud when the load changes a lot, for example many builds on release day and none at night, and you would rather pay for machines only while they work. For machines that always exist, such as devkits and your own build PCs, use permanent agents (see Install an agent ). What this version includes. A cloud needs a cloud provider plug-in : a plug-in that knows how to create agents on one platform. The plug-ins that come with this version of Buildspawn do not include one, so the Clouds page starts empty. Add a cloud provider plug-in first. Then you can set up a cloud here. The Docker and Kubernetes plug-ins are examples of cloud providers. Check the plug-in list (see Plugins ) for what is available for your server. How to open the page. Click Agents in the top bar, then the Clouds tab. Or open Settings › Clouds. Both open the same page. You need the permission to read system settings. The Clouds page when no cloud is set up. The page without a cloud. The page says There are no clouds configured and Buildspawn can provision agents on demand from a cloud. Install a plugin that provides a cloud implementation, like Docker or Kubernetes. Button. What it does. New cloud. Greyed out ( Install a cloud plugin first ) until a provider plug-in is installed. Find cloud plugins. Opens the plug-in manager at the cloud category. How to add a cloud. After you have installed a cloud provider plug-in: Open Agents › Clouds and click New cloud. Type a Cloud name. Pick the type of cloud (the provider's name), or Copy Existing Cloud and type the name of the cloud to copy. Fill in the provider's own settings: the connection, the images or templates, and how many agents it may create. These fields come from the plug-in, so they differ for each provider. Click Save. When there is more than one cloud, the table lists them in order, and clouds are tried in the order they appear in this table when a new agent is needed. Change the order, then click Save. A cloud's own pages. Page. What it does. Status ( Cloud NAME ). The cloud's overview. The provider can add its own information, such as the agents it is creating. Configure ( Cloud NAME Configuration ). Change the provider's settings. Delete Cloud. Asks Delete the cloud 'NAME'? and removes it. It needs the permission to change system settings. Clouds and the Agents page. Agents that a cloud creates are shown like other agents. Their type is cloud : the parameter agent.type is cloud (see Labels and agent requirements ). While the cloud is creating agents, the provider can add rows to the Agents list for them. A cloud agent belongs to a pool like any other agent (see Pools ). A worked example. Your Linux builds mostly run during the day. A provider plug-in for your container platform is installed. Open Agents › Clouds › New cloud. Name it linux-containers and choose the provider. In the provider's settings, add a template with the label linux docker and a limit of 6 agents. Save. A build that needs linux && docker now makes the cloud create an agent when no other agent is free. What you see when it runs. A waiting build makes a new agent appear in the Agents list, offline at first and then idle when it has connected. When the agent has been idle for the time the provider sets, the agent is removed. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. New cloud is greyed out. No cloud provider plug-in is installed. Click Find cloud plugins. Builds wait and no agent is created. Check that a template of the cloud has the labels the build asks for, and that the cloud's limit is not reached. You cannot see Clouds. You lack permission to read system settings. Ask an administrator. Related pages. The Agents page. Pools. Plugins."},
{"title":"Install an agent","href":"agents/install-agent","headings":["Install and register","Create it first","Download the agent","Install and start it","Windows","macOS","Linux","Registration and approval","Agent registration settings","A ready-made zip for one agent","Tips","Related pages"],"text":"Add a build machine in a few minutes. The agent download includes Java, so the machine needs nothing else installed. There are two ways to add an agent: Install and register. Download the agent, give it the server address and start it. It asks to join, and an administrator approves it. Create it first. Click New Node , then download a zip made for that agent. It connects with its own secret, with no approval step. Download the agent. Open Agents in the top bar. Click Install agents. A panel opens with the downloads. Find the row for the machine's system. For macOS and Linux, pick the CPU first. Click Download. The Install agents panel on the Agents page. There are five downloads with Java 21 inside, plus the bare agent file: System. CPU choice. File. Service. Windows. x64. buildspawn-agent-windows-x64.zip. Windows service (WinSW). macOS. Apple silicon or Intel. buildspawn-agent-macos-aarch64.zip , buildspawn-agent-macos-x64.zip. launchd. Linux. x64 or ARM64. buildspawn-agent-linux-x64.zip , buildspawn-agent-linux-aarch64.zip. systemd. Any. none. agent.jar only. none: for containers and custom setups. Needs Java 17 or later on the machine. Each zip holds a buildspawn-agent folder with the agent, Java, start scripts, service scripts and the settings file conf/buildspawn-agent.properties. The scripts use the Java inside the folder first. The bin folder holds the agent's own tools: the terminal program buildspawn-agent-tui on every system and, on Windows, the tray app Buildspawn Agent.exe. Windows also gets bin\buildspawn-agent-service.xml , the service definition. The scripts are described in Agent service scripts. Install and start it. The How to install part of the panel shows these steps for each system, with your server's address filled in. Pick the tab for your system. Windows. Unzip the download into C:\\. You get C:\\buildspawn-agent\\. tar -xf buildspawn-agent-windows-x64.zip -C C:\\. Open an administrator command prompt in that folder and install the service in one go: install-service.bat --url http://buildserver:8080/. The service is called Buildspawn Agent and starts right away and at every boot. Other options: --name NAME and --token TOKEN (see Connect an agent ). To run it in a window instead, set serverUrl in conf\\buildspawn-agent.properties and start run-agent.bat. To see its status next to the clock, start bin\\Buildspawn Agent.exe (see Windows tray app ). macOS. Unzip the download into your home folder: unzip buildspawn-agent-macos*.zip -d ~. Register the launchd service in one go: cd ~/buildspawn-agent./install-service.sh --url http://buildserver:8080/. The service starts when you log in. Or set serverUrl in conf/buildspawn-agent.properties and run./run-agent.sh. Linux. Unzip the download into /opt : sudo unzip buildspawn-agent-linux*.zip -d /opt. Register the systemd service in one go: cd /opt/buildspawn-agent sudo./install-service.sh --url http://buildserver:8080/. systemd enables and starts the service. It runs as the user who ran sudo ; add --user build to choose another account. Or set serverUrl in conf/buildspawn-agent.properties and run./run-agent.sh. Replace http://buildserver:8080/ with your server's address. The panel shows the right one for your server. Registration and approval. When self-registration is on (see Agent registration settings below), an agent started with only the server address asks to join. It shows up under the Unauthorized tab of the Agents page and runs nothing until an administrator approves it. Start the agent. On the Agents page, an Unauthorized tab appears with a count. The tab is hidden while no agent is waiting. Open the tab. It lists the agents that registered themselves. For each agent, choose the Agent pool it joins and, if you like, a Comment. The comment is kept in the agent's description with your name. Click Authorize. The agent joins the pool and starts taking builds of its projects. You can move it later. Or click Reject to turn it away. Rejected agents are told to stop tr"},
{"title":"Personal agents","href":"agents/personal-agents","headings":["When to use it","How personal machines work","Before you start","How to add your machine","The My machines page","One machine","Workspace options","Run on my machine","If the machine is offline","If a credential is not allowed","Shelf checks","Share a build","Credentials on personal machines","Settings › Security › Personal machines","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"A personal agent is your own computer, connected to Buildspawn so that you can run a build on it. Only you can build there. Use it to try a build configuration, or to check a shelf, on your own hardware before the change goes to the build farm. When to use it. Use a personal machine when a build needs your hardware (your graphics card, your devkit) or when you do not want to wait for a free farm agent. The most common use in a game studio is the shelf check : you shelve your Perforce change, then build it on your own PC to see that it compiles and cooks before you submit. How personal machines work. Each personal machine has one owner. Only builds that the owner starts by hand run on it. Nothing automatic lands on it: triggers, schedules, finished-build chains and other people's builds never use a personal machine. Other users do not see it in the Run dialog. Only the owner and administrators see it. A build on a personal machine is a personal build. It does not change the status of the build configuration, is never the \"last successful\" build, and starts no other builds. See Personal builds. The Pool Scheduler never moves a personal machine (see The Pool Scheduler ), and it shows as Personal on the board. The agent connects out to the server over WebSocket. There is no port to open on your side. Before you start. An administrator must turn personal machines on (see Allow personal machines below). It is off by default. You need the permission Use a personal machine. How to add your machine. Open your user menu in the top bar and click My machines. Click Add this machine. Type a Machine name , for example dana-pc. If an agent with that name exists, the page says There is already an agent named dana-pc. Choose another name. Pick the System : Windows , macOS or Linux. Click Next. Step 1. Download : download the agent made for this machine. Unzip it and start it (on Windows run bin\\Buildspawn Agent.exe from the folder, see Windows tray app ). It connects by itself. Step 2. Your secret : the secret is shown once. Click Copy if you need it for a manual start. You won't see this again. Revoke it any time. The page says Waiting for dana-pc to connect… and changes to dana-pc is connected. Click Done. If the server has no agent download set up, the dialog says so and tells you to start the agent you already have with the secret shown. My machines, with one machine online. The Add this machine dialog. The My machines page. The page says Your own computers. Only you can run builds on them. It lists your machines with these columns: Column. What it shows. Name. The machine's name. Click it to open the machine. System. Operating system. CPU. Number of cores. Free disk. Free space in the work folder. Last seen. How long ago the machine was last online, or Never. Builds at a time. How many builds may run on it at once. Default 1. You can change it. State. Online, offline, paused or stopped. Actions. Pause, Resume, Revoke secret, Remove. An administrator sees the machines of other users too, headed Computers that belong to NAME. Only NAME can run builds on them. One machine. Open a machine to see its status, CPU, free disk and these parts: Part. What it does. Builds at a time. Raise or lower the number of builds that run together. Pause / Resume. Stop new builds on this machine. The running one finishes. Use it when you need your CPU. Revoke secret. Asks Revoke secret for NAME? The agent on that machine disconnects. It connects again only with the new secret, which you see next. Remove. Asks Remove NAME? This deletes the machine and its secret. Builds waiting for it are cancelled. Workspaces per build configuration. Choose where each build configuration builds here. See below. Allowed credentials. Read only list of the credentials this machine may use. An administrator chooses them. No credentials are allowed on personal machines. when the list is empty. My builds on this machine. Your builds here, with result and time, and a Share link for ea"},
{"title":"The Pool Scheduler","href":"agents/pool-scheduler","headings":["Overview","Move agents on the board","Scheduled moves","Priority rules and weights","Add a rule","How balancing works","Pool weights","Pause","Settings","Activity","On each pool page","Tips","Related pages"],"text":"The Pool Scheduler moves agents between pools for you: by hand on a board, at set times, or by itself when urgent builds are waiting. Use it to lend idle Windows machines to console builds at night, or to clear the queue before a release. Open Agents and click the Pools tab. The scheduler has five tabs: Overview , Board , Scheduled moves , Priority rules and Settings. Everyone who can see agents can look at the scheduler. Changing it needs the Administer permission. Overview. The Overview tab: capacity per pool, recent moves and borrowed agents. The strip at the top says whether the scheduler is Running or Paused , and the time zone it uses. The cards count Waiting builds , Borrowed agents (agents outside their home pool now), Moves today and the Next scheduled move. Capacity per pool shows, for each pool, its ready, busy and offline agents, the builds waiting for it, borrowed agents, its weight and its status. Short of 1 means builds wait and the pool has no free agent. Recent moves is the activity log, newest first. Move agents on the board. The Board: one column per pool. Locked agents can't be moved now. Open the Board tab. Drag an agent card to another pool's column. Or tick several cards and click Move selected. Tick Clean work folder first if the agent should wipe its work folders before it takes builds in the new pool. A message confirms the move. Click Undo within 10 seconds to put the agent back. Busy, offline and personal agents are locked (a lock icon on the card). A busy agent can be moved once its build ends. Use the chips (All, Ready, Busy, Offline, Borrowed, Personal) and the search box to find agents on a big farm. You can also move a single agent from the Agents page row menu. That move has Undo too. Scheduled moves. A scheduled move puts an agent in another pool at a set time, on chosen days. For example, lend a Windows machine to the Consoles pool every weekday evening, and take it back in the morning. A scheduled move that sends win-build-02 to the Consoles pool at 20:00. Open Scheduled moves and click Add scheduled move. Pick the Agent. Personal agents are not listed. Pick From pool (or Any pool , to move it from wherever it is) and To pool. Set the Time , in the scheduler's time zone. Click the Days , or use a quick pick: Every day, Mon–Fri or Weekend. Tick Clean work folder first if needed. Tick Move it back at and enter a time to add the return move as a second rule, on the same days. Click Save. The Add scheduled move dialog. How scheduled moves behave: A rule runs once, at its minute. If the agent is busy then, it moves as soon as its build ends, up to 2 hours late. After that the move is skipped and logged. Offline agents are skipped. The On switch turns a rule off without deleting it. The pencil edits it; ⋯ deletes it. Run due rules now runs every move that is due right away, even while the scheduler is paused. Priority rules and weights. Priority rules borrow idle agents from other pools while matching builds wait, and give them back when they are idle again. A priority rule and the pool weights. Add a rule. Open Priority rules and click Add priority rule. Give it a Name , for example Release builds first. Enter the Parameter and Value to match on the waiting build, for example system.pool.priority = high. Names can use the env. and system. prefixes, as in build parameters; without a prefix it is a configuration parameter. An empty value matches true , 1 , yes or on. Set the Priority (higher is served first), the Target pool that gets the agents, and Max agents (how many agents this rule may have borrowed at once). Tick Clean work folder first if needed, and click Save. The Add priority rule dialog. Then start a build with that parameter, for example from the Run dialog with system.pool.priority set to high. How balancing works. Automatic balancing must be on. The scheduler checks every minute and whenever the queue changes. For each waiting build, the matching rule with the highest priority wins."},
{"title":"Pools","href":"agents/pools","headings":["How pools work","Create a pool","Icons and colours","The sidebar","Move an agent to another pool","Delete a pool","Borrowed agents","Why a build waits","Tips","Related pages"],"text":"A pool is a group of agents that serves a set of projects. Pools keep console builds on devkits, Windows builds on Windows machines, and so on. Each pool can have its own icon and colour. How pools work. Every agent belongs to exactly one pool. A pool has a list of projects. Its agents run builds only of those projects. The Default pool is special: its agents run every build configuration that is not assigned to a pool. A build of a project that belongs to a pool waits in the queue until an agent of that pool is free. You can assign a single build configuration or a whole project to a pool. A build configuration in an assigned project can use the pool. A build configuration that is assigned to pools can run only on agents of those pools. A pool does not replace labels. A build needs an agent that is in a pool its project may use and that meets its label expression and requirements (see Labels and agent requirements ). A new agent that you create with New Node , or that an administrator approves, joins the pool you pick, or the Default pool. Personal machines (see Personal agents ) are never moved by pools or the scheduler. Create a pool. Open Agents and click the Pools tab. Click Create pool. Type a name, for example Consoles. Choose an icon and a colour (see below). Add the projects whose builds the pool should run, and the agents that belong to it. Click Save. To change a pool later, click its name in the side panel or on the Pools tab. The pool page has the same settings, plus an Automation card from the Pool Scheduler and Delete pool at the bottom. Renaming or deleting a pool also updates the scheduler's moves and rules. Icons and colours. The Consoles pool with the PlayStation icon and a purple colour. An icon shows at a glance what runs in a pool. On the pool page, find Icon and colour. Pick an Icon : Server, Windows, Linux, Docker, Apple, Console, PlayStation, Xbox, Nintendo Switch, Steam, Android, iOS or GPU. Custom… lets you pick any icon from the built-in icon library. Pick an Icon colour from the list, or choose Custom and enter any colour. Without a choice, the theme's colour is used. Click Save. Without an icon, the pool keeps the plain server icon. The icon and colour appear everywhere the pool does: the side panel of the Agents pages; the Agent column of the agent list; the Pools tab and the Pool Scheduler board; the pool page; the Move to pool dialog. The sidebar. The side panel with every pool opened. On every Agents page, the panel on the left lists your pools. Overview opens the agent list. Click a pool's name to open its page. Click the arrow before it to show its agents. Each agent shows its state: green when idle, a busy count such as 1/2 (busy executors of all executors) while it builds, and red, greyed out, when offline. A red number beside a pool counts its offline agents. The number at the top, beside POOLS , counts all offline agents. Search by name filters pools and agents. The pencil beside POOLS and the + at the top help you manage pools and agents. Move an agent to another pool. There are three ways: On the Agents page, open the row menu ⋯ and choose Move to pool…. See The Agents page. On the agent's own page, under Agent pool , click Move to pool…. On the Pool Scheduler board, drag the agent to another pool. See The Pool Scheduler. To move agents automatically, at set times or when builds wait, use the Pool Scheduler. Delete a pool. On the pool page click Delete pool , or use the ⋯ menu of the pool on the Pools tab. Buildspawn asks Delete pool NAME? and explains: Its agents move to the Default pool and its projects can then run on any agent of the Default pool. The Default pool cannot be deleted, renamed or given a name that is already used. Names must not be empty. Borrowed agents. An agent that the Pool Scheduler has lent to another pool is shown as borrowed with the pool it came from and the rule that borrowed it. It is not saved as a member of the pool it is in now, so saving the pool does n"},
{"title":"Telemetry","href":"agents/telemetry","headings":["How it works","The agent's Telemetry tab","Time range","Live and Pause","Cards and charts","Configure monitors","Turn telemetry off for one agent","Tips","Related pages"],"text":"Telemetry is live health data from your agents: CPU, memory, disk, network and GPU. It shows which machines are busy, full or struggling, before a build fails. How it works. Telemetry comes from the Agent Telemetry plug-in that is built into Buildspawn. Its settings page is Agents › Configure monitors , and is also listed as Agent Telemetry in Settings. Telemetry is on by default. Every agent sends a sample every 30 seconds. While someone has an agent's Telemetry tab open, that agent sends a sample every 2 seconds. GPU values come from the vendor's tool ( nvidia-smi , amd-smi / rocm-smi or xpu-smi ). They are read only on machines that have one of these tools. Other agents show \"—\". You see telemetry in three places: the live columns and expanded rows of the Agents page , the summary strip above it, and each agent's own Telemetry tab. The agent's Telemetry tab. Open an agent: click its name on the Agents page. Click the Telemetry tab. (From the Agents page, you can also open a row and click Telemetry.). An agent's Telemetry tab, with the last 15 minutes. Time range. Pick how far back the charts go: 15 min , 1 h , 6 h or 24 h. Live and Pause. Live · 2 s means new samples arrive every 2 seconds and the charts move on. Click Pause to freeze the charts, for example to read a spike. Click it again to go live. Cards and charts. Card / chart. What it shows. CPU. Use in percent, and the number of logical cores. Memory. Use in percent and in GB of the total. GPU. Use, temperature and video memory (VRAM). The chart shows use and VRAM as two lines. Disk free / Disk. Free space on the work drive, and read and write speed. Network. Data in and out, over all network interfaces. The dashed lines on each chart are the Warning and Critical thresholds. A value past a line turns yellow or red. The thresholds colour the cells and charts. On the screenshots in this guide the disk is Critical on every agent because the demo machine's D: drive is nearly full. Configure monitors. The switch for the whole server is labelled Collect telemetry from agents. All telemetry settings are on one page. On the Agents page, click Configure monitors. The Telemetry card on the Configure monitors page. Setting. What it does. Collect telemetry from agents. Switches telemetry on or off for the whole server. On by default. Background interval. How often every agent sends a sample: 5 to 300 seconds. Default 30. Live interval. How often an agent sends samples while its Telemetry tab is open: 1 to 10 seconds. Default 2. Collect GPU use. Reads the GPU on agents that have a GPU tool. The page says how many agents have one. Thresholds. The Warning and Critical values for CPU use, memory used, GPU use, disk and so on. Yellow from the warning value, red from the critical value. Agents that don't send telemetry. A list of agents left out. Pick an agent and click Add. Click Save (or Apply to stay on the page). Telemetry thresholds only colour the pages. They never fail or stop a build. The older monitors further down the page, such as Free Disk Space , can mark an agent offline when it runs low, unless you tick Don't mark agents temporarily offline. Turn telemetry off for one agent. Open the agent and click the Configure tab. In Node Properties , untick Collect telemetry from this agent. It is ticked by default (see Agent settings ). Click Save. The agent then shows \"—\" in the live columns, and it is listed under Agents that don't send telemetry on the Configure monitors page. Tips. Use the 24 h range to see a night of builds at once and spot machines that run out of memory. Lower the warning thresholds for disk on machines that keep big Perforce workspaces, so you are warned early. A Stale freshness means no sample came in for a while. Check that the agent is still connected. Related pages. The Agents page. Agent settings : disk space thresholds and the telemetry box. Spot unhealthy agents."},
{"title":"Agent service scripts","href":"agents/windows-service","headings":["When to use it","The scripts","Options of install-service","How to install the service","Run install-service again","Upgrade an agent without registering it again","Logs","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"A service starts the agent at boot, restarts it when it stops and keeps it running after you sign out. This page covers the Windows service scripts and the matching scripts for Linux and macOS. When to use it. Use a service on every build machine that nobody signs in to. Run the agent in a window ( run-agent ) only to try it, or on a personal computer where the owner's tray app starts it. The scripts. All scripts sit in the agent folder, for example C:\\buildspawn-agent\\. Script (Windows). What it does. install-service.bat. Installs the Buildspawn Agent service and starts it. Needs an administrator prompt. uninstall-service.bat. Stops and removes the service. Needs an administrator prompt. stop-agent.bat. Stops the service ( sc.exe stop buildspawn-agent ). Run it as administrator. run-agent.bat. Runs the agent in the window, in the foreground. Closing the window stops it. Script (Linux and macOS). What it does. install-service.sh. Linux: writes /etc/systemd/system/buildspawn-agent.service , then enables and starts it. Needs sudo. macOS: with sudo installs a LaunchDaemon (starts at boot); as yourself installs a LaunchAgent (starts when you sign in). The name is com.buildspawn.agent. uninstall-service.sh. Stops and removes the service. stop-agent.sh. Stops the service. run-agent.sh. Runs the agent in the foreground. Press Ctrl + C to stop. Options of install-service. Option. What it does. Systems. --url URL. Sets serverUrl in the settings file. All. --token TOKEN. Sets registrationToken (see Registration token ). All. --name NAME. Sets the agent's name. Default: the machine's host name. All. --user USER. The account the service runs as. Default: the user who ran sudo. Linux, macOS. --print. Writes the service definition to the screen and changes nothing. No administrator needed. All. run-agent also takes --url , --name , --token and --work-dir. How to install the service. Unzip the agent download (see Install an agent ). Open an administrator command prompt in the agent folder. On Linux and macOS, use a terminal with sudo. Run: install-service.bat --url https://buildspawn.example.com/ --name win-build-04. Leave out the options when you downloaded the zip made for one agent ( New Node ). It already holds the address and the secret. The script says Service \"Buildspawn Agent\" installed and started. Logs: C:\\buildspawn-agent\\logs. What the Windows script does, in order: Checks that it runs as administrator. Finds Java: the java folder in the agent folder first, then JAVA_HOME , then the search path. Java 17 or later. Writes the options you gave into conf\\buildspawn-agent.properties and stops with an error if serverUrl is empty. Locks the settings file so that only the system account and administrators can read it. It holds the agent's secret. Writes bin\\buildspawn-agent-service.xml with the Java it found and your javaOptions. The secret is not in it. Registers the service with WinSW, a small open-source service wrapper (MIT licence, see bin\\LICENSE-WinSW.txt ), and starts it. The service is set to start automatically. If the agent stops, it restarts after 10 seconds, then 30, then 60. The failure count resets after an hour. Run install-service again. You can run install-service.bat again on a machine where the service already exists. It stops the old service, removes it and installs it again from the folder you are in. Do this: after you upgrade the agent files (see Upgrade agents ); after you move or replace the agent folder, because the service points at the old folder; after you change javaOptions , because the Windows service definition holds the Java options; when the service does not start and logs is empty. The script then says Service \"Buildspawn Agent\" installed again from C:\\buildspawn-agent\\ and started. If it says could not remove the old service definition , close the Services window ( services.msc ) and try again. The same applies to Linux and macOS: run install-service.sh again after you change javaOptions , because th"},
{"title":"Windows tray app","href":"agents/windows-tray-app","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","The tray icon","The tray menu","Every tab","Status","Builds","Connection log","Agent log","Work folders","Service","Certificates","Settings","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Log files","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"The tray app is a small Windows program that sits next to the clock and shows what the agent on this machine is doing. You can pause the agent, read its logs, clean work folders and change its settings from it. When to use it. Use the tray app on any Windows machine that runs a Buildspawn agent, whether it is a build machine, a devkit host or your own computer (see Personal agents ). It works with the agent running as a Windows service and with an agent that the app starts itself. It talks to the agent through a local connection on the same machine, so it works even when the server cannot be reached. It then shows the last data it knows. How to set it up. Every Windows agent download already holds the app. You do not install it separately. Unzip the agent download, for example into C:\\buildspawn-agent\\. Open the bin folder and double-click Buildspawn Agent.exe. The icon appears in the notification area (click the small arrow next to the clock if it is hidden). If the agent is not running yet, right-click the icon and choose Start the agent. The app starts the agent in the background and hides its window. To start the app every time you sign in to Windows, open the Settings tab and tick Open this app when I sign in to Windows , then click Save. The app finds the agent folder by itself: the folder above the bin folder it runs from. To point it at another agent folder, start it with --home \"D:\\other-agent\". A second start of the app does not open a second copy. It brings the first window to the front. The tray icon. The icon changes colour with the state of the agent. Hover over it to see a short line of text. Icon. Meaning. Tooltip text. Green. Connected and idle. Agent name · Idle · Connected. Blue, pulsing. A build is running. Build name · time so far. Amber. Paused. The running build finishes, then no new builds start. Paused · finishes the current build. Red. Offline, connecting, or the agent is not running. Offline · reconnecting in 12 s. The app also shows short pop-up messages: Agent disconnected , Agent reconnected , and one for each build that finishes (passed, failed or stopped). The tray menu. Right-click the icon. A left click opens the window. Item. What it does. Start the agent. Starts the agent. Shown only when it is not running. Open as administrator. Shown when the agent runs as another account (for example the service account). Opens the app again with administrator rights so it can control that agent. Open (bold). Opens the window. Pause / Resume. Pause: the build that runs now finishes, and no new build starts here. Resume: take builds again. The server applies it within a few seconds, and it is remembered while the agent is offline. Open in browser. Opens this agent's page on the server. Show connection log. Opens the window on the Connection log tab. Restart agent. Stops and starts the agent. A running build is stopped. Exit. Closes the app. It asks whether the agent keeps running. Keep running leaves the agent alone. Stop agent stops it (a service is stopped after Windows asks for permission). Cancel keeps the app open. The window closes to the tray when you click the close button or press Esc. Only Exit ends the app. Every tab. The window has eight tabs. The title bar shows the agent's name. Status. The first look at the machine. Field. What it shows. Owner. Only on a personal machine: the user it belongs to. Connection. Connected, Connecting, or Offline with a countdown to the next try. If the agent runs as another account: This agent runs as another account. Open as administrator to control it. If it is stopped: The agent isn't running. Server. The server address the agent uses. Last contact. How long ago the server was last heard from. Reconnects today. How many times the connection came back since midnight. Current build. The running build, a progress bar against its usual time, the current step and the time so far. (+2 more) when several builds run. CPU , RAM , Disk. Bars with the percent used on thi"},
{"title":"Atom feeds","href":"build-results/atom-feeds","headings":["When to use it","Where the feeds are","Use a feed","What you see","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"An Atom feed is a web address that lists the newest builds. Add it to a feed reader or a chat app, and you hear about a failed build without opening Buildspawn. When to use it. Use a feed when a team channel or a reader should show build results: for example every failed build of the whole server, or the changes that went into one build configuration. Where the feeds are. The feed links sit at the bottom of the Projects page (the directory), and at the bottom of the History tab of a build configuration. They are links, so you can copy the address with a right click. Link. Address. What it lists. Atom feed for all. /rssAll. Every build of the projects in the page, or of the build configuration. Atom feed for failures. /rssFailed. Only failed builds. Atom feed for just latest builds. /rssLatest. Only the newest build of each build configuration. On the Projects page. Changes feed. /job/…/rssChangelog. The changes that went into the builds of one build configuration. Add the path to the server address, or to the address of a build configuration. For example, the failed builds of the whole server: http://build-server:8080/rssFailed. For one build configuration: http://build-server:8080/job/Game/job/Windows%20Build/rssFailed. Add ?flavor=rss20 to get the same feed as RSS 2.0, for readers that do not know Atom. Use a feed. Open the Projects page, or the History tab of a build configuration. Right click Atom feed for failures and copy the link address. Paste it into your feed reader or chat app. If the reader asks for a user name and password, use your user name and an API token instead of your password. Create a token under Account in your account menu. See API tokens. What you see. Each entry is one build. Its title holds the project, the build configuration, the build number and the result, for example Game &raquo; Windows Build #7 (stable). The entry links to the build page. A feed only lists what its user may see, so the same address shows different builds to different users. Personal builds of other people are never listed. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The reader says it needs a sign-in. The server needs sign-in. Give the reader your user name and an API token. The feed is empty. The server has no builds that match, or you may not see them. An empty failures feed is good news. The links are missing. Feeds are on the Projects page and on the History tab of a build configuration, not on the build page. Related pages. The project directory. History. API tokens."},
{"title":"The build console","href":"build-results/build-console","headings":["Open the console","Blocks","The rows of the console","Errors and warnings","Times","Filters","Search","Line links","Running builds and Follow","Other tools","Write your own blocks with ::group::","Tips"],"text":"The Console Output tab shows the whole log of a build. It splits the log into blocks, puts a time on every line, and helps you find errors fast. A failed build. The failed step is open, and inside it the script's own block Compile Game holds the error line. Open the console. Open a build. See The build page. Click the Console Output tab, or click Log beside a step on the Overview tab to jump to that step. Blocks. The log is a tree of blocks. Each build step is a block, and blocks can hold smaller blocks. Version control work has blocks of its own, for example the Perforce workspace, the sync and the list of changes since the last build. Each block header shows its status (a tick, a cross or a spinner), the step's runner, its warning and error counts, and how long it took. Blocks that failed open by themselves. Blocks that passed start closed, so you see the problem first. Click a header to open or close a block. Expand all and Collapse all in the card header do it for every block. Very long logs load quickly: the console shows the start and end of each block and loads the rest when you open it. The rows of the console. Row. What it is. Step N/M: name. A step. The header shows its status, the runner (for example Execute Windows batch command or PowerShell ), its error and warning counts, its lines and its duration. A step that has not started shows a spinner. A step that did not run shows Skipped: a previous step failed or Skipped. Done in #N. In a resumed build , a step that was not run again. It says Done in #1 and has an Open in #1 log link to the output in the original build. Perforce sync , workspace and changes. Blocks for the source control work, before the first step. Your own groups. Blocks your script makes with ::group:: They can be nested, and can show details after the name, such as 1,204 files. Post-build actions. A block for the actions after the steps, such as archiving artifacts. Lanes. For a Matrix block, a lane for each combination. A chip shows the lane. Click it to show only that combination. A side build of a Parallel block has its own block, named Side build. First and last lines. The first lines say who started the build, as which user and on which agent and workspace. The last line is Finished: SUCCESS , FAILURE , UNSTABLE or ABORTED. Errors and warnings. A line the tool marks as an error has a red band and a count in its block header. The error and warning counters at the top add them all up, also for blocks that are closed or not loaded yet. Click a counter to jump to the first line, press E for the next error and W for the next warning. Your scripts can mark lines with ::error:: and ::warning:: A step that fails also writes a line such as Build step 'Compile' failed: the batch script exited with code 1 , and a last line Failed: Step \"Compile\" exited with code 1. The build page's Decided by row repeats it. Times. Buildspawn records a time for every line of every build. You don't need to switch anything on in the build configuration. Use the Time switch in the toolbar: Choice. What you see. Clock. The time of day each line was written, for example 05:36:29. Elapsed. The time since the build started. Good for spotting slow parts. Off. No times, so the log has more room. Your choice is saved with your account and used for every build. An administrator can switch time recording off for the whole server under Settings › System › Build console. Builds from before Buildspawn 1.1 have no times. Filters. The buttons on the left of the toolbar choose which lines you see: All : every line. Important only : block headers, warnings and errors. Errors only : only the lines that report errors. The errors and warnings counters count the whole log, also the parts that are closed or not loaded yet. Press E to jump to the next error, or W to jump to the next warning. Warning lines have a yellow band and error lines a red band. Search. Click Search in log and type a word, for example C4996. The box shows the matches as \""},
{"title":"The build name and status text","href":"build-results/build-name-and-status","headings":["When to use it","Name a build","Set the status text","Know who started the build","What you see","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"A build script can give its build a meaningful name, such as 1.4.212-CL48213 , and a short status text, such as Uploaded to Steam branch qa. Both show on the build page, in History and on chain pages. When to use it. A build number like #212 says little. A name with the game version and the changelist tells the QA team which build to test. A status text says what the build did when \"Success\" is not enough: where it uploaded, or how many tests passed. Name a build. In a step of the build configuration, print this line. Put it in a PowerShell, Command Line or any other script step. Write-Host \"##buildspawn[buildNumber '1.4.%build.counter%-CL%build.vcs.number%']\". Run the build. The name replaces the plain number in the header of the build page, in the History list and on chain pages. The build's real number, its address and its links stay the same. If several steps print the message, the last name counts. The name can be up to 200 characters. A name that refers to an unknown parameter, or to a password, is refused. The build keeps its name and the log says why. Write. To get. %build.counter%. The build number, for example 212. %build.vcs.number%. The changelist or commit the build used. %build.triggeredBy.username%. The user who started the build. %name%. A build parameter, or a value an earlier step set with setParameter. {build.number}. The build's name so far, to add to it. %%. One percent sign. Set the status text. In a step, print this line: Write-Host \"##buildspawn[buildStatus text='{build.status.text}, uploaded to Steam branch qa']\". When the build ends, the text shows on the build page and in History. {build.status.text} stands for the text the build would show anyway, so a build that says Tests passed: 120 now says Tests passed: 120, uploaded to Steam branch qa. Attribute. Rule. text. The new status text, up to 500 characters. The last text a build sends counts. A text that holds a password parameter's value is refused. status. Optional: SUCCESS , UNSTABLE or FAILURE. It is applied when the build ends. It can only make the result worse, never better: a failed build stays failed even if a script sends SUCCESS. # PowerShell: mark the build unstable if the upload was partial if ($uploaded -lt $total) { Write-Host \"##buildspawn[buildStatus text='Uploaded $uploaded of $total depots' status='UNSTABLE']\" }. Know who started the build. A script can read %build.triggeredBy.username% , or the environment variable BUILDSPAWN_TRIGGERED_BY. It holds the user who started the build. It follows chains and retries, and is empty for a schedule or a version control trigger. What you see. The build's name in the header, History list and chain pages. The status text next to the result chip, once the build has finished. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The name did not change. Spell the message exactly: ##buildspawn[buildNumber '.'] , with single quotes. Only a step's own output counts: the same line in a commit message or after the step ended is refused. Search the console for the line. A refusal says why. The status stays Success although I sent status='FAILURE'. The status is applied when the build ends. Check the log for a refusal line. I want status='SUCCESS' to turn a failed build green. It cannot. A script can only make the result worse. Related pages. Service messages : every message a script can print. Set parameters from a build. The build console."},
{"title":"The build page","href":"build-results/build-page","headings":["Open a build","The header","A running build","The Overview tab","The tabs","A failed build","Problems and fixes","Tips","Related pages"],"text":"Each build has its own page. It shows how the build went, where it ran, what it built and what to do next. The Overview tab of a finished build. Open a build. Click a build number in the History tab of a build configuration, or on the History page in the top bar. Click a build in the chart or list on the build configuration's Overview tab. On the Agents page, click the build an agent is running. Click a build in the Queue once it has started. Press Ctrl + K and type the build configuration's name. The header. The band at the top is the same on every tab of the build. Part. What it shows. Build number and status chip. For example #7 Success. Other states are Running , Failed , Unstable and Aborted. A script can give the build a different name and status text. See The build name and status text. Build configuration chip. The project and build configuration, with its platform icon. Click it to go to the build configuration. A Personal chip marks a personal build. Info line. When it started, how long it took, who or what started it, the agent it ran on, and the Perforce changelist or Git commit it built. Previous / next. The two arrow buttons step to the build before and after this one. Resume from step N. On a failed or stopped build: starts a new build at the failed step. Greyed out, with the reason, when it cannot resume. See Resume a build. Re-run. Runs the build again with the same settings. It reads Re-run from the start and is the main button when a resume is not possible. The button beside it, with the sliders, opens the dialog Re-run with changes…. See Re-run a build. Stop. Replaces Re-run while the build runs. See Stop a build. Share / Stop sharing. On a personal build you started. See Shared builds. Promote…. On a finished build that other build configurations depend on: starts the builds that use it, with this build as their source. Pin. Keeps this build forever, so clean-up rules never delete it. Its tooltip says Keep this build forever. A pinned build shows a filled pin. Visual. Opens the build in the visual editor's run view, drawn over the graph of steps. See Run view. … (More actions). Edit the build's name and description, label the build in Perforce, or delete it. On a personal build, a note under the title says who can see it: Only you can see this build , Only name and admins can see this build or, on a shared one, that people with the link can view it. It also says that the build does not change the build configuration's status or Last successful , and does not start other build configurations. A running build. While a build runs, the header shows Running , a Stop button instead of Re-run, and a progress bar. The bar compares the time so far with the usual duration of this build configuration. When the build takes longer than usual, the bar says so. The fastest way to see what a running build does is the Console Output tab. It follows the output live. See The build console. A running build: the progress bar, the running step and a Stop button. The Overview tab. Card. What it holds. Build steps. Every step with its status, a short reason, and its duration, for example Failed — the batch script exited with code 1 or Skipped — a previous step failed. Click Log on a step to jump to it in the console. A step whose tests failed shows Unstable. Post-build actions are on the last line. Steps of a resumed build show Done in #1. Summary. Started at, Duration, Waited in queue, Executed on (agent and executor), Cause, Parameters, and for a failed build Decided by , which says what made it fail, for example Failed: Step \"Compile\" exited with code 1. A personal build has a Personal build row. A re-run says Re-run of #6 by you with a link to the original. Version control details. The revision, repository and branch, or the changelist. Resume from here / Resumed from. On a failed build, the three checks for a resume. On a resumed build, the agent and workspace it continued in. Matrix. For a build with a Matrix block: a grid "},
{"title":"Changes, artifacts and tests","href":"build-results/changes-artifacts-tests","headings":["Changes","Artifacts","Tests","Tips"],"text":"Three tabs on the build page tell you what went into a build, what came out of it and whether its tests passed. Changes. The Changes tab lists the code changes that are new in this build, compared with the build before it. With Perforce, each entry is a changelist: its number, author, description and files. With Git, each entry is a commit: its short hash, author, message and files. With several version control roots, the changes are grouped by root. Each entry shows the description, the changelist number or commit hash, the author, when it was committed, and the Affected paths with the action on each file ( edit , add , delete ). The tab title shows how many changes there are. The detail link opens the details of the change. To see the changes of many builds in one list, open the Change Log tab of the build configuration. The info line in the header always shows the changelist or commit the build used, for example Changelist 259 or Commit 65a8b39 , even when there are no new changes. The Changes tab of the first build: one changelist, with its author and the files it touched. A build that used the same changelist as the one before it shows No changes. The Overview tab shows the newest changes in a small card. Click View all there to open this tab. For more on Perforce and Git roots, see Perforce and Git. Artifacts. Artifacts are files that a build keeps after it ends, such as game packages, symbols or reports. A build configuration chooses which files to keep in its build features (the \"Archive the artifacts\" post-build action, with a file pattern such as Output/** ). When a build kept files, it has an Artifacts tab: The files kept by a Windows build. Click a folder to open it, or a file to download it. Click (all files in zip) to download everything at once. Type a pattern in the Pattern box, for example **/*.pdb , and press the arrow to list only matching files. Artifacts are deleted with their build. Pin a build on its page to keep its artifacts for good. Tests. When a build publishes a test report (for example JUnit XML files from NUnit, Unity or Unreal Engine test runs), it gets a Test Result tab. A test report with every test passing. The top card counts the failures, skipped tests and all tests. The numbers in brackets compare with the previous build, so 0 failures (-2) means two tests that failed last time now pass. The bar shows the share of failed, skipped and passed tests. History shows how the counts changed over the last builds. All Tests lists the packages. Click one to see its classes and tests, and click a failed test to read its error message and stack trace. The Overview tab of the build also shows a one-line test summary, and the build configuration page shows a chart of test results over time. Tips. A build can show several of these tabs at once. Look at Changes first when a test suddenly fails: the culprit is often in the list. Keep artifacts small. Archive packages and symbols, not whole work folders."},
{"title":"The Dashboard","href":"build-results/dashboard-widgets","headings":["When to use it","Open it","The widgets","Arrange the dashboard (administrators)","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"The Dashboard is the first page many people open. It is a grid of widgets that show what matters most: failing builds, the queue, the agents and the numbers of the last days. The suggested widgets in edit mode. When to use it. Use the Dashboard for a quick look: is the game building? Is the queue growing? Are the agents healthy? For the full list of projects and build configurations, use Projects. Open it. Click Dashboard in the top bar. Everybody sees the same dashboard. An administrator arranges it, and each widget shows only what the viewer may see. Widgets refresh by themselves. If the server cannot be reached, a widget keeps its last data and says Showing last known data. The widgets. Widget. What it shows. Metric cards. Four numbers: failing build configurations, running builds, builds waiting in the queue and available executors. Each card links to its list. Builds per day. A bar chart of builds per day, stacked by result, with the success rate, the average duration and the average queue time. The period is the last 24 hours, 7, 14 or 30 days. Recent builds. The latest builds of all build configurations, with result, changes, start time and duration. Build queue. How many builds wait, the oldest wait, why each build waits, and the waits over the last hour. Build executor status. Online, busy and offline agents, and idle executors. It can list the offline agents. Needs attention. Everything that fails, failing first. It can include unstable builds. Watched builds. The build configurations an administrator chose, with their status, recent history and the progress of running builds. Arrange the dashboard (administrators). An administrator clicks Edit dashboard , adds, moves, resizes or removes widgets and clicks Save. An empty dashboard offers Start with suggested widgets. The steps, with every widget option, are on Dashboard in the administration section. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The Dashboard says Arrange the dashboard and shows no widgets. Nobody has added widgets yet. Ask an administrator, who can click Start with suggested widgets. A build configuration I watch is missing from a widget. You may not see it. Widgets hide what you may not see. A widget says Showing last known data. The server did not answer. Reload the page in a moment. Related pages. Dashboard : set it up. The project directory. History."},
{"title":"History","href":"build-results/history","headings":["When to use it","The History page","The columns","The History tab of a build configuration","What you see in a row","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"History lists every build that ran, newest first, with a timeline that shows which agent ran what and when. The History page in the top bar. When to use it. Use History to answer \"what ran last night?\" or \"which builds failed today?\" across all projects. For the builds of one build configuration, use its own History tab (see below). The History page. Click History in the top bar. The title line says how many builds the list holds and how many are in progress, for example 36 builds · 0 in progress. You see only the builds of projects you may see. Personal builds of other people are not listed. Part. What it does. Icon size S, M, L. Sets the size of the status icons: small, medium or large. Legend. Explains the status icons and the health icons. Timeline. The last 6 hours, one lane for each agent, with its number of executors. Each build is a bar from its start to its end, coloured by result: Success , Failed , Unstable , Aborted or In progress. Use it to see which agents are busy and where a night build waited. All , Failed , Running , Mine. The result filter. Failed shows failed builds, Running builds in progress, and Mine builds you started. No builds match. says the filter has no result. The columns. Column. What it shows. Build. The build number or name. Click it to open the build page. Result. The status, such as Success or Failed. A script's status text can show here. See The build name and status text. Configuration. The build configuration, with its project. Changes. The newest change in the build, or No changes. Started (GMT). When it started. Click the heading to sort the other way. Duration. How long it took. Agent. The agent that ran it. Console and Visual buttons. Open the console, or the build in the visual editor's run view. The History tab of a build configuration. The History tab of one build configuration. Filter builds narrows the list by what you type. All , Failed and Mine filter by result and by who started the build. The columns are Build, Result, Changes, Started, Duration and Agent. Each row has a Visual button, and a … menu with Re-run and Re-run with changes…. Side builds of Parallel blocks are nested under their main build and read Side build of #N. Newer builds and Older builds page through the list. The Queue card below lists the build configuration's waiting builds and why they wait. Atom feed for all and Atom feed for failures are feeds for this build configuration. See Atom feeds. The Build time graph draws how long each build took. The History tab is one of the five fixed first tabs of a build configuration. Pipelines have it too. What you see in a row. A row for a failed build has a red result and the build number links to the build page, where you read Decided by. See Find out why a build failed. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. A build I started is missing. Click Mine. If it is a personal build, it shows only for you and for administrators. The timeline is empty. It shows the last 6 hours only. Older builds are in the list. The list is long. Use Failed or Running , or open the build configuration's History tab. Related pages. The build page. The Statistics tab : charts of the same builds. Atom feeds."},
{"title":"The project directory","href":"build-results/project-directory","headings":["When to use it","Open the directory","The Directory table","Filter by name","Filter by result","Icon size","Views","Legend and feeds","The root project page","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"The Projects page lists every project and build configuration with its latest result. Filter it by name or by result, open projects in place, and choose how large the icons are. The Projects page and its Directory. When to use it. Use the directory as your start page: to see at once what fails, to find a build configuration, and to open a build. For a short list of the things everybody must see, use the Dashboard. Open the directory. Click Projects in the top bar. The title line counts what is there, for example 4 projects · 21 build configurations · 0 building · 1 queued. The Directory table. Column. What it shows. Name. A project (folder icon) or a build configuration (its platform icon), with its description. Click the arrow before a project to open it and see what is inside. Click a name to open its page. Click a column heading to close every open project again. Items. For a project, how many items are directly inside it. Status. For a project, how many build configurations fail, for example 2 failing , or All 12 passing. For a build configuration, its last result: Success , Failed , Unstable , Running or Not built. Latest build. The newest build, with its number. For a project, the newest build of anything inside it. Started (GMT). When that build started. Duration. How long it took. Health. A weather icon for the share of recent builds that passed. A project shows the worst health of what is inside it. Hover the icon for the percent. The heading line of the table says Sorted by name , or Custom order when administrators put the items in their own order. See A tour of the interface. Filter by name. Click Filter projects and configurations and type part of a name, for example windows. The table shows only the matching rows. No project or configuration matches. appears when nothing fits. Clear the box to see everything again. Filter by result. The list beside the filter box shows one result at a time: Choice. Shows. Result: Any. Everything. This is the default. Result: Failed. Build configurations whose last build failed. Result: Unstable. Builds that passed but have failing tests. Result: Success. Builds that passed. Result: Running. Build configurations that are building now. Result: Not built. Build configurations that never ran. You can use the name filter and the result together, for example Result: Failed and console to find failing console builds. Icon size. The buttons S , M and L beside Icon: set how large the icons and rows are: small (16 pixels), medium (24) or large (32). Your choice is kept in your browser. Views. The tab All lists everything. A view is an extra tab with only the items you pick, for example one for the console builds. Click the + beside the tabs to add a view. The list of build configurations there has a search box, Select all and Clear and a count. Build Chains opens the page of build chains: build configurations that depend on each other. Legend and feeds. Under the table, Legend explains every status icon and health icon. The three links beside it are Atom feeds. See Atom feeds. The root project page. The page of the root project ( Root project ) is a second way in. It shows Configurations and subprojects in a table with Name, Result, Latest build, Duration and Health, and a Filter box. A project that is paused or archived says so. Hide archived projects hides the archived ones. Its tabs are Overview, Build History, Perforce roots, Credentials, Build settings and Settings. Templates are listed in their own card below. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. A project or build configuration is missing. You see only what you may see. Ask a project administrator for the project viewer role. Archived projects are hidden: use Hide archived projects on the root project page to toggle them. The filter finds nothing. The result list may still say Failed. Set it to Result: Any. Related pages. History. The Dashboard. Projects : how to build the tree."},
{"title":"The Statistics tab","href":"build-results/statistics","headings":["When to use it","Open it","The controls","The five default charts","Read a chart","Custom charts","Report a number from a build","Add a chart","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Charts of how a build configuration does over time: how often it passes, how long it takes, how long builds wait, how many tests pass and how big the artifacts are. You can add charts for numbers your own builds report. The Statistics tab with the five default charts. When to use it. Use it to spot a build that slowly gets longer, a flaky build whose success rate drops, a queue that grows, or a package that doubles in size. You see it at once, without reading single builds. Open it. Open the build configuration, for example Game / Windows Build. Click the Statistics tab. It is one of the five fixed first tabs. The controls. Control. What it does. Default. Week , Month , Quarter , Year , All. The time range: the last 7 days, 30 days, 90 days, 365 days, or all builds. Under the title, a line says how many builds are in the range. Month. Agent. Show only the builds that ran on one agent. All agents shows every build. Use it to compare machines. All agents. The five default charts. Chart. What it shows. Success rate. The share of finished builds that did not fail, in percent. One point for each day (for each week in the Year and All ranges). Aborted builds and builds that did not run do not count. A red point means a failure on that day. Build duration. How long each build took. Time spent in queue. How long each build waited for an agent before it started. Test count. Three lines: Passed tests , Failed tests and Ignored tests. It says No data in this range when no build published a test report. Artifacts size. The total size of the files each build kept. It is recorded only when the build keeps them in the standard storage on the server. Builds that keep artifacts in remote storage have no point. Read a chart. The horizontal axis is time. For a short range it shows times as well as days, and never repeats a label. The vertical axis picks units that fit, such as s , ms , B or %. Each point is one build. A failed build has a larger red point. Move the mouse over a point (or press ← and → , Home and End while the chart has focus) to see a tip: the build number, the date and each value. Click a point, or press Enter on it, to open that build. The charts redraw when the page gets narrower, for example when you fold or resize the sidebar. Custom charts. Below the default charts, Custom charts plots any number you choose. Anyone who can configure the build configuration can add and remove charts. Report a number from a build. A build step prints a line like this. The number is then kept for that build: ##buildspawn[buildStatisticValue key='coverage' value='81.5']. # PowerShell: report the size of the Windows package in megabytes $mb = [math]::Round((Get-Item Build\\Game-Win64.zip).Length / 1MB, 1) Write-Host \"##buildspawn[buildStatisticValue key='package.win64.mb' value='$mb']\". key is a name of letters, digits,. , - and _ , up to 100 characters. value must be a number, for example 12 , -3.5 or 1.2e6. If a build prints the same key twice, the last value counts. A build keeps up to 200 keys. A line with a bad key or value is ignored. You cannot overwrite the numbers Buildspawn measures itself, such as the build duration. See Service messages for every message a script can print. Add a chart. On the Statistics tab, under Custom charts , click Add new chart. Type a Title , or leave it empty to name the chart after its statistics (up to 100 characters). Under Statistics to plot , choose one or more, up to 10. The list holds the built-in numbers and every key your builds reported. It says No custom statistics yet. Report one from a build log. when there are none. Click Add. A message says Chart added. To change the order, use Move up and Move down on the chart. To delete it, use Remove chart. A build configuration can have up to 30 custom charts. What you see when it runs. Charts fill in as builds finish. A new build configuration says No builds in this range and shows the line to print. Values come from the end of each build, so a running bu"},
{"title":"Ant","href":"build-steps/ant","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Runs Apache Ant targets with a build file and properties. When to use it. Use it for Java projects that still build with Ant, such as an Android launcher or an old server tool. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type Ant in Filter step types if you like, then click the Ant tile (group Build tools ). Type a Step name , for example Build launcher. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the Ant step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Targets. One target per line. Empty runs the default target of the build file. Empty. clean then dist. Build file from. Path to a build file or Content written here. Path to a build file. Path to a build file. Build file. Passed as -f. Empty lets Ant use build.xml in the working directory. Empty. tools/build.xml. Build file content. The build.xml itself. It is written to a temporary file in the working directory, passed as -f and removed afterwards. Build parameters are in the environment as env.NAME. Empty. An Ant project. Properties. One name=value per line, passed as -Dname=value. Other processes can see them, so pass a secret through the environment. Empty. version=1.4. Ant home path. Where Ant is installed. Empty takes ant from the PATH , and the build configuration then needs an agent that has it. May use %name% parameters. Empty. C:\\tools\\ant. Code coverage. None or JaCoCo, as in Gradle and Maven. None. JaCoCo. JaCoCo folder on the agent. An unpacked JaCoCo download with lib/jacocoagent.jar and lib/jacococli.jar. The agent goes into JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS , so every JVM prints a Picked up JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS line. Empty. C:\\tools\\jacoco. Class folders for the report. Folders with compiled classes, one per line, relative to the working directory. Empty keeps only the exec file and makes no report. Empty. build/classes. Include classes. One class pattern per line, such as com.acme.*. A star matches any characters, a question mark one. Empty measures every class. Only letters, digits, dot, star, question mark, dollar, underscore and dash. Empty. com.studio.*. Exclude classes. One class pattern per line. A match is left out of the totals. The HTML report still lists it. Empty. *.Generated*. JDK. Inherit uses the JDK of the build configuration, or the java of the agent. A JDK from Settings › Tools sets JAVA_HOME for this step. Not available in a container: the image brings its own Java. Inherit. JDK 21. JVM command line parameters. One parameter per line, such as -Xmx2g. They go in the variable the tool reads and are added to what is set there. A parameter cannot hold a space or quote. Do not put a secret here. Empty. -Xmx2g. Working directory, Environment variables, Test reports, Run step within container. The same in every runner. See Fields every runner has. A worked example. Build a distribution jar for the launcher. Step name Build launcher , Targets clean and dist on two lines, Properties version=1.4.%build.number%. What you see when it runs. The console shows $ ant -Dversion=1.4.57 clean dist and Ant's target list. A BUILD FAILED line fails the step. With JaCoCo on, the build page shows the coverage totals. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Ant was not found on this agent. Install Ant or set Ant home path. The Ant step is set to a build file written in the step, and it is empty. Type the build file, or switch to Path to a build file. A property of the Ant step is not name=value. Use name=value. A name holds letters, digits, dot, dash and underscore. The JaCoCo folder is not set / has no lib/jacocoagent.jar. Point JaCoCo folder on the agent to the unpacked download. Related pages. Gradle. Maven. Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"Execute Windows batch command","href":"build-steps/batch-file","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Runs a Windows batch script with cmd. It is the classic step, kept as it was. When to use it. Use it on Windows agents for plain.bat scripts. For new work, Command Line does the same and adds a code editor, stderr colours and container settings. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type Execute Windows batch command in Filter step types if you like, then click the Execute Windows batch command tile (group Other ). Type a Step name , for example Run build script. Type the script into Command. Open More options for the unstable error level. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Command. The batch script. It runs with the workspace as the current directory. %name% build parameters are put into the text before it runs; %NAME% for an environment variable is left for cmd. See The script editor for the safeguards. Empty. call Build\\build.bat %CONFIG%. ERRORLEVEL to set build unstable (More options). A batch error level that makes the build unstable instead of failed. The next steps still run. The value 0 is ignored. Allowed values go from -2147483648 to 2147483647. Empty. 2. Environment filters (More options). Rules that remove variables from what this step sees. A parameter that a filter removes stays removed. None. A worked example. A Windows agent runs the studio's build script and treats exit code 2 (warnings) as unstable. Step name Run build script , Command : call Build\\build.bat %CONFIG% || exit /b %errorlevel%. What you see when it runs. The console block is titled [Step 1/1] Run build script (Execute Windows batch command). The script is saved as a temporary buildspawn….bat file and run with cmd /c call. When the exit code equals the unstable value, the step is shown as unstable and the build continues. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The script ends with exit code 1 but you wanted success. End the script with exit /b 0 , or fix the failing command. A variable shows as %NAME% text. Name it in the build configuration's parameters, or set it in an earlier step. Related pages. Command Line. Execute shell. Step statuses. Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"Code Inspections","href":"build-steps/code-inspections","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Runs an analysis tool of your own, or reads reports an earlier step wrote (SARIF, Checkstyle, PMD, SpotBugs), and lists the issues on the build. When to use it. Use it when your analysis tool already writes a standard report. You get the issues on the build page without a special plugin. The step keeps only path, line, rule and message, never source text. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type Code Inspections in Filter step types if you like, then click the Code Inspections tile (group Code quality and coverage ). Type a Step name , for example Inspections. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the Code Inspections step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Tool. Optional. The tool first, then its arguments, one per line. Each line stays one argument. Leave empty to only read reports an earlier step wrote. Empty. pmd then check. Ignore the exit code of the tool. Many tools exit with a number other than 0 when they find issues. Tick this to keep going and read the report anyway. Off. On. Report files. An Ant pattern of report files inside the workspace. SARIF, Checkstyle, PMD and SpotBugs XML are recognised by their content. A path that leaves the workspace is refused. Empty. **/checkstyle-result.xml,**/*.sarif. Limit. Optional. The step is worse when it finds more issues than this number. Empty means no limit. Empty. 0. When over the limit. The build turns unstable or The step fails. The build turns unstable. The step fails. Working directory, Environment variables, Test reports, Run step within container. The same in every runner. See Fields every runner has. A worked example. Read the SARIF report that the C++ analyser wrote in an earlier step. Step name Inspections , leave Tool empty, Report files build/analysis/*.sarif , Limit 0 , When over the limit The step fails. What you see when it runs. The console says Read build/analysis/engine.sarif as SARIF. and Found 14 issues. The build page has a Code Inspections page with the issue counts and the list. Over the limit: Found 14 issues, more than the limit of 0. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. No report was read. Pattern: …. Check the pattern and that the earlier step wrote the file. A report pattern must stay inside the workspace. Remove absolute paths, drive letters, ~ and. More than N report files matched. Narrow the pattern. The report … is larger than N MB and is not read. Write a smaller report, or split it. The inspection tool was not found on this agent. Install the tool or put it on the PATH. Related pages. Duplicates finder. Inspections (ReSharper). FxCop. Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"Command Line","href":"build-steps/command-line","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Runs a script you type, or one program you name. It is a shell script on Linux and macOS agents and a batch file on Windows agents. When to use it. Use Command Line for most small jobs: copy files, call a build tool, print a version. Choose Custom script when you want several commands. Choose Executable with parameters to start one program directly, with no shell reading the parameters, which is safer for values from outside. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type Command Line in Filter step types if you like, then click the Command Line tile (group Command line and scripts ). Type a Step name , for example Compile shaders. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the Command Line step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Run. Custom script or Executable with parameters. Custom script. Executable with parameters. Script. The script text, in a code editor. %name% is replaced with that build parameter when the step runs. Type % to pick one. Secret parameters are listed by name only. See The script editor. Empty (required). Build\\copy-content.bat lines. Command executable. The program to start, with its path if it is not on the agent's PATH. It is started directly, without a shell, so nothing in the parameters is read as a command. Empty (required). C:\\Tools\\p4\\p4.exe. Command parameters. Split on spaces and line breaks into separate parameters. Put double quotes around a parameter that holds spaces: /c \"echo hi there\". A backslash is kept as it is. Other processes on the agent can see parameters, so pass a secret through the environment. Empty. sync //depot/Game/. Format stderr output as. Show stderr lines in orange or red in the log. Plain, as in the log today. Warning. Fail the step when anything is written to stderr. Off: the exit code alone decides. Off. On. Working directory, Environment variables, Test reports, Run step within container. The same in every runner. See Fields every runner has. A worked example. A Windows agent compiles a shader pack and prints the version. Step name Compile shaders , Run = Custom script : @echo off echo Building shaders for %CONFIG% (build %build.number%) Build\\ShaderCompiler.exe -platform Win64 -config %CONFIG% -out Saved\\Shaders if errorlevel 1 exit /b 1. Here %CONFIG% and %build.number% are build parameters, put into the script text when the step runs. What you see when it runs. The console block is titled [Step 1/3] Compile shaders (Command Line). The script is saved to a temporary file on the agent and run. A line with $ shows the command. Output follows. A non-zero exit code fails the step: Command Line exited with code 1. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The Command Line step has no script / has no executable. Fill in the Script or the Command executable. … could not start. The program was not found. Give its full path, or put it on the agent's PATH. This tool is a Windows batch file, which cannot take an argument holding a double quote, %, ! or a line break. Remove those characters from the parameters, or use a script. Parameter X was given a value other than its default by someone who may not configure this build configuration. Use the default value, or ask someone with Configure permission to run it. Parameter X holds a secret and is not written into the script. Read the environment variable of the parameter instead. wrote to standard error, and the step is set to fail when it does. Untick Fail the step when anything is written to stderr , or fix the tool's warnings. Related pages. The script editor. Execute shell. Execute Windows batch command. PowerShell. Secrets. Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"Fields every runner has","href":"build-steps/common-step-fields","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","Use build parameters in script text","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Most runners end with the same fields: working directory, environment variables, test reports, container settings and, for script runners, how stderr is shown. They work the same in every runner. When to use it. Read this page once. Each runner page then only lists what is special about it, and points here for these fields. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Add a step or click Edit on one. Scroll to the end of the runner's fields. Fill in the fields below and click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Working directory. Relative to the workspace. Empty means the workspace itself. It is created if missing. Inside a container it must be inside the workspace. Empty. Build/Tools. Environment variables. One NAME=value per line. They are stored in plain text in the build configuration: use a password parameter for a secret. A line that is not NAME=value fails the step. Empty. UE_BUILD_TARGET=Game. Test reports. JUnit XML files, as an Ant pattern relative to the workspace. They are recorded as test results, also when the step fails. Empty. reports/*.xml. Run step within container. The step runs in a new container from this image, with the workspace mounted, and the build continues on the agent. The agent then needs Docker, not the tool. Empty runs the step on the agent. Parameters, the variables above and what the build adds go in; the agent's own variables and PATH stay out. Empty. alpine:3.20. Image platform (More options). Any takes what Docker on the agent runs. Linux or Windows asks for that kind of image. On a Windows agent a Linux image sees the workspace at /workspace. A Windows image is refused on Linux and macOS agents. Any. Linux. Pull policy (More options). If missing on the agent , Always or Never. If missing on the agent. Always. Force pull on each run (More options). Pulls the image on each run, whatever the pull policy says. Off. On for a moving tag such as latest. Additional run arguments (More options). One argument per line, such as --network=host. They go to docker run as given and nothing checks them. Anyone with Configure on the build configuration chooses them. Do not put a secret here. Empty. --memory=4g. Format stderr output as. Command Line and PowerShell only. Lines written to stderr show in orange ( Warning ) or red ( Error ) in the log. The text itself is not changed. Plain, as in the log today. Warning. Fail the step when anything is written to stderr. Command Line and PowerShell only. Off: the exit code alone decides. Many tools print progress to stderr and still succeed. Off. On for a strict script. Docker, Docker Compose and Xcode Project have no container settings: they use the tool of the agent itself. Use build parameters in script text. In the script of Command Line, PowerShell, Python, Node.js, Kotlin Script and C# Script steps, write %name% to use a build parameter. Secrets are never written into script text. Read them from the environment instead. See The script editor. A worked example. A Python step that runs the asset checker in the Tools folder and records its JUnit XML: Field. Value. Working directory. Tools. Environment variables. ASSET_ROOT=Content. Test reports. reports/*.xml. Run step within container. python:3.12. What you see when it runs. The console prints the command that was started, after a $ , then its output. With a container image, a line says that the step runs in the container. When test reports match, the build gets a Tests tab, and a failed test makes the step unstable. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. This step needs a workspace and the build has none. Run the build on an agent that has a workspace. Line N of the environment variables is not NAME=value. Fix the line. One NAME=value per line. This step runs in a container and docker could not be started on this agent. Install Docker on the agent, or clear Run step within container. Docker on this agent did not answer. Start the Docker service on t"},
{"title":"Configured build step","href":"build-steps/configured-build-step","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"The Configured build step is the wrapper around the steps you edit on the Build Steps page. It holds what is the same for every runner: name, ID, on/off switch, when it runs, conditions, retry, outputs and where it runs. When to use it. You never add this step yourself. Buildspawn wraps a runner in it when the step has any setting that differs from the plain default, such as a name, a condition or a retry. You meet it in settings files and in the API, where a step appears as a Configured build step that holds the real runner. Knowing this helps you read those files. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Edit on a step, or add a new one. Change any field in the table below and click Save. Buildspawn wraps the runner if it needs to. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Step name. Shown in the list, on the build page and in the console. The runner name. Package for Steam. Step ID. Fixed after creation. Used in BUILD_STEP_STATUS_<ID>. Made from the name. package_steam. Enabled. A disabled step stays in the list and does not run. On. Off. Execute step. The execution policy. Five choices; see The steps list. If all previous steps finished successfully. Only if build status is failed. Conditions. All must be true for the step to run. None. PLATFORM matches regex Win.*. Retry on failure, Wait between attempts, Only for exit codes. Try a failed step again. See Resume and retry. 0 tries. 2 tries, 30 seconds. Outputs. Values the step declares. A step that ends without setting one fails. None. version. Run on, Passes on, Fallback after, Files sent back. Run the step on another machine. See Parallel blocks and overrides. Same machine as before. A pool or label. Environment variables. Set for this step only, over the build configuration's own. None. CONFIG=Shipping. A worked example. You add a Command Line step named Collect crash dumps and set it to run Only if build status is failed. It is now a Configured build step with the Command Line runner inside. A step left at all defaults stays a plain runner, so older settings keep working. What you see when it runs. The console block is titled [Step 3/5] Collect crash dumps (Command Line) : the step name, then the runner. The runner's own output is inside the block. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. A step in a settings file looks different from the page. The file shows the wrapper and the runner inside it. The page shows both as one step. A step shows no name. The runner name is used, for example Command Line. Give the step a name. Related pages. The steps list and the step editor. Steps shared from a project or template. Settings files."},
{"title":"Container Deployer","href":"build-steps/container-deployer","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Deploys a WAR or EAR file to Tomcat, JBoss or GlassFish. Credentials are chosen by id. When to use it. Use it to put a web service, such as a store backend or a leaderboard, on an application server after the build. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type Container Deployer in Filter step types if you like, then click the Container Deployer tile (group Upload and deploy ). Type a Step name , for example Deploy leaderboard. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the Container Deployer step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. WAR/EAR files. The files to deploy, relative to the workspace root. Ant-style globs work, such as **/*.war. Empty. target/leaderboard.war. Context path. The context path the container uses to publish the WAR or EAR. It may be overridden if the file has its own container configuration. Empty. /leaderboard. Containers. Add one or more containers to deploy to. Choose the kind: Tomcat 4.x to 9.x, JBoss AS 3.x to 7.x, or GlassFish 2.x to 4.x. Each has its own fields, below. None. Tomcat 9.x. Credentials (every container). The username and password credential for the container's manager. None. tomcat-deployer. Tomcat URL. The Tomcat base URL, such as https://tomcat.studio.example:8443. Empty. https://tomcat.studio.example:8443. Alternative deployment context (Tomcat). Overwrites the context path from the main block. Empty. /lb. Manager context path (Tomcat). Overwrites the default /manager for Tomcat 4 to 6 or /manager/text for Tomcat 7 and newer. Default. /manager/text. JBoss URL. The JBoss URL to deploy to. Empty. http://jboss.studio.example:8080. GlassFish home, GlassFish admin port, GlassFish hostname. Where GlassFish is installed, and where its admin server listens. Empty. /opt/glassfish4 , 4848 , glassfish.studio.example. A worked example. Deploy the leaderboard service to the studio Tomcat. Step name Deploy leaderboard , WAR/EAR files target/leaderboard.war , Context path /leaderboard , container Tomcat 9.x with Credentials tomcat-deployer and Tomcat URL https://tomcat.studio.example:8443. What you see when it runs. The console shows the files found and a line for each deployment to the container. A deployment that the container refuses fails the step. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The credential is not in the list. Create a username and password credential first. See Secrets. Nothing is deployed. Check the pattern in WAR/EAR files : it is relative to the workspace root. 401 or 403 from Tomcat. The user needs the manager script role in Tomcat. Related pages. Secrets. SSH Exec. Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"C# Script","href":"build-steps/csharp-script","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Runs a C# script, typed into the step or from a.csx file, with dotnet-script or csi. When to use it. Use it for build logic you would rather write in C# than in a shell script, such as editing a version file or checking asset names. It needs dotnet-script or csi on the agent. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type C# Script in Filter step types if you like, then click the C# Script tile (group Build tools ). Type a Step name , for example Stamp version. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the C# Script step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Tool. dotnet-script (a.NET global tool) or csi. Both must be installed on the agent and found on its PATH. dotnet-script. dotnet-script. Path to the tool. Path of the tool chosen above. Empty finds it on the agent's PATH , and the build configuration then needs an agent that has it. May use %name% parameters. Empty. C:\\Users\\build\\.dotnet\\tools\\dotnet-script.exe. Script in this step / File. Choose one. Script is the C# text, in a code editor. Script file is a.csx file relative to the working directory. Script in this step. Build/stamp.csx. NuGet package sources. One source per line, a URL or a folder, each passed to dotnet-script as -s. csi has no such switch and the step stops. Put no user name or password in an address: use a NuGet.Config on the agent. Empty. https://nuget.studio.example/v3/index.json. Arguments. One argument per line, passed to the script. Other processes on the agent can see them, so pass a secret through the environment. Empty. %build.number%. Working directory, Environment variables, Test reports, Run step within container. The same in every runner. See Fields every runner has. A worked example. Write the build number into a version file. Step name Stamp version , choose Script in this step : var version = \"1.4.\" + Args[0]; System.IO.File.WriteAllText(\"Build/version.txt\", version); Console.WriteLine(\"Version \" + version); Set Arguments to %build.number%. What you see when it runs. The console shows the dotnet-script command after a $ , then the script output: Version 1.4.57. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. … was not found on this agent. Install the tool and put it on the PATH , or set Path to the tool. NuGet package sources need dotnet-script. Choose dotnet-script , or empty the sources. A NuGet package source must not start with a dash or hold a user name or password. Move the credentials to a NuGet.Config on the agent. The C# Script step has no script / no script file. Fill in the field for the mode you chose. Related pages. The script editor.NET. Command Line. Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"Docker Compose","href":"build-steps/docker-compose","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Runs docker compose with a file, a command and services. When to use it. Use it to start the services a test needs, such as a game server and its database, and to stop them again after the tests. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type Docker Compose in Filter step types if you like, then click the Docker Compose tile (group Containers ). Type a Step name , for example Start services. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the Docker Compose step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Compose files. One file per line, each passed as -f. Empty lets docker compose find its default file. Empty. docker-compose.test.yml. Command. up (starts in the background) , down , build , pull , ps , logs , stop or Other command. up. down. Custom command. Used with Other command only: the compose command, such as restart. Empty. restart. Services. One service per line, named after the command. Empty means all of them. Empty. gameserver. Arguments. One argument per line, placed before the services. Empty. --wait. Pull images explicitly. Runs docker compose pull first, with the same files and services, before up or build. A failed pull stops the step. Off. On. Working directory, Environment variables, Test reports. The same in every runner. See Fields every runner has. This runner has no container settings. A worked example. Start the test server, run the tests, then stop it. Step 1 Start services : Compose files docker-compose.test.yml , Command up , Arguments --wait. Step 2 runs the tests. Step 3 Stop services : Command down , with Execute step set to Even if some of the previous steps failed. What you see when it runs. The console shows $ docker compose -f docker-compose.test.yml up --wait and the container events. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The docker command was not found on this agent, or it has no compose command. Install Docker with Docker Compose, or put docker on the PATH. The Docker Compose step has no command. Fill in Custom command for Other command. Services stay running after a failed build. Add a down step that runs even if previous steps failed. Related pages. Docker. Run steps in a Docker container. Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"Docker","href":"build-steps/docker","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Runs the docker command: build an image, push it, or run any other docker command. When to use it. Use it to build and push images, for example the image of your dedicated game server. The agent needs Docker running. To run a step inside a container instead, use Run step within container in any other runner. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type Docker in Filter step types if you like, then click the Docker tile (group Containers ). Type a Step name , for example Build server image. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the Docker step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Command. Build an image , Push an image or Other command. Build an image. Push an image. Dockerfile source. File , URL or File content. Build only. File. File. Dockerfile. Build only. Path of the Dockerfile. Empty uses the Dockerfile in the build context. Empty. Docker/Server.Dockerfile. Dockerfile URL. Build only. An http or https address of a Dockerfile. It replaces the build context, which is then empty, so the Dockerfile cannot copy files. Put no user name or password in the address. Empty. https://example.com/Dockerfile. Dockerfile content. Build only. The Dockerfile itself. It is written to a temporary file in the working directory, passed as -f and removed afterwards. Do not put a secret here: use a build argument from the environment. Empty. A Dockerfile. Image platform. Build only. Passed as --platform , such as linux/amd64 or linux/arm64. Empty builds for the platform of the Docker daemon. Empty. linux/amd64. Build context. Build only. Folder to build from, relative to the working directory. Empty uses the working directory. Empty. Builds/LinuxServer. Tags. Build only. One name:tag per line, each passed as -t. Empty. registry.studio.example/skyforge/server:%build.number%. Image. Push only. The image to push, as name:tag. The agent must be signed in to the registry already. Empty (required). registry.studio.example/skyforge/server:57. Arguments. One argument per line. Each line stays one argument. For Other command this is everything after docker. Pass a secret through the environment. Empty. --build-arg then CONFIG=Shipping. Working directory, Environment variables, Test reports. The same in every runner. See Fields every runner has. This runner has no container settings. A worked example. Build the Linux server image from the staged build. Step name Build server image , Command Build an image , Dockerfile Docker/Server.Dockerfile , Build context Builds/LinuxServer , Tags registry.studio.example/skyforge/server:%build.number%. A second Docker step with Push an image pushes the same name. What you see when it runs. The console shows $ docker build -f … -t … and Docker's build output, then $ docker push …. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The docker command was not found on this agent. Install Docker there or put docker on the PATH. The Docker step has no command / no image name. Fill in Arguments for Other command, or Image for push. The Docker build step is set to a Dockerfile written in the step, and it is empty. Type the Dockerfile or change the source. The Dockerfile address must start with http:// or https://. Fix the address and remove any user name or password. The image platform holds letters, digits, dot, dash, underscore, slash and comma only. Use a value like linux/arm64. Push is denied. Sign the agent in to the registry first, as the account the agent runs as. Related pages. Docker Compose. Run steps in a Docker container. Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"dotCover","href":"build-steps/dotcover","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Runs a program under the dotCover command line tool and writes a coverage report in HTML, XML or JSON. When to use it. Use it to measure how much of your C# code your tests reach. The target is usually dotnet with test , or a test console runner. The tool has its own licence, which the step does not check. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type dotCover in Filter step types if you like, then click the dotCover tile (group Code quality and coverage ). Type a Step name , for example Coverage. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the dotCover step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Target program. The program run under coverage, such as dotnet or a test console runner. Empty (required). dotnet. Target arguments. One argument per line. dotCover takes them as one string, so an argument with a space is put in double quotes. Empty. test then Tests/Game.Tests.csproj. Filters. One filter per line, joined with semicolons, such as +:MyApp.* or -:*.Tests. Empty covers everything dotCover finds. Empty. +:Game.*. Report type. XML , HTML , JSON or Detailed XML. XML. HTML. Report file. Relative to the working directory. Empty writes dotCover.xml , dotCover.html or dotCover.json. Empty. reports/coverage.html. dotCover tool. Path of the dotCover command line tool. Empty takes dotCover from the PATH. Empty. C:\\tools\\dotCover\\dotCover.exe. Working directory, Environment variables, Test reports, Run step within container. The same in every runner. See Fields every runner has. A worked example. Measure the coverage of the game library tests. Step name Coverage , Target program dotnet , Target arguments test and Tests/Game.Tests.csproj , Filters +:Game.* , Report type HTML. What you see when it runs. The console shows the dotCover command and the target's output. The build page shows the coverage totals, with the report archived. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The dotCover command was not found on this agent. Install the dotCover command line tool or put dotCover on the PATH. The dotCover step has no target program. Fill in Target program. No … coverage report was found. The target may have stopped before the report was written. Related pages. NUnit (with dotCover or Coverlet).NET. Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":".NET","href":"build-steps/dotnet","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Runs the dotnet command: build, test, publish, restore, run or your own command. When to use it. Use it to build and test C# projects with the.NET SDK, such as your game's tools, a launcher or a Unity package with tests. For a Visual Studio.sln that needs full MSBuild, see MSBuild. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type.NET in Filter step types if you like, then click the.NET tile (group Build tools ). Type a Step name , for example Test tools. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the.NET step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Command. build , test (results recorded) , publish , restore , run or Custom command. build. test. Custom command. Used with Custom command only: the word after dotnet , such as format or tool. Empty. format. Project. A project, solution or file. Empty lets dotnet look in the working directory. For run it follows --project. Empty. Tools/BuildTools.sln. Configuration. Passed as -c to build, test, publish and run. Empty. Release. Required SDK. The.NET SDK the agent must have, such as 8.0 (any 8.0.x) or 8.0.100. The build configuration waits for an agent that listed it. Not passed to dotnet. Empty. 8.0. Framework. The target framework, passed as -f to build, test, publish and run. Empty. net8.0. Runtime. The runtime identifier, passed as -r to build, test, publish, restore and run. Empty. win-x64. Output directory. Passed as -o to build, test and publish. Empty. out/tools. Version suffix. Passed as --version-suffix to build and publish. Empty. rc.%build.number%. Logging verbosity. Passed as -v to every command except a custom one: quiet, minimal, normal, detailed, diagnostic. Default. minimal. Arguments. One argument per line. Each line stays one argument, whatever it holds. Pass a secret through the environment. Empty. --no-restore. Working directory, Environment variables, Test reports, Run step within container. The same in every runner. See Fields every runner has. A worked example. Run the unit tests of the build tools. Step name Test tools , Command = test (results recorded) , Project Tools/BuildTools.sln , Configuration Release , Required SDK 8.0. What you see when it runs. The console shows $ dotnet test Tools/BuildTools.sln -c Release and the test output. The results of dotnet test are recorded: the build gets a Tests tab, and a failed test makes the step unstable. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The dotnet command was not found on this agent. Install the.NET SDK there or put dotnet on the PATH. The.NET step has no custom command. Fill in Custom command. … logging verbosity that dotnet does not know. Use quiet, minimal, normal, detailed or diagnostic. The value for … must not start with a dash. Remove the leading dash from that field. Related pages. MSBuild. NUnit. Build a.sln with MSBuild. Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"Duplicates finder","href":"build-steps/duplicates-finder","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Runs the PMD copy-paste detector ( pmd cpd ) or reads its report, and lists the duplicate blocks on the build. When to use it. Use it to find copy-pasted code in your game scripts before it spreads. It needs PMD 7 on the agent if the step runs the tool itself. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type Duplicates finder in Filter step types if you like, then click the Duplicates finder tile (group Code quality and coverage ). Type a Step name , for example Find duplicates. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the Duplicates finder step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Language. The PMD language name, for example java , cs , javascript , python , kotlin. java. cs. Minimum tokens. Blocks shorter than this many tokens are not reported. 100. 150. Paths. Folders or files to search, one per line, relative to the working directory. Runs pmd cpd , which must be on the agent. Leave empty to only read a report an earlier step wrote. Empty. Assets/Scripts. Report file. Used when Paths is empty: an Ant pattern of PMD CPD XML files inside the workspace. A path that leaves the workspace is refused. Empty. reports/cpd.xml. Limit. Optional. The step is worse when it finds more duplicate blocks than this number. Empty means no limit. Empty. 20. When over the limit. The build turns unstable or The step fails. The build turns unstable. The build turns unstable. Working directory, Environment variables, Test reports, Run step within container. The same in every runner. See Fields every runner has. A worked example. Search the Unity scripts for duplicates. Step name Find duplicates , Language cs , Minimum tokens 150 , Paths Assets/Scripts , Limit 20. What you see when it runs. The console shows the pmd cpd command and Found 7 duplicate blocks. The build page lists the blocks, with lines and tokens: 7 duplicate blocks, 212 duplicated lines, largest block 340 tokens. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The pmd command was not found on this agent. Install PMD 7 or put pmd on the PATH. The Duplicates finder step has no paths to search. Fill in Paths (or a Report file to read). Related pages. Duplicates (ReSharper). Code Inspections. Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"FTP Upload","href":"build-steps/ftp-upload","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Copies files to an FTP or FTPS server, with include patterns. Use FTPS: plain FTP sends passwords unprotected. In the step picker this is called Send files over FTP. When to use it. Use it to hand builds to a partner or a platform holder's drop server that only offers FTP. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type FTP Upload in Filter step types if you like, then click the FTP Upload tile (group Upload and deploy ). Type a Step name , for example Send patch. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the FTP Upload step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Name (FTP Server). The server to use, from the servers defined once under Settings › System. First server. partner-drop. Verbose output in console, Retry, Label, Fail the build if an error occurs. As in SSH Upload. Source files. Files to upload: comma-separated Ant includes, base folder is the workspace. Empty. Builds/Patch/**. Remove prefix. The first part of the path that is not created on the server. Empty. Builds/Patch. Remote directory. Optional destination folder, below the one in the server settings. Created if missing. Empty. /patches/1.4. Exclude files (More options). Comma-separated Ant excludes. Empty. **/*.pdb. Pattern separator (More options). Regular expression that splits the patterns. [, ]+. ,. No default excludes, Make empty dirs, Flatten files, Remote directory is a date format (More options). As in SSH Upload. Off. A worked example. Send the patch files to the partner. Step name Send patch , Name partner-drop , Source files Builds/Patch/** , Remove prefix Builds/Patch , Remote directory /patches/1.4. What you see when it runs. The console says it is connecting to the configuration, then Transferred N file(s) , then disconnecting. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The server list is empty. An administrator must add an FTP server under Settings › System. Passwords seen on the network. Use an FTPS server. Plain FTP sends passwords unprotected. Related pages. SSH Upload. SMB Upload. Global connections. Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"FxCop","href":"build-steps/fxcop","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Runs FxCopCmd on assemblies or an FxCop project and lists the issues on the build. It runs on Windows agents only, and the tool is not part of Buildspawn. When to use it. Use it to check compiled.NET assemblies against code rules, for example your gameplay library before release. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type FxCop in Filter step types if you like, then click the FxCop tile (group Code quality and coverage ). Type a Step name , for example FxCop. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the FxCop step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. FxCop detection. Autodetect installation runs FxCopCmd from the agent's PATH and the build configuration asks for an agent that has it. Specify installation root names the folder. Autodetect installation. Specify installation root. Installation root. The folder that holds FxCopCmd.exe. Shows for Specify installation root. Empty. C:\\Program Files (x86)\\Microsoft Fxcop 10.0. Assemblies. One assembly, folder or wildcard per line, relative to the working directory. Each is passed as /file: Give assemblies, a project file, or both. Empty. bin/Release/Game.Core.dll. FxCop project file. Passed as /project: Empty. Rules/Game.FxCop. Ignore generated code. Passed as /ignoregeneratedcode. Off. On. Report XSLT file. Passed as /outXsl: The XML report stays XML, with a reference to the file, and its issues are listed on the build. Empty. Rules/report.xsl. Additional FxCopCmd options. One option per line, for example /dictionary:CustomDictionary.xml. Empty. /dictionary:Words.xml. Fail on analysis errors. On: any exit code other than 0 fails the step. Off: analysis errors and rule exceptions (codes 1 and 2) are tolerated and the issues are still listed. On. On. Limit. Optional. The step is worse when it finds more issues than this number. Empty means no limit. Empty. 0. When over the limit. The build turns unstable or The step fails. The build turns unstable. The step fails. Working directory, Environment variables, Test reports, Run step within container. The same in every runner. See Fields every runner has. A worked example. Check the core library. Step name FxCop , Assemblies bin/Release/Game.Core.dll , tick Ignore generated code , Limit 0 , When over the limit The build turns unstable. What you see when it runs. The console shows the FxCopCmd command. The build page lists the issues on its Code Inspections page: how many issues, errors, warnings and notes, in how many files. Over the limit, the build turns unstable or the step fails. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The FxCop step runs on Windows agents only. Run it on a Windows agent. The FxCopCmd command was not found on this agent. Install FxCop, put FxCopCmd on the PATH , or give the installation root. The FxCop step has no assemblies and no project file. Fill in Assemblies or FxCop project file. Related pages. Code Inspections. Inspections (ReSharper). Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"Gradle","href":"build-steps/gradle","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Runs Gradle tasks with a build file and switches. It uses the project's Gradle wrapper when there is one. When to use it. Use it for Android and Java projects, such as a mobile build of your game or a backend service. Test reports under build/test-results are recorded for you. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type Gradle in Filter step types if you like, then click the Gradle tile (group Build tools ). Type a Step name , for example Assemble release. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the Gradle step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Tasks. One task per line, such as clean and build. Empty runs the project's default tasks. Empty. assembleRelease. Build file. Passed as -b. Empty lets Gradle find the build file. Gradle 9 removed -b : leave it empty there and set the working directory. Empty. app/build.gradle. Switches. One switch per line, such as --info or -Pname=value. Pass a secret through the environment. Empty. -PversionName=1.4. Gradle wrapper. Automatic uses gradlew of the working directory when there is one, otherwise gradle. Use the Gradle wrapper fails the step if the script is missing and needs no Gradle on the agent. Use Gradle on the agent never runs a wrapper. Automatic. Use the Gradle wrapper. Path to the wrapper script. Relative to the working directory, such as tools/gradlew. Empty uses gradlew. May use %name%. Empty. tools/gradlew. Debug. Adds --debug. The log then holds a lot of detail: do not use it with secrets in the environment. Off. Off. Stacktrace. Adds --stacktrace to print the stack trace of a failure. Off. On. Record test reports. Records the JUnit XML under build/test-results of the working directory and its sub-projects, also when the build fails. On. On. Build cache. Gradle default , Use the build cache ( --build-cache ) or Do not use the build cache ( --no-build-cache ). Gradle default. Use the build cache. Code coverage, Include classes, Exclude classes. As in Maven. JaCoCo is fetched from the repositories of the project. None. JaCoCo. JaCoCo version. The version of the JaCoCo plugin. Empty uses the default of your Gradle version. Needs Gradle 7 or newer. Empty. 0.8.15. Gradle home path. Where Gradle is installed. It replaces the project wrapper. Empty uses the wrapper, or gradle from the PATH. May use %name%. Empty. C:\\tools\\gradle-8.7. JDK. Inherit uses the JDK of the build configuration, or the java of the agent. A JDK from Settings › Tools sets JAVA_HOME for this step. Not available in a container: the image brings its own Java. Inherit. JDK 21. JVM command line parameters. One parameter per line, such as -Xmx2g. They go in the variable the tool reads and are added to what is set there. A parameter cannot hold a space or quote. Do not put a secret here. Empty. -Xmx2g. Working directory, Environment variables, Test reports, Run step within container. The same in every runner. See Fields every runner has. A worked example. Build the Android release of the game's companion app. Step name Assemble release , Tasks clean and assembleRelease on two lines, Switches -PversionCode=%build.number% , Gradle wrapper Use the Gradle wrapper. What you see when it runs. The console shows $./gradlew -PversionCode=57 clean assembleRelease , then Gradle's task lines. Test results are listed on the build. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The Gradle wrapper script was not found. Check Path to the wrapper script , or choose Use Gradle on the agent. Gradle was not found on this agent. Add a gradlew to the project, install Gradle there or put gradle on the PATH. Gradle 9 complains about -b. Empty Build file and set the Working directory. No … coverage report was found. The tests may have stopped before the report was written. Related pages. Maven. Ant. Kotlin Script"},
{"title":"Kotlin Script","href":"build-steps/kotlin-script","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Runs a Kotlin script, typed into the step or from a.kts file, with kotlinc -script. When to use it. Use it when your build tools are written in Kotlin or the JVM suits you better than shell. The agent needs the Kotlin compiler and a Java runtime. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type Kotlin Script in Filter step types if you like, then click the Kotlin Script tile (group Build tools ). Type a Step name , for example List platforms. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the Kotlin Script step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Script in this step / File. Choose one. Script is the Kotlin text in a code editor. Script file is a.kts file relative to the working directory. Script in this step. Build/tools.kts. Arguments. One argument per line. Each line stays one argument, whatever it holds. Pass a secret through the environment. Empty. Shipping. Compiler. Path of kotlinc. Empty takes kotlinc from the agent's PATH. Empty. C:\\kotlinc\\bin\\kotlinc.bat. Compiler version. Such as 2.1 or 2.1.10. The build configuration then asks for an agent whose kotlinc reports this version. Ignored when a compiler path is set. Empty. 2.1. JDK. Inherit uses the JDK of the build configuration, or the java of the agent. A JDK from Settings › Tools sets JAVA_HOME for this step. Not available in a container: the image brings its own Java. Inherit. JDK 21. JVM command line parameters. One parameter per line, such as -Xmx2g. They are added to the variable the tool reads. A parameter cannot hold a space or quote. Do not put a secret here. Empty. -Xmx2g. Working directory, Environment variables, Test reports, Run step within container. The same in every runner. See Fields every runner has. A worked example. Print the platform list for the matrix. Step name List platforms , Script : val platforms = listOf(\"Win64\", \"PS5\", \"XboxSeriesX\") println(\"Building \" + platforms.joinToString()). What you see when it runs. The console shows the kotlinc -script command after a $ and then the output Building Win64, PS5, XboxSeriesX. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The kotlinc command was not found on this agent. Install the Kotlin compiler or put kotlinc on the PATH. A JVM parameter holds a space, quote or one of …. Write one parameter per line with no spaces. A JDK of the Tools configuration is on the agent and cannot be used inside a container. Set the JDK to Inherit. Related pages. The script editor. Gradle. Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"Matrix mode for Parallel blocks","href":"build-steps/matrix-mode","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Write the steps once, list the values they should run with, and Buildspawn runs one side for every combination, such as every platform and every configuration. When to use it. Use Matrix mode when the same steps must run for several platforms or configurations, for example Win64, PS5 and Xbox, each in Development and Shipping. In plain Sides mode you would write each side by hand. In Matrix mode you write the steps once and use the values as %platform% and %config%. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add Parallel block. Name it, for example Cook all platforms. Under How the block is built click Change to Matrix (the choices are Sides and Matrix ). If the block already has several sides, Buildspawn asks first: the steps of the first side stay as the matrix steps and the other sides are dropped. Under Axes click Add axis. Type an Axis name such as platform and its values, separated by commas, such as Win64, PS5, XboxSeriesX. Add more axes if you need them. Optional: add Exclude rows for combinations that must not run, and Include rows for extra values or new combinations. Under Steps click Add step and add the steps. In any step field write %platform% for the axis value. Every step also gets the value as an environment variable of the same name. Choose Run on rules, Files sent back , Max at once , Try first and If a combination fails. Click Save block. Matrix mode: two axes make four combinations. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. How the block is built. Sides : you write each side by hand. Matrix : sides are made from values. Sides. Matrix. Axes. Each axis has an Axis name (letters, digits and underscores) and its values. Every value of one axis meets every value of the others. At most 4 axes and 256 combinations in all. None. platform = Win64, PS5; config = Development, Shipping. Exclude. Rows of combinations that must not run. A combination that has all the values of a row is dropped. None. platform = PS5 and config = Development. Include. Extra values for the combinations a row matches, or a new combination when it matches none. None. platform = Win64, sign = yes. Steps. Written once. Every combination runs them with its own values. Read a value as %platform%. None. Build.bat %platform% %config%. Run on. Where each combination runs. Rules are checked top to bottom and the first match wins. A rule says When an axis has some values, or Anything else , and then picks an Agent requirement , A pool or label (for example pool:Consoles or console-%platform% ) or Same machine as before. As the rules. When platform is PS5: pool Consoles. Files sent back. Rules for the files each combination sends back to the main build. %platform% stands for the value of that combination. All changed files. Saved/StagedBuilds/%platform%/**. Max at once. How many combinations run at the same time. The rest wait in the queue. Empty means all at once. Empty. 3. Try first. One combination that runs alone first. The rest start only if it passes. It ignores Max at once. None. platform = Win64, config = Development. If a combination fails. Let the others finish : every combination runs to its end and results are kept. Fail fast : no new combination starts, running ones finish. Stop the others : no new one starts and the running ones are stopped at once. The build fails after the block in all three cases. Let the others finish. Fail fast. Fallback after, Passes on. As in Parallel blocks. 10 minutes. A worked example. The Game / Windows Build build configuration cooks a Unreal Engine game for three platforms and two configurations. Add a Parallel block Cook and change it to Matrix. Axis platform : Win64, PS5, XboxSeriesX. Axis config : Development, Shipping. That makes 6 combinations. Exclude platform = PS5 with config = Development. 5 combinations remain. Add one Command Line step: Build\\Cook.bat %platform% %config%. Run on: when platform is PS5 or XboxSeriesX , A pool or label pool:Consol"},
{"title":"Maven","href":"build-steps/maven","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Runs Maven goals with a POM, a Maven version, JVM options and properties. In the step picker this is called Invoke top-level Maven targets. When to use it. Use it for Java services in your pipeline, such as a matchmaking backend. A non-zero exit code from Maven fails the step. Surefire and Failsafe test reports are recorded for you. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type Maven in Filter step types if you like, then click the Maven tile (group Build tools ). Type a Step name , for example Build lobby. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the Maven step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Maven Version. The Maven installation, chosen from Settings › Tools. Default (version) uses the default installation, Agent PATH uses mvn from the agent. Shown when an installation exists. Default. Maven 3.9. JDK. (Inherit) uses the JDK of the build configuration or the java of the agent. Not used inside a container. Inherit. JDK 21. Goals. The goals and options, such as clean install. Build parameters and environment variables work as ${env.NAME}. Empty. clean verify. Record test reports. Records Surefire and Failsafe reports after the run, unless the build configuration has a JUnit publisher. On. On. Code coverage, Include classes, Exclude classes, JaCoCo version (More options). JaCoCo. Maven fetches jacoco-maven-plugin from the repositories it is configured with. The goals prepare-agent and report run from the command line and the POM is not changed. A surefire argLine in the POM must keep the argLine property. JaCoCo version empty uses 0.8.15. None. JaCoCo. Incremental building (More options). Builds only the modules with changed files and the modules that depend on them, with -pl and -amd. A change in the root folder, a path outside the workspace, or a build with no changes builds everything. Modules the changed ones depend on must be in the local repository. Off. On. POM (More options). The root POM file, if it is not pom.xml. Empty. services/pom.xml. Properties (More options). One name=value per line, passed as -Dname=value. Lines starting with # are comments. Empty. skipITs=true. JVM Options (More options). Options for the JVM that runs Maven. Empty. -Xmx1g. Additional command line parameters (More options). Placed before the goals and split on white space. Empty. -T 4 -P release. Working directory (More options). A folder in the workspace to run Maven in. Empty runs it in the workspace. Empty. services/lobby. Inject build variables (More options). Passes all build variables to Maven as Java properties. Seldom needed, because they are environment variables anyway. Better map the ones you need in Properties : MY_VAR=${MY_VAR}. Off. Off. Use private Maven repository (More options). Gives this build its own local repository, so builds do not share downloaded files. Off. On. Settings file, Global Settings file (More options). Which Maven settings.xml to use. By default the settings of the Maven installation and the user on the agent. Default settings. A settings file in the workspace. A worked example. Build and test the lobby service. Step name Build lobby , Goals clean verify , Working directory services/lobby , Record test reports on. What you see when it runs. The console shows the mvn clean verify command and Maven's output. Test results show on the build. With incremental building on, the log says Incremental building: N changed module(s), and the modules that depend on them: M. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The Maven Version list is missing. No installation is defined. Add one under Settings › Tools , or use mvn from the agent. Everything is built although incremental building is on. The log says why: no changes, a change in the root folder, or a path outside the workspace. JaCoCo re"},
{"title":"MSBuild","href":"build-steps/msbuild","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Builds a Visual Studio project or solution with MSBuild. MSBuild installations are defined once, under Tools. When to use it. Use it for C# and C++ solutions that need the full Visual Studio build tools: your engine tools, plugins and anything with a.sln. Run it on a Windows agent that has Visual Studio or the Build Tools. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type MSBuild in Filter step types if you like, then click the MSBuild tile (group Build tools ). Type a Step name , for example Build tools. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the MSBuild step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. MSBuild Version. The MSBuild installation to use, chosen from the ones defined under Settings › Tools. (Default) uses the default installation. If none is defined, the step asks you to add one. (Default). MSBuild 17. Project File. The.proj or.sln file, from the workspace root. You can use build variables as ${var}. Empty. Tools/BuildTools.sln. Command Line Arguments. A whitespace-separated list, the same as you would type after msbuild. The installation's default parameters are added too. Empty. /p:Configuration=Release /m. Pass build variables as properties. Passes build variables to MSBuild as /p:name=value pairs. Passwords and other sensitive variables are not added. Off. Off. Do not use chcp command. Tick to leave out the chcp code page change that normally runs before MSBuild. Off. Off. Continue build on build failure. Keep the build going even when MSBuild fails. Off. Off. Set build to unstable if there are warnings. A compile with warnings makes the build unstable. Off. On. A worked example. Build the engine tools solution in Release. Step name Build tools , MSBuild Version MSBuild 17 , Project File Tools/BuildTools.sln , Command Line Arguments /p:Configuration=Release /m. What you see when it runs. The console shows the MSBuild command line, then its output. Compiler errors and warnings are marked in the log as MSBuild error and MSBuild warning. A failed build fails the step unless you ticked Continue build on build failure. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The MSBuild Version list is empty. Add an installation under Settings › Tools (open More tool types if MSBuild is not listed). Its Path to MSBuild Installation is the folder with MSBuild.exe , for example C:\\Program Files\\Microsoft Visual Studio\\2022\\BuildTools\\MSBuild\\Current\\Bin. Warnings do not change the result. Tick Set build to unstable if there are warnings. Related pages.NET. Tools. Build a.sln with MSBuild. Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"MSTest","href":"build-steps/mstest","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Runs unit tests with the MSTest command line tool and can report the results. Installations are defined once, under Tools. When to use it. Use it for older Visual Studio test projects that run with MSTest.exe. For new projects prefer.NET test or VSTest. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type MSTest in Filter step types if you like, then click the MSTest tile (group Tests ). Type a Step name , for example MSTest. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the MSTest step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. MsTest Version. The MSTest installation, from Settings › Tools. Default installation. MSTest 2022. Test Files. The paths to your compiled test assemblies. Several, separated by new lines. Relative to the workspace or full paths; spaces are allowed. Ant-style filters work: foo/* , foo/**/bar.dll. Passed as /testcontainer. Empty. Tests/**/*.Tests.dll. Fail if no tests. Fail the build when no test files are found. Off. On. Test Categories. Optional. Empty runs all tests. Use & and | and ! : Priority1&!SpeedTest runs tests with Priority1 but not SpeedTest. Passed as /category. Empty. Priority1. Result File Name. Name of the result file. It is deleted before each run and created relative to the workspace. Passed as /resultsfile. Empty. mstest.trx. Command Line Arguments. Extra arguments, separated by spaces, as you would give MSTest on the command line. Empty. /detail:errormessage. Continue on failed tests. Unticked: the build fails when a test fails. Ticked: the build continues. Off. Off. A worked example. Run the priority-1 tests. Step name MSTest , Test Files Tests/**/*.Tests.dll , Test Categories Priority1 , Result File Name mstest.trx. What you see when it runs. The console shows the MSTest command line and its output, then the result file name. A failed test fails the build unless Continue on failed tests is ticked. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The version list is empty. Add an installation under Settings › Tools. No tests ran. Check the patterns in Test Files , and tick Fail if no tests to be told. Related pages. NUnit. VSTest. Tools. Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"Node.js","href":"build-steps/nodejs","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Runs a JavaScript file or script with node , or an npm or yarn command. When to use it. Use it for web tools in your pipeline: a launcher website, an asset pipeline, or a script that talks to a store API. The agent needs Node.js. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type Node.js in Filter step types if you like, then click the Node.js tile (group Build tools ). Type a Step name , for example Build site. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the Node.js step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Run. Script in this step , File , npm command or yarn command. Script in this step. npm command. Script. JavaScript in a code editor. %name% is replaced with that build parameter. Empty. console.log(process.version). Script file. Relative to the working directory. Empty. tools/notify.js. Arguments. One argument per line. For a script or file they go to the script. For npm and yarn this is the command and what follows it, such as run on one line and build on the next. Pass a secret through the environment. Empty. run then build. Working directory, Environment variables, Test reports, Run step within container. The same in every runner. See Fields every runner has. A worked example. Build the studio's patch-notes site. Step name Build site , Run = npm command , Working directory site , Arguments : ci. Add a second step with arguments run and build. What you see when it runs. The console shows $ npm ci and then the npm output. A failing npm command fails the step with its exit code. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. npm was not found on this agent (or node, yarn). Install Node.js there or put the command on the PATH. The Node.js step has no npm or yarn command. Put the command in Arguments , for example install. The Node.js step has no script / no script file. Fill in the field for the mode you chose. Related pages. The script editor. Command Line. Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"NuGet Installer","href":"build-steps/nuget-installer","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Restores the NuGet packages of a solution or project with nuget restore , from the sources you name. When to use it. Use it before a MSBuild or.NET step, so the packages your solution needs are on the agent. It can also install the packages of one packages.config and update packages afterwards. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type NuGet Installer in Filter step types if you like, then click the NuGet Installer tile (group Packages ). Type a Step name , for example Restore packages. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the NuGet Installer step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Solution. A solution, project or packages.config , relative to the working directory. Runs nuget restore on it. Empty lets nuget look in the working directory. Empty. Tools/BuildTools.sln. Package sources. One source per line, each passed as -Source. Empty uses the sources nuget is configured with. Empty. https://nuget.studio.example/v3/index.json. Mode. Restore runs nuget restore on a solution or project. Install runs nuget install on one packages.config. Restore. Restore. Disable looking up packages from the local machine cache. Passed as -NoCache. Off. Off. Restore options. One argument per line, passed to the restore or install after the sources. Do not put a secret here. Empty. -Verbosity then quiet. Update packages after restoring. Runs nuget update on the same file after the restore succeeds, with the same sources. Off. Off. Update mode. Update via solution file : the file above is a solution or project. Update via packages.config file : it must be a.config file, or the step stops before it runs. Update via solution file. Update via solution file. Include pre-release packages. Passed as -Prerelease to the update. Off. Off. Perform safe update. Passed as -Safe to the update: only the smallest version change. Off. On. Update options. One argument per line, passed to the update only. Empty. -FileConflictAction then Ignore. NuGet tool. Path of nuget.exe. Empty takes nuget from the agent's PATH. Empty. C:\\tools\\nuget.exe. Arguments. One argument per line, such as -PackagesDirectory. Pass a secret through the environment. Empty. -PackagesDirectory then packages. Working directory, Environment variables, Test reports, Run step within container. The same in every runner. See Fields every runner has. A worked example. Restore the tools solution before the build. Step name Restore packages , Solution Tools/BuildTools.sln , Package sources https://nuget.studio.example/v3/index.json. What you see when it runs. The console shows $ nuget restore Tools/BuildTools.sln -Source … and the list of restored packages. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The nuget command was not found on this agent. Install nuget, put it on the PATH or set NuGet tool. The NuGet Installer step updates packages but has no solution or packages.config. Fill in Solution. … updates via a packages.config file, but its file is not a.config file. Choose Update via solution file , or name a.config file. Related pages. NuGet Pack. NuGet Publish. MSBuild. Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"NuGet Pack","href":"build-steps/nuget-pack","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Builds a.nupkg package from a.nuspec or project file with nuget pack , and can keep it as a build artifact. When to use it. Use it to package shared game libraries, such as your gameplay framework, so other projects can use them from a feed. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type NuGet Pack in Filter step types if you like, then click the NuGet Pack tile (group Packages ). Type a Step name , for example Pack framework. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the NuGet Pack step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Specification. A.nuspec or a project file, relative to the working directory. Empty (required). Packaging/Framework.nuspec. Output directory. Passed as -OutputDirectory. Empty writes the package to the working directory. Empty. out. Version. Passed as -Version. Empty uses the version in the specification. Empty. 1.4.%build.number%. Keep the packages as build artifacts. Archives the.nupkg files of the output directory with the build, also after a failed run. Off. On. Base directory. Passed as -BasePath : the folder the files in the specification are looked up in. Empty uses the working directory. Empty. Binaries. Exclude files. One pattern per line, each passed as -Exclude. Empty. **/*.pdb. Properties. One Name=value per line, joined with semicolons into the single -Properties argument. A line cannot hold a semicolon. Empty. Configuration=Release. Create tool package. Passed as -Tool. Off. Off. Include sources and symbols. Passed as -Symbols : a symbols package is built with the sources. Off. On. Clean output directory. Empties the output directory before packing. It must be a folder inside the working directory, never the working directory itself, so Output directory is required. Off. On. NuGet tool. Path of nuget.exe. Empty takes nuget from the PATH. Empty. C:\\tools\\nuget.exe. Arguments. One argument per line, such as -Symbols. Pass a secret through the environment. Empty. -NoDefaultExcludes. Working directory, Environment variables, Test reports, Run step within container. The same in every runner. See Fields every runner has. A worked example. Package the gameplay framework. Step name Pack framework , Specification Packaging/Framework.nuspec , Output directory out , Version 1.4.%build.number% , tick Keep the packages as build artifacts and Clean output directory. What you see when it runs. The console shows $ nuget pack … and Successfully created package. The package Framework.1.4.57.nupkg appears under Artifacts of the build. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The NuGet Pack step has no specification file. Fill in Specification. A property of the NuGet Pack step is not Name=value, or holds a semicolon. Use one Name=value per line, with no semicolons. The NuGet Pack step cleans its output directory but has none. Set Output directory to a folder inside the working directory. The output directory … is not a folder inside the working directory, so it is not cleaned. Use a relative folder such as out. Related pages. NuGet Installer. NuGet Publish. Pack and publish a NuGet package. Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"NuGet Publish","href":"build-steps/nuget-publish","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Pushes packages to a NuGet feed with nuget push. The API key comes from a Secret text credential and is masked in the log. When to use it. Use it after NuGet Pack to put your package on the studio feed. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type NuGet Publish in Filter step types if you like, then click the NuGet Publish tile (group Packages ). Type a Step name , for example Publish framework. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the NuGet Publish step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Packages. The package to push, or a pattern such as out/*.nupkg , relative to the working directory. Empty (required). out/*.nupkg. Package source. Passed as -Source , a feed URL. Empty uses the default source of nuget. Empty. https://nuget.studio.example/v3/index.json. API key. A Secret text credential that holds the API key. It is read when the step runs, passed to nuget as one argument and masked in the log. It is never stored in the build configuration. Other processes on the agent can still see the argument while nuget runs. None. The credential nuget-feed-key. NuGet tool. Path of nuget.exe. Empty takes nuget from the PATH. Empty. C:\\tools\\nuget.exe. Arguments. One argument per line, such as -SkipDuplicate. Do not put a key here: use the API key field. Empty. -SkipDuplicate. Working directory, Environment variables, Test reports, Run step within container. The same in every runner. See Fields every runner has. A worked example. Push the package that the earlier step made. Step name Publish framework , Packages out/*.nupkg , Package source the studio feed, API key the credential nuget-feed-key , Arguments -SkipDuplicate. What you see when it runs. The console shows $ nuget push out/Framework.1.4.57.nupkg -Source … -ApiKey ********. The key is masked. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The API key credential … was not found, is not Secret text, or this build may not use it. Create a Secret text credential and check that this project may use it. See Secrets. The NuGet Publish step has no packages to push. Fill in Packages. A 409 Conflict from the feed. The version exists already. Add -SkipDuplicate or raise the version. Related pages. NuGet Pack. Secrets. Pack and publish a NuGet package. Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"NUnit","href":"build-steps/nunit","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Runs the NUnit 3 console runner on test assemblies and records the results. When to use it. Use it to run the unit tests of your game code or tools written in C#. Results show on the build. It can run the tests that failed last time first, so you hear about them sooner, and measure code coverage. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type NUnit in Filter step types if you like, then click the NUnit tile (group Tests ). Type a Step name , for example Unit tests. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the NUnit step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Test assemblies. One test assembly or project per line, relative to the working directory. Empty (required). Tests/bin/Release/Game.Tests.dll. Console runner. Path of the NUnit 3 console runner. Empty takes nunit3-console from the agent's PATH. Empty. C:\\tools\\nunit\\nunit3-console.exe. NUnit console version. The agent must report this version: 3 matches any 3.x, 3.17 any 3.17.x. Empty adds none. With a console path there is no requirement. Empty. 3.17. Do not run tests from. Assemblies to leave out, one per line. * matches any characters. A match in Test assemblies is dropped before the run. Empty. legacy/*.dll. Path to application configuration file. Passed as --configfile=PATH , relative to the working directory. Empty. Tests/app.config. Include categories. One category per line. Only tests in one of them run. Both category lists become one --where filter, so do not add another --where to the arguments. Empty. Fast. Exclude categories. One category per line. Tests in any of them do not run. A name with a quote or a backslash is refused. Empty. Slow. Run recently failed tests first. Runs the tests that failed in the last build of this build configuration, with this option on, in a pass of their own, then the whole suite. Results on the build come from the whole suite, so no test counts twice. The first build with the option on runs the suite once. Off. On. Run new tests first. Lists the tests with nunit3-console --explore , runs those that the last build with this option did not run in a pass of their own, then the whole suite. A step in a container runs once. Off. Off. Code coverage. None , Coverlet (runs coverlet.console around the runner and reads its Cobertura report) or dotCover (runs the dotCover command line tool and reads its XML report). Neither tool is installed by the step. The report is archived and the totals show on the build page. None. Coverlet. Include assemblies. Assembly name patterns, one per line, such as MyApp.*. Empty lets the tool pick. Empty. Game.*. Exclude assemblies. Patterns to leave out, such as *.Tests. Empty. *.Tests. Coverage tool path. Path of coverlet or dotCover. Empty uses the one on the agent's PATH. Empty. C:\\tools\\coverlet.exe. Arguments. One argument per line, such as --where=cat==Fast. The step asks for the NUnit 3 result file itself and records the results, so Test reports is for extra files only. Empty. --timeout=60000. Working directory, Environment variables, Test reports, Run step within container. The same in every runner. See Fields every runner has. A worked example. Run the fast unit tests of the game code and put the failed ones first. Step name Unit tests , Test assemblies Tests/bin/Release/Game.Tests.dll , Exclude categories Slow , tick Run recently failed tests first. What you see when it runs. The console shows $ nunit3-console …. With failed-first on, a line says Running the 2 test(s) that failed in the last build first. and then the full run. The build gets a Tests tab with passed, failed and skipped tests. A failed test makes the step unstable. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The nunit3-console command was not found on this agent. Install the NUnit 3 console runner or set Console "},
{"title":"Parallel blocks and overrides","href":"build-steps/parallel-blocks","headings":["Parallel blocks","This machine or Separate machines","Matrix mode","Per-step \"Runs on\"","Overrides","Related pages"],"text":"Run steps side by side and choose where each one runs. Cook three platforms at once, or send the shader compile to a bigger machine. Parallel blocks. A Parallel block is one entry in the step list that holds two or more sides. Each side is a list of steps. The sides run at the same time; the steps after the block wait until all sides are done. Open Settings › Build Steps. Click Add Parallel block. A block named Parallel with two empty sides is added to the list. Under How the sides run , choose Separate machines or This machine (below). Under If one side fails , choose Let the others finish or Stop the others. Give each side a name, such as PS5 and Xbox , and add its steps with Add step in the side's header. Add side adds another side. Set Run on and Files sent back for each side, and Passes on and Fallback after for the block. A new Parallel block in the step list, opened. The tags show its mode and how many sides it has. You can also drag Parallel from the Flow group of the visual editor 's palette. This machine or Separate machines. Mode. What happens. This machine. The sides run at the same time on the build's agent, in the same workspace. Each side has its own block in the build console. The editor warns you when the sides write to the same files. Separate machines. Each side runs as a side build on its own agent, fetching the same revision as the main build. While the sides run, the main build frees its agent slot and keeps its workspace. When the sides finish, it comes back to the same agent, copies in the files the sides sent back, and goes on. For Separate machines you also set: Run on for each side, for example the pool Consoles for the PS5 side. Files sent back for each side, as rules such as Saved/StagedBuilds/**. If one side fails : let the others finish (the default) or stop the others. Either way, the build fails after the block. Fallback : if the first agent is not free again within 10 minutes (you can change this, or choose never), or it went offline, the build goes on on any matching agent. It fetches the same revision there and copies in the files it needs. Side builds show under their main build in the build list and the queue, never as builds of their own. Links such as Last successful build skip them. Matrix mode. Instead of writing each side by hand, you can write the steps once and give values such as platform and configuration. Every combination then runs as its own side. Open the block and click Change to Matrix. See Matrix mode for Parallel blocks. Per-step \"Runs on\". Any step can run on another machine than the rest of the build. Open the step and find Run on : Choice. Meaning. Same machine as before. The default. No new fetch. Any matching machine. Any agent that meets the build configuration's agent requirements. A pool or label. For example pool Consoles or label vs2022. Another expression… lets you type a label expression. A specific agent. One agent by name. If it is offline, the step waits for it and shows why, unless you set a fallback. Fallback after … minutes , or Never fall back : how long to wait for the chosen machine. Passes on : files that later steps need if they run on another machine, such as Intermediate/**. They are copied only when a later step really lands elsewhere. Files sent back : when the step runs on another machine, the files it creates or changes there come back to the build's own workspace. Rules such as Saved/Logs/** narrow them; choose Nothing to bring nothing back. Overrides. A step can override environment values at run time, for one step only: Environment variables on the step: one NAME=value per line, added for this step's process only. Environment filters , under Advanced : keep only the variables you name. A parameter that a filter removes stays removed. Outputs : a step can declare values it sets, such as version (text, number, true/false or file). The script sets them with a ##buildspawn[setParameter …] message, and later steps read them as %version%. A step t"},
{"title":"PowerShell","href":"build-steps/powershell","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Runs a PowerShell script you type, or a.ps1 file, with Windows PowerShell or pwsh (PowerShell 7). When to use it. Use it for build scripts you already wrote in PowerShell: packaging, signing, copying to a share, calling the console SDK tools. It works on Windows agents, and on Linux and macOS agents that have pwsh. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type PowerShell in Filter step types if you like, then click the PowerShell tile (group Command line and scripts ). Type a Step name , for example Pack build. Choose Run : type the script, or give a script file. Set the other fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the PowerShell step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Run. Script in this step or File. Script in this step. File. Script. The script text in the code editor. %name% is replaced with that build parameter when the step runs. Empty. Write-Host \"Packing $env:CONFIG\". Script file. A.ps1 file, relative to the working directory. The agent's execution policy applies to it. Empty. Build/package.ps1. Script execution mode. Execute.ps1 from external file runs -File. Put script into PowerShell stdin with -Command - writes the script, or the file, to PowerShell's standard input. Script arguments are hidden and not passed in that mode. Execute.ps1 from external file. Put script into PowerShell stdin. Arguments. One argument per line, passed to the script. Each line stays one argument, whatever it holds. Other processes on the agent can see arguments, so pass a secret through the environment. Empty. -Config on one line, Shipping on the next. PowerShell version. The agent must report this version. 5.1 is Windows PowerShell, 7 is pwsh , unless Edition is set. Any adds no requirement. A step in a container needs none. Any. 7. Platform. Windows agents only. x86 starts the 32-bit Windows PowerShell, x64 the 64-bit one. Auto starts the edition chosen below. On a Unix agent x86 or x64 stops the step. Auto. x64. Edition. Default for the agent (Windows PowerShell on Windows, pwsh on Unix agents), PowerShell 7 (pwsh) or Windows PowerShell. Default for the agent. PowerShell 7 (pwsh). Bypass the execution policy. Off: the agent's policy is used as it is, and a policy that blocks scripts fails the step. On: runs with -ExecutionPolicy Bypass on Windows agents. No effect on Unix agents. Off. On. Add -NoProfile argument. On: PowerShell does not load the profile of the account the agent runs as. Untick to load it. On. On. Additional command line parameters. Split on spaces and line breaks into separate PowerShell parameters, put before the script. Double quotes group a parameter that holds spaces. Script arguments go after the script; these go to PowerShell. Empty. -NonInteractive. Format stderr output as. Show stderr lines in orange or red in the log. Plain. Error. Fail the step when anything is written to stderr. Off: the exit code alone decides. Off. On. Working directory, Environment variables, Test reports, Run step within container. The same in every runner. See Fields every runner has. A worked example. A Windows agent packs a Unity build into a zip and passes the configuration as a script argument. Step name Pack build , Run = File , Script file = Build/pack.ps1 , Arguments : -Config %CONFIG% -Out Builds\\Win64. The script Build/pack.ps1 : param([string]$Config, [string]$Out) Compress-Archive -Path \"$Out\\*\" -DestinationPath \"Skyforge-$Config.zip\" -Force Write-Host \"Packed Skyforge-$Config.zip\". What you see when it runs. The console shows the PowerShell command line after a $ , then the output. A non-zero exit code fails the step. With Format stderr output as set, error text shows in colour. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The PowerShell command was not found on this agent. Install PowerShell there or put it o"},
{"title":"Python","href":"build-steps/python","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Runs a Python script you type, a.py file or a module ( python -m ), with the interpreter on the agent. When to use it. Use it for build helpers and tests written in Python, such as asset checks or a pytest run. It can start a virtual environment for you. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type Python in Filter step types if you like, then click the Python tile (group Command line and scripts ). Type a Step name , for example Check assets. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the Python step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Run. Script in this step , File or Module. Script in this step. Module. Script. Python in a code editor. %name% is replaced with that build parameter. Empty. print(\"ok\"). Script file. Relative to the working directory. Empty. tools/check_assets.py. Module name. Run as python -m with this dotted name. The arguments follow the module name. Empty. pytest. Arguments. One argument per line. Each line stays one argument. Pass a secret through the environment. Empty. --junitxml=reports/py.xml. Interpreter. Path of the interpreter. Empty takes python3 on Unix agents and python on Windows agents from the PATH. Empty. C:\\Python312\\python.exe. Python version. The agent must report this version: 3 matches any 3.x, 3.12 matches any 3.12.x. Empty adds no requirement. Not used with an interpreter path, pipenv or poetry. Empty. 3.12. Environment tool. None runs the interpreter as it is. venv makes the folder.buildspawn-venv in the working directory if missing and runs in it. pipenv and poetry run the command through pipenv run or poetry run. None of them installs packages. In a container it must stay None. None. venv. Working directory, Environment variables, Test reports, Run step within container. The same in every runner. See Fields every runner has. A worked example. Run the asset checker as a module and record its test report. Step name Check assets , Run = Module , Module name pytest , Arguments tools/tests and --junitxml=reports/assets.xml on separate lines, Test reports reports/assets.xml. What you see when it runs. The console shows $ python -m pytest tools/tests --junitxml=reports/assets.xml and the pytest output. The build gets the test results, and a failed test makes the step unstable. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The Python step has no script / file / module. Fill in the field for the mode you chose. The Python module must be a dotted name. Use a name like pytest or http.server. An environment tool cannot be used in a container. Set Environment tool to None. The step waits for an agent. No agent reports the Python version you asked for. Lower the version or install Python on an agent. Related pages. The script editor. Run Python tests and see the results. Command Line. Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"Rake","href":"build-steps/rake","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Runs rake with tasks, a Rakefile and arguments. When to use it. Use it for Ruby tooling in your pipeline. It can record test reports from RSpec, Cucumber and Test::Unit. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type Rake in Filter step types if you like, then click the Rake tile (group Build tools ). Type a Step name , for example Run specs. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the Rake step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Tasks. One task per line, such as spec or db:migrate. Empty runs the default task. Empty. spec. Rakefile from. Path to a Rakefile or Content written here. Path to a Rakefile. Path to a Rakefile. Rakefile. Passed as -f. Empty lets rake look for a Rakefile in the working directory. Empty. tools/Rakefile. Rakefile content. The Rakefile itself. It is written to a temporary file in the working directory, passed as -f and removed afterwards. Empty. A Rakefile. Bundler: run bundle exec rake. Runs bundle exec rake , so the gems of the Gemfile apply. The agent must have bundler. Off. On. Debug: track invoke and execute stages. Adds --trace : logs when each task is invoked and executed and prints the full backtrace of a failure. Off. Off. RSpec test report. Asks RSpec for JUnit XML through SPEC_OPTS , written to buildspawn-reports/rspec.xml and recorded as test results. The project needs the rspec_junit_formatter gem. Off. On. Cucumber test report. Asks Cucumber for JUnit XML through CUCUMBER_OPTS , written under buildspawn-reports. Cucumber has this formatter built in. Off. Off. Test::Unit test report. Adds the ci:setup:testunit task before the tasks and sets CI_REPORTS. The project needs the ci_reporter_test_unit gem. Off. Off. Test-Spec test report, Shoulda test report. Both run on Test::Unit and use the same setup task and gem. Tick any of the three and the task is added once. Off. Off. Additional interpreter parameters. Options for the ruby interpreter, one per line, such as -W0 or -Ilib. Rake then starts as ruby , the options, -S rake. Empty. -Ilib. Arguments. One argument per line, placed before the tasks, such as --trace. Empty. --trace. Working directory, Environment variables, Test reports, Run step within container. The same in every runner. See Fields every runner has. A worked example. Run the specs of the patch-notes generator. Step name Run specs , Tasks spec , tick Bundler and RSpec test report. What you see when it runs. The console shows $ bundle exec rake spec. The RSpec results are recorded as test results on the build. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The rake command was not found on this agent. Install Ruby with rake there or put rake on the PATH. The Rake step is set to a Rakefile written in the step, and it is empty. Type the Rakefile or choose a path. No test results from RSpec. Add the rspec_junit_formatter gem to the Gemfile. Related pages. Command Line. Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"Duplicates (ReSharper)","href":"build-steps/resharper-duplicates","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Runs the dupfinder command line tool on a solution and lists the duplicate blocks on the build, as Duplicates finder does. The tool is not part of Buildspawn. When to use it. Use it for C# solutions when you have the ReSharper command line tools on the agent. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type Duplicates (ReSharper) in Filter step types if you like, then click the Duplicates (ReSharper) tile (group Code quality and coverage ). Type a Step name , for example Find duplicates. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the Duplicates (ReSharper) step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Solution or project. The solution (.sln ) or project file to search, relative to the working directory. Empty (required). Game.sln. Lowest duplicate cost. Passed as --discard-cost. Duplicates cheaper than this are not reported. Empty uses the tool default. Empty. 70. Exclude. One file glob per line, joined with semicolons and passed as --exclude. Empty. **/Generated/**. Arguments. One argument per line, placed before the solution. Empty. --show-text. Limit. Optional. The step is worse when it finds more duplicate blocks than this number. Empty means no limit. Empty. 20. When over the limit. The build turns unstable or The step fails. The build turns unstable. The build turns unstable. Working directory, Environment variables, Test reports, Run step within container. The same in every runner. See Fields every runner has. A worked example. Search the game solution, leaving out generated code. Step name Find duplicates , Solution or project Game.sln , Exclude **/Generated/**. What you see when it runs. The console shows the dupfinder command. The duplicate blocks are listed on the Code Inspections page of the build. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The dupfinder command was not found on this agent. Install the dupfinder command line tool or put dupfinder on the PATH. The … step has no solution to search. Fill in Solution or project. Related pages. Duplicates finder. Inspections (ReSharper). Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"Inspections (ReSharper)","href":"build-steps/resharper-inspections","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Runs the jb inspectcode command line tool on a solution and lists the issues on the build, as Code Inspections does. The tool is not part of Buildspawn. When to use it. Use it for C# solutions, including Unity and Unreal Engine C# code, when you have the ReSharper command line tools on the agent. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type Inspections (ReSharper) in Filter step types if you like, then click the Inspections (ReSharper) tile (group Code quality and coverage ). Type a Step name , for example Inspect code. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the Inspections (ReSharper) step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Solution. The solution (.sln ) or project file to inspect, relative to the working directory. Empty (required). Game.sln. Lowest severity. Passed as --severity. Issues below it are not reported. Choices: Tool default, Information, Hint, Suggestion, Warning, Error. Tool default. Warning. InspectCode platform. Passed as --x86 or --x64. Automatic passes nothing. Automatic. x64. Do not build. Passed as --no-build. Use it when an earlier step already built the solution. Off. On. Arguments. One argument per line, for example --properties:Configuration=Release. Empty. --properties:Configuration=Release. Limit. Optional. The step is worse when it finds more issues than this number. Empty means no limit. Empty. 0. When over the limit. The build turns unstable or The step fails. The build turns unstable. The step fails. Working directory, Environment variables, Test reports, Run step within container. The same in every runner. See Fields every runner has. A worked example. Inspect the game solution after the build. Step name Inspect code , Solution Game.sln , Lowest severity Warning , tick Do not build. What you see when it runs. The console shows $ jb inspectcode Game.sln --severity=WARNING --no-build …. The issues are listed on the Code Inspections page of the build. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The jb command was not found on this agent. Install the jb command with inspectcode or put jb on the PATH. The … step has no solution to inspect. Fill in Solution. The solution must be a file name. A name that starts with a dash or holds a line break is refused. Related pages. Code Inspections. Duplicates (ReSharper). Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"Resume and retry","href":"build-steps/resume-and-retry","headings":["Resume a build","Retry a step","When a step runs","Related pages"],"text":"Pick up a failed build where it stopped, or try a step again. A two-hour cook should not run again because the packaging step failed. Resume a build. Resume starts a new build that skips the steps that already passed. It runs on the same agent, in the same workspace, and does not fetch the sources again: the revision and the parameters are copied from the failed build. A failed build with Resume from step 2. The card on the right checks that the build can resume. Open the failed build. Look at the Resume from here card. It checks that: the agent is online; the workspace still exists; no other build has used the workspace since. Click Resume from step N in the header, on the failed step, or Resume from here in the card. By default it starts at the failed step; you can start at any earlier step too. The new build appears in the queue and runs only the remaining steps. On the resumed build, the steps that were skipped show as Done earlier , with a link to the build that ran them. If a check fails, for example because another build used the workspace, Resume is refused. Use Re-run instead: it builds the same sources from the start. See Re-run and Re-run with changes. Resume needs the Build permission, the same as Re-run. It works for build configurations with build steps. A build with a Parallel block reuses the sides that passed and resumes the side that failed on its own machine. Retry a step. Some failures are flaky: a network share drops, a device kit times out, a tool crashes. Retry on failure runs a step again before the build gives up. Open the step from Settings › Build Steps. Scroll to Execution. Set Retry on failure to the number of extra tries, from 0 to 10. Set Wait between attempts in seconds. The agent keeps the workspace while it waits. Optional: list exit codes under Only for exit codes. Then the step retries only for those codes. Click Save. Retry settings in the step's Execution section. List the codes of a crash, such as -1 or 3221225477 , so that a crash retries while a real compile error (exit code 1) fails at once. Codes are compared as signed 32-bit numbers, so 3221225477 and -1073741819 are the same. When a step runs. The same Execution section sets when a step runs at all, with Execute step : If all previous steps finished successfully (the default); Only if build status is successful ; Even if some of the previous steps failed ; Always, even if build stop command was issued , for clean-up steps; Only if build status is failed , for steps that collect crash dumps or send alerts. Add Conditions to run a step only when a parameter equals, contains or matches a value, for example only when CONFIG equals Shipping. Related pages. The steps list and the step editor. Step statuses and BUILD_STEP_STATUS. Retry a flaky step. Re-run from the failed step. Re-run and Re-run with changes."},
{"title":"Run with timeout","href":"build-steps/run-with-timeout","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Wraps one other step with a time limit. When the time is up, the actions you choose run. When to use it. Use it for a step that can hang, such as a device-kit test or a store upload, so it cannot hold an agent for hours. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type Run with timeout in Filter step types if you like, then click the Run with timeout tile (group Other ). Type a Step name , for example Devkit tests. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the Run with timeout step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Time-out strategy. When the step counts as too slow. Absolute : after a number of minutes. Elastic : a percentage of the mean duration of the last non-failing builds. Likely stuck : many times longer than previous builds. No Activity : no log output for a number of seconds. Deadline : a time of day. Absolute. No Activity. Timeout minutes (Absolute). Minutes before the step is stopped. Empty. 45. Timeout seconds (No Activity). Terminate when there has been no log output for this many seconds. Empty. 300. Deadline time, Deadline tolerance in minutes (Deadline). The time of day by which the step must be done, and how many minutes more it may take. Empty. 06:00 , 5. Timeout as a percentage of recent non-failing builds, Number of Builds, Timeout minutes, Timeout minutes as the shortest timeout (Elastic). Elastic limit: the percentage of the mean duration, how many builds to look at, a default when there is no history, and the shortest allowed limit. Empty. 150 , 3 , 60 , 10. Build Step. The step to run under the limit. Pick any step type. None. Command Line. Time-out actions. What to do on a time-out: Abort the build , Fail the build , Abort and restart the build (with Maximum restarts ), Writing the build description (with a Description ) or Perform BuildStep (with Ignore failure of the build step and Create a launcher for this build step ). Abort the build. Fail the build. A worked example. Stop the PS5 device-kit test if it prints nothing for five minutes. Step name Devkit tests , Time-out strategy No Activity with 300 seconds, Build Step a Command Line step that runs the tests, Time-out actions Fail the build. What you see when it runs. The wrapped step runs as usual. When the limit is reached, the log says the step timed out and your actions run. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The step never times out. Check that the strategy has its number filled in, and that the build step is set. The build is aborted but you wanted only a note. Use Writing the build description instead of Abort the build. Related pages. The steps list and the step editor. Resume and retry. Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"Runners","href":"build-steps/runners","headings":["Add a step","What every step has","All runners","Command line and scripts","Build tools","Containers","Tests","Packages","Upload and deploy","Code quality and coverage","Other","Related pages"],"text":"A runner is the kind of work a step does, such as a command line or a Unity build. You pick one each time you add a build step. Each runner has its own page. Resolve artifact dependencies is a step for Pipeline scripts, where you write it as resolveArtifactDependencies() inside node. It copies the artifact dependencies of the build configuration into the workspace. The step picker of an ordinary build configuration does not offer it, because a build configuration resolves its artifact dependencies by itself before the first step. See Dependencies. Add a step. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. The runner picker opens, grouped by kind: Command line and scripts , Build tools , Containers , Tests , Packages , Upload and deploy , Code quality and coverage , Source control and Other. Type in Filter step types to find a runner fast, then click its tile. Give the step a Step name , such as Compile. The Step ID is made from the name and cannot change later. Fill in the runner's fields and click Save. The runner picker. Each tile says what the runner does. Each tile says what the runner does and which tool the agent needs. Editing a step. Change beside the runner type picks another runner. What every step has. Below the runner's own fields, every step has the same extra settings: Working directory , Environment variables , Test reports and Container settings (most runners). See Fields every runner has. Outputs , Run on and Files sent back : see Parallel blocks and overrides. Execution : when the step runs, conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor and Resume and retry. Every step also gets a variable BUILD_STEP_STATUS_<ID> that later steps can read: SUCCESS , FAILURE , UNSTABLE , SKIPPED , DISABLED or ABORTED. See Step statuses. All runners. Command line and scripts. Runner. What it does. Command Line. A script or one program. Shell on Linux and macOS, batch on Windows. PowerShell. A script or.ps1 file, with Windows PowerShell or pwsh. Python. A script, file or module with the Python interpreter. Build tools. Runner. What it does.NET. The dotnet command: build, test, publish, restore, run. MSBuild. Builds a Visual Studio project or solution. Maven. Maven goals, with test reports and optional coverage. Gradle. Gradle tasks, using the project wrapper when there is one. Ant. Ant targets with a build file and properties. Node.js. A script or file with node , or an npm or yarn command. Kotlin Script. A Kotlin script with kotlinc -script. C# Script. A C# script with dotnet-script or csi. Rake. Ruby rake tasks, with RSpec and Cucumber reports. Simple Build Tool. Scala sbt commands. Xcode Project. Builds and tests with xcodebuild on a macOS agent. Unity. Builds and tests a Unity project with the Editor in batch mode. Unreal Engine. BuildCookRun, BuildGraph, automation tests and more. Containers. Runner. What it does. Docker. Build an image, push it, or run any docker command. Docker Compose. Start, stop and build compose services. Tests. Runner. What it does. NUnit. The NUnit 3 console runner, with failed-first and coverage. VSTest. Tests with vstest.console.exe. MSTest. Tests with the MSTest command line tool. Packages. Runner. What it does. NuGet Installer. Restore or install NuGet packages. NuGet Pack. Build a.nupkg. NuGet Publish. Push packages to a feed with a secret key. Upload and deploy. Runner. What it does. SSH Exec. Run commands on a remote server. SSH Upload. Copy files over SFTP. FTP Upload. Copy files to an FTP or FTPS server. SMB Upload. Copy files to a Windows or Samba share. Container Deployer. Deploy a WAR or EAR to Tomcat, JBoss or GlassFish. Steam upload and the other Steam steps. Upload a build to Steam. Code quality and coverage. Runner. What it does. Code Inspections. Reads SARIF, Checkstyle, PMD and SpotBugs reports, or runs a tool. Duplicates finder. PMD copy-paste detector. FxCop. FxCopCmd on.NET assemblies (Windows only). Inspections (ReS"},
{"title":"Simple Build Tool","href":"build-steps/sbt","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Runs sbt with commands and a version. Test reports under target/test-reports are recorded for you. When to use it. Use it for Scala projects, such as a game-analytics job. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type Simple Build Tool in Filter step types if you like, then click the Simple Build Tool tile (group Build tools ). Type a Step name , for example Test analytics. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the Simple Build Tool step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Commands. One sbt command per line, such as clean and test. A command with spaces, like testOnly MySpec , stays one argument. Empty starts the interactive shell, which has no input here, so give at least one command. Empty. clean then test. sbt version. Passed as -sbt-version. Empty uses the version the project sets. Empty. 1.10.0. sbt home. The folder of an sbt installation on the agent. The step runs bin/sbt in it. Empty uses sbt from the PATH. Empty. C:\\tools\\sbt. JDK home. The folder of the JDK sbt runs on, set as JAVA_HOME for this step. Empty. C:\\jdk-21. JVM parameters. Options for the JVM, one per line, such as -Xmx2G. Each is passed to sbt as -J plus the option. Empty. -Xmx2G. Arguments. Launcher arguments, one per line, placed before the commands. Empty. -J-Xmx2G. Record test reports. Records the JUnit XML under target/test-reports of the working directory and its sub-projects, also when the build fails. On. On. Working directory, Environment variables, Test reports, Run step within container. The same in every runner. See Fields every runner has. A worked example. Run the tests of the analytics job. Step name Test analytics , Commands clean and test on two lines. What you see when it runs. The console shows the sbt command and its output. Test results are listed on the build. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The sbt command was not found on this agent. Install sbt or set sbt home. The step hangs. sbt waits for input when no command is given. Always give at least one command. Related pages. Gradle. Maven. Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"The script editor","href":"build-steps/script-editor","headings":["Edit a script","Use %name% references","Safeguards","Related pages"],"text":"Write step scripts with highlighting and parameter hints. The editor knows the build's parameters and warns you about mistakes before the build runs. The script editor is used in the Command Line , PowerShell , Python , Node.js , Kotlin Script and C# Script steps. The classic Execute shell and Execute Windows batch command steps have a plain code box and also fill in %name% build parameters. A Command Line script. Parameter references are highlighted. Edit a script. Open the step from Settings › Build Steps with Edit , or add a new one. For Command Line, set Run to Custom script. Type the script in the editor. It has line numbers and colours for the language, in the light and the dark theme. Click Save. The script is saved exactly as you typed it, with its indentation and last line break. The bar above the script has: the language : for Command Line, choose Shell , Batch or Auto (decided by the agent the step runs on); Find ( Ctrl + F ) to search the script; Full screen for long scripts. The line below the script shows hints, for example an unknown parameter, an unclosed quote or a missing bracket, and the cursor position. When all is well it says No problems found. Hints are advice only. They never stop you from saving. Use echo ::group::Name and echo ::endgroup:: to make your own collapsible block in the build console, and ::warning:: or ::error:: to flag a line. See The build console. Use %name% references. Write %NAME% in a script to use a build parameter. When the step runs, Buildspawn puts the value in its place. For example, with the parameter CONFIG set to Shipping : Build/ci.ps1 -Stage compile -Config %CONFIG%. runs as Build/ci.ps1 -Stage compile -Config Shipping. Type % in the editor. A list of the build's parameters opens. Keep typing to narrow it, then press Enter to insert one. The list holds the build configuration's own parameters, inherited ones and the built-in ones, such as %build.number% , %build.vcs.number% and %build.checkoutDir%. See Parameters and Version control parameters. Safeguards. Secrets are never written into a script. Password parameters show by name only in the list. Read them from the environment variable instead, for example $env:STEAM_UPLOAD_KEY in PowerShell. A value changed in the Run dialog by someone without the Configure permission is refused. Branch names and other values from version control are only put in when they hold safe characters. Related pages. Command Line. PowerShell. Fields every runner has. Secrets. The build console."},
{"title":"Steps shared from a project or template","href":"build-steps/shared-steps","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every option","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"A project or a template can hold build steps that every build configuration under it gets. You can override or switch off a shared step in one build configuration without touching the others. When to use it. Use shared steps when many build configurations do the same work, for example the same Unity build for Windows, PS5 and Xbox. Write the steps once in the project (the settings home ) or in a template. See Projects , Settings homes and Templates. How to set it up. Open the project (or the template) and click Settings › Build Steps. Add the steps with Add build step and click Save. Open a build configuration under it and click Settings › Build Steps. The shared steps are in the list, tagged From project Game , From template … or From Root project. To change a shared step for this build configuration only, click Override , change the fields and click Save. The row now says Overridden. To switch it off here, click Disable in this build configuration. To go back, click Reset to inherited. The step is then as the project or template defines it. A build configuration whose list shows steps from its project. Every option. Action. What it does. Default. Example. Override. Copies the shared step into this build configuration so you can change it. Other build configurations keep the shared version. Not overridden. Change the Unity build method for one platform. Edit override. Opens the overridden step again. Disable in this build configuration. Skips the shared step here. It stays enabled everywhere else. Enabled. No Steam upload in the test build configuration. Reset to inherited. Throws away the override and gives the step back as the project defines it. Disabled above. The step was switched off higher up. A worked example. The project HT1 Shared holds the steps Compile and Package. HT1 Windows Build uses both. HT1 PS5 Build overrides Package to add the console tool and keeps Compile as it is. When you change Compile in the project, both build configurations get the change. Package in the PS5 build stays as overridden until you click Reset to inherited. What you see when it runs. A shared step runs like any other step, in the order of the list. The console block carries the step name, and the build page shows it in the Build steps card with the others. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. A shared step does not show in a build configuration. Check that the build configuration is inside the project, or uses the template. You changed the project and one build configuration did not change. That build configuration overrides the step. Click Reset to inherited there. A shared step has no edit option. It belongs to the project. Edit it in the project, or use Override. Related pages. What a project passes down. Templates. Settings homes. Share settings with a project. The steps list and the step editor."},
{"title":"Execute shell","href":"build-steps/shell-script","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Runs a shell script with sh on the build's agent. It is the classic step, kept as it was. When to use it. Use it on Linux and macOS agents for shell scripts. For new work, Command Line does the same and adds a code editor and container settings. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type Execute shell in Filter step types if you like, then click the Execute shell tile (group Other ). Type a Step name , for example Build server. Type the script into Command. Open More options for the unstable exit code. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Command. The shell script. It runs with the workspace as the current directory. Without a first line such as #!/bin/bash the system-wide shell is used with -ex : every command is printed, and the first command that fails stops the step. A #! line changes the shell or the options, and lets you write in another language. %name% build parameters are put into the text; read secrets from the environment. Empty./build.sh Shipping. Exit code to set build unstable (More options). A shell exit code, from 1 to 255, that makes the build unstable instead of failed. The next steps still run. 0 is ignored. Empty. 3. Environment filters (More options). Rules that remove variables from what this step sees. None. A worked example. A Linux agent builds the dedicated server and marks warnings (exit code 3) as unstable. Step name Build server , Command : #!/bin/bash set -e./Scripts/build-server.sh Shipping. Set Exit code to set build unstable to 3. What you see when it runs. The console block is titled [Step 1/2] Build server (Execute shell). With the default shell options each command is printed with a + in front before it runs. The exit code decides the result. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The script fails on the first small error. That is -e at work. Start the script with #!/bin/bash and no -e if you want to carry on. The shell is not found on Windows. The step looks for sh. Put it on the PATH , or use Command Line. A secret does not show in the script. That is by design. Read the parameter from the environment: $STEAM_KEY. Related pages. Command Line. Execute Windows batch command. The script editor. Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"SMB Upload","href":"build-steps/smb-upload","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Copies files to a Windows or Samba share, with include patterns. It sends to a Windows file share. In the step picker this is called Send files to a windows share. When to use it. Use it to publish builds to the studio file server, so testers can fetch them from a share. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type SMB Upload in Filter step types if you like, then click the SMB Upload tile (group Upload and deploy ). Type a Step name , for example Publish to share. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the SMB Upload step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Name (SMB Server). The share to use, from the servers defined once under Settings › System. First server. build-share. Verbose output in console, Retry, Label, Fail the build if an error occurs. As in SSH Upload. Source files. Files to upload: comma-separated Ant includes, base folder is the workspace. Empty. Builds/Patch/**. Remove prefix. The first part of the path that is not created on the server. Empty. Builds/Patch. Remote directory. Optional destination folder, below the one in the server settings. Created if missing. Empty. /patches/1.4. Exclude files (More options). Comma-separated Ant excludes. Empty. **/*.pdb. Pattern separator (More options). Regular expression that splits the patterns. [, ]+. ,. No default excludes, Make empty dirs, Flatten files, Remote directory is a date format (More options). As in SSH Upload. Off. A worked example. Publish the Windows build to the share. Step name Publish to share , Name build-share , Source files Builds/Windows/** , Remove prefix Builds/Windows , Remote directory Skyforge/%build.number%. What you see when it runs. The console says it is connecting, then Transferred N file(s) , then disconnecting. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Access denied. The account in the server settings needs write rights on the share. The share list is empty. An administrator must add the share under Settings › System. Related pages. FTP Upload. SSH Upload. Global connections. Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"SSH Exec","href":"build-steps/ssh-exec","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Runs commands on a remote server over SSH. The servers and their keys are defined once, under System settings. When to use it. Use it to restart a game server, run a deploy script on a Linux box, or unpack an upload. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type SSH Exec in Filter step types if you like, then click the SSH Exec tile (group Upload and deploy ). Type a Step name , for example Restart server. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the SSH Exec step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Name (SSH Server). The server to use, chosen from the servers defined once under Settings › System. A server marked [No Exec] cannot run commands. First server. build-linux. Verbose output in console. More detail in the log. Off. On. Credentials. Optional credentials to use instead of the server's own. Server's own. Retry (Retries, Delay). Try again when a transfer fails: how many times and the delay in milliseconds. Off. 3 retries, 10000 ms. Label. Only publish to this server when the label matches. For parameterised publishing. None. Publish to other SSH servers if an error occurs. Keep going with the other servers when one fails. Off. On. Fail the build if an error occurs. Fail the step when a transfer or command fails. On. On. Always SSH from master. Make the transfer from the server instead of from the agent. Off. Off. Exec command. The command to run on the remote server. Environment variables of the build can be used. Empty (required). sudo systemctl restart gameserver. Exec timeout (ms) (More options). Time limit for the command, in milliseconds. 0 turns it off. Empty. 120000. Exec in pty (More options). Runs the command in a pseudo terminal. This lets sudo work when the server's requiretty setting is on. Off. On. Exec using Agent Forwarding (More options). Forward the SSH agent to the command. Off. Off. Use SFTP for Exec (More options). Run the command through SFTP instead of the normal exec channel. Off. Off. A worked example. Restart the test server after an upload. Step name Restart server , Name build-linux , Exec command sudo systemctl restart gameserver , tick Exec in pty. What you see when it runs. The console says it is connecting to the configuration, runs the command, prints its output and says it is disconnecting. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The server list is empty. An administrator must add a server under Settings › System. See Global connections. Failed to find configuration named …. The server was removed or renamed. Pick another. sudo says it needs a tty. Tick Exec in pty. Related pages. SSH Upload. Global connections. Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"SSH Upload","href":"build-steps/ssh-upload","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Copies files from the workspace to a remote server over SFTP, with include patterns, and can run a command afterwards. When to use it. Use it to put a Linux server build or a patch on a test server. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type SSH Upload in Filter step types if you like, then click the SSH Upload tile (group Upload and deploy ). Type a Step name , for example Upload server. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the SSH Upload step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Name (SSH Server). The server to use, chosen from the servers defined once under Settings › System. A server marked [No Exec] cannot run commands. First server. build-linux. Verbose output in console. More detail in the log. Off. On. Credentials. Optional credentials to use instead of the server's own. Server's own. Retry (Retries, Delay). Try again when a transfer fails: how many times and the delay in milliseconds. Off. 3 retries, 10000 ms. Label. Only publish to this server when the label matches. For parameterised publishing. None. Publish to other SSH servers if an error occurs. Keep going with the other servers when one fails. Off. On. Fail the build if an error occurs. Fail the step when a transfer or command fails. On. On. Always SSH from master. Make the transfer from the server instead of from the agent. Off. Off. Source files. Files to upload: a comma-separated list of Ant includes, such as **/*.jar. The base folder is the workspace. Empty. Builds/LinuxServer/**. Remove prefix. The first part of the file path that is not created on the server. All source paths must start with it. Empty. Builds/LinuxServer. Remote directory. Optional destination folder, below the one in the server settings. It is created if missing. Empty. /srv/skyforge/server. Exec command. A command to run on the server after the files are copied. A transfer set needs a source pattern, an exec command, or both. Empty./deploy.sh. Exclude files (More options). Comma-separated Ant excludes. Empty. **/*.log,**/*.tmp. Pattern separator (More options). Regular expression that splits the include and exclude patterns. Default [, ]+. Use a single comma to keep spaces in names. [, ]+. ,. No default excludes (More options). Do not use Ant's default excludes. Off. Off. Make empty dirs (More options). Create directories that match the pattern even when empty. Off. Off. Flatten files (More options). Create only files, no folders. File names must be unique or the step fails. Off. Off. Remote directory is a date format (More options). Treat the remote directory as a date pattern. Off. Off. Exec timeout (ms), Exec in pty (More options). As in SSH Exec. Add Transfer Set. Add more sets. They run in order, so put an exec before a copy by using two sets. One set. A worked example. Copy the Linux server build to the test box and unpack it. Step name Upload server , Name build-linux , Source files Builds/LinuxServer/** , Remove prefix Builds/LinuxServer , Remote directory /srv/skyforge/server , Exec command./post-deploy.sh. What you see when it runs. The console says it is connecting, then Transferred N file(s) , the output of the command, and that it is disconnecting. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Failed to remove prefix from file named …. All source paths must start with Remove prefix. Duplicate filename detected in flatten mode. Two files have the same name. Turn off Flatten files. Could not create or change to directory. Check the remote directory and the account's rights. Related pages. SSH Exec. FTP Upload. SMB Upload. Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"The steps list and the step editor","href":"build-steps/step-list","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","The list","Every field in the step editor","Execute step: when a step runs","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"The Build Steps page lists what a build does, in order. Here you add, edit, switch off, copy, delete and reorder steps. When to use it. Use this page whenever you change what a build configuration does: add a compile step, turn off a slow step for one test run, or move packaging after the tests. Steps run from top to bottom on the build's agent. Post-build actions, such as archiving files, run after all steps and have their own page. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. The list shows each step with its name, its runner, its Step ID and, when set, how many conditions and retries it has. Click Add build step. The Add build step page opens with a tile for each runner. Type in Filter step types to narrow them. See Runners. Click a tile. The editor opens. Type a Step name , such as Compile. Leave Step ID empty to have it made from the name. Fill in the runner's fields, then the Execution section (when the step runs, conditions, retry). Click Save. Back in the list, drag a row by its handle to reorder it, or use Move up and Move down in its More actions menu. The Build Steps list of a build configuration. The list. The line under the title says how many steps will run, for example 3 of 4 steps run, in this order; the others are disabled. Each row has: Enable and Disable : a switch. A disabled step stays in the list and shows Disabled. The build does not run it and the console says Disabled — not run. Edit : opens the step editor. More actions : Copy step , Move up , Move down and Delete step. Deleting asks first and cannot be undone. Tags such as Retry 2× and 1 condition when the step has them. A Parallel block is one row with a tag such as Block · 2 sides. See Parallel blocks and Matrix mode. A step that comes from a project or a template shows where it came from, for example From project Game. See Steps shared from a project or template. Every field in the step editor. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Runner type. The kind of work. Change beside it picks another runner. The tile you clicked. Command Line. Step name. The name shown in the list, on the build page and in the console. The runner name. Compile shaders. Step ID. A short name. Start with a letter, then use lowercase letters, digits and _. It cannot change once the step exists. Later steps read the result in BUILD_STEP_STATUS_<ID>. See Step statuses. Made from the name. compile_shaders. Enabled. Switch the step on or off. On. Off while you test. (the runner's own fields). See the page of each runner. Execute step. When the step runs. See below. If all previous steps finished successfully. Always, even if build stop command was issued. Conditions. The step runs only if all conditions are met. Each has a Parameter , an Operator and a Value. Parameters are build parameters and environment variables. None. CONFIG equals Shipping. Retry on failure. How many extra tries a failed step gets, 0 to 10. 0. 2. Wait between attempts. Seconds between tries. The agent keeps the workspace. 0. 30. Only for exit codes. Retry only for these codes. Empty retries for any failure. Empty. -1, 3221225477. Outputs. Values the step sets for later steps. See Setting parameters from a build. None. version (Text). Run on. Run the step on another machine. See Parallel blocks and overrides. Same machine as before. A pool or label: pool Consoles. Files sent back. When the step runs elsewhere, which changed files come back. All changed files. Saved/Logs/**. Execute step: when a step runs. By default a failed step stops the build and the steps after it are skipped. Execute step changes this for one step: Choice. Meaning. If all previous steps finished successfully. The default. The step runs only when nothing before it failed. Only if build status is successful. Runs only while the build result is still successful. A step that made the build unstable stops it. Even if some of the previous steps failed. Runs after a failure. Use it for a report step that m"},
{"title":"Step statuses and BUILD_STEP_STATUS","href":"build-steps/step-statuses","headings":["When to use it","The statuses","How to read a status in a script","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Every step ends with a status. Later steps can read it, so a clean-up step can act differently after a failure. When to use it. Use the status to print a clear summary at the end of a build, or to do something only after a certain step passed or failed. The statuses. Status. Meaning. SUCCESS. The step ran and finished well. FAILURE. The step ran and failed, or could not start. UNSTABLE. The step ran to its end but made the build unstable, for example because tests failed. SKIPPED. The step did not run because of its Execute step setting or a condition. DISABLED. The step is switched off. ABORTED. The build was stopped while the step ran. How to read a status in a script. A finished step sets an environment variable named BUILD_STEP_STATUS_ plus its Step ID in capitals. For the ID unit_tests it is BUILD_STEP_STATUS_UNIT_TESTS. The steps after it can read it. Open the first step and find its Step ID. The list shows it under the step name. In a later Command Line step on Windows, read the variable: if \"%BUILD_STEP_STATUS_UNIT_TESTS%\"==\"FAILURE\" echo Tests failed, collecting logs. In PowerShell: if ($env:BUILD_STEP_STATUS_UNIT_TESTS -eq \"FAILURE\") { Write-Host \"Tests failed\" }. Set the later step to Execute step › Even if some of the previous steps failed. Otherwise it is skipped before it can read anything. A worked example. The build configuration Engine / Unit Tests has a step with the ID nunit. A last step runs Even if some of the previous steps failed and prints: echo NUnit step: %BUILD_STEP_STATUS_NUNIT%. What you see when it runs. The console prints NUnit step: FAILURE (or SUCCESS , UNSTABLE and so on). The Build steps card on the build page shows the same result in words: succeeded, failed, unstable, skipped, disabled or stopped. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The variable is empty. The step you read has not finished yet, or its ID is spelled differently. IDs are lowercase in the editor and capitals in the variable name. The reading step never runs. It was skipped because an earlier step failed. Set it to run even if previous steps failed. Related pages. The steps list and the step editor. Resume and retry. The build page."},
{"title":"VSTest","href":"build-steps/vstest","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Runs unit tests with vstest.console.exe and can report the results. Installations are defined once, under Tools. When to use it. Use it for MSTest, NUnit or xUnit tests that you run through Visual Studio's test platform, on a Windows agent. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type VSTest in Filter step types if you like, then click the VSTest tile (group Tests ). Type a Step name , for example VSTest. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the VSTest step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. VsTest Version. The VSTest installation, from Settings › Tools. Default installation. VSTest 17. Test Files. The compiled test assemblies. Separate several with a new line or a space. Empty. Tests\\bin\\Release\\Game.Tests.dll. Settings File. Run tests with additional settings such as data collectors. Passed as /Settings: Empty. Local.runsettings. Test Name. Run tests whose names match. Separate several with commas. Passed as /Tests: Cannot be used with Test Case Filter. Empty. TestMethod1,TestMethod2. Test Case Filter. Run tests that match an expression of the form property=value , joined with ||. Passed as /TestCaseFilter: Empty. TestCategory=Nightly. Enable Code Coverage. Enables the code coverage data collector. Default settings are used unless a settings file says otherwise. Off. On. InIsolation. Runs the tests in an isolated process. The runner is less likely to stop on an error in the tests, but tests may run slower. Off. On. Use Vsix Extensions. Use or skip the installed VSIX extensions in the test run. Off. Off. Use VS 2017 or Greater. Adjusts the arguments for Visual Studio 2017 and newer. Off. On. Platform. Target platform: x86, x64 or ARM. Empty. x64.NET Framework Version. Target framework: Framework35, Framework40 or Framework45. Empty. Framework45. Specify a logger for test results. For example trx for a Visual Studio Test Results File. Empty. trx. Command Line Arguments. Extra arguments for vstest.console.exe. Empty. /Parallel. Fail build on test failure. Fail the build when a test fails. On. On. A worked example. Run the game's unit tests with a runsettings file. Step name VSTest , Test Files Tests\\bin\\Release\\Game.Tests.dll , Settings File CI.runsettings , Specify a logger trx. What you see when it runs. The console shows the vstest.console.exe command and its output, ending with the totals of passed and failed tests. A failed test fails the build when Fail build on test failure is on. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The version list is empty. Add an installation under Settings › Tools with the folder of vstest.console.exe. Test Name and Test Case Filter both set. Use only one of them. Related pages. NUnit. MSTest. Tools. Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"Xcode Project","href":"build-steps/xcode","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Runs xcodebuild on a project or workspace with a scheme or target, configuration, SDK and actions. It needs a macOS agent with Xcode. When to use it. Use it to build and test the iOS or macOS build of your game, for example the Xcode project Unity generates. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step. Type Xcode Project in Filter step types if you like, then click the Xcode Project tile (group Build tools ). Type a Step name , for example Build iOS. Fill in the fields described below. Optional: set Execute step , Conditions and Retry on failure. See The steps list and the step editor. Click Save. The fields of the Xcode Project step, as the step editor shows them. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Project or workspace. An.xcodeproj or.xcworkspace , relative to the working directory. A workspace is passed as -workspace , a project as -project. Empty lets xcodebuild find the project there. Empty. Builds/iOS/Unity-iPhone.xcodeproj. Build. Scheme-based passes -scheme , Target-based passes -target. Only the fields of the chosen kind show. Scheme-based. Scheme-based. Scheme. Passed as -scheme. Use a scheme or a target, not both. Empty. Unity-iPhone. Target. Passed as -target. Empty. Game. Configuration. Passed as -configuration. Empty. Release. SDK. Passed as -sdk. Empty. iphoneos. Actions. One action per line, such as clean , build or test. Empty runs build. Empty. clean then build. Run tests. Adds the test action after the actions above, unless they already have it. Use a scheme-based build. Off. On. Arguments. One argument per line, placed before the actions, such as -destination or a build setting. Empty. CODE_SIGN_STYLE=Manual. Working directory, Environment variables, Test reports. The same in every runner. See Fields every runner has. This runner has no container settings. A worked example. Build the iOS project generated by Unity. Step name Build iOS , Project or workspace Builds/iOS/Unity-iPhone.xcodeproj , Scheme Unity-iPhone , Configuration Release , Actions clean and build. What you see when it runs. The console shows the xcodebuild command and its output. Xcode writes test results as a result bundle, not JUnit XML: if another tool converts them, name its files in Test reports. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The xcodebuild command was not found on this agent. This step needs a macOS agent with Xcode installed. The Xcode Project step has both a scheme and a target. Use one of them. Related pages. Command Line. Unity. Runners. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"Epic: copy binary","href":"game-builds/epic-copy","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","Connection","Epic: copy binary","Options","Hands on to later steps","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Copy a binary from one artifact to another on Epic, without uploading it again. When to use it. Use it when you test a build on a staging artifact first and then want the same build on the live artifact. Copying is fast because nothing is uploaded again. Every Epic step runs the Epic BuildPatch Tool on the agent. The agent needs the tool: see Install BuildPatch Tool. A step that needs a newer tool than the agent has stops with a message that names the version the agent has. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, then Settings › Build steps › Add build step. Type Epic in Filter step types and click Epic: copy binary. Pick the credential. Type the Artifact ID of the source in Artifact ID. Type the Build version and the Destination artifact ID. Click Save. The Epic: copy binary step. Every field. Connection. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Epic Games Store credential. The credential that holds your BuildPatch Tool keys. The list shows the credentials you may use. The secret is never typed in the step, never written to a file and never shown in the log. None. The step fails if you pick nothing. Epic Store (Skyforge). Artifact ID. The Epic artifact to work on. Leave it empty to use the Default artifact ID of the credential. Empty (the credential default). 0f2b8a6c9d1e4c37a5e8b1d6c2f94a70. BuildPatch Tool program. The full path of the BuildPatch Tool program on the agent. Leave it empty to use the tool the agent reported. Empty (the tool the agent reported). D:/Tools/BuildPatchTool/BuildPatchTool.exe. For this step the artifact ID above is the source. Epic: copy binary. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Build version. The version of the binary to copy. Empty. 1.4.57-Windows. Destination artifact ID. The artifact the binary is copied to. It must not be the same as the source. Empty (required). 7e3c1b9a5d2f48c6b0a4e8d1f5c2a936. Options. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Stop after (minutes). The step is stopped after this time. 30. 30. Tries on network errors. How many times the step runs BuildPatch Tool when it fails because of a network problem. A wrong key or a missing permission is never tried again. 1. 3. Wait between tries (seconds). The pause between two tries. 30. 60. Save the tool&rsquo;s logs with the build. Whether the logs BuildPatch Tool wrote on the agent are kept as build artifacts in epic-logs. Choices: Only when the step fails , Always , Never. Only when the step fails. Always. Fail the build on warnings. Fails the step when BuildPatch Tool gave warnings, for example when metadata could not be written. Without it, warnings only show in the log. Off. On. Hands on to later steps. Name. What it holds. EPIC_COPIED_VERSION. The version that was copied. EPIC_DEST_ARTIFACT_ID. The artifact it went to. A worked example. QA tested 1.4.57-Windows on the staging artifact Skyforge Windows Staging. A build named Epic Promote copies it to the live artifact: credential Epic Store (Skyforge) , Artifact ID the staging ID, Build version 1.4.57-Windows , Destination artifact ID the live ID. A label step then puts it live. What you see when it runs. Epic: credential Epic Store (Skyforge), product 8c1d5f, artifact 0f2b8a., Publishing: Labels set by builds Epic: copy version 1.4.57-Windows from artifact 0f2b8a. to artifact 7e3c1b. Epic: Step output EPIC_COPIED_VERSION = 1.4.57-Windows Epic: Step output EPIC_DEST_ARTIFACT_ID = 7e3c1b. Problems and fixes. Message. What to do. The destination artifact ID is required. Fill in Destination artifact ID. The source and the destination are the same artifact. The destination must differ from the source. Check both IDs. Epic says the Client ID has no permission for the artifact. The credential must be allowed on both artifacts. Ask the person who runs the Developer Portal. Related pages. Epic Games Store. Epic: label binary. Epic: inspect binaries."},
{"title":"Epic Games Store credential","href":"game-builds/epic-credentials","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","Test access","BuildPatch Tool folder","The BuildPatch Tool page","Packages","Agents","Permissions","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Store your Epic BuildPatch Tool keys once, and set where the tool lives on each agent. When to use it. Every Epic step needs an Epic Games Store credential. An administrator or project owner adds it once. Build steps then pick it by name and never see the secret. Only people with the Owner, Admin or Store role in your Epic organization can see the client ID and secret in the Developer Portal. Use a build user of its own on the agent: a process of the same user can read the environment of another one. How to set it up. Open Credentials (for the server under Manage , or for one project in the project's menu) and click Add credentials. For Kind , choose Epic Games Store. Fill in the fields below. Copy the values from the Epic Developer Portal. Choose how labels are set under Publishing. Optional: use Test access to check the keys on an agent. Click Create. The Epic Games Store credential form. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. BuildPatch Tool client ID. The ID of the BuildPatch Tool keys. Developer Portal: your product, Product Settings, BPT credentials. These are not the keys of the Epic Online Services SDK. Empty. xyz123abc. BuildPatch Tool client secret. The secret that goes with the client ID. Kept encrypted and never shown again. It reaches BuildPatch Tool only through an environment variable of that one process. Empty. (a secret). Organization ID. Developer Portal: Organization, Settings, General Settings. Empty. o-4k2m9p. Product ID. Developer Portal: Product Settings, SDK Download and Credentials, EOS SDK Credentials, Product ID. Empty. 8c1d5f. Default artifact ID. The artifact the steps use when they name none. Developer Portal: Artifacts and Binaries, open the artifact, Edit Details. Empty. 0f2b8a6c9d1e4c37a5e8b1d6c2f94a70. Sandbox ID. The sandbox that Epic: label binary steps use, unless the step names one. Empty (the public sandbox). a1b2c3. Used for. A note for people, for example the game and the platform. It is not enforced. Empty. Skyforge, Windows. Publishing. Who sets the labels that put a binary live. Labels set by builds : Label and Unlabel steps run the BuildPatch Tool and set Live or Archive. Self-service : your organization assigns platforms to the artifact in the Developer Portal, and Epic gives it the Live label. Label and Unlabel steps then never run the tool for this credential. Labels set by builds. Self-service. Epic does not support labels from the BuildPatch Tool for organizations that use its self-service publishing tools. Buildspawn cannot tell which kind you are, so you choose. If Epic refuses a label, the step says so and suggests Self-service. Test access. The Test access card reads the binaries of the artifact on an agent. It labels and changes nothing. Field. What it does. Default. Agent. The machine that runs the test. (built-in). BuildPatch Tool program. The path of the tool on that agent. Empty (the tool the agent reported). Test access button. Runs the check with the values typed in the form. The result shows next to the button, for example how many binaries Epic returned, or the reason it refused. -. BuildPatch Tool folder. Each agent has a BuildPatch Tool folder setting. It says where the agent keeps the BuildPatch Tool, for example on a fast disk. The Epic steps look for the program there, and Install BuildPatch Tool installs there unless the step names another folder. Open Manage › Agents , open the agent and click Configure. Under the node properties, tick BuildPatch Tool folder and type the Folder. Click Save. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Folder. Where this agent keeps BuildPatch Tool. Empty: tools/buildpatchtool in the agent folder. D:/Tools/BuildPatchTool. The BuildPatch Tool page. Open Manage › Tools › BuildPatch Tool (administrators only). It has two cards. Packages. The ZIP files of the tool that you uploaded once. The table shows the package, its system, version, the start of its SHA-256 and its size. Remove deletes a package. Upload field. What it "},
{"title":"Epic: delete binary","href":"game-builds/epic-delete","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","Connection","Epic: delete binary","Options","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Remove one binary from an Epic artifact at once. When to use it. Use it to clear a bad or test upload. Epic removes binaries without a label after about a week anyway. This step does it at once. It cannot be undone, so it has no default version and needs a tick to confirm. This step needs the permission Epic Games Store / Delete binary for the person who starts the build. The step fails without it. Every Epic step runs the Epic BuildPatch Tool on the agent. The agent needs the tool: see Install BuildPatch Tool. A step that needs a newer tool than the agent has stops with a message that names the version the agent has. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, then Settings › Build steps › Add build step. Type Epic in Filter step types and click Epic: delete binary. Pick the credential and, if needed, the artifact. Type the Build version to delete. Tick I know this removes the binary from Epic. Click Save. The Epic: delete binary step. Every field. Connection. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Epic Games Store credential. The credential that holds your BuildPatch Tool keys. The list shows the credentials you may use. The secret is never typed in the step, never written to a file and never shown in the log. None. The step fails if you pick nothing. Epic Store (Skyforge). Artifact ID. The Epic artifact to work on. Leave it empty to use the Default artifact ID of the credential. Empty (the credential default). 0f2b8a6c9d1e4c37a5e8b1d6c2f94a70. BuildPatch Tool program. The full path of the BuildPatch Tool program on the agent. Leave it empty to use the tool the agent reported. Empty (the tool the agent reported). D:/Tools/BuildPatchTool/BuildPatchTool.exe. Epic: delete binary. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Build version to delete. The version to remove. There is no default, so nothing is deleted by mistake. Type the version, or use the output of an earlier step. Empty. 1.4.55-Windows. I know this removes the binary from Epic. The step fails when this is not ticked. Off. On. Options. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Stop after (minutes). The step is stopped after this time. 30. 30. Tries on network errors. How many times the step runs BuildPatch Tool when it fails because of a network problem. A wrong key or a missing permission is never tried again. 1. 3. Wait between tries (seconds). The pause between two tries. 30. 60. Save the tool&rsquo;s logs with the build. Whether the logs BuildPatch Tool wrote on the agent are kept as build artifacts in epic-logs. Choices: Only when the step fails , Always , Never. Only when the step fails. Always. Fail the build on warnings. Fails the step when BuildPatch Tool gave warnings, for example when metadata could not be written. Without it, warnings only show in the log. Off. On. A worked example. A test upload 0.0.1-Windows-test is no longer needed. A build Epic Cleanup has one step: credential Epic Store (Skyforge) , Build version to delete 0.0.1-Windows-test , confirmation ticked. Only people with Delete binary can start it. What you see when it runs. Epic: credential Epic Store (Skyforge), product 8c1d5f, artifact 0f2b8a., Publishing: Labels set by builds Epic: delete version 0.0.1-Windows-test from artifact 0f2b8a. Epic: version 0.0.1-Windows-test was deleted from artifact 0f2b8a. Problems and fixes. Message. What to do. Tick \"I know this removes the binary from Epic\" in the step first. Open the step and tick the confirmation. The person who started this build may not delete a binary. Ask an administrator for the permission Epic Games Store / Delete binary. A version is required. Fill in Build version to delete. Related pages. Epic Games Store. Epic: inspect binaries : list versions first. Epic Games Store credential : permissions."},
{"title":"Epic: download binary","href":"game-builds/epic-download","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","Connection","Epic: download binary","Options","Hands on to later steps","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Download a binary from Epic to a folder on the agent, to test it before you release it. When to use it. Use it to check that what players will get really starts. A test build can download the binary you just uploaded, then run a smoke test on it. It needs BuildPatch Tool 1.8.1. Every Epic step runs the Epic BuildPatch Tool on the agent. The agent needs the tool: see Install BuildPatch Tool. A step that needs a newer tool than the agent has stops with a message that names the version the agent has. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, then Settings › Build steps › Add build step. Type Epic in Filter step types and click Epic: download binary. Pick the credential. Type the Build version and the Install folder. Choose What to download. Click Save. The Epic: download binary step. Every field. Connection. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Epic Games Store credential. The credential that holds your BuildPatch Tool keys. The list shows the credentials you may use. The secret is never typed in the step, never written to a file and never shown in the log. None. The step fails if you pick nothing. Epic Store (Skyforge). Artifact ID. The Epic artifact to work on. Leave it empty to use the Default artifact ID of the credential. Empty (the credential default). 0f2b8a6c9d1e4c37a5e8b1d6c2f94a70. BuildPatch Tool program. The full path of the BuildPatch Tool program on the agent. Leave it empty to use the tool the agent reported. Empty (the tool the agent reported). D:/Tools/BuildPatchTool/BuildPatchTool.exe. Epic: download binary. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Build version. The version to download. ${EPIC_BUILD_VERSION} (the upload step's version). 1.4.57-Windows. Install folder. The folder the files go to. epic-install. epic-install. Everything. Every file of the binary. Chosen. -. Install tags. Only the files that have these tags. Not chosen. -. Install tags (field). Shown for Install tags. Tags separated by commas. At least one is needed. Empty. movies,music. Only these files. A list of files of the binary. Needs BuildPatch Tool 1.8.3. Not chosen. -. Files. Shown for Only these files. One path of the binary per line. Each entry must match the binary exactly. The list may not be empty. Empty. Content/Movies/intro.bk2. Options. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Stop after (minutes). The step is stopped after this time. 120. 120. Tries on network errors. How many times the step runs BuildPatch Tool when it fails because of a network problem. A wrong key or a missing permission is never tried again. 1. 3. Wait between tries (seconds). The pause between two tries. 30. 60. Save the tool&rsquo;s logs with the build. Whether the logs BuildPatch Tool wrote on the agent are kept as build artifacts in epic-logs. Choices: Only when the step fails , Always , Never. Only when the step fails. Always. Fail the build on warnings. Fails the step when BuildPatch Tool gave warnings, for example when metadata could not be written. Without it, warnings only show in the log. Off. On. Hands on to later steps. Name. What it holds. EPIC_INSTALL_DIR. The full path of the folder the binary was downloaded to. A worked example. After the upload, a step downloads the new version to epic-install with Everything. A PowerShell step then starts ${EPIC_INSTALL_DIR}\\Skyforge.exe -smoketest. What you see when it runs. Epic: credential Epic Store (Skyforge), product 8c1d5f, artifact 0f2b8a., Publishing: Labels set by builds Epic: download version 1.4.57-Windows to D:\\agent\\workspace\\Epic Publish\\epic-install Epic: Step output EPIC_INSTALL_DIR = D:\\agent\\workspace\\Epic Publish\\epic-install. Problems and fixes. Message. What to do. \"Only these files\" is chosen but the list is empty. Add at least one path, or choose another option. \"Install tags\" is chosen but no tag is given. Type at least one tag. A file in your list is not in the binary. Entries must match the manifest exactly. Check the spelling and the slashes. Epic: inspect binaries (Metadata) "},
{"title":"Epic: inspect binaries","href":"game-builds/epic-inspect","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","Connection","List binaries","Compare two builds","Metadata of a build","Options","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Read from Epic: list the binaries of an artifact, compare two builds, or save the metadata of a build. Nothing is changed. When to use it. Use it to find the newest version, to check how large an update will be for players, or to keep a record of what went into a build. It only reads, so it is safe to run at any time. Every Epic step runs the Epic BuildPatch Tool on the agent. The agent needs the tool: see Install BuildPatch Tool. A step that needs a newer tool than the agent has stops with a message that names the version the agent has. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, then Settings › Build steps › Add build step. Type Epic in Filter step types and click Epic: inspect binaries. Pick the credential. Choose a card under What to do : List binaries , Compare two builds or Metadata of a build , and fill in its fields. Click Save. The Epic: inspect binaries step. Every field. Connection. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Epic Games Store credential. The credential that holds your BuildPatch Tool keys. The list shows the credentials you may use. The secret is never typed in the step, never written to a file and never shown in the log. None. The step fails if you pick nothing. Epic Store (Skyforge). Artifact ID. The Epic artifact to work on. Leave it empty to use the Default artifact ID of the credential. Empty (the credential default). 0f2b8a6c9d1e4c37a5e8b1d6c2f94a70. BuildPatch Tool program. The full path of the BuildPatch Tool program on the agent. Leave it empty to use the tool the agent reported. Empty (the tool the agent reported). D:/Tools/BuildPatchTool/BuildPatchTool.exe. List binaries. The binaries of an artifact, newest first. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Number of binaries. How many binaries to list. Must be a whole number. Empty lists all. Empty (all). 10. Only binaries that have a label. Leaves out binaries with no label. Off. On. Skip the encryption status. Faster. Needs BuildPatch Tool 1.8.6. Off. Off. It hands on ${EPIC_BINARIES_FILE} (the saved list, epic/binaries.json ), ${EPIC_BINARY_COUNT} , ${EPIC_NEWEST_VERSION} and, when one is live, ${EPIC_LIVE_VERSION}. Compare two builds. Shows how much a player downloads to move from one version to the other. The result is saved as epic/diff.json. Field. What it does. Default. Example. From version. The version players have now. Empty. 1.3.2-Windows. To version. The new version. ${EPIC_BUILD_VERSION}. 1.4.57-Windows. Install tags of the from version. Compare only files that have these tags. Empty (all files). movies. Install tags of the to version. The same for the new version. Empty (all files). movies. Compare tag sets. One comma list of tags per line. Two quote marks ( \"\" ) stand for the files that have no tag. Empty. movies,music. Fail when no optimised update exists. Fails the step when Epic has no optimised update for the pair. Needs BuildPatch Tool 1.8.8. Without it, Epic gives plain statistics. Off. On. It hands on ${EPIC_DIFF_FILE}. Metadata of a build. What went into a binary, written to a file. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Version. The version to read. ${EPIC_BUILD_VERSION}. 1.4.57-Windows. Format. JSON or Text (human form). JSON. JSON. Encoding. UTF-8 or UTF-16. UTF-8. UTF-8. It hands on ${EPIC_METADATA_FILE} , a build artifact. Options. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Stop after (minutes). The step is stopped after this time. 30. 30. Tries on network errors. How many times the step runs BuildPatch Tool when it fails because of a network problem. A wrong key or a missing permission is never tried again. 1. 3. Wait between tries (seconds). The pause between two tries. 30. 60. Save the tool&rsquo;s logs with the build. Whether the logs BuildPatch Tool wrote on the agent are kept as build artifacts in epic-logs. Choices: Only when the step fails , Always , Never. Only when the step fails. Always. Fail the build on warnings. Fails the step when BuildPatch Tool gave warnings, for example when metadata could not be writ"},
{"title":"Epic: label binary","href":"game-builds/epic-label","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","Connection","Epic: label binary","Options","Hands on to later steps","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Put an uploaded binary live for Windows, Win32 or Mac, or take its label away. When to use it. Use it after the upload, when the build should reach players. A label tells Epic which binary is Live or Archive for a platform. Epic deletes a binary with no label after about a week. Setting the label Live needs the permission Epic Games Store / Set Live for the person who started the build. If the credential is set to Self-service , Epic gives the Live label itself when you assign a platform in the Developer Portal. This step then does nothing and never starts BuildPatch Tool. The form shows a note at the top. See Epic Games Store credential. Every Epic step runs the Epic BuildPatch Tool on the agent. The agent needs the tool: see Install BuildPatch Tool. A step that needs a newer tool than the agent has stops with a message that names the version the agent has. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, then Settings › Build steps › Add build step. Type Epic in Filter step types and click Epic: label binary. Pick the credential. Choose the Action and the Label , and tick the platforms. Keep the Build version as the version of the upload step, or type another. Click Save. The Epic: label binary step. Every field. Connection. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Epic Games Store credential. The credential that holds your BuildPatch Tool keys. The list shows the credentials you may use. The secret is never typed in the step, never written to a file and never shown in the log. None. The step fails if you pick nothing. Epic Store (Skyforge). Artifact ID. The Epic artifact to work on. Leave it empty to use the Default artifact ID of the credential. Empty (the credential default). 0f2b8a6c9d1e4c37a5e8b1d6c2f94a70. BuildPatch Tool program. The full path of the BuildPatch Tool program on the agent. Leave it empty to use the tool the agent reported. Empty (the tool the agent reported). D:/Tools/BuildPatchTool/BuildPatchTool.exe. Epic: label binary. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Action. Label puts the label on. Unlabel takes it away. Label. Label. Build version. The binary to label. It must be uploaded first, or Epic rejects the call. ${EPIC_BUILD_VERSION} (the version of the upload step). 1.4.57-Windows. Label. Live or Archive. Live. Live. Platforms. Tick Windows , Win32 and Mac. The step makes one call to the tool for each platform. A binary that works on Windows and Win32 needs two labels. Epic offers no Linux label. At least one must be ticked. Windows ticked. Windows. Sandbox ID. The sandbox to label in. Empty: the sandbox of the credential, else the public sandbox. a1b2c3. Fail instead of skipping. For a Self-service credential: the step fails, and does not just say so and continue. Off. Off. Options. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Stop after (minutes). The step is stopped after this time. 30. 30. Tries on network errors. How many times the step runs BuildPatch Tool when it fails because of a network problem. A wrong key or a missing permission is never tried again. 1. 3. Wait between tries (seconds). The pause between two tries. 30. 60. Save the tool&rsquo;s logs with the build. Whether the logs BuildPatch Tool wrote on the agent are kept as build artifacts in epic-logs. Choices: Only when the step fails , Always , Never. Only when the step fails. Always. Fail the build on warnings. Fails the step when BuildPatch Tool gave warnings, for example when metadata could not be written. Without it, warnings only show in the log. Off. On. Hands on to later steps. Name. What it holds. EPIC_LABELLED. true after a label, false after an unlabel or a skip. EPIC_LABEL_PLATFORMS. The platforms that were labelled, separated by commas. Empty when skipped. A worked example. After Epic Games Store upload and Epic: optimise patch , add Epic: label binary : Action Label, Label Live, Platforms Windows, Build version left as ${EPIC_BUILD_VERSION}. To retire the old build, add a second step with Action Unlabel, Label Live and Build v"},
{"title":"Epic: optimise patch","href":"game-builds/epic-optimise","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","Connection","Epic: optimise patch","Options","Hands on to later steps","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Make the update between two uploaded binaries smaller, so players download less. When to use it. Use it after an upload, before the build goes live. Epic builds the update between two binaries from the data they hold. Optimising gives players a smaller download. Run it several days before the release, in case the network fails. Every Epic step runs the Epic BuildPatch Tool on the agent. The agent needs the tool: see Install BuildPatch Tool. A step that needs a newer tool than the agent has stops with a message that names the version the agent has. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, then Settings › Build steps › Add build step. Type Epic in Filter step types and click Epic: optimise patch. Pick the credential. Type the From version , the one your players have now. Keep To version as the version of the upload step, or type another. Choose whether to skip large updates. Click Save. The Epic: optimise patch step. Every field. Connection. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Epic Games Store credential. The credential that holds your BuildPatch Tool keys. The list shows the credentials you may use. The secret is never typed in the step, never written to a file and never shown in the log. None. The step fails if you pick nothing. Epic Store (Skyforge). Artifact ID. The Epic artifact to work on. Leave it empty to use the Default artifact ID of the credential. Empty (the credential default). 0f2b8a6c9d1e4c37a5e8b1d6c2f94a70. BuildPatch Tool program. The full path of the BuildPatch Tool program on the agent. Leave it empty to use the tool the agent reported. Empty (the tool the agent reported). D:/Tools/BuildPatchTool/BuildPatchTool.exe. Epic: optimise patch. Field. What it does. Default. Example. From version. The binary your players have now, usually the one that is Live. It must differ from the To version. Empty. 1.3.2-Windows. To version. The new binary. ${EPIC_BUILD_VERSION}. 1.4.57-Windows. Skip the optimisation when the update is large. Never skip tries every update, however large. Skip above a limit stops early when the plain update is already above the limit. Never skip. Skip above a limit. Limit. Shown for Skip above a limit. The size limit. The field checks the number as you type. 8. 8. Unit. GB , GiB or % of the size of the to version. Needs BuildPatch Tool 1.8.8. GB. GB. The optimisation streams the existing data and can take longer than an upload. If the pair was optimised already, the step ends with success and says so. Options. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Stop after (minutes). The step is stopped after this time. 180. 180. Tries on network errors. How many times the step runs BuildPatch Tool when it fails because of a network problem. A wrong key or a missing permission is never tried again. 1. 3. Wait between tries (seconds). The pause between two tries. 30. 60. Save the tool&rsquo;s logs with the build. Whether the logs BuildPatch Tool wrote on the agent are kept as build artifacts in epic-logs. Choices: Only when the step fails , Always , Never. Only when the step fails. Always. Keep working data in memory. Uses no cloud folder on disk. For agents with a small disk. Needs BuildPatch Tool 1.8.8. Off. Off. Fail the build on warnings. Fails the step when BuildPatch Tool gave warnings, for example when metadata could not be written. Without it, warnings only show in the log. Off. On. The Stop after default of 180 minutes is the default of the upload step too, because both can run long. Hands on to later steps. Name. What it holds. EPIC_PATCH_IMPROVEMENT. How much smaller the update became, as the tool reports it. EPIC_PATCH_BYTES. The size of the update in bytes. A worked example. Skyforge 1.3.2 is live. A build uploads 1.4.57 and runs Epic: optimise patch with From version 1.3.2-Windows , To version left at the upload's version, and Skip above a limit 8 GB. A later label step then makes 1.4.57 live. What you see when it runs. Epic: credential Epic Store (Skyforge), product 8c1d5f, artifact 0f2b"},
{"title":"Epic Games Store","href":"game-builds/epic-overview","headings":["What it is","When to use it","The steps","How the steps fit together","Where things are set","Rules that apply to every step","Related pages","Epic Games Store upload","Publish a build to Epic","Steam","Perforce"],"text":"Upload your game to Epic, label it live and manage its binaries from a build, with the Epic BuildPatch Tool. What it is. Epic publishes games through artifacts and binaries. An artifact is one product you ship, for example your game on Windows. A binary is one uploaded version of it. Epic's command line program, the BuildPatch Tool , uploads and manages binaries. Buildspawn runs it for you in build steps, so a successful build can publish itself. Buildspawn keeps the secret keys in a credential. They never appear in a step, a file or the log. When to use it. Use these steps when your studio ships on the Epic Games Store and you want the build machine, not a person, to upload and label builds. The steps. Step. What it does. Install BuildPatch Tool. Puts the tool on an agent that does not have it. Epic Games Store upload. Uploads a packaged game as a new binary. Epic: label binary. Puts a binary live for a platform, or takes the label away. Epic: optimise patch. Makes the update between two binaries smaller for players. Epic: inspect binaries. Lists binaries, compares two builds, or reads the metadata of a build. Epic: download binary. Downloads a binary to test it before release. Epic: copy binary. Copies a binary to another artifact without uploading it again. Epic: delete binary. Removes a binary at once. How the steps fit together. An administrator adds an Epic Games Store credential once. An administrator uploads the BuildPatch Tool ZIP once, under Manage › Tools › BuildPatch Tool. Your build makes the package, for example with an Unreal Engine or Unity step. Epic Games Store upload sends it. It hands the new version on to later steps as ${EPIC_BUILD_VERSION}. Epic: optimise patch and Epic: label binary use that version by default. Each step hands values on to the steps after it. The log shows them as Epic: Step output NAME = value. Later steps can use them as ${NAME} or %NAME%. The Add build step page filtered to Epic. Where things are set. What. Where. Keys, product, default artifact. A credential of the kind Epic Games Store. See Epic Games Store credential. The BuildPatch Tool ZIP, and which agent has it. Manage › Tools › BuildPatch Tool. See The BuildPatch Tool page. Where an agent keeps the tool. The BuildPatch Tool folder setting of the agent. See BuildPatch Tool folder. Who may set a binary live or delete one. The permissions Epic Games Store / Set Live and Epic Games Store / Delete binary. Rules that apply to every step. The Build version must be new for the artifact, for every platform. Put the platform in it, for example 1.4.${BUILD_NUMBER}-Windows. Epic deletes a binary with no label after about a week. Label what you want to keep. When a step fails, the log says why in plain words and names the credential or artifact. The tool's own logs are kept in epic-logs when you ask for them. The tool runs with the secret in the environment of that one process. Use a build user of its own on the agent, because a process of the same user can read the environment of another one. Related pages. Epic Games Store upload. Every setting of the upload step. Publish a build to Epic. Upload, optimise and label in one build. Steam. Ship the same build on Steam. Perforce. Where the game comes from."},
{"title":"Epic Games Store upload","href":"game-builds/epic-upload","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","Connection","Build","Files","File attributes","Prerequisites and uninstall","Metadata and checks","Options","Hands on to later steps","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Upload a packaged game to Epic as a new binary, with its launch program, files, prerequisites and metadata. When to use it. Use it at the end of a build that has made a packaged game folder, when you want that build on the Epic Games Store. It is the step that creates a new binary. After it, optimise the patch and label the binary. Every Epic step runs the Epic BuildPatch Tool on the agent. The agent needs the tool: see Install BuildPatch Tool. A step that needs a newer tool than the agent has stops with a message that names the version the agent has. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, then Settings › Build steps › Add build step. Type Epic in Filter step types and click Epic Games Store upload. Under Connection , pick the Epic Games Store credential. The box below it shows the product, artifact, publishing mode and tool that were found. Under Build , type the Build root , the Build version and the Launch program. Choose which files go in, and fill in any other section you need. Check the Command summary at the bottom. The secret shows only as the name of an environment variable. Click Save. The Epic Games Store upload step: Connection and Build. Every field. Connection. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Epic Games Store credential. The credential that holds your BuildPatch Tool keys. The list shows the credentials you may use. The secret is never typed in the step, never written to a file and never shown in the log. None. The step fails if you pick nothing. Epic Store (Skyforge). Artifact ID. The Epic artifact to work on. Leave it empty to use the Default artifact ID of the credential. Empty (the credential default). 0f2b8a6c9d1e4c37a5e8b1d6c2f94a70. BuildPatch Tool program. The full path of the BuildPatch Tool program on the agent. Leave it empty to use the tool the agent reported. Empty (the tool the agent reported). D:/Tools/BuildPatchTool/BuildPatchTool.exe. Build. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Build root. The folder in the workspace that holds the packaged game. Put it near the drive root: Windows paths are limited to 260 characters. Buildspawn warns when a file path in it is longer than 240. Empty (required). build/WindowsPackaged. Build version. The version Epic knows this binary by. It must be new for the artifact, for every platform. One to 100 characters: letters, digits and. + - _ only. The field checks the rule as you type. ${BUILD_NUMBER}. 1.4.${BUILD_NUMBER}-Windows. This is downloadable content (DLC). Tick it when this upload is a DLC item. Buildspawn writes an empty launch program. Each DLC item has its own artifact. The base game and other DLC must not be in the build root, or must be left out with the list below. No two items may hold the same file path. Off. Off. Launch program. The program inside the build root that the launcher starts. For a Mac app use the path inside it. It is marked executable for you. Required unless DLC is ticked, and it must exist in the build root. Empty. Skyforge.exe or Game.app/Contents/MacOS/Game. Launch arguments. Arguments the launcher gives the program. Empty. -windowed. Files. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Everything in the build root. Every file below the build root goes in. Chosen. -. Only these files. Nothing but the files in the list goes in. Not chosen. -. Everything except these. All files except the ones in the list. Not chosen. -. List. One entry per line: a path in the build root with forward slashes. In the list to leave out you can also write R\"regular expression\" or W\"wildcard*\". Lines that start with # are comments. The list is written to a file outside the build root. Needs BuildPatch Tool 1.6.0 for R\".\" and W\".\". Empty. Content/Movies/intro.bk2. File attributes. Mark files executable, or give them tags. The launch program does not need to be listed. Tags feed the launcher's selective download, which Epic calls beta: ask an Epic representative before you use it. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Files › Add file. Adds on"},
{"title":"Install BuildPatch Tool","href":"game-builds/install-buildpatch-tool","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Put Epic's BuildPatch Tool on an agent, or check that it is there, so the Epic steps can run. When to use it. Use it as the first step of a build that publishes to Epic, when you are not sure the agent has the BuildPatch Tool, or to keep the tool up to date. If you install the tool by hand on each agent, you do not need this step. Epic gives the BuildPatch Tool ZIP only to people who sign in to the Developer Portal (Artifacts and Binaries, Download build patch tool). Buildspawn does not sign in for you. Download the ZIP once and upload it to Buildspawn, or put it on a web address or share you control. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, then Settings › Build steps › Add build step. Type BuildPatch in Filter step types and click Install BuildPatch Tool. Choose where the tool comes from (see the table below) and fill in the fields that appear. Leave Update when a newer package is available and Check that the tool starts ticked. Click Save. The Install BuildPatch Tool step. Every field. Choose one of three cards under Install BuildPatch Tool. Field. What it does. Default. Example. From Buildspawn. Uses the ZIP an administrator uploaded once. Buildspawn sends it to the agent over its own connection. Chosen. -. Package. Shown for From Buildspawn. Which uploaded package to use. The list holds the uploaded packages. Empty (a package for the agent's system). BuildPatchTool-win.zip. From a web address or share. The agent fetches the ZIP itself from an address you run, or from a shared folder. Not chosen. -. Address or folder. The web address or share that holds the ZIP. Empty. https://tools.studio.example/BuildPatchTool-win.zip. SHA-256. The checksum of the ZIP. When it is set and the file does not match, the step fails. Empty (not checked). 9f2c4e7a. (the full 64 characters). Already on the agent. A folder on the agent already holds the tool. The step only checks it. Not chosen. -. Folder on the agent. That folder. Empty. D:/Tools/BuildPatchTool. Install into. Where to unpack the tool, for the first two cards. The BuildPatch Tool folder of the agent, else tools/buildpatchtool in the agent folder. D:/Tools/BuildPatchTool. Update when a newer package is available. Installs a newer package next to the old one. The folder of the older version is kept. On. On. Check that the tool starts. Runs the tool once with -help to prove that it starts. On. On. A worked example. Your Windows agent win-build-02 is new. An administrator already uploaded BuildPatchTool-win.zip , version 1.8.8, under Manage › Tools › BuildPatch Tool. Add Install BuildPatch Tool as the first step of Epic Publish , keep From Buildspawn and leave the other fields alone. The first run installs the tool. Later runs only check that it is there. What you see when it runs. The ZIP is sent to the agent (or fetched by it) and checked. It is unpacked into a folder for the version. On Linux and macOS the program is made executable. The tool is started once with -help , if you asked for that. The agent reports the path and the version as buildpatchtool.path and buildpatchtool.version. The log lines start with Epic: The step hands on ${EPIC_BPT_PATH} (the program) and ${EPIC_BPT_VERSION} (its version). The BuildPatch Tool page under Manage › Tools then shows the agent as installed. The BuildPatch Tool page. Problems and fixes. Message. What to do. The step names no package, or no package fits. Upload the ZIP under Manage › Tools › BuildPatch Tool , then choose it. The package. is for Windows, but agent. runs Linux. Upload a package for the agent's system, and say which system it is when you upload. The SHA-256 of the file does not match. The file changed or is damaged. Download it again and update the checksum. The package has no BuildPatch Tool program. The ZIP is the wrong one. The program must be named BuildPatchTool.exe on Windows, or BuildPatchTool or BuildPatchTool.sh on Linux and macOS. BuildPatch Tool was put on the agent but does not start. Run it by hand on the ag"},
{"title":"Install SteamCMD","href":"game-builds/install-steamcmd","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Download Valve's SteamCMD tool onto the agent and run it once so it is ready for the Steam steps. When to use it. Use it as the first Steam step on agents that are new or short-lived, such as container or cloud agents, or any agent where nobody installed SteamCMD by hand. If SteamCMD is already installed, the step says so and does nothing. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Build steps › Add build step. Type Steam in Filter step types and click Install SteamCMD. Type a Step name , for example Install SteamCMD. Change the fields below only if you need to. The defaults suit most agents. Click Save. Drag the step above your Steam upload step. The Install SteamCMD step. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Install folder. Where SteamCMD goes on the agent. tools/steamcmd in the folder of the agent. D:\\tools\\steamcmd. Download again if it is already there. Download even when SteamCMD is installed. SteamCMD updates itself at each start anyway. Off. Off. Download address. Where to download the package. Use an internal mirror if your agents sit behind a proxy. The address Valve gives for the system of the agent. https://mirror.studio.example/steamcmd.zip. Stop after (minutes). Stops the step if it takes longer. 30. 30. Hands on to later steps. Shows the two values the step sets: ${STEAMCMD_PATH} and ${STEAMCMD_VERSION}. Not editable. The form also has a What happens card. It says the step needs nothing installed, only an internet connection to Valve. A worked example. Your studio builds on a fresh Windows cloud agent named win-cloud-07. Add Install SteamCMD with the defaults. It installs SteamCMD in tools\\steamcmd under the agent folder. Your next step, Steam upload , finds it there without a path. What you see when it runs. First run: a line that it is downloading the package to the folder, then that it runs SteamCMD once so it updates itself. The step sets STEAMCMD_PATH and STEAMCMD_VERSION. Later steps read them as %STEAMCMD_PATH%. Later runs: SteamCMD is already installed, version … and nothing is downloaded. Problems and fixes. Message. What to do. The download from … failed. The agent cannot reach Valve. Check its network or proxy, or set Download address to a mirror. The package holds no SteamCMD program. The address is wrong. Use Valve's address for the agent's system. SteamCMD was installed but did not report a version. Run SteamCMD once by hand on the agent to see why. On Linux it often needs 32-bit runtime libraries. SteamCMD ended with the code …. Read the lines above it in the log. Related pages. Steam upload: overview. Steam upload. Build Unity from Perforce and upload to a Steam branch."},
{"title":"Game builds and stores","href":"game-builds/overview","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","The pages","Engines","Unity step","Unity build settings","Unreal Engine step","UGS metadata badge","Steam","Steam overview","Steam build account","Install SteamCMD","Steam upload","Steam: DRM wrap","Steam: check build","Steam: set branch live","Steam: workshop item","Epic Games Store","Epic overview","Epic credential and tool setup","Install BuildPatch Tool","Epic Games Store upload","Epic: optimise patch","Epic: label binary","Epic: inspect binaries","Epic: download binary","Epic: copy binary","Epic: delete binary","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Build your game with Unity or Unreal Engine, then send it to Steam or the Epic Games Store, all from one build configuration. When to use it. Use these steps for a game pipeline: get the code from Perforce or Git , build with the engine, then upload the result to a store and put it on a branch. How to set it up. Set the version control of the build configuration. Add a build step for your engine: Unity or Unreal Engine. Add the store steps you need. Each store has an install step, an upload step and some extra steps. Add the store credential first. Click Save and Run. The pages. Engines. Unity step. Batch-mode builds, targets and the Editor tool. Unity build settings. Unity account and serial. Unreal Engine step. BuildCookRun, BuildGraph, tests. UGS metadata badge. Post a result badge to the metadata server. Steam. Steam overview. How the pieces fit. Steam build account. The credential and Steam Guard. Install SteamCMD. Get the Steam upload tool. Steam upload. Depots, branch, description. Steam: DRM wrap. Wrap the game executable. Steam: check build. Check a build after upload. Steam: set branch live. Put a build on a branch. Steam: workshop item. Publish a workshop item. Epic Games Store. Epic overview. How the eight steps fit. Epic credential and tool setup. Credential and tool folder. Install BuildPatch Tool. Get the upload tool. Epic Games Store upload. Upload a binary. Epic: optimise patch. Make smaller patches. Epic: label binary. Put a label on a build. Epic: inspect binaries. List and compare. Epic: download binary. Get a build back. Epic: copy binary. Copy to another label. Epic: delete binary. Remove an old build. A worked example. Skyforge builds a Unity game from Perforce on a Windows agent, then uploads it to Steam. See Build Unity from Perforce and upload to a Steam branch. For the Epic Games Store, see Publish a build to the Epic Games Store. What you see when it runs. Each step is a block in the build log. Upload steps print the files sent and the build ID the store gave. Secrets are hidden in the log. Problems and fixes. You see. What to do. The build waits in the queue. No agent has the engine or the tool. Open the queue and read the wait reason. An upload step fails on sign-in. Check the store credential on its page. Related pages. Build an Unreal Engine game from Perforce , Build a Unity project from Git."},
{"title":"Steam build account","href":"game-builds/steam-account","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","The three Steam Guard modes","Stored session (default)","Machine login","Generated codes","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"The credential that holds the Steam account your build signs in with, and how it handles Steam Guard. When to use it. Create one for every Steam account the steps Steam upload , Steam: DRM wrap and Steam: workshop item use. The password and Steam Guard data stay in the credential. They are never typed into a step, written into a script or shown in the log. Use a dedicated build account with only the permissions Edit App Metadata and Publish App Changes To Steam , and access to the apps it builds. How to set it up. Open the project (or Manage Buildspawn › Credentials for the whole server) and go to its Credentials. Click Add credentials. Choose the kind Steam build account. Fill in User name and Password. Choose a Steam Guard mode (below) and follow its steps. Click Save. The Steam build account form. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. User name. The account name, not an email address. None. skyforge_build. Password. The account password. Stored encrypted and never shown. None. Used for. A note for people, such as which game the account is for. Not enforced. Empty. Skyforge only. Steam Guard. One of three cards: Stored session , Machine login , Generated codes. Stored session. Stored session. Agents that may use it. One agent name per line. Empty means any agent. Empty. win-build-01. Agent for the sign-in and the test. Which agent runs the sign-in or the test. The built-in node is also offered. First in the list. win-build-01. The buttons Sign in and Test sign-in run SteamCMD on that agent. The three Steam Guard modes. Stored session (default). You sign in once on the credential. Click Sign in , type the Steam Guard code Steam sends you, and click the button in the dialog. The result is kept encrypted in the credential, sent to the agent for each run and then removed from it. The State shows Ready , Sign-in expired or Never signed in , and who signed in. If Steam changes the stored session during a run, Buildspawn saves the new one back. Each agent gets its own private copy. Jobs that use this account on one agent wait for each other. Machine login. Sign in once by hand on each agent, in its SteamCMD folder. Buildspawn never sees the files. The step signs in with the account name only, so no password is used. Never sign in again with a password on that agent, because that changes the Steam Guard token. Generated codes. Type the Shared secret of the account's Steam authenticator. Buildspawn makes the Steam Guard code from it at each run, so no one needs to sign in. This is not an official feature of Steam. The shared secret is the second factor of the account and it is kept on this server. Use it only for an account that can do nothing but build. A worked example. Create the credential steam-build for the user skyforge_build. Keep Stored session , pick the agent win-build-01 , click Sign in , type the code Steam emailed you, and save. The State changes to Ready. In your Steam upload step, choose steam-build. What you see when it runs. The step log shows account skyforge_build (credential steam-build, stored session), app …. The password is never shown. When the stored session was renewed: The stored session of … was refreshed. Test sign-in shows Signed in as skyforge_build. Problems and fixes. Message. What to do. Steam asked for a Steam Guard code. Sign in again on the credential. Stored session ran out. Click Sign in again. Buildspawn does not retry a login, to protect the account. Steam asked for a Steam Guard code for … Sign in again by hand on the agent. Machine login: sign in by hand in the agent's SteamCMD folder. Steam refused the password for the account …. Fix the password and save. The sign-in of … has expired. The credential is marked Sign-in expired. Sign in again. The build account … may not be used on the agent …. Add the agent to Agents that may use it or empty the list. The Steam build account … does not exist, or this job may not use it. Create it where the build configuration can see it, such "},
{"title":"Steam: check build","href":"game-builds/steam-check-build","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Wait until Steam lists your uploaded build, and check that a branch exists. When to use it. Use it between Steam upload and Steam: set branch live. Steam can take a moment to list a new build, and setting a build live on a branch that does not exist fails. This step needs no SteamCMD. It calls Steam's Web API. How to set it up. Add a Secret text credential that holds your publisher Steam Web API key. See Credentials. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Build steps › Add build step › Steam: check build. Choose the Steam Web API key. Type the App ID and the Branch. Leave Build ID on %BUILD_ID% to check the build of the earlier upload step. Click Save. The Steam: check build step. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Steam Web API key. A Secret text credential with the key. It is not the build account. None. steam-web-key. App ID. Your app number. None. 1000480. Build ID. The build to look for. Must be a number once parameters are filled in. %BUILD_ID%. %BUILD_ID%. Branch. The branch that must exist. Empty. nightly. Wait until the build is listed. The step fails when the build does not show in time. On. On. Fail when the branch does not exist. A branch must be made in Steamworks first. On. On. Call Steam from the server. Off: the call is made from the agent of the build. On: from the Buildspawn server. Off. Off. Wait at most (minutes). How long to wait for the build to be listed. 10. 10. Hands on to later steps. The values it sets: BUILD_FOUND and BRANCH_BUILD_ID (the build that is live on the branch). Not editable. A worked example. After uploading build 15870342 for app 1000480 , check branch nightly with the default wait of 10 minutes. When it passes, your tests run, and then Set branch live publishes the build. What you see when it runs. Build 15870342 is not in the list of builds yet. Waiting. repeated until Steam lists it. the branch nightly exists. The key is shown as ********. Problems and fixes. Message. What to do. No Steam Web API key is chosen / The Web API key … does not exist, or this job may not use it. Choose a Secret text credential the build configuration may use. The build ID … is missing or is not a number. An earlier upload step must set BUILD_ID. A preview upload gives none. Build … did not show in the list of builds within … minutes. Raise Wait at most (minutes) , and check the App ID. the branch … does not exist. Create it on the Builds page in Steamworks, or turn off Fail when the branch does not exist. Steam answered …. Steam refused the call. The text after it is Steam's answer. Check the key and the App ID. Related pages. Steam upload. Steam: set branch live. Steam upload: overview."},
{"title":"Steam: DRM wrap","href":"game-builds/steam-drm-wrap","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Wrap a game program with the Steam DRM wrapper, as a step of its own. When to use it. Use it to wrap a program and look at the result before you upload, or when the wrapped file goes somewhere other than a Steam upload. To wrap and upload in one sign-in, use the Wrap these files with the Steam DRM wrapper first option inside Steam upload instead. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Build steps › Add build step › Steam: DRM wrap. Choose the Steam build account. Type the App ID and the Program to wrap. Choose a Level. Click Save. The Steam: DRM wrap step. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Steam build account. The credential to sign in with. The password and Steam Guard data stay in the credential. None. steam-build. SteamCMD program. Path of SteamCMD. Found on the agent. D:\\tools\\steamcmd\\steamcmd.exe. App ID. Your app number. None. 1000480. Program to wrap. Path in the workspace of the program to wrap. It must exist. None. Builds\\Win64\\Skyforge.exe. Where the wrapped file goes. Path for the wrapped file. Empty replaces the program. Replaces the program. Builds\\Win64\\Skyforge_wrapped.exe. Level. How strict the wrapper is. Normal , Compatible , Skip debugger check , or Compatible, skip debugger check. Normal. Compatible. Stop after (minutes). Stops the step after this time. 120. 30. Valve's notes, shown on the form: every build for release must be wrapped. Wrap before other DRM, in compatibility mode. Valve does not support.NET games. Antivirus tools can flag a wrapped file. A worked example. After the Unreal Engine step packages Skyforge, wrap Builds\\Win64\\Skyforge.exe for app 1000480 with level Compatible , and leave Where the wrapped file goes empty. The Steam upload that follows then sends the wrapped program. What you see when it runs. The sign-in lines of Steam steps, then wrapped Builds\\Win64\\Skyforge.exe. The wrapped file is at the place you chose. Problems and fixes. Message. What to do. The program to wrap is missing. Fill in Program to wrap. The program … is not in the workspace. The path is wrong or an earlier step did not build it. Check the path, which is relative to the workspace. Steam asked for a Steam Guard code. Sign in again on the credential. SteamCMD was not found on the agent. Add Install SteamCMD first. Related pages. Steam upload. Steam upload: overview."},
{"title":"Steam upload: overview","href":"game-builds/steam-overview","headings":["When to use it","Words you need","The steps","Install SteamCMD","Steam upload","Steam: DRM wrap","Steam: check build","Steam: set branch live","Steam: workshop item","How the pieces fit","Two kinds of secret","What Buildspawn will not do","Permission to set a branch live","Agents","Related pages"],"text":"Buildspawn can send your game build to Steam, check that Steam has it, and set it live on a beta branch, all from build steps. When to use it. Use these steps when your studio ships on Steam and you want every good build to reach a Steam branch without anyone running tools by hand. Steam delivers games with SteamPipe. You upload files to Steam, and players download only the parts that changed. Words you need. Word. What it means. App ID. The number Steam gives your game, for example 1000480. Depot. A group of files in the app. Studios often use one depot per system or language. Each depot has its own number. Build ID. The number Steam gives to one upload. Branch. A named channel players can opt into, such as nightly. Players do not see a build until you set it live on a branch. SteamCMD. Valve's command-line tool that does the upload. The steps run it for you on the agent. Build account. The Steam account the upload signs in with. You store it as a Steam build account credential. The steps. All Steam steps are in the Upload and deploy group of the step picker. Install SteamCMD. Download SteamCMD on an agent that does not have it. Steam upload. Upload depots to Steam, and optionally set the build live on a branch. Steam: DRM wrap. Wrap a program with the Steam DRM wrapper on its own. Steam: check build. Wait for the build to show on Steam and check that a branch exists. Steam: set branch live. Set a build live on a branch after your tests pass. Steam: workshop item. Create or update a Steam Workshop item. The account used by the steps that run SteamCMD is described on Steam build account. How the pieces fit. Add a Steam build account credential and sign in once. Add Install SteamCMD as the first step, unless your agents already have SteamCMD. Build your game with an earlier step, for example Unity or Unreal Engine. Add Steam upload. It uploads the files and gives later steps the BUILD_ID. Optionally add Steam: check build and Steam: set branch live after your tests. These two call Steam's Web API and need a Web API key, not SteamCMD. Two kinds of secret. The Steam build account (user name, password and Steam Guard data) is used by the steps that run SteamCMD: Steam upload, DRM wrap and Workshop item. The Steam Web API key is a Secret text credential. Check build and Set branch live use it. Neither is ever written into a script or shown in the log. What Buildspawn will not do. A build cannot set the default branch live. The names default and public are refused. Set the default branch live on the Builds page in Steamworks. A build cannot create a branch. Make the branch in Steamworks first. If Steam wants the account holder to confirm a change on the Steam Mobile app, the step stops with a message. It never waits for a phone. Permission to set a branch live. Setting a build live has its own permission, Steam / Set branch live , on the build configuration. The person who starts a build that sets a branch live needs it. Without it the step stops before anything is sent to Steam. Agents. The SteamCMD steps need steamcmd on the agent. Buildspawn reads it from the agent's tool report and holds the build back from agents that lack it. Steam upload works on Windows, Linux and macOS, and a Windows depot can be uploaded from a Linux agent, because an upload only copies files. The agent needs a connection to Steam. Related pages. Build Unity from Perforce and upload to a Steam branch. Unity and Unreal Engine. Perforce."},
{"title":"Steam: set branch live","href":"game-builds/steam-set-live","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","Who may use it","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Set an uploaded build live on a Steam beta branch, after your tests have passed. When to use it. Use it when you do not want to publish at upload time. Upload with Steam upload and leave Set live on branch empty, run your tests, and then use this step. It needs no SteamCMD. It calls Steam's Web API with your Web API key. How to set it up. Add a Secret text credential that holds your publisher Steam Web API key. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Build steps › Add build step › Steam: set branch live. Choose the Steam Web API key. Type the App ID and the Branch. Leave Build ID on %BUILD_ID%. Click Save. The Steam: set branch live step. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Steam Web API key. A Secret text credential. It is sent from Buildspawn, not from a shell, and shown as ******** in the log. None. steam-web-key. App ID. Your app number. None. 1000480. Build ID. The build to set live. Must be a number. %BUILD_ID%. %BUILD_ID%. Branch. The beta branch. Required. default and public are refused, and the form tells you so. None. nightly. Description. Optional text for the build in Steamworks. Empty. Passed smoke tests. Call Steam from the server. Off: the call is made from the agent of the build. On: from the Buildspawn server. Off. Off. Hands on to later steps. LIVE_BUILD_ID : the build that is live on the branch. Not editable. Steam can ask the account holder to confirm a change on the Steam Mobile app. The step then stops with a message and fails. It never waits for a phone. The default branch is never set live by a build. Who may use it. Both adding this step and running a build that sets a branch live need Steam / Set branch live on the build configuration. See the overview. A worked example. A nightly job uploads build 15870342 , runs automated tests, then runs this step with branch nightly and the description Passed smoke tests. Testers who opted into nightly in Steam get the build. What you see when it runs. A line with the app, build and branch and the key as ******** , then answered …. LIVE_BUILD_ID is set for later steps. Problems and fixes. Message. What to do. The branch … cannot be set live by a build. Set it live on the Builds page in Steamworks. You used default or public. Use a beta branch. Steam wants the account holder to confirm this change in the Steam Mobile app. A job cannot confirm it. Set the build live on the Builds page in Steamworks, or confirm on the phone and run the step again. The person who started this build may not set a branch live on Steam. Give that person Steam / Set branch live. Nothing was sent to Steam. The branch is missing / The build ID … is missing or is not a number. Fill in the field, and make sure the upload step set BUILD_ID. Steam answered …: …. Steam refused the call. Check the key, the App ID and that the branch exists. Steam did not answer. The network call failed. Check that the agent (or the server) can reach Steam. Related pages. Steam upload. Steam: check build. Steam upload: overview."},
{"title":"Steam upload","href":"game-builds/steam-upload","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","Connection","App and depots","Branch and release","Options","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Upload your game's files to Steam as depots, and optionally set the new build live on a beta branch. When to use it. Use it at the end of a game build, after the files you want to ship are on the agent. It runs SteamCMD with the Steam build account , writes the Steam build scripts for you from the form (or runs your own), and gives later steps the BUILD_ID. How to set it up. Make sure the agent has SteamCMD. Add Install SteamCMD before this step if not. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Build steps › Add build step › Steam upload. Under Connection , choose the Steam build account. The card shows the account, its Steam Guard mode and the SteamCMD found. Under App and depots , keep Scripts on Generate. Type the App ID , the Content root and add one row for each depot. Under Branch and release , type the beta branch in Set live on branch , or leave it empty to upload only. Click Save. The Steam upload step. The Scripts card at the end of the form shows the generated app build and depot scripts, so you can check them before you run. They hold no secret. Every field. Connection. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Steam build account. The credential to sign in with. Required. None. steam-build. Account, Steam Guard, SteamCMD (read only). Show the user name, the Steam Guard mode (stored session, machine login or generated codes) and where SteamCMD was found. SteamCMD program. Full path of SteamCMD. Leave empty to use the one the agent reported. Found on the agent. D:\\tools\\steamcmd\\steamcmd.exe. App and depots. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Scripts. Generate writes the Steam scripts from this form. Own VDF files runs scripts you keep in the workspace. Generate. Generate. App ID. The number of your app on Steam. Must be a number. None. 1000480. Content root. Folder with the files to upload, relative to the workspace. Builds/Win64. Depots. One row per depot. At least one. See the next table. One empty row. App build script (own mode). Path of your own app build script in the workspace. Buildspawn runs a copy with the description, the branch and the preview switch written in. Your file is never changed. The depot scripts it names are used as they are. None. steam/app_build_1000480.vdf. Each depot row has: Field. What it does. Default. Example. Depot ID. The depot number. Must be a number. A depot listed twice is uploaded once. None. 1000481. Name for the log. A label for the build log only. Empty. Windows. Files to include › File mapping. Rows that choose files. Wildcards * and ? are allowed. Each row has Local path , Depot path (a dot means the depot root) and Include subfolders. A depot with no row uploads nothing, and the step warns. None. Local * , depot. , subfolders on. Leave out (one pattern per line). Files to skip. Empty. *.pdb. Install script. A file Steam runs on the player's machine at install, for example for redistributables. Empty. redist\\install.vdf. Content root of this depot. Overrides the app's content root for this depot. Same as the app. Builds/Win64. File properties. Rows of File and Kind : Player configuration (the player may change the file) or Versioned configuration (Steam keeps the player's changes between versions). None; kind defaults to Player configuration. settings.ini. Branch and release. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Set live on branch. Beta branch to set the build live on as part of the upload. Empty means upload only. default and public are refused. The branch must exist in Steamworks. Empty (upload only). nightly. Build description. Text shown in the build list in Steamworks. Quotes and line breaks are removed. You can use parameters. Empty. %build.number% CL%build.vcs.number%. Preview build. Writes the logs and the file list, uploads nothing. Steam gives no build ID. Off. Off. Upload to a local content server. Sends the build to a local content server instead of Steam. Shows Folder of the content server. The branch named local must exist in Steamworks and have a"},
{"title":"Steam: workshop item","href":"game-builds/steam-workshop","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Create or update a Steam Workshop item from a folder of files. When to use it. Use it when you publish mods or content packs to the Steam Workshop from a build. Only the fields you fill in are changed, so you can update the files and leave the title alone. Valve calls this route of SteamCMD a route for testing. A studio that uploads Workshop items often should look at the tools Valve made for it. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Build steps › Add build step › Steam: workshop item. Choose the Steam build account. Type the App ID. For a new item leave Item ID empty and fill in Content folder. Fill in the other fields you want to set. Click Save. The Steam: workshop item step. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Steam build account. The credential to sign in with. It must be allowed to publish to the app. None. steam-build. SteamCMD program. Path of SteamCMD. Found on the agent. D:\\tools\\steamcmd\\steamcmd.exe. App ID. Your app number. None. 1000480. Item ID. The Workshop item to update. Empty creates a new item. Must be a number. Empty (new item). 2841190077. Content folder. Folder with the item's files. A new item needs it. Empty. Workshop/PackA. Preview image. Picture shown on the item's page. Empty. Workshop/preview.png. Visibility. Leave as it is , Public , Friends only , Private or Unlisted. Leave as it is. Private. Title. Item title. Empty. Skyforge Starter Pack. Description. Item description. Empty. New skins for the hangar. Change note. Note for this update. Empty. Build %build.number%. Stop after (minutes). Stops the step after this time. 120. 60. Hands on to later steps. ITEM_ID : the item's ID, also after it was created. Not editable. A worked example. Publish a content pack for Skyforge as a private item to test it. Set Content folder to Workshop/PackA , Visibility to Private , Title to Skyforge Starter Pack and leave Item ID empty. Later builds set Item ID to the number from the first run and only the content folder changes. What you see when it runs. The Steam sign-in lines, then SteamCMD output. Step output ITEM_ID = …. Copy that number into Item ID for later updates. Problems and fixes. Message. What to do. The item ID … is not a number. Type digits only, or leave it empty for a new item. A new item needs a content folder. Fill in Content folder. Steam did not report the ID of the item. Read the SteamCMD output above it. Check that the account may publish to the app. Steam asked for a Steam Guard code. Sign in again on the credential. Related pages. Steam build account. Steam upload: overview."},
{"title":"UGS metadata server badge","href":"game-builds/ugs-badge","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages","Unreal Engine step","Perforce"],"text":"A post-build action that shows the result of a build as a badge next to a changelist in UnrealGameSync (UGS). When to use it. Use it when your team works with Unreal Engine and Perforce, and developers use UnrealGameSync to pick a good changelist to sync. A badge such as Editor or Win64 shows next to each changelist: Starting while the build runs, then Success , Failure or Skipped. The metadata server is the small UGS service that stores these badges. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Post-build actions. Click Add post-build action and choose UGS metadata server badge. Fill in the Server URL , the Badge name and the Project. Click Test connection. It says Connected. The metadata server uses API version N. Click Save. The UGS metadata server badge action. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Server URL. The metadata server. You can use build parameters in it. Empty (required). http://ugs.studio.example:3000. Badge. The badge name shown in UGS. Empty (required). Editor. Project. The depot path of the project folder, not the.uproject file. Empty (required). //UE5/Main/Games/Skyforge. Credentials. Optional. A Secret text credential sent as a bearer token. UGS metadata servers usually run in a closed network and need none. None. ugs-token. Button Test connection. Checks the server and the credentials. -. -. A worked example. The Windows Editor build configuration syncs //UE5/Main from Perforce and builds the editor. The post-build action uses Server URL http://ugs.studio.example:3000 , Badge Editor and Project //UE5/Main/Games/Skyforge. In UnrealGameSync, each changelist the build covers gets an Editor badge. What you see when it runs. When the build starts, the badge is set to Starting. The log says Badge Editor set to Starting for change 15204. When it ends, the badge becomes Success (the build is successful or unstable), Failure , or Skipped (the build was stopped). The badge links back to the build page. One badge is posted for each Perforce changelist the build used. For badges that come from a BuildGraph script, use Post badges on the Unreal Engine step instead. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. This build has no Perforce change, so no badge is posted. The badge only works for Perforce builds. Add a Perforce root. The UGS badge needs a server URL, a badge name and a project. Fill in all three fields. Could not post the badge. Read the reason after it. Check the address with Test connection. The credentials. were not found. Pick an existing credential, or clear the field. Related pages. Unreal Engine step. BuildGraph badges and the other commands. Perforce. Roots and changelists."},
{"title":"Unity build settings","href":"game-builds/unity-license","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","Unity license","Unity installation","Asset cache","How activation and return work","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages","Unity step","Use a secret"],"text":"A build feature that activates a Unity license before your Unity steps, returns it afterwards, and sets the asset cache server. When to use it. Use it when your build agents do not already have a signed-in, licensed Unity Editor. A Unity Plus or Pro license is tied to a serial number and a Unity account. Buildspawn activates it, builds, and gives the seat back so another agent can use it. A Unity Personal license uses a license file. You can also use the feature only to point Unity at an asset cache server, which makes imports faster. How to set it up. First add the secrets as credentials. The feature never takes a typed password. For a Plus or Pro license, add a Secret text credential that holds the serial number, and a Username with password credential for the Unity account. For a Personal license, add a Secret file (or Secret text ) credential that holds the.ulf file, up to 100 KB. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Build features. Click Add build feature and choose Unity build settings. There is one per build configuration. Choose the License , the License scope and pick the credentials. Click Save. The Unity build settings build feature. Every field. Unity license. Field. What it does. Default. Example. License. None leaves licensing to the agent. Unity Plus or Pro license uses a serial number and a Unity account. Unity Personal license uses a license file. None. Unity Plus or Pro license. License scope. Build step : activate and return for every Unity step in the build configuration. Build configuration : activate once for the whole build and return at the end. Build step. Build configuration. Serial number. A Secret text credential that holds the serial number. Shown for a Plus or Pro license. None. unity-serial. Unity account. A Username with password credential for the Unity account. Used to activate and to return a Plus or Pro license. None. unity-build-account. License file. A Secret file or Secret text credential that holds the.ulf file. It can only be used on the machine that the matching.alf file came from. Shown for a Personal license. None. unity-personal-ulf. Unity installation. These say which Editor the license commands use. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Detection mode. Auto picks an Editor the agent found. Manual uses the folder below. Auto, or Manual when a folder is set. Auto. Unity version. Auto mode. Such as 2022.3. Empty takes the latest Editor on the agent. Unity steps of the build configuration use it when they name none. Empty. 6000.0. Unity folder. Manual mode. The folder that holds the Unity Editor. Empty. C:\\Program Files\\Unity\\Hub\\Editor\\6000.0.23f1. Asset cache. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Cache server address. A Unity Accelerator or cache server, as host:port. If the project's asset pipeline is version 1 or unknown, Unity gets -CacheServerIPAddress. If it is version 2, Unity gets -EnableCacheServer and -cacheServerEndpoint. The log says which one was used. Empty. unity-cache.studio.example:10080. How activation and return work. Plus or Pro, scope Build step: before each Unity step, Buildspawn runs the Editor with -serial , -username and -password to activate. After the step, it runs the Editor with -returnlicense. It returns the license even when the step fails or is stopped. Plus or Pro, scope Build configuration: it activates once before the first step and returns once before the build ends. Personal: the.ulf content is written to a temporary file, the Editor runs with -manualLicenseFile , and the file is deleted afterwards. A Personal license is never returned. The serial number, the password and the license file content are hidden in the whole build log, not only in the license steps. While the license commands run, other programs on the agent can see their arguments. Use an agent that only your team can log on to. A worked example. Skyforge has two Windows agents and one Unity Pro seat. The build configuration Windows Build has a Unity step to run tests "},
{"title":"Unity step","href":"game-builds/unity","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","Unity project","Unity tests","Unity installation","Unity log and arguments","The Unity Editor tool","The agent's Unity tab","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages","Unity build settings","Build a Unity project from Git","Unity from Perforce to Steam","Runners"],"text":"Build, test or run a method of a Unity project with the Unity Editor in batch mode, and read the Unity log in clear blocks. When to use it. Use the Unity step when your game is a Unity project and you want Buildspawn to make a player (a Windows, Linux, Mac, Android or console build), run the project's tests, or run one of your own build methods. The step finds the Unity Editor on the agent for you. Batch mode means the Editor runs without windows, so it works on a build machine. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Build steps. Click Add build step , type Unity in Filter step types and click the Unity tile. Give the step a name, for example Build Windows player. Under Unity project , type the Project path if the project is not at the top of the workspace. Say what to run: an Execute method (your own build method), a Standalone player with a Player output path , a Build profile , or Run tests from the project. Under Unity installation , leave Detection mode on Auto and type the Unity version , or choose Manual and name the folder. Click Save. The Unity step. The settings every step has (step name, working directory, environment variables, when the step runs, and so on) are the same for all steps. They are explained on Runners. Every field. Unity project. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Project path. The Unity project folder, relative to the working directory. Passed to Unity as -projectPath. Empty: the working directory. Mobile. Execute method. A static method to run once the project is loaded. Passed as -executeMethod. The class must be in an Editor folder. The box suggests the static methods found in Assets/Editor of the last workspace. Empty. BuildScript.BuildWindows. Build profile. The project path of a Unity Build Profile asset. Passed as -activeBuildProfile , and used instead of Build target. Needs Unity 6 (6000.x) or later. The path must end in.asset. Before the build, a first quiet run loads the project with the profile. Empty. Assets/Settings/Build Profiles/Android-Release.asset. Build target. The active build target before the project loads. Passed as -buildTarget. Not used when a build profile is set. The box suggests the names Unity knows: Android, EmbeddedLinux, GameCoreXboxOne, GameCoreXboxSeries, iOS, Linux64, LinuxHeadlessSimulation, OSXUniversal, PS4, PS5, QNX, Standalone, StandaloneLinux64, StandaloneOSX, StandaloneWindows, StandaloneWindows64, Switch, tvOS, WebGL, WindowsStoreApps and XboxOne. Empty. StandaloneWindows64. Standalone player. Builds a player with the matching switch, for example -buildWindows64Player. Choices: Linux 32-bit, Linux 64-bit, Linux 32-bit and 64-bit, Mac OSX 32-bit, Mac OSX 64-bit, Mac OSX 32-bit and 64-bit, Windows 32-bit, Windows 64-bit. Needs a Player output path. Select (none). Windows 64-bit. Player output path. Where the player is written. A relative path starts at the working directory. With a build profile, it is passed to -build. Empty. Builds/Win64/Skyforge.exe. Editor options. Three switches. Do not initialize the graphics device passes -nographics. Do not display the error dialog when a standalone player crashes passes -silent-crashes. Do not quit the Unity Editor after other commands have finished leaves out -quit , so the step runs until your project's code quits the Editor. All off. First one on. Unity tests. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Run tests from the project. Runs the Unity Test Framework. Passed as -runEditorTests , or as -runTests with the platform below. -quit is left out. The results are converted and recorded as test results of the build. Off. On. Test platform. Default runs the Editor tests. Edit mode and Play mode run that mode. All modes in sequence runs Edit mode and then Play mode as two Unity runs. Default. Play mode. Test categories. One category per line, or separated by commas or semicolons. Passed as -editorTestsCategories. Empty. Smoke. Test names. One test name per line, or separated by commas or se"},
{"title":"Unreal Engine step","href":"game-builds/unreal","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","Engine (all commands)","Command: BuildCookRun","Command: BuildGraph","Command: Automation tests","Command: Custom automation command","Command: Commandlet","How the engine is found","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages","UGS metadata server badge","Build an Unreal Engine game from Perforce","Perforce","Epic Games Store"],"text":"Compile, cook, package and test an Unreal Engine game: BuildCookRun, BuildGraph on one machine or many, automation tests, commandlets and custom automation commands. When to use it. Use it when your game is an Unreal Engine project. One step type covers the five jobs a build machine does: make a packaged game, run a build graph script, run automation tests, run a commandlet (a small program the editor runs, such as a content check), and run any other automation command. The step finds the engine on the agent, runs the Unreal tools without windows or prompts, and picks errors and warnings out of the engine log. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Build steps. Click Add build step , type Unreal in Filter step types and click the Unreal Engine tile. Give the step a name, for example Build, cook and package. Choose the Engine detection mode. In Automatic , type the engine version in Identifier. In Manual , type the engine folder in Root dir. Choose the Command. The fields below it change with your choice. Fill in the fields for that command (see the tables below). Click Save. The Unreal Engine step. Settings every step has (step name, working directory, environment variables, when the step runs) are on Runners. Every field. Engine (all commands). Field. What it does. Default. Example. Engine detection mode. Automatic picks an engine the agent found. Manual uses a folder you name. Automatic. Automatic. Identifier. Automatic mode. A version such as 5.2 or 5.3.4 for engines installed from the Epic launcher, or the custom name of a source build. The step runs only on agents that report a matching engine. If several match, the latest one is used. Empty. 5.4. Root dir. Manual mode. The Unreal Engine root folder, relative to the workspace or absolute. Empty. D:\\UE\\UE_5.4. Command. What the step runs: BuildCookRun , BuildGraph , Automation tests , Custom automation command or Commandlet. BuildCookRun. BuildCookRun. Additional arguments. Added after the command. The default makes the build tools and the editor run unattended on a build machine. The step also sets UE_LOG_JSON_TO_STDOUT=1 so errors and warnings are found in the log. -utf8output -buildmachine -unattended -noP4 -nosplash -stdout -NoCodeSign. Add -clean. Command: BuildCookRun. Builds the game, cooks the content (turns assets into the format of the platform), stages the files, packages and archives. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Project. Path to the.uproject file, relative to the workspace or absolute. The button Detect projects lists the.uproject files in the top folder of the last workspace and in the folders a.uprojectdirs file lists. Empty (required). Skyforge.uproject. Build configuration. What kind of build: Standalone game , Client , Server or Client and server. It decides which target fields are shown. Standalone game. Client and server. Target configurations. Standalone. Debug, DebugGame, Development, Test or Shipping, joined with +. Tick the boxes or type them. Development. Development+Shipping. Target platforms. Standalone. Platform names joined with + : Android, HoloLens, IOS, Linux, LinuxArm64, Mac, TVOS, Win64. Names not on the list are accepted. Win64. Win64+Linux. Client target configurations , Client target platforms. Same, for the client. Shown for Client, and Client and server. Development, Win64. Shipping , Win64. Server target configurations , Server target platforms. Same, for the server. Shown for Server, and Client and server. Development, Linux. Development , Linux. Steps. Four switches. Cook : perform cooking (off passes -skipcook ). Stage : put the executables and content in a stage directory (off passes -skipstage ). Package : package for the target platform. Archive : place the build in an archive directory. All on. All on. Under Advanced : Field. What it does. Default. Example. Target. Name the build target when the project has several of one type. Join several with +. Empty. SkyforgeGame. Maps to cook. Map n"},
{"title":"First start and sign-in","href":"get-started/first-start","headings":["Open the server in your browser","Create the admin user","Sign in","What to do next"],"text":"Open Buildspawn for the first time, create the admin user and sign in. Open the server in your browser. When the server runs, open its address in a web browser. On the server itself that is: http://localhost:8080/. From another computer, use the server's name or IP address instead of localhost , for example http://build-server:8080/. In the terminal program 's live monitor, press b to open the page. In the Windows app , use the link shown on the Run here tab. 8080 is the default port. If you chose another port during setup, use that one. You can change it later with buildspawn set port=8090 --apply. Create the admin user. On the very first start the page asks for the initial admin password. It is a one-time password that proves you own the server. Find the password. Any of these shows it: the Admin password step of the terminal program's launch flow ( v shows it, c copies it); the command buildspawn admin-password (add --copy to put it on the clipboard; on the Linux packages, run it with sudo ); the file secrets/initialAdminPassword inside the server's home folder. Paste the password into the page and continue. Create your own administrator account: user name, password, full name and e-mail address. Confirm the server's address. Buildspawn uses it in links, for example in e-mails. When setup is done, the one-time password stops working and the file is removed. From now on you sign in with your own account. Create more users under Settings › Users , and give them rights under Settings › Security. See Users, groups and permissions. Sign in. Next time you open Buildspawn, it shows the sign-in page: the logo on the left and the form on the right. It follows the theme you chose, or your system's light or dark setting when you have not chosen one. The sign-in page. Type your Username and Password. Tick Keep me signed in if you use your own computer, so you don't have to sign in every day. Click Sign in. Buildspawn opens the Projects page. To sign out, open your account menu (the round button with your initial, top right) and choose Sign out. To change your password or create an API token for scripts, open the account menu and choose Security , or go to http://<server>:8080/me/security. Keep the admin account for administration. Give everyday users their own accounts with fewer rights, so one shared password never unlocks everything. What to do next. Open Settings and check System. The server address you confirmed is used in links and e-mails. If you serve Buildspawn under a path such as https://build.example.com/buildspawn/ , set the address to match and start the server with --prefix=/buildspawn. If your company uses its own certificate authority, add its root under Settings &rsaquo; Security &rsaquo; Trusted certificates. The certificates are kept when you upgrade. Add a build machine: Agents &rsaquo; Install agents. See Install an agent. For a first try you can use the built-in agent, which runs small builds on the server itself. Create your first project and build configuration with New Item. See Create a build configuration. Press Run and watch the build. See The Run dialog and The build page. User names may contain dots, for example sam.lee."},
{"title":"Install the server","href":"get-started/install","headings":["Before you start","Downloads","Windows app and setup","Upgrade and remove","Silent install","Portable zip","Linux","As a systemd service","Debian, Ubuntu and RHEL packages","macOS","The server alone (.war)","Check the download","Upgrade from an older version","Next"],"text":"Choose the download for your system and get the server running. Before you start. Get the files from the Buildspawn 1.9.3 release page. Every download includes Java 21 (Eclipse Temurin 21.0.13.1). You don't need to install Java or anything else. Systems: Windows x64, Linux x64 and ARM (any glibc Linux, not Alpine), and macOS on Apple silicon or Intel. Web port: 8080 by default. You can change it in the settings at any time. Memory: on Windows you set the Java memory in the app. On Linux and macOS the default is a quarter of the computer's memory, between 2 GB and 16 GB. Disk: about 620 MB for the program, plus room for build history and artifacts in the data folder. Buildspawn keeps everything it needs in one data folder , also called the home: jobs, build history and settings. Back it up, and keep it when you upgrade. Each download has a.sha256 file next to it. Use it to check that the download is complete before you install. Downloads. Pick one file for your system. All files are for version 1.9.3 (build 2.589-buildspawn.21). System. File. Size. What it is. Windows. buildspawn-2.589-buildspawn.21-setup.exe. 513 MB. Setup: installs to Program Files, adds Start menu shortcuts and can install the Windows service. Windows. buildspawn-2.589-buildspawn.21-windows-x64.zip. 513 MB. Portable: unzip and run; everything stays in the folder. Linux x64. buildspawn-2.589-buildspawn.21-linux-x64.tar.gz. 516 MB. Portable folder for any glibc Linux. Linux ARM. buildspawn-2.589-buildspawn.21-linux-aarch64.tar.gz. 515 MB. The same for ARM servers. Debian, Ubuntu. buildspawn_2.589-buildspawn.21_amd64.deb. 517 MB. Package: installs to /opt/buildspawn and starts the service. RHEL, Rocky, Fedora. buildspawn-2.589-buildspawn.21.x86_64.rpm. 517 MB. The same as an rpm. macOS Apple silicon. buildspawn-2.589-buildspawn.21-macos-aarch64.tar.gz. 512 MB. Portable folder (M1 and later). macOS Intel. buildspawn-2.589-buildspawn.21-macos-x64.tar.gz. 506 MB. Portable folder (Intel Macs). Any system with Java. buildspawn-2.589-buildspawn.21.war. 463 MB. The server alone, for people who run their own Java. Windows app and setup. Setup is the easiest way to install Buildspawn on a Windows server or build machine. Run buildspawn-2.589-buildspawn.21-setup.exe. Follow the steps. Setup installs the program to C:\\Program Files\\Buildspawn , adds Start menu shortcuts and an entry in Apps & features. Choose whether to install the Windows service. The service starts with Windows and restarts Buildspawn if it stops. You can also install it later. Open Buildspawn from the Start menu. This is the Windows app: it starts and stops the server and shows its log. See The Windows app. Open http://localhost:8080/ in your browser and sign in. See First start and sign-in. With setup, the settings file is C:\\ProgramData\\Buildspawn\\buildspawn.cfg. Data and logs are in C:\\ProgramData\\Buildspawn. Setup also puts the program's bin folder on the PATH, so you can type buildspawn in any new Windows Terminal to open the terminal program. Upgrade and remove. Upgrade: run the newer setup. It stops the service, replaces the program files, keeps your settings and data, and starts the service again. Remove: use Apps & features, or \"Uninstall Buildspawn\" in the Start menu. It asks before it deletes data. The default keeps it. Silent install. To install without questions, for example from a script: buildspawn-2.589-buildspawn.21-setup.exe /S /D=D:\\Buildspawn /DATA=D:\\BuildspawnData /PORT=8080 /SERVICE=1. /D is the program folder, /DATA the data folder, /PORT the web port and /SERVICE 1 or 0 to install the service or not. Run setup.exe /? to list every switch. Portable zip. The zip needs no installation and no administrator rights. It is good for a try-out, or for a server you want to keep in one folder. Unzip buildspawn-2.589-buildspawn.21-windows-x64.zip , for example to D:\\Buildspawn. Open Buildspawn.exe in that folder and click Start. Open http://localhost:8080/ in your browser. The settings ( b"},
{"title":"The terminal program","href":"get-started/terminal-program","headings":["What it is","Start it","The launch flow","The live monitor","The seven views","The Queue view","The Agents view","Keys","Actions need an API token","Commands for scripts","Good to know","The agent's terminal screen"],"text":"Start the server and watch it live from a terminal. What it is. buildspawn is a terminal program that comes with every download. It is the same program on Windows, Linux and macOS. It sets up and starts the server, and then shows a live monitor of builds, the queue and agents. Run buildspawn with nothing after it, and it opens one of two screens: the launch flow , on a first run, when this copy has no settings file and no service yet; the live monitor , every other time. Start it. Download. How to start it. Windows setup. Open a new Windows Terminal and type buildspawn. Setup put it on the PATH. Windows zip. In the program folder, type.\\bin\\buildspawn.exe. Linux and macOS folders. In the folder, type bin/buildspawn. Linux packages. Type buildspawn. The package put it on the PATH. On Windows, in the program folder, type bin\\buildspawn , not just buildspawn. Windows doesn't tell upper and lower case apart, so plain buildspawn there opens Buildspawn.exe , the app. The launch flow. The launch flow has five steps. Enter goes forward and Esc goes back. Home: the folder for jobs, build history and settings. Port & memory: the web port (8080 by default), whether to listen on the network or this computer only, and the Java memory. Run as: pick one. Service (recommended): starts at boot, runs without anyone signed in and restarts after a crash. It needs administrator rights once to install it. On macOS it is a LaunchAgent and needs none. Ticks under it: start at boot, open the firewall (Windows only), start now. Portable: runs in this window and stops when you close it. No administrator rights. Good for a try-out. Admin password: the first-login password. Press v to show it and c to copy it. Start: a summary. Then it starts the server and switches to the live monitor. You can save your answers as a preset and use them again on another machine: buildspawn launch --preset NAME. To open the launch flow again on a copy that is already set up, run buildspawn launch. The live monitor. The monitor reads the running server and refreshes on its own, every 2 seconds by default. The top line shows the server's state and address. The second line lists the seven views; press a number to switch. Buildspawn 1.9.3 · ● Serving http://127.0.0.1:8090 · 1 of 6 executors busy · 1 queued LIVE [1 Overview] 2 Builds 1 3 Queue 1 4 Agents 4/5 5 Logs 6 Service 7 Settings ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── ┌─ Server ─────────────────────────┐┌─ Builds running ─────────────────┐┌─ Queue ──────────────────────────┐ │ ● Running ││ 1 of 6 executors ││ 1 waiting · oldest 6:21 │ │ 2.589-buildspawn.21 ││ ████ ░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░ 16% ││ █ last 30 min │ └──────────────────────────────────┘└──────────────────────────────────┘└──────────────────────────────────┘ ┌─ Builds / hour ──────────────────┐┌─ Success rate ───────────────────┐┌─ Agents ─────────────────────────┐ │ 12 now · peak 12 ││ 72 % 8 of 11 · 24 h ││ 4 online · 1 offline │ │ ▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁█ 24 h ││ ███████████████████ ░░░░░░░ 72% ││ 6 executors · 1 busy │ └──────────────────────────────────┘└──────────────────────────────────┘└──────────────────────────────────┘ ┌─ JVM heap ───────────────────────┐┌─ CPU ────────────────────────────┐┌─ Disk free · home ───────────────┐ │ not reported ││ not reported ││ 354 GB free │ │ ││ ││ │ └──────────────────────────────────┘└──────────────────────────────────┘└──────────────────────────────────┘ ┌─ Recent builds ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── 9 of 12 · 24 h ──┐ │ Start Job # Result Time Agent Cause │ │ ▸ 05:40 Game/PS5 Build #2 ▶ ██████ 99% 2:57 ps5-devkit-01 docs │ │ 05:39 Game/Windows Build #4 ✓ success 0:09 win-build-01 docs │ │ 05:39 Game/Windows Build #3 ✗ failed 0:05 win-build-01 docs │ │ 05:39 Game/Windows Build #2 ✓ success 0:09 win-build-01 docs │ │ 05:39 Game/PS5 Build #1 ✓ success 0:07 ps5-devkit-01 docs │ │ 05:39 Game/Windows Build "},
{"title":"A tour of the interface","href":"get-started/tour","headings":["The top bar","The sidebar","Make the sidebar wider or narrower","Pages and tabs","Words you will see","Themes","Where to go next","Run a build","Read a build","See trends"],"text":"Every Buildspawn page has the same three parts: the top bar, the sidebar and the page itself. Learn them once and you can find anything. The Projects page: top bar, sidebar and page. The top bar. The dark bar at the top is always there. From left to right: Item. What it does. Logo. Takes you back to the start page. Dashboard. Widgets with the builds, failures, queue and agents your team should see first. An administrator arranges it. See The Dashboard. Projects. All projects and build configurations, with their latest results. See The project directory. History. Every build that ran, newest first, with a timeline by agent and the filters All, Failed, Running and Mine. See History. Queue. Builds that wait for a free agent. The number shows how many wait now. See The queue. Agents. The build machines, their pools and live telemetry. A red number counts agents that are offline or disconnected. Settings. Server administration: users, security, tools, plugins and more. A red number counts notices that need an administrator, for example a certificate that is about to expire. Search workspace. Finds projects, build configurations, builds, agents and settings pages by name. Press Ctrl + K to jump to it from anywhere. Actions menu. The three-line button beside the theme switch holds extra actions for the page you are on. Light / dark switch. Switches between the light and the dark theme. Your choice is saved with your account. Account menu. The round button with your initial. It opens Profile , My changes , Account (password and API tokens), Appearance and Sign out. If an administrator allows personal machines and you may use them, My machines is here too. The sidebar. The panel on the left changes with the page you are on. On the Projects, Dashboard and build pages it shows the project tree. Click the arrow beside a project to open it and see what is inside. The coloured dot after each name is its latest result: green passed, red failed, blue running. The number counts the build configurations inside. Type in Search by name to filter the tree. The three buttons beside the title: New Item (+) creates a project or build configuration, Reorder mode lets administrators drag items into their own order, and the last one hides the panel. Everyone sees the order administrators choose. Administrators can also add named separators to group items. Make the sidebar wider or narrower. Drag the right edge of the sidebar. The width can go from 200 pixels to half of the window. Your width is remembered in your browser. Double-click the edge to reset it. With the keyboard, press Tab until the edge has focus, then use the arrow keys (hold Shift for bigger steps). On the Agents page the same panel lists the agent pools, and on the Settings page it lists the settings sections. The Agents page. The sidebar lists the pools; the table shows each agent live. Pages and tabs. Most pages follow the same pattern: A breadcrumb at the top shows the path, for example Dashboard &rsaquo; Game &rsaquo; Windows Build. Click any part to go up. The small arrow beside a part opens a menu of its neighbours. The title with the status chips and the main actions on the right, such as Run. Tabs for the parts of the page. More holds the tabs that don't fit. A build configuration always starts with the same five tabs, whatever else it has: Overview , Change Log , History , Statistics and Settings. Tabs from plugins, such as Perforce pages, come after them. A build configuration: the five fixed tabs first, then the others. Words you will see. Word. Meaning. Project. A folder that groups build configurations and other projects, and passes its settings down. Build configuration. One kind of build, with its steps, triggers and settings. Build. One run of a build configuration, with a number such as #7. Themes. Click the light / dark switch in the top bar to change the theme. More options are under Settings &rsaquo; Appearance. See Appearance and themes. Where to go next. Run a build"},
{"title":"What is Buildspawn","href":"get-started/what-is-buildspawn","headings":["What it does","The main ideas","Made for game studios","The visual editor","What you need","The parts of this guide","Get started","Running builds","Build results"],"text":"Buildspawn is a build server. It checks out your code, runs your build steps on your own machines and shows you what happened. What it does. A build server takes the boring, repeatable work off your team. When someone submits a change, Buildspawn: notices the change in Git or Perforce; picks a free machine (an agent ) that can build it; runs your build steps there: compile, cook, test, package, upload; keeps the result: the log, the test results and the files you want to keep ( artifacts ); shows everyone on the team whether the build passed or failed, and why. The Projects page of a game studio's server. The main ideas. Word. What it means. Project. A folder that groups build configurations and other projects, for example Game or Engine. Settings you put on a project, such as parameters or version control connections, pass down to everything inside it. Build configuration. One kind of build, for example Game / Windows Build. It says where the code comes from, which steps to run, when to start and what to keep. A template is a build configuration others can start from. Build. One run of a build configuration, with a number (#1, #2 …), a result, a log and its changes. Step. One thing a build does: a command line, a PowerShell script, a Unity or Unreal Engine build, a test run, a store upload and so on. Agent. A machine that runs builds. Each agent has one or more executors, so it can run that many builds at once. Pool. A group of agents, for example Windows, Linux or Consoles. You give pools to projects, so builds land on the right machines. Parameter. A named value a build can use, such as CONFIG=Shipping. Secrets are parameters whose value is hidden everywhere. Queue. Where a build waits until an agent that can run it is free. Made for game studios. Buildspawn works for any software, but it has extra help for games: Perforce as a first-class version control system: streams, shelves, review status, a workbench page and a Perforce operations build step, next to Git. Unity and Unreal Engine build steps, with the right fields for each engine. Store uploads: build steps for Steam and the Epic Games Store. Agent telemetry , including GPU use, so you can see which build machines are busy or short of disk. Pools with platform icons for Windows, Linux, macOS and consoles, and a Pool Scheduler that moves agents between pools at set times or when urgent builds wait. Parallel blocks and Matrix builds that cook several platforms at once, and resume from the failed step so a two-hour cook does not run again. Personal builds and personal machines: test a shelf, or run a build on your own computer, before anything reaches the depot. The visual editor. You can set up the steps of a build configuration as a graph: boxes for steps, lines for the order, and a run view that draws a real build over the graph. You can also use plain forms. Both edit the same settings. What you need. One computer for the server: Windows, Linux or macOS. Java is included in every download, so you don't install it yourself. Any number of computers as agents. The agent download also includes Java. The server can run small builds itself while you try things out. A web browser on a desktop or laptop screen. You use Buildspawn through its web pages. There is also a terminal program on every system and, on Windows, a small app to start and stop the server. The parts of this guide. Get started. Install, first start, a tour of the interface. Running builds. Run, queue, re-run, resume, stop. Build results. The build page, console, charts and history. Ready to try it? Go to Install the server. It takes a few minutes."},
{"title":"The Windows app","href":"get-started/windows-app","headings":["Open the app","Run here","Settings","Windows service","The service account","When the service stops at once","From a command prompt","Certificates","About","The terminal program"],"text":"Start, stop and set up Buildspawn on Windows from one small window. Open the app. The app is Buildspawn.exe in the program folder. After setup, open Buildspawn from the Start menu. With the portable zip, open Buildspawn.exe in the folder you unzipped. The top of the window shows the version, whether this copy is installed or portable, and the state of the server on the right ( Stopped or Running ). The app has five tabs: Run here, Settings, Windows service, Certificates and About. Run here. The Run here tab. The black area shows the server's log while it runs. This tab runs Buildspawn on this computer, inside the app. Click Start. The log fills the black area as the server starts. On the very first start, the app shows the initial admin password with a Copy button. You need it once, on the first web page. See First start and sign-in. Click Open in browser to open the web interface. Click Stop to stop the server. Open logs folder opens the folder with buildspawn.log , gc.log and any heap dumps. If you close the window while the server runs, the app asks whether to stop it or keep it running in the notification area. The tray icon has Open , Open in browser , Stop and Exit. Settings. The Settings tab. The port check shows straight away whether the port is free. Here you change how the server runs: Setting. What it does. Port. The web port. 8080 by default. The app tells you if another program already uses it. Listen on. This computer only , or Network (all interfaces) so other machines and agents can connect. Memory. How much memory Java may use for the server. Data folder. Jobs, build history and settings. Logs folder. buildspawn.log , gc.log and heap dumps. Java options (Advanced). Extra options for Java. The default is the recommended set. Reset to recommended brings it back. Extra arguments (Advanced). Extra server arguments, for example --sessionTimeout=480. In the folders and Java options, {data} , {logs} and {install} (the program folder) stand for those folders. Change what you need. Click Check with Java to try the options before you rely on them. Click Save. The app writes buildspawn.cfg. Revert drops your changes. Restart Buildspawn so the new settings take effect. The app and the Windows service share one settings file, buildspawn.cfg. After setup it is in C:\\ProgramData\\Buildspawn. In the portable zip it is next to Buildspawn.exe. Windows service. The Windows service tab. Buttons with a shield ask Windows for administrator rights. The service starts with Windows and restarts Buildspawn if it stops. Use it for a build server that must keep running when nobody is signed in. Click Install. Windows asks for administrator rights. Click Start , or restart the computer. Click Open in browser to check that it runs. Remove , Start , Stop and Restart also ask for administrator rights. You don't need to open the app as administrator. Buildspawn runs either in the app or as the service on one data folder, never both at once. Stop it on the Run here tab before you start the service. The service account. The service runs as the LocalService account. It can change only the data and logs folders. If your builds need more, such as tools or files elsewhere or network shares, use another account: Open Windows Services and find Buildspawn. On the Log On tab, choose the account. Give that account Modify rights on the data and logs folders. Restart the service. When the service stops at once. If Buildspawn stops within seconds of starting, for example because a Java option names a folder that can't be created, the reason goes to the Windows Application event log (source Buildspawn ) and to logs\\buildspawn.log. From a command prompt. In the program folder, from a command prompt opened as administrator: buildspawn-service install | uninstall | start | stop | restart | status. Without administrator rights: buildspawn-service check buildspawn-service run. check checks the settings, runs Java with the options and shows the Java command. run r"},
{"title":"Add a Linux or macOS agent","href":"how-to/add-linux-mac-agent","headings":["Before you start","Steps","On Linux","On macOS","Watch it from the terminal","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Next steps"],"text":"Add a Linux server or a Mac as a build agent that runs as a service: systemd on Linux, launchd on macOS. Before you start. You are an administrator in Buildspawn, and you can use sudo on the machine. The machine can reach the server's address, for example http://buildserver:8080/. You know the machine's CPU. Run uname -m : x86_64 means x64 (Intel or AMD); aarch64 or arm64 means ARM64 (Apple silicon on a Mac). Nothing else needs to be installed: the download includes Java. Steps. Open Agents , click New Node , type a name such as linux-build-02 , choose Permanent Agent and click Create. Set Remote root directory to a full path on the machine, for example /opt/buildspawn-agent/work on Linux or /Users/build/buildspawn-agent/work on a Mac. Add Labels such as linux or macos , and click Save. Open the agent. Under Download the agent for this node , pick the CPU on the row for the machine's system: Apple silicon or Intel for macOS, x64 or ARM64 for Linux. The file name below changes to match. Click Download. Pick the CPU on the macOS or Linux row, then click Download. On Linux. Copy the zip to the machine and unzip it into /opt : sudo unzip buildspawn-agent-linux-x64-linux-build-02.zip -d /opt. To try it first, run it in the foreground. Press Ctrl + C to stop: cd /opt/buildspawn-agent./run-agent.sh. Install the systemd service: sudo./install-service.sh. The zip already holds the server address. The script writes /etc/systemd/system/buildspawn-agent.service , then enables and starts it. The service runs as the user who ran sudo ; add --user build to choose another account. On macOS. Copy the zip to the Mac and unzip it into your home folder: unzip buildspawn-agent-macos-aarch64-mac-build-01.zip -d ~. To try it first, run./run-agent.sh in ~/buildspawn-agent. Press Ctrl + C to stop. Install the launchd service. Choose one:/install-service.sh as yourself: a LaunchAgent that runs while you are logged in. Use it for builds that need the screen, such as iOS simulators. sudo./install-service.sh : a LaunchDaemon that starts at boot, with nobody logged in. Add --user build to run it as another account. The service is named com.buildspawn.agent. On both systems,./install-service.sh --print shows the systemd unit or the launchd plist and changes nothing. The zip holds this agent's secret. The install script makes the settings file readable only by the service's account. Watch it from the terminal. Every download holds a terminal program. Run it on the machine, or over SSH:/bin/buildspawn-agent-tui. Press 1 to 8 to move between Overview, Builds, Connection log, Work folders, Service, Settings, Agent log and Certificates. Press p to pause the agent and q to leave. The agent keeps running. See Agent terminal program. Check it worked. Open Agents. The agent shows Idle , with Linux or macOS under its name and live CPU, memory and disk values. On Linux, systemctl status buildspawn-agent shows active (running). On macOS, launchctl list | grep buildspawn lists com.buildspawn.agent. The log is in logs/agent.log inside the agent folder. Troubleshooting. Problem. What to do. cannot execute binary file or Exec format error. You picked the wrong CPU. Check uname -m and download the other zip. ERROR: run with sudo on Linux. Run sudo./install-service.sh. Without sudo, the script can't write the systemd unit. macOS says the Java in the folder can't be opened. The download was quarantined. Run xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine ~/buildspawn-agent and start again. The agent connects but builds fail at once. The service account can't write to Remote root directory. Give it the folder, for example sudo chown -R build /opt/buildspawn-agent. To stop the agent, run./stop-agent.sh. To remove the service, run./uninstall-service.sh (with sudo if you installed it with sudo ). Next steps. Install an agent : all downloads, self-registration and approval. Add a Windows build agent. Pools : put the agent in the pool that builds its platform. Agent service scripts and Upgrade agen"},
{"title":"Add a Windows build agent","href":"how-to/add-windows-agent","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Optional: the tray app","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Next steps"],"text":"Turn a Windows machine into a build agent that starts with Windows and takes builds on its own. Before you start. You are an administrator in Buildspawn and on the Windows machine. The machine can reach the server's address, for example http://buildserver:8080/. Nothing else needs to be installed: the download includes Java. Steps. Open Agents in the top bar and click New Node. In Node name , type the agent's name, for example ht-win-agent-01. Choose Permanent Agent and click Create. Name the agent and choose Permanent Agent. On the settings page, set Number of executors (how many builds it runs at once, for example 2 ), Remote root directory (its work folder, for example C:\\buildspawn-agent\\work ) and Labels , such as windows win64. Click Save. Open the new agent. It says Waiting for the agent to connect. Under Download the agent for this node , click Download on the Windows row. This zip already holds the server address, the agent's name and its secret. Copy the zip to the Windows machine and unzip it into C:\\. You get the folder C:\\buildspawn-agent\\. tar -xf buildspawn-agent-windows-x64-ht-win-agent-01.zip -C C:\\. To try it first, double-click run-agent.bat in that folder. The agent stays connected while the window is open. To keep it running after you sign out and across restarts, close that window, open a command prompt as administrator in C:\\buildspawn-agent and install the service: install-service.bat. The zip already holds the server address, so no options are needed. The service is called Buildspawn Agent. It starts at once and at every boot, and restarts itself if it stops. To see what the service will run before you install it, add --print. It shows the service definition and changes nothing: <service> <id>buildspawn-agent</id> <name>Buildspawn Agent</name> <executable>C:\\buildspawn-agent\\java\\bin\\java.exe</executable> <startmode>Automatic</startmode> <onfailure action=\"restart\" delay=\"10 sec\"/>. <logpath>%BASE%\\.\\logs</logpath> </service>. The zip holds this agent's secret. Give it only to the person who installs the agent. When the service is installed, only the system account and administrators can read the settings file. Optional: the tray app. Open the bin folder and start Buildspawn Agent.exe. An icon appears next to the clock: green when idle, blue while it builds, amber when paused and red when offline. Right-click it for Pause , Open in browser and Show connection log. In its Settings tab, tick Open this app when I sign in to Windows and click Save. See Windows tray app. The service runs as the system account. The tray app asks for administrator permission ( Open as administrator ) the first time it must control it. Check it worked. Open Agents. The new agent's row shows Idle and its free executors, with live CPU, memory and disk values. The agent's own page shows Online instead of Never connected. On the machine, Services ( services.msc ) lists Buildspawn Agent as Running. The new agent is online and sends telemetry. Troubleshooting. Problem. What to do. install-service.bat says run this from an administrator prompt. Right-click Command Prompt , choose Run as administrator , go to C:\\buildspawn-agent and run it again. The agent stays Never connected. Read the files in C:\\buildspawn-agent\\logs. Check that the machine can open the server's address in a browser and that no firewall blocks it. Every build on the agent fails at once with a path error. Check Remote root directory on the agent's Configure tab. It must be a full folder path, such as C:\\buildspawn-agent\\work. The service does not start and logs is empty. Check that bin\buildspawn-agent-service.xml is there, then run install-service.bat again. It also repairs a service whose folder was moved. The tray app says The agent isn't running. Read logs un-agent.log (what the agent printed when the app started it) and logs ray.log (errors of the app). The agent is online but runs no builds. An agent runs only builds of the projects in its pool, and new agents"},
{"title":"Sign in with Entra ID","href":"how-to/admin-sign-in-entra-id","headings":["Before you start","Steps","In the Entra admin centre","In Buildspawn","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Next steps"],"text":"Let your studio sign in to Buildspawn with Microsoft work accounts, and give the Game Dev group its rights, from the first click to the first sign-in. Before you start. You are a system administrator of Buildspawn, and you can reach the Entra admin centre with a role that may register applications (for example Application Administrator ). Someone with the Global Administrator or Privileged Role Administrator role must grant admin consent in step 9. Buildspawn URL on Settings › System is the address people really use, for example https://build.nightlight.example/. See System settings. The group Game Dev exists in Entra ID and holds the developers. Keep a second browser window open and signed in to Buildspawn while you work. Steps. In the Entra admin centre. Open entra.microsoft.com , go to Identity › Applications › App registrations and click New registration. Name it Buildspawn. Choose Accounts in this organizational directory only. Under Redirect URI choose Web and type https://build.nightlight.example/securityRealm/finishLogin. Click Register. Copy the Application (client) ID and the Directory (tenant) ID from the Overview. Open Certificates & secrets › New client secret , add it, and copy the secret Value at once. Open API permissions › Add a permission › Microsoft Graph › Application permissions. Add User.Read.All and Group.Read.All , then click Grant admin consent. In Buildspawn. Open Settings › Security. Under Security Realm choose Entra ID. Paste the Client ID , keep Client Secret and paste the Secret , paste the Tenant. Type your own work account, dana.k@nightlight.example , in Test user principal name or object id , and click Verify configuration. Wait for the green message. Under Authorization choose Entra ID Matrix-based security. In Add from Entra ID type your name, pick yourself and click Add. Open the card and tick Administer. Add the group Game Dev in the same way. Tick Read under Overall , and under Build configuration tick Read , Build , Cancel and Workspace. On Authenticated Users tick Read under Overall. Click Save. Permission cards. With Entra ID, the same cards show people and groups from your directory. Check it worked. Open a private browser window and go to the Buildspawn address. You are sent to the Microsoft page. Sign in with your work account. You come back to Buildspawn and the top bar shows your name. Open Settings. You can, because you have Administer. Ask a developer from Game Dev to sign in. They see the projects and can run builds, but Settings is missing. Troubleshooting. Problem. What to do. Microsoft says the redirect URI does not match. It must be exactly <Buildspawn URL>/securityRealm/finishLogin. Fix it in the app registration under Authentication , or tick Callback URL from request under Advanced. Verify configuration fails. Check the client ID, tenant and secret. Copy the secret Value , not its ID. You are locked out after saving. Follow the recovery steps on Security realms. The picker finds nobody. Admin consent for the Graph permissions is missing. A developer signs in but sees nothing. Add Read under Overall for the group or for Authenticated Users. Next steps. Entra ID sign-in : every field of the form. Authorization : permission cards and project-based security. Put a calendar reminder before the client secret expires. Sign-in stops on that day."},
{"title":"Trust your company’s root CA","href":"how-to/admin-trust-root-ca","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Next steps"],"text":"Make the server and all agents trust the root certificate of your company, so builds can reach your internal Perforce, Git and package servers over TLS. Before you start. You are a system administrator. Your builds fail with an error like PKIX path building failed or unable to find valid certification path when they reach an internal server. You can get the root certificate from your IT team, as a.crt ,.cer ,.pem ,.der or.p7b file, and its SHA-256 fingerprint. Never ask for or paste a private key. You can restart the server at a quiet time, and agents are version 1.9 or later. Steps. Download nightlight-root-ca.crt from your IT portal and write down the fingerprint it shows, for example 3F:9A:C1:07:…. Open Settings › Security › Trusted certificates. Click Add certificates , then Choose file… , and pick the file. Read the card. It should say Root CA. Compare the SHA-256 with the one from IT. They must match exactly. If they do not, stop and ask IT. Click Add 1 certificate. Check that Send these to agents is on. On a Windows server whose computers get the root through group policy, you can instead switch on Also trust Windows’ certificate store. Click Save if you changed a switch. Click Restart the server in the yellow bar, when no important build is running. When the server is back, look at the Agents table. Each agent goes from Waiting for restart to Up to date after its current build. Restart any agent that stays on Waiting for restart and says Restart the agent to use them. The Trusted certificates page after adding the root certificate. You can do the same from a terminal: buildspawn certs add nightlight-root-ca.crt , then restart. See Trusted certificates. Check it worked. The yellow Restart needed bar is gone. The table Added to this server shows Nightlight Root CA and its expiry date. Run a build configuration that talks to the internal server. The console shows no PKIX error. Every agent says Up to date , or Offline (it will get the certificates when it connects). Troubleshooting. Problem. What to do. The error is still there on the server. Restart the server. Java reads the list only when it starts. The error is still there on one agent. Look at its state. Older agent : upgrade it. Waiting for restart : restart it. Offline : start it. The card says Not a CA certificate. You have the certificate of one server, not the root. It works, but must be replaced when the server’s certificate changes. Ask IT for the root. The card says Expired. Get a current certificate from IT. The card says Already trusted, skipped. Nothing to do. It is already trusted. If the error continues, the problem is another certificate in the chain (an intermediate). Add that one too. One agent needs a certificate that other agents should not have. On that machine, use the agent’s own Certificates tab (tray app) to add it. See Windows tray app. Next steps. Trusted certificates : every part of the page. Watch for the notice that warns you 30 days before a certificate you added expires."},
{"title":"Upgrade agents","href":"how-to/admin-upgrade-agents","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Check it worked","Upgrade many agents","Troubleshooting","Next steps"],"text":"Move a build agent to the files of a new Buildspawn version, and keep its name, its secret, its pool and its work folders. Before you start. You can open the agent machine, and on Windows you have an administrator prompt (on Linux and macOS, sudo ). You have the agent download of the new version: Agents › Install agents on the upgraded server (see Install an agent ). Pick a quiet time, or mark the agent offline first, so that no build is running (see below). An agent keeps its name and secret in conf/buildspawn-agent.properties. If you keep that file, the agent does not register again. It stays the same agent, in the same pool, with the same history. Agents that are older than the server still connect, but some features, such as trusted certificates from the server (1.9), the Certificates tab (1.9.2) and the terminal program's certs command, need new agent files. Steps. Open Agents , find the agent, open the row menu ⋯ and choose Mark offline. Running builds finish first. Wait until the status is Offline. On the agent machine, close the tray app: right-click its icon, choose Exit , then click Stop agent. (A running tray app would block the new Buildspawn Agent.exe from being written.). Stop the agent: # Windows, administrator prompt, in the agent folder stop-agent.bat # Linux and macOS./stop-agent.sh. Unzip the new download over the agent folder. When your tool asks about conf\\buildspawn-agent.properties , keep your own file. # Windows: unzip over C:\\buildspawn-agent tar -xf buildspawn-agent-windows-x64.zip -C C:\\ # Linux sudo unzip -o buildspawn-agent-linux-x64.zip -d /opt. Do not delete the work folder. It holds the workspaces, so the first build after the upgrade does not sync everything again. If you downloaded the zip made for one agent, it holds that agent's secret and may replace the settings file. Use the general download with the file you keep, or copy your old file back. Install the service again, so that it points at the new files and picks up new Java options: # Windows, administrator prompt install-service.bat # Linux and macOS sudo./install-service.sh. Windows says Service \"Buildspawn Agent\" installed again from … and started. You do not need --url , because the settings file already holds it. On Windows, open bin\\Buildspawn Agent.exe again to get the tray app back. Tick Open this app when I sign in to Windows in its Settings tab if you want it to start by itself. On the Agents page, open the row menu of the agent and choose Bring back online. Check it worked. The agent turns Idle on the Agents page, in its old pool, with its old name. On the agent page its Parameters show the new agent.version. In the tray app, the Certificates tab is there. In the terminal program, bin/buildspawn-agent-tui --version shows the new version. Run a small build on it. Upgrade many agents. Do a few at a time, so the pool keeps capacity. Put one agent of each pool through the steps first, run a build on it, then do the rest. Use the Pool Scheduler to lend idle agents to a pool while some of its agents are offline for the upgrade. Troubleshooting. Problem. What to do. The agent asks to register again and appears under Unauthorized. The settings file was replaced and lost its secret. Copy the old conf\\buildspawn-agent.properties back (it holds secret=… ), or approve the new request. See Connect an agent. install-service.bat says could not remove the old service definition. Close the Services window ( services.msc ) and run it again. The service does not start and logs is empty. Check that bin\\buildspawn-agent-service.xml is there. Run install-service.bat again. The agent runs from a different folder after the unzip. You unzipped to another place. Run install-service from the folder you want. It replaces the old service entry. The tray app is the old one. Exit it first. A running Buildspawn Agent.exe blocks the new file from being written. Certificates that you added are gone. They live in work\\trust. If you deleted the work folder, add "},
{"title":"Back up and upgrade the server","href":"how-to/back-up-and-upgrade","headings":["Before you start","Steps","1. Find your home folder","2. Back up","3. Upgrade","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Next steps"],"text":"Take a full copy of your server's data, then move to a new version of Buildspawn without losing projects, history or settings. Before you start. You can sign in to the server computer as an administrator (Windows) or with sudo (Linux). On macOS, your own account is enough. You have room for the copy: another disk or a network share at least as large as the home folder. You have the new version's download for the same system, from the same place you got the first one. Read its release notes. See Release notes. You have picked a quiet time. The server is stopped while you copy and upgrade, so no builds run. Everything Buildspawn knows is in one folder, the home : projects, build configurations, build history, users, credentials and settings. The program files are separate. Back up the home and the settings file, and you can always go back. Download. Home (back this up). Settings file. Stop and start. Windows setup. C:\\ProgramData\\Buildspawn\\data. C:\\ProgramData\\Buildspawn\\buildspawn.cfg. buildspawn-service stop / start. Windows portable zip. data\\ in the Buildspawn folder. buildspawn.cfg next to Buildspawn.exe. The app: Run here › Stop / Start. Linux.deb and.rpm. /var/lib/buildspawn. /etc/default/buildspawn (.deb), /etc/sysconfig/buildspawn (.rpm). sudo systemctl stop / start buildspawn. Linux and macOS tar.gz. data/ in the Buildspawn folder. buildspawn.conf in the folder. bin/buildspawn stop / start. Steps. 1. Find your home folder. In the web interface, click Settings , then System. Read Home directory under General. If it is not the folder in the table above, the settings file moves it: back up that folder instead. The home folder of this server. Copy it with the button on the right. 2. Back up. Let running builds finish. To stop new builds from starting, click Prepare for Shutdown on the Settings page. Type a reason, such as Backup until 18:00 : everyone sees it in a banner. Click Cancel Shutdown if you decide to carry on without stopping. Stop Buildspawn, with the command for your download in the table above. A copy taken while builds write files can be incomplete. Copy the whole home folder and the settings file to a safe place. Windows setup, in a command prompt opened as administrator: cd \"C:\\Program Files\\Buildspawn\" buildspawn-service stop robocopy C:\\ProgramData\\Buildspawn D:\\Backup\\Buildspawn /MIR /XD logs buildspawn-service start. Linux package: sudo systemctl stop buildspawn sudo tar -czf /backup/buildspawn-$(date +%F).tar.gz -C /var/lib buildspawn /etc/default/buildspawn sudo systemctl start buildspawn. On RHEL, Rocky and Fedora, write /etc/sysconfig/buildspawn instead of /etc/default/buildspawn. Linux or macOS tar.gz, from the Buildspawn folder: bin/buildspawn stop tar -czf ~/buildspawn-backup-$(date +%F).tar.gz data buildspawn.conf bin/buildspawn start. Start Buildspawn again, unless you go on to upgrade now. Always keep the secrets folder and the secret.key files of the home in the same backup. Without them, Buildspawn can't read the saved passwords and credentials. To save space you can leave out workspace/ (builds check the sources out again), war/ (unpacked at each start) and the logs folder. 3. Upgrade. Pick the steps for your download. Each one keeps your home and your settings file. Windows setup. Run the new buildspawn-<version>-setup.exe. The setup stops the service, replaces the program files, keeps the settings and data, and starts the service again. For a silent upgrade, run setup.exe /S. Windows portable zip. Stop Buildspawn in the app ( Run here › Stop ), or stop the service in the app's Windows service tab. Unpack the new zip into a new folder. Copy buildspawn.cfg and data\\ from the old folder into the new one. Start the new Buildspawn.exe. If you used the Windows service, install it again from the new folder, in the Windows service tab. Debian and Ubuntu (.deb), RHEL, Rocky and Fedora (.rpm). # Debian, Ubuntu sudo apt install./buildspawn_<version>_amd64.deb # RHEL, Rocky, Fedora sudo dnf install."},
{"title":"Give release builds priority on agents","href":"how-to/borrow-agents-for-release","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Next steps"],"text":"When a release build waits, let Buildspawn borrow an idle agent from a less important pool and give it back afterwards. Before you start. You are an administrator. The release build configuration is in a pool, for example HT2 Devkits. The release build has a parameter that marks it as a release. Here it is a tick box named release (a boolean parameter). See Parameters. Decide which pools may lend agents. Only pools with a lower weight than the target pool lend. Steps. Open Agents , click the Pools tab, then Priority rules. Check that Automatic balancing is switched on. Under Pool weights , give the release pool a higher number than the pools that should lend to it, for example HT2 Devkits 100 and HT2 Spare 20. Click Save weights. Click Add priority rule. In Name , type HT2 Release builds first. In Parameter , type release. Leave Value empty: an empty value matches true , 1 , yes or on. Set Priority (higher is served first) to 200 , Target pool to HT2 Devkits , and Max agents (how many agents this rule may borrow at once) to 1. Click Save. The rule: which builds, how important, and where agents go. The rule in the list, and the pool weights. Start a release build: open the build configuration, click Run with options, tick release , and run it. Check it worked. Within a minute, open the Overview tab of the Pool Scheduler. Borrowed agents counts 1, and Capacity per pool shows +1 in on the target pool and 1 out on the lending pool. Recent moves lists the move with Rule: HT2 Release builds first. The release build's page says it ran on the borrowed agent. When the agent is idle and no matching build waits, it goes back to its home pool. Its state in Borrowed agents reads Going back when idle until then. An agent borrowed for the release build. Troubleshooting. Problem. What to do. The build waits and nothing is borrowed. No pool with a lower weight has an idle agent. Pools with equal or higher weight never lend. Busy, offline and personal agents, and agents on the Never move list, are never borrowed. The rule doesn't match. Check the parameter name. Without a prefix it is a configuration parameter; use env. or system. for those kinds. The value must match exactly, or be empty for a tick box. Agents never go back. Turn on Return borrowed agents when idle under Settings. Also check that the scheduler is not Paused. Too many agents are borrowed. Lower Max agents on the rule. Next steps. Priority rules and weights : how balancing chooses agents. Move agents to another pool every evening. The Run dialog."},
{"title":"Build every Perforce submit","href":"how-to/build-every-submit","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Connect the build configuration to Perforce","Add the trigger","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Next steps","Test a shelved change","Perforce","Triggers","Version control parameters"],"text":"Start a build on its own each time someone submits a change to your Perforce depot. Before you start. A build configuration with at least one build step. See Create a build configuration. A Perforce connection (a P4 credential) with the server address and a build user, for example Studio Helix Core (buildbot). You can also add one while you follow the steps. The Configure permission on the build configuration. At least one online agent that can run the build and reach the Perforce server. In this guide the build configuration is HT1 Every Submit in the How-to project. It syncs //depot/skyforge/. Steps. Connect the build configuration to Perforce. Open the build configuration and click Settings , then Version Control. Choose Perforce Software. (If several build configurations share one Perforce connection, choose Perforce root and pick the shared root instead. Several roots lets one build configuration use more than one.). Under P4 Credentials , pick your connection, for example Studio Helix Core (buildbot). If there is none yet, click Add and type the server, the user and the password or ticket. Set Workspace behaviour to Manual (custom view). In Workspace name , type a name for your studio with the agent's name at the end, for example skyforge-ci-${NODE_NAME}. Each agent then gets its own workspace. In View Mappings , add a row for each line of the view. The view is a table: each row has a mode ( Include , Exclude or Overlay ), a depot path and a workspace path. Type the depot path //depot/skyforge/. The workspace path fills in from the workspace name, so it should read: //depot/skyforge/. //skyforge-ci-${NODE_NAME}/. You can also switch to the text view and paste the lines. The table and the text always stay in step. A preview shows what each line maps. Under Populate options , keep Auto cleanup and sync. It syncs the new files and removes files that should not be there. Click Save. The Perforce connection, the workspace name and the view. Add the trigger. Still in Settings , open Triggers. Click Add new trigger. A list of trigger kinds opens. Click Add on the VCS Trigger row. Pick VCS Trigger. Here it is already on the build configuration, so the row says Edit. In Poll schedule , type how often Buildspawn asks Perforce for new changes. H/2 * * * * checks about every 2 minutes. Leave Quiet period empty, or type a number of seconds, such as 60. Buildspawn then waits that long after a submit, so several submits close together go into one build. Click Save. The VCS Trigger checks every Perforce and Git root of the build configuration. Click Run once. The first build tells Buildspawn which change it has already built. From then on, each new submit starts a build. Polling is the simplest way and needs nothing on the Perforce server. If your Perforce administrator can add a server trigger that calls Buildspawn after each submit, add P4 triggered build as well. Builds then start at once instead of at the next poll. Check it worked. Open the Perforce Software Polling Log tab of the build configuration. It shows the last check: the change it found and whether it is new. Polling no changes found means the latest change is already built. Submit a change to a file inside the view. Within the poll time (plus the quiet period) a new build appears in History. Open the build and click Changes. It lists the changelist you submitted. The polling log after a check with nothing new to build. Troubleshooting. Problem. What to do. A submit did not start a build. Open the Perforce Software Polling Log. Check that the submitted files are inside your View Mappings ; changes outside the view are ignored. Also check that the trigger is not disabled and that the build configuration or its project is not paused. Every submit starts its own build and the queue fills up. Set a Quiet period on the trigger, for example 120 seconds. Submits that arrive during it go into the same build. Two agents fight over one workspace, or files are missing. The workspace "},
{"title":"Build a Unity project from Git","href":"how-to/build-unity-from-git","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Create the build configuration and connect Git","Add the Unity step","Keep the player and run it","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","The wrong Unity version ran","The step can't find a Unity Editor","Unity says the executeMethod class could not be found","A passed step shows a red error count","Next steps","Git","Runners","Triggers","Pass a version number between steps"],"text":"Check out a Unity project from Git and build a Windows player with the Unity Editor in batch mode. Before you start. A project to hold the build configuration. See Projects. The Git repository of your Unity project: its address, the branch, and credentials if it is private (an SSH key or an access token). A build script in the project: a static method in a class under Assets/Editor that calls BuildPipeline.BuildPlayer. There is an example below. An agent with the Unity Editor installed through Unity Hub, ideally the version your project uses, and the git command. Open the agent and click the Unity tab to see which Editors it found. The agent's Unity tab lists the Editors a Unity step can use. This is the build script used in this guide, in Assets/Editor/BuildScript.cs : using UnityEditor; public static class BuildScript { public static void BuildWindows() { var scenes = new[] { \"Assets/Scenes/Main.unity\" }; BuildPipeline.BuildPlayer(scenes, \"Builds/Win64/SkyforgeMobile.exe\", BuildTarget.StandaloneWindows64, BuildOptions.None); } }. Steps. Create the build configuration and connect Git. Open your project, click New Item , type a name such as Mobile Windows Build , choose Freestyle project and click OK. On the Settings tab, click Version Control and choose Git. Type the Repository URL , for example https://git.studio.example/skyforge/mobile.git. Pick the Credentials , or click Add to add an access token or SSH key. A public repository needs none. Under Branches to build , type the branch in Branch Specifier , for example */main. Click Save. A Git root on the main branch. This demo uses a repository on the server's own disk. Add Library/ , Temp/ , Logs/ and Builds/ to your.gitignore. Unity rebuilds them, and they make checkouts slow. Add the Unity step. Click Build Steps , then Add build step. Type Unity in Filter step types and click the Unity tile. In Step name , type Build Windows player. Leave Project path empty if the Unity project is at the top of the repository. Otherwise type its folder, for example Mobile. In Execute method , type the class and method of your build script: BuildScript.BuildWindows. The box suggests methods it found in Assets/Editor after a first checkout. In Build target , type StandaloneWindows64. Unity switches to that platform before the project loads. Keep Do not initialize the graphics device ticked. Build machines often have no screen. The project, the build method and the target platform. With no build script, choose a Standalone player and fill in the Player output path instead. On Unity 6 you can also name a Build profile asset. Under Unity installation , leave Detection mode on Auto. In Unity version , type the version your project needs, for example 2022.3. Left empty, the step uses the version in ProjectSettings/ProjectVersion.txt if the agent has it, and the newest Editor on the agent if not. To run your tests too, tick Run tests from the project under Unity tests and pick a Test platform. The step records the test results on the build. Click Save. Tests and the Unity version. Keep the player and run it. Click Post-build Actions , add Archive the artifacts and type Builds/** in Files to archive. Click Save. Click Run. Check it worked. Open the build. The Build steps card shows Build Windows player with the Unity runner and its result, the summary shows the commit it built, and the player is under Build Artifacts. A real build of the demo project: the step passed in 2 minutes 28 seconds. On the Console Output tab, the Git checkout comes first, then one block for the Unity step. Expand it to read the Unity log. The first lines name the Editor the step picked and the full command line it ran. The checkout, then the Unity step as one block you can open. Troubleshooting. The wrong Unity version ran. In Auto mode with an empty Unity version , the step takes the newest Editor on the agent when the project's own version isn't installed. Unity then upgrades the project in the workspace. Type the vers"},
{"title":"Build an Unreal Engine game from Perforce","href":"how-to/build-unreal-from-perforce","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Create the build configuration","Connect it to Perforce","Add the Unreal Engine step","Keep the package and run it","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","The build waits in the queue","Two agents fight over one workspace","The step can't find the project","The sync fails with a password error","Next steps","Build every Perforce submit","Test a shelved change","Unreal Engine step","Perforce"],"text":"Sync your game from a Perforce depot, then compile, cook and package it for Windows with one Unreal Engine step. Before you start. A project to hold the build configuration, for example Game. See Projects. A Perforce connection : the server address and a build user with its password or ticket. If nobody has added one yet, you add it in step 4. The depot path of your game, for example //depot/skyforge/. , and the name of its.uproject file. A Windows agent with Unreal Engine installed , from the Epic launcher or built from source. Buildspawn looks for engines when the agent connects. Open the agent and click the Unreal Engines tab to see what it found. The agent's Unreal Engines tab. This demo agent has no engine; yours should list one. Steps. Create the build configuration. Open your project and click New Item. Type a name, for example Windows Build , choose Freestyle project and click OK. The build configuration opens on its Settings tab. Connect it to Perforce. Click Version Control and choose Perforce Software. Under P4 Credentials , pick your studio's connection, for example Studio Helix Core (buildbot). If there is none, click Add and fill in the server ( ssl:perforce.studio.example:1666 ), the user and the password or ticket. Set Workspace behaviour to Manual (custom view). In Workspace name , type a name for the Perforce workspace that ends with the agent's name, for example skyforge-ue-win64-${NODE_NAME}. Each agent then gets its own workspace. In View Mappings , map the depot into that workspace: //depot/skyforge/. //skyforge-ue-win64-${NODE_NAME}/. Under Populate options , choose Auto cleanup and sync. It syncs only what changed and removes files that should not be there, such as old build output. Click Save. The Perforce connection and a workspace name per agent. If your depot uses streams, choose Streams as the workspace behaviour and type the stream, for example //skyforge/main. Buildspawn writes the view for you. See Perforce. Add the Unreal Engine step. Click Build Steps , then Add build step. Type Unreal in Filter step types and click the Unreal Engine tile. In Step name , type Build, cook and package. Leave Engine detection mode on Automatic and type the engine version in Identifier , for example 5.4. The step then runs only on agents that have that engine. For an engine in a folder of your own, choose Manual and fill in Root dir instead. Leave Command on BuildCookRun. In Project , type the path of the.uproject file in the workspace, for example Skyforge.uproject. After a first build, Detect projects finds it for you. The engine and the project. Leave Build configuration on Standalone game. Under Target configurations , tick Development. Under Target platforms , tick Win64. To build more than one, tick more boxes; the field joins them with +. Keep Cook , Stage , Package and Archive ticked. Open Advanced and set the Archive directory to Output. Leave Additional arguments as they are. They make the engine run without windows or prompts on a build machine. Click Save. What to build and which stages to run. Keep the package and run it. Open More › Post-build Actions , add Archive the artifacts and type Output/** in Files to archive. Click Save. Click Run. The finished build configuration: one Unreal Engine step. Check it worked. The build log starts with the Perforce sync into skyforge-ue-win64- plus the agent's name, then [Step 1/1] Build, cook and package. Errors and warnings from the engine are picked out of the log for you. See The build console. The Changes tab of the build lists the changelists since the last build. The package is under Artifacts on the build page. The demo server used for this guide has no Unreal Engine on its agents, so the screenshots stop at the settings and at the queue below. On a machine with the engine, the same settings run the full build. Troubleshooting. The build waits in the queue. Open Queue and read the Wait reason. has no Unreal Engine that matches '5.4' means no online agent "},
{"title":"Test a shelved change before you submit","href":"how-to/check-shelf-before-submit","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Shelve your change","Run a personal build","Check it worked","Run it on your own computer","Show the result to a colleague","Build every new shelf on its own","Troubleshooting","Next steps","Build every Perforce submit","Perforce","The Run dialog","Triggers"],"text":"Run a personal build of a shelved changelist, so you know your change builds before it reaches the depot. Before you start. A build configuration with a Perforce connection whose view holds the files you changed. See Build every Perforce submit for how to set one up. A change you have not submitted yet, in a pending changelist in your own workspace. The permission to run builds of that build configuration. A shelf (shelved changelist) stores your files on the Perforce server without submitting them. A personal build syncs the latest code, puts your shelved files on top and runs the steps. It does not change the status of the build configuration. Steps. Shelve your change. In P4V, right-click your pending changelist and choose Shelve. Or, on the command line: p4 shelve -c 261. Use your own changelist number instead of 261. Note the changelist number. You pick it in Buildspawn in a moment. Run a personal build. Open the build configuration, for example HT1 Shelf Check in the How-to project. Click the Shelves tab. Your shelf is listed with its description, user, workspace and files. If you don't see it, click Refresh at the bottom. The Shelves tab lists the shelves in the build configuration\\'s view. The Personal build column fills in after the build. Click the button beside Run ( Run custom build ). Open the Shelved changes tab. Tick your shelf in the list, or type its number in Shelved changelist IDs. To build several shelves together, separate the numbers with commas. Pick the shelf on the Shelved changes tab. Click Run Build. Check it worked. Open the new build. It has a Personal chip beside its result. In Summary , the Personal build row names your shelf and its owner, and Parameters shows p4.status = shelved and p4.review with your changelist number. Changes lists your shelved changelist. If the build passed, submit your change. If it failed, fix it, shelve again and run another personal build. A personal build of a shelf. In the log you can see the step reading the shelved version of the file. Run it on your own computer. If an administrator has turned on personal machines, you can run the same personal build on your own computer. Add your computer once under My machines in your account menu. Then, on the Shelved changes tab, pick your shelf and click Run on my machine , or choose My machine in the Agent list. Only you can build there, and the shelved files are reverted after the build. See Personal builds and shelves. Show the result to a colleague. A personal build is private. Open the build and click Share to copy a link that lets someone else read it. Click Stop sharing to turn the link off. See Shared builds. Build every new shelf on its own. If your team wants every shelf checked, add a shelve trigger. People then only add a keyword to the changelist description. Open the build configuration's Settings , then Triggers. Click Add new trigger and click Add on Perforce shelve trigger. Keep the Schedule , for example H/2 * * * *. It says how often to look for new shelves. Keep the Keyword #buildspawn , or type your own. Only shelves whose description holds it are built. If you like, limit it further with a Description pattern (a regular expression) or a list of Users. Click Save. The shelve trigger. Each new revision of a matching shelf starts one personal build. Shelves that already exist when you add the trigger are not built straight away. They are built when someone shelves them again. The same happens when you change the filters. Troubleshooting. Problem. What to do. My shelf is not in the list. The list only shows shelves with files inside the build configuration's view. Check the view on Settings › Version Control. The list is also kept for a while: click Refresh. The build fails before the steps and names some files. Your shelf conflicts with newer submits. Sync your workspace, resolve the files, shelve again and run a new personal build. The shelve trigger did not start a build. Check that the description holds "},
{"title":"Find out why a build failed","href":"how-to/find-why-build-failed","headings":["Before you start","Steps","If the failed step has a name from your script","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Next steps"],"text":"Go from a red build to the line, the test or the change that broke it in a few clicks. Before you start. You can see the project: you need at least the Project viewer role on it. A build has failed. In the project tree on the left, a build configuration with a red dot has a failed last build. Steps. Open the failed build. Click the build configuration in the project tree, then the build number with a red Failed label in its History list. The Failed button above that list hides the builds that passed. The History page in the top bar lists the builds of every project. On the Overview tab, look at Build steps. The failed step has a red band and a red cross, with the reason beside it, for example Failed — the process exited with code 1. Read Decided by in the Summary card. It says what made the build fail, for example Failed: Step \"Upload build\" exited with code 1. Click Log on the failed step. The Console Output tab opens at that step. Click Errors only in the console toolbar. You now see only error lines, inside the blocks they belong to. Press E to jump from one error to the next, and W for warnings. Click All to see the lines around an error again. To find something else, click Search in log and type a word, such as a file name or an error code like C4996. Move between matches with the up and down arrows beside the box. If the build has a Test Result tab, click it. All Failed Tests lists the tests that failed. Click the arrow beside a test to read its error message and stack trace. Click the Changes tab to see the changelists or commits that are new in this build. When a test that passed yesterday fails today, the cause is often in this list. Step 2: the failed step stands out on the Overview tab. The Summary also names the failed test. Step 5: Errors only keeps the block structure, so you see which step and block each error came from. Step 8: the failed test. The numbers in brackets compare with the build before. If the failed step has a name from your script. Steps that print ::group:: lines show their own blocks inside the step. A failed block opens by itself, so the error is often in view as soon as you open the console. Failed steps also write the line Failed: Step \"Name\" exited with code N , and Decided by on the Overview repeats it. If a script set a status text, it shows beside the result. See The build name and status text. Check it worked. You found the cause when you can say three things: Where it failed: the step and block, from the Overview and the console. What failed: the error line or the failed test with its message. Why now : the change in the Changes tab, or \"nothing changed\" (then it is the machine or the network, see below). Click a line number in the console to put a link to that line in the address bar. Send it to the person who can fix the problem. Troubleshooting. Problem. What to do. Errors only shows nothing useful. Some tools write errors as plain lines. Click All , open the failed step and read its last lines, or search for error. Make your own scripts print ::error:: lines; see Organise your build log with blocks. The Changes tab says No changes. The build used the same changelist or commit as the build before it. The info line in the header still shows which one. If the code did not change, compare the agent, the parameters and the time with the last good build. The same code passes on a re-run. The failure is flaky: a network share, a device kit or a crashing tool. Add Retry on failure to that step. See Resume and retry. There is no Test Result tab. The step did not publish a test report. Set Test reports on the step, for example reports/*.xml. See Runners. Next steps. Re-run a failed build from the failing step once the problem is fixed. The build console : every filter, key and tool in the console. Changes, artifacts and tests. Watch the server from a terminal : list failed builds without a browser. Compare two builds : find what differs between the last good build and the failed one. The Stat"},
{"title":"Clean a Perforce workspace","href":"how-to/game-clean-workspace","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Clean only what you choose","Try it first","Or clean in the root","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Next steps","Perforce operations","Build an Unreal Engine game"],"text":"Remove old build output and restore changed files before a build, with a safety limit so a wrong path cannot wipe a big workspace. Before you start. A build configuration that uses a Perforce root. You know what to keep. In an Unreal Engine project, Intermediate/ , Saved/ and DerivedDataCache are slow to rebuild, so they are usually kept. Steps. Clean only what you choose. Open Settings › Build steps and click Add build step. Add Perforce operations. Name it Clean workspace and click the Clean card. In Files type the folder, for example //skyforge-win64-${NODE_NAME}/Game/. If you leave it empty, the whole workspace is cleaned and the form warns you. Leave Added files , Edited files and Deleted files on. Leave Ignore.p4ignore off, so the files your.p4ignore lists are kept. In Fail if more files would be deleted than type 2000. Drag the step to the top, then click Save. The Perforce operations step with Clean chosen. Try it first. Tick Preview only and run the build. The log counts the files that would be deleted, restored and refreshed, and changes nothing. Then turn it off. Or clean in the root. On the Perforce root , set How to sync to Auto Clean to clean at every checkout, or switch on Remove the workspace after each build for a fresh workspace every time. For one clean build, use the build configuration action that enforces a clean checkout; the next build then starts with an empty workspace. Check it worked. $ p4 clean -a -e -d //skyforge-win64-win-build-01/Game/. deleted: 214 files restored: 0 files refreshed: 3 files. The counts tell you what happened. The next compile step finds a clean tree. Troubleshooting. You see. What to do. Clean would delete N files, more than the limit. Nothing was deleted. Check the Files path. Raise the limit if the number is right. The cache is gone after every build. Switch Ignore.p4ignore off and list the cache folders in.p4ignore. The step cannot run. The build configuration needs a Perforce root. See Perforce operations. Next steps. Perforce operations. All operations. Build an Unreal Engine game. Sync, cook and package."},
{"title":"Copy a stream (Main to Drop)","href":"how-to/game-copy-stream","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Next steps","Perforce operations","Streams"],"text":"Promote a tested build from //skyforge/main to the release stream //skyforge/drop with a build that copies and submits. Before you start. Two streams, for example the parent //skyforge/main and the target //skyforge/drop. The Drop stream is a child of Main, so a copy goes up the flow. A Perforce root whose Stream is the target , //skyforge/drop. The copy opens files in the workspace, so the workspace must be on the target stream. A build user that may submit to //skyforge/drop. Steps. Create a build configuration Promote to Drop. Under Settings › Version Control choose Perforce root and pick the root on //skyforge/drop. Go to Settings › Build steps and add Perforce operations. Name it Copy Main to Drop. Click the Copy card. In Branch mapping or stream type //skyforge/drop. In Source type //skyforge/main. The Command box now shows p4 copy -S //skyforge/drop -P //skyforge/main. Set Preview only on the first time, to see what would be copied. Run it once, read the log, then turn it off. Add a second Perforce operations step, Submit the copy. Click the Submit card. In Description type Promote main to drop (build ${BUILD_NUMBER}). Click Save , then Run. The Copy step with the stream and the command preview. Check it worked. The log of step 1 shows $ p4 copy -S //skyforge/drop -P //skyforge/main and the files opened. The log of step 2 shows $ p4 submit -d. and P4: Step output CHANGELIST =. with the new changelist. In the Submitted changes tab, the new changelist is on //skyforge/drop. Troubleshooting. You see. What to do. Nothing to copy. Drop is already the same as Main. The submit step then fails with No files are open ; that is the correct result. The step says it needs a source and a target. Fill in Branch mapping or stream , or both Source and Target paths. Submit fails with a permission error. The build user needs write access to the target stream in the Perforce protections table. Next steps. Perforce operations. Every operation of the step. Streams. Build one stream or many."},
{"title":"Use P4 commands in a script with the P4 ticket","href":"how-to/game-p4-ticket-script","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Next steps","Perforce variables","Perforce connections"],"text":"Run the p4 program in a PowerShell or shell step, signed in with the ticket Buildspawn gives the build, with no password in the script. Before you start. A build configuration with a Perforce root or Perforce Software. The p4 program installed on the agent. The setting Hide TICKET in the Perforce part of the system settings is off (the default). If it is on, P4_TICKET is not given to steps. For common jobs (sync, clean, submit, shelve) the Perforce operations step needs no p4 program and no ticket. Use a script only for something it cannot do. Steps. Open Settings › Build steps and add a PowerShell step. Name it List my changes. Type the script: $ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop' # P4_PORT, P4_USER and P4_CLIENT are set for you. p4 -p $env:P4_PORT -u $env:P4_USER -P $env:P4_TICKET -c $env:P4_CLIENT changes -m 5 \".#head\". Click Save and Run. -P gives the ticket as the password, -p the server, -u the user and -c the workspace. In a batch step use %P4_TICKET%. The PowerShell step. Do not print the ticket. Do not write it to a file that stays after the build, and do not pass it on the command line of programs that log their arguments. A ticket in the output is hidden in the log only when Buildspawn knows it, so keep it out of echo lines. Check it worked. The log shows the five changelists. If the ticket is wrong you see Your session has expired, please login again. Change 48512 on 2026/11/26 by dana.k@skyforge-win64 'Fix door collision' Change 48508 on 2026/11/26 by t.osei@skyforge-art 'Update fog settings'. Troubleshooting. You see. What to do. P4_TICKET is empty. Check Hide TICKET. Check that the step runs after the checkout, which happens before the steps start, and that the connection can log in. The term 'p4' is not recognized. Install the p4 command line on the agent and add it to the path. Session expired. Use a ticket credential made with p4 login -ap , or a password credential, so Buildspawn logs in for each build. Next steps. Perforce variables. All the P4 variables. Perforce connections. Password and ticket credentials."},
{"title":"Publish a build to the Epic Games Store","href":"how-to/game-publish-epic","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Add the Epic steps after the packaging step","Make the update small and put it live","Run it","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Next steps","Epic Games Store upload","Epic: label binary","Epic Games Store","Steam"],"text":"Package your game, upload it to Epic as a new binary, make the update smaller and put it live, all in one build. Before you start. An Epic Games Store credential with your client ID, client secret, organization ID, product ID and default artifact ID. See Epic Games Store credential. The BuildPatch Tool uploaded under Manage › Tools › BuildPatch Tool , so an agent can get it. A Windows agent that builds your game. The build must leave a packaged game folder, for example build/WindowsPackaged. See Build an Unreal Engine game from Perforce. The permission Epic Games Store / Set Live for whoever starts the build. Steps. Add the Epic steps after the packaging step. Open the build configuration that packages the game, then Settings › Build steps. Click Add build step , type BuildPatch and choose Install BuildPatch Tool. Keep From Buildspawn. Move this step above the others if you like: it only needs to run before the first Epic step. Click Add build step again and choose Epic Games Store upload. Pick your credential, for example Epic Store (Skyforge). Set Build root to build/WindowsPackaged. Set Build version to 1.4.${BUILD_NUMBER}-Windows. It must be new every time, so keep the build number in it. Set Launch program to Skyforge.exe. Choose Everything except these and list W\"*.pdb\" to leave out debug files. The upload step, filled in. Make the update small and put it live. Add Epic: optimise patch. Set From version to the version that is live now, for example 1.3.2-Windows. Leave To version alone: it is the version just uploaded. Add Epic: label binary. Keep Action on Label , Label on Live and tick Windows. Click Save. Do not put the label step in the same build when you want a person to approve the release. Run upload and optimise on every build, and keep the label step in a separate build configuration that someone starts when the release is approved. Type the version in its Build version field. The steps of the build configuration. Run it. Click Run. Check it worked. The log shows Epic: access check: version. is new , then the upload, then Epic: Step output EPIC_BUILD_VERSION =. The label step prints Epic: label Live for Windows, version. and EPIC_LABELLED = true. The metadata file epic/metadata-.json is under Artifacts. In the Epic Developer Portal the binary is active and live for Windows. Troubleshooting. Problem. What to do. Version. already exists. The version must be new for the artifact. Keep ${BUILD_NUMBER} in Build version. BuildPatch Tool was not found on the agent. The Install step is missing or ran on another agent. Add it to this build, or set the BuildPatch Tool folder. Epic refused the label. Your organization may use self-service publishing. Set Publishing to Self-service on the credential and assign the platform in the Developer Portal. The launch program is not in the build root. Check the folder the packaging step made, and the spelling of the program. Next steps. Epic Games Store upload. Every setting of the upload step. Epic: label binary. Live, Archive and platforms. Epic Games Store. All the Epic steps. Steam. Ship the same build on Steam."},
{"title":"Build Unity from Perforce and upload to a Steam branch","href":"how-to/game-unity-perforce-steam","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Connect Perforce","Build the player with Unity","Install SteamCMD","Upload to the Steam branch","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Next steps","Steam upload","Steam upload: overview","Perforce","Unity"],"text":"Sync your game from Perforce, build a Windows player with Unity, and upload it to a Steam beta branch, all in one build configuration. Before you start. A Perforce connection (server, build user and password or ticket) and the depot path of your game, for example //depot/skyforge/. See Perforce. A Windows agent with the Unity Editor and a build script in the project, for example BuildScript.BuildWindows , that writes the player to Builds/Win64. See Unity. A Steam build account credential you have signed in with. See Steam build account. Your Steam App ID , the Depot ID , and a beta branch that already exists in Steamworks, for example nightly. Steps. Connect Perforce. Open your project, click New Item , name it Steam Nightly , choose Freestyle project and click OK. Go to Settings › Version Control and choose Perforce Software. Pick the P4 Credentials , set Workspace behaviour to Manual (custom view) , and type the Workspace name skyforge-steam-${NODE_NAME}. In View Mappings type //depot/skyforge/. //skyforge-steam-${NODE_NAME}/. Choose Auto cleanup and sync and click Save. The Perforce settings. Build the player with Unity. Open Build steps › Add build step › Unity. Set Step name to Build Windows player , fill in the project path and the execute method BuildScript.BuildWindows. Every Unity field is on the Unity page. Click Save. Install SteamCMD. Add the step Install SteamCMD and keep its defaults. See Install SteamCMD. Skip this if your agent already has SteamCMD. Place it before the Unity step or right after it. It only has to come before the upload. Upload to the Steam branch. Add the step Steam upload. Choose the Steam build account steam-build. Keep Scripts on Generate. Type App ID 1000480 and Content root Builds/Win64. In the depot row, type Depot ID 1000481. Add a file mapping with Local path * , Depot path. and Include subfolders on. In Leave out type *.pdb. Under Branch and release , type nightly in Set live on branch. Type %build.number% CL%build.vcs.number% as the Build description. Click Save. The Steam upload step. The step list now reads: Install SteamCMD, Unity, Steam upload. Click Run. Turn on Preview build for a first run. It writes the file list and logs and uploads nothing, so you can check the depot mapping safely. Check it worked. The log shows the Perforce sync, the Unity build, and then Steam: Step output BUILD_ID = … and set live on branch nightly. The build page has the saved SteamCMD logs under Artifacts › steam-logs. In Steamworks, open the Builds page. The build and its description are listed, and branch nightly points to it. The console of the finished build. Troubleshooting. Problem. What to do. The step says Steam asked for a Steam Guard code. Sign in again on the credential. See Steam build account. Branch … does not exist. Create the branch in Steamworks. A build cannot create it. The branch cannot be set live by a build. You typed default. Use a beta branch and set the default branch live in Steamworks. Depot … matches no file. The Content root is wrong. It is relative to the workspace. SteamCMD was not found on the agent. Add Install SteamCMD before the upload. To set the build live only after tests pass, leave Set live on branch empty and add Steam: check build and Steam: set branch live after the tests. Next steps. Steam upload. Every setting of the upload step. Steam upload: overview. How the Steam steps fit together. Perforce. Streams, shelves and shared roots. Unity. All fields of the Unity step."},
{"title":"Give a team access to its projects","href":"how-to/give-team-access","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Create the group","Create the users","Give the group a role on the project","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Next steps"],"text":"Put a team in a group and give the group a role on its project, so everyone in it can see and run that project's builds, and nothing else. Before you start. You are a system administrator (you have the Overall: Administer permission). People sign in with Buildspawn's own user database. With single sign-on, users and their groups come from your identity provider, so skip the parts about creating users. Settings › Security › Authorization is set to Project-based security. Only this choice gives every project a Roles tab. With another choice, Settings › Roles shows a yellow note that roles are off. The group All users has Overall: Read in the permission cards under Settings › Security. Without it, people can sign in but see nothing at all. In this example, the art team gets the Developer role on the project How-to : they can see its build configurations, run and stop builds and read the logs. Steps. Create the group. Click Settings in the top bar, then Groups under Security. Click Create group. Type a Name , for example ht3-artists. The ID is filled in from the name. Change it now if you want, because it can't change later. Type a Description , for example The art team. Leave Parent group at None (top level) , unless this team is part of a bigger group that already has roles. Click Create group. The Create group dialog. Create the users. Click Users under Security , then Create User. Type a Username , for example ht3-artist. Use letters, digits, dashes and underscores only. Type the Password twice, the Full name (for example Alex Artist ) and the Email. Click Create User. In the list, click Add to group on the new user's row and pick ht3-artists. Repeat for each person in the team. Someone who already has an account only needs step 5. Give the group a role on the project. Open the project, for example How-to , from the project tree. Click the Roles tab. Click Assign a role. Choose Group and type the group name, ht3-artists. Pick the role Developer and click Assign. Click Save. The role applies to the project and everything inside it: its subprojects and build configurations. Which role to pick: The team should…. Role. see builds and download artifacts. Project viewer. also run and stop builds and read settings. Developer. also change build configurations and give roles on the project. Project administrator. Check it worked. The new group with one member. Once the role is given, the Roles column lists it. On Settings › Groups , the group's row shows the number of Members and, after you assign it, the role in Roles. Open the project's Roles tab. The group is listed with Developer. Sign out, and sign in as one of the team (or ask them to). They see the How-to project in the project tree, with a Run button on its build configurations. Other projects are not shown. Troubleshooting. Problem. What to do. There is no Roles tab on the project. Authorization is not set to Project-based security. Change it under Settings › Security. Before you save, make sure your own user still has Overall: Administer , or you lock yourself out. The user signs in and sees an empty page, or Access denied. All users lacks Overall: Read. Add it in the permission cards under Settings › Security. Create User says the user name is not allowed. User names can only hold letters, digits, underscores and dashes. Write alex-artist instead of alex.artist. The ID on a permission card has a yellow ambiguous label. A user and a group share that name. Open the row and choose user or group. Next steps. Users and permissions : every role, custom roles and the permission cards. Roles , Users and groups and Authorization : each in full. With Entra ID, your groups come from Entra ID: see Sign in with Entra ID. Projects : how a project passes settings down to everything inside it. Start builds from a script with a user that has only the rights the script needs."},
{"title":"Organise your build log with blocks","href":"how-to/group-build-output","headings":["Before you start","The lines you can print","Steps","PowerShell","Batch (Command Line step on Windows)","Bash (Command Line step on Linux or macOS)","A block that ends in failure","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Next steps"],"text":"Make your scripts print ::group:: lines, so a long build log turns into named blocks you can open and close, with their own times, warnings and errors. Before you start. You can edit a build configuration: you need the Project administrator role, or the Configure permission, on its project. The build configuration has a build step that runs a script, for example a PowerShell or Command Line step. See Runners. You know how to open the build console. See The build console. The lines you can print. Your script only has to write these lines to its output. Each one must start the line (spaces before it are fine). Line. What it does. ::group::Name. Starts a block called Name. Blocks can sit inside blocks. ::group::Name::Details. Starts a block and shows Details in grey after the name, for example ::group::Textures::1,204 files. ::endgroup:: Ends the innermost open block. ::endgroup::FAILURE. Ends the block and marks how it ended: SUCCESS , UNSTABLE , FAILURE , ABORTED or SKIPPED. ::warning::Text. Writes Text as a warning: a yellow line that counts in the warnings counter. ::error::Text. Writes Text as an error: a red line that counts in the errors counter. Steps. Open the build configuration and click Settings , then Build Steps. Click the step that runs your script to open it. To start from scratch, click Add build step and pick PowerShell or Command Line. In the script, print ::group:: before each long task and ::endgroup:: after it. Use the example for your shell below. Where your script finds a problem, print ::warning:: or ::error:: with a short message. Click Save. Click Run to start a build, then open it and click the Console Output tab. Click Expand all to open every block at once. PowerShell. For a PowerShell step, or a.ps1 file the step runs: Write-Output \"::group::Compile game::Win64 Development\" foreach ($module in 'Core', 'Renderer', 'Audio', 'Gameplay', 'UI') { Write-Output \"Compiling module $module\" } Write-Output \"::endgroup::\" Write-Output \"::group::Cook content\" Write-Output \"::group::Textures::1,204 files\" Write-Output \"::warning::Texture T_Rock_D.png is larger than 4K\" Write-Output \"::endgroup::\" Write-Output \"::group::Maps::3 maps\" foreach ($map in 'Frontend', 'Harbour', 'Mountains') { Write-Output \"Cooking map $map\" } Write-Output \"::endgroup::\" Write-Output \"::endgroup::\". Use Write-Output or Write-Host. Inside a foreach whose result you store in a variable, use Write-Host , because Write-Output would go into the variable instead of the log. Batch (Command Line step on Windows). @echo off echo ::group::Package build echo Writing Skyforge-Win64.zip echo ::endgroup:: echo ::group::Check package::symbols and size echo ::warning::Debug symbols are missing for Audio.dll echo ::endgroup::SUCCESS. Write echo ::group:: with no quotes. In batch, echo prints the quotes too, so the line would start with \" and not count as a marker. Start the script with @echo off , so the commands themselves are not printed as well. Bash (Command Line step on Linux or macOS). #!/bin/bash set -e echo \"::group::Build server::Linux Development\" make -j\"$(nproc)\" server echo \"::endgroup::\" echo \"::group::Run tests\" if !/run-tests.sh; then echo \"::error::Server tests failed\" echo \"::endgroup::FAILURE\" exit 1 fi echo \"::endgroup::\". Python, Node.js and any other tool work the same way: print the lines to standard output. A block that ends in failure. Mark the block before you stop the script, so its header shows a red cross and it opens by itself: Write-Output \"::group::Upload to the build share\" if (-not (Test-Path \\\\fileserver\\builds)) { Write-Output \"::error::Cannot reach \\\\fileserver\\builds\" Write-Output \"::endgroup::FAILURE\" exit 1 } Write-Output \"::endgroup::\". Check it worked. The PowerShell example above, in the console. Each block has a tick, its details, a warning count and how long it took. Your blocks appear inside the step's block, with the names you printed. Blocks inside blocks are indented. Each block header shows a time on the"},
{"title":"Watch the server from a terminal","href":"how-to/monitor-from-terminal","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Use it in scripts","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Next steps"],"text":"Point the buildspawn terminal program at any Buildspawn server to watch its builds, queue and agents live, and stop builds or take agents offline, with no browser. Before you start. You have the terminal program. It comes with every server download: bin\\buildspawn.exe on Windows, bin/buildspawn on Linux and macOS. The Windows setup and the Linux packages put it on the PATH. You don't need a server on your own computer to use it. You can reach the server's address from your computer, for example http://buildserver:8080. A terminal at least 80 columns wide. From 110 columns the monitor uses its wide layout. Windows Terminal shows colours and symbols best. To stop builds, cancel queue items or take agents offline, you need an API token. Without one the monitor is read only. Steps. In the web interface, make an API token: your initial at the top right, Security , Add new token , a name such as terminal on studio-pc , Generate. Copy the token. See API tokens. Save the token in a text file with one line: your user name, a colon and the token. Keep the file private. On Windows, in PowerShell: Set-Content -NoNewline -Path \"$env:USERPROFILE\\.buildspawn-token\" -Value \"docs:11f3c9a0e5b27d48c6a1f09e3b7d5c2a84\". On Linux and macOS: echo \"docs:11f3c9a0e5b27d48c6a1f09e3b7d5c2a84\" > ~/.buildspawn-token chmod 600 ~/.buildspawn-token. Start the monitor with the server's address and the token file: buildspawn --url http://buildserver:8080 --token-file ~/.buildspawn-token. On Windows, in the program folder, type.\\bin\\buildspawn.exe instead of buildspawn , and $env:USERPROFILE\\.buildspawn-token for the file. The Overview opens. Press 2 for Builds : every build of the last 24 hours, newest first. Press f to show only one result, for example only failed builds. Press / and type to filter by job, agent or cause, such as HT3. Use ↑ and ↓ to select a build. The card below the list shows its agent, cause and last five results. Press Enter to read its log, o to open it in your browser, or x to stop a running build. Press 3 for Queue to see why each build waits. Press c to cancel the selected item or p to move it to the front. Press 4 for Agents. Select an agent and press d to take it offline, for example before you restart the machine. Press q to quit. Buildspawn 1.9.3 · ● Serving http://127.0.0.1:8090 · 0 of 10 executors busy · 1 queued LIVE 1 Overview [2 Builds] 3 Queue 1 4 Agents 6/7 5 Logs 6 Service 7 Settings ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── / type to filter jobs, agents, causes all running 0 failed 7 unstable 1 aborted 0 ┌─ Builds · last 24 h ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────1-15 of 36──┐ │ Start Job # Result Time Agent Cause │ │▸ 11:36 How-to/HT3 Scripted build #4 ✓ success 0:03 ht3-win-agent docs │ │ 11:36 How-to/HT3 Scripted build #3 ✓ success 0:03 ht3-win-agent docs │ │ 11:35 How-to/HT2 Release Build #1 ✓ success 0:14 ht-win-agent-01 docs │ │ 11:31 How-to/HT2 Devkit Build #2 ✓ success 0:20 ht-win-agent-01 docs │ │ 11:27 How-to/HT3 Scripted build #2 ✓ success 0:03 ht3-win-agent docs │ │ 11:27 How-to/HT3 Scripted build #1 ✓ success 0:05 ht3-win-agent docs │ │ 11:26 How-to/HT3 Nightly upload #2 ✓ success 0:01 ht3-win-agent Resumed #1… │ │ 11:25 How-to/HT1 Shelf Check #3 ✓ success 0:08 ht3-win-agent docs │ │ 11:25 How-to/HT1 Unity Windows Bu… #2 ✓ success 3:07 ht3-win-agent docs │ │ 11:23 How-to/HT2 Parallel Cook #1 ✓ success 0:30 win-build-01 docs │ │ 11:22 How-to/HT1 Unity Windows Bu… #1 ✗ failed 0:00 ht-win-agent-01 docs │ │ 11:22 How-to/HT1 Shelf Check #2 ✓ success 0:10 ht3-win-agent docs │ │ 11:22 How-to/HT1 Every Submit #2 ✓ success 0:07 ht3-win-agent docs │ │ 11:22 How-to/HT1 Shared/HT1 Windo… #2 ✓ success 0:00 ht3-win-agent docs ▼ │ │ 11:21 How-to/HT1 Secrets/HT1 Stor… #1 ✓ success 0:00 ht3-win-agent docs │ └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ ┌─ How-to/"},
{"title":"Move agents to another pool every evening","href":"how-to/night-pool-schedule","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Next steps"],"text":"Lend a machine to another pool every weekday evening, for example to run night devkit builds, and get it back before people arrive. Before you start. You are an administrator. Both pools exist, for example HT2 Spare (where the agent lives by day) and HT2 Devkits (where it works at night). See Create a pool for console devkits. Check the scheduler's time zone: it is shown at the top of the Pool Scheduler, for example Times in Europe/London. Change it under Settings if needed. Steps. Open Agents , click the Pools tab, then Scheduled moves. Click Add scheduled move. In Agent , pick the machine, for example ht-win-agent-01. The list shows which pool each agent is in now. In From pool , pick HT2 Spare. Or pick Any pool to move it from wherever it is. In To pool , pick HT2 Devkits. Set Time to 20:00. Under Days , click the quick pick Mon–Fri. Tick Move it back at and set 07:00. This adds the return move as a second rule, on the same days. Tick Clean work folder first only if the night builds need a clean disk. Click Save. One dialog adds the evening move and the morning return. Check it worked. The Scheduled moves list now has two rows for the agent: HT2 Spare → HT2 Devkits at 20:00, and back at 07:00, both Mon–Fri and switched On. Last run says Never until the first evening. On the Overview tab, the Next scheduled move card shows the next time and agent. The next morning, check Recent moves on the Overview tab: it lists both moves with the time and the rule that made them. The evening move and its return. The Overview tab shows the next move and, later, the moves made. To test the rules without waiting for the evening, click Run due rules now. It runs every move whose time has already passed today. Troubleshooting. Problem. What to do. The agent didn't move at 20:00. If it was busy, it moves as soon as its build ends, up to 2 hours late; after that the move is skipped and logged. Offline agents are skipped. Check Recent moves for the note. Nothing moved at all. The scheduler may be Paused (see the strip at the top), or the rule's On switch is off. The agent moved an hour early or late. The scheduler's time zone differs from yours. Change it under Settings. The agent is not in the Agent list. Personal agents are never moved, so they are not listed. Next steps. The Pool Scheduler : the board, pause, settings and the activity log. Give release builds priority on agents."},
{"title":"Ask for a value when a build starts","href":"how-to/params-ask-for-value","headings":["When to use this","Steps","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Make the Run button ask for a version before the build begins. When to use this. Use it when a person must decide something for each build, such as the version to release. Steps. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Parameters. Click Add parameter. Name it RELEASE_VERSION , kind Configuration parameter , value type Text. Click Show allowed value , choose Regex and type \\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+. Type a message: Use a version like 1.4.0. Click Customize the run dialog. Set Display to Prompt , Label to Version to release. Click Save. Click Run on the build configuration. The Run dialog opens. Type the version and click Run. A worked example. A step uses it: powershell -File Build\\release.ps1 -Version %RELEASE_VERSION%. What you see when it runs. The Run dialog shows Version to release. A value like abc is refused with your message and the build does not start. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. A schedule starts the build with an empty value. Give a default value, or run the build only by hand. Related pages. Asked parameters. Parameter with a value type."},
{"title":"Run nightly at 02:00","href":"how-to/params-nightly-0200","headings":["When to use this","Steps","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Build every night at two o'clock on weekdays. When to use this. Use it for a nightly build, cook or test run. Steps. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Triggers. Click Add new trigger , then Add next to Schedule Trigger. Choose Custom. In Cron line type 0 2 * * 1-5. Click Save. A worked example. For every day at 02:00, choose Every day , hour 2 , minute 0. To spread several builds, leave the minute empty: it becomes H. What you see when it runs. The Triggers page shows On a cron schedule or Daily at 02:00 , and the summary says Runs every day at 02:00. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. It runs at the wrong hour. The time zone of the server is used. Add TZ=Europe/London as the first line of a custom schedule. Related pages. Schedule trigger."},
{"title":"Pass a value from one step to the next","href":"how-to/params-pass-value-between-steps","headings":["When to use this","Steps","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Work out a value in one step and use it in a later step. When to use this. Use it when a step discovers something that later steps need: a version, a package name, a folder. Steps. Open the first step of the build configuration ( Settings › Build steps ). In its PowerShell script, read the value and print a setParameter message (below). In a later step, type %version% where the value belongs. Save and run. A worked example. $v = (Get-Content Build\\version.txt).Trim() Write-Host \"##buildspawn[setParameter name='version' value='$v']\". A later step: powershell -File Build\\ci.ps1 -Stage package -Version %version%. What you see when it runs. The console shows the message line. The later step shows the value in its command. Use the name env.VERSION instead to get an environment variable. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The value is missing in the same step. It reaches only the steps after the one that printed it. The message is refused. Only a step's own script can send it. The log says why. Related pages. Set parameters from a build. Service messages."},
{"title":"Run B when A succeeds","href":"how-to/params-run-b-after-a","headings":["When to use this","Steps","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Start the Unit Tests build after the Linux Server Build succeeds. When to use this. Use it when one build must follow another. Steps. Open the build configuration Unit Tests (this is B) and go to Settings › Triggers. Click Add new trigger , then Add next to Finish Build Trigger. In Build configurations to watch type Game/Linux Server Build (this is A). Keep Stable so that only a good A starts B. Click Save. If B must test exactly A's code, also add a snapshot dependency on A ( Settings › Dependencies ). A worked example. Each stable Linux Server Build now starts Unit Tests. If B needs A's files, add an artifact dependency. What you see when it runs. The Triggers page of B says after Game/Linux Server Build finishes. The log of A says it triggered B. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. B does not start. The result of A was worse than Stable, or the name is wrong. Related pages. Finish Build trigger. Dependencies."},
{"title":"Run on every Perforce submit","href":"how-to/params-run-on-perforce-submit","headings":["When to use this","Steps","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Start a build when a change is submitted to Perforce. When to use this. Use it to build each submit quickly. You can poll, or let Perforce call Buildspawn. Steps. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Triggers. To poll: add the VCS Trigger , set Poll schedule to H/5 * * * * and a Quiet period of 30. To start at once: add P4 triggered build. (or Perforce root: build when the Perforce server triggers it on a Perforce root). Copy the line it shows. On the Perforce server, run p4 triggers and add the line to the list. Save, then submit a test change. A worked example. Skyforge uses the second way for the Windows Build. A submit to //depot/game/main starts a build within seconds. What you see when it runs. The build's change log lists the changelist. %build.vcs.number% is that number. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Nothing starts. Open the P4 Trigger Log page and check the trigger line and API token. Related pages. Perforce trigger (P4). Perforce root trigger. VCS trigger. Build every submit."},
{"title":"Use a secret in PowerShell","href":"how-to/params-secret-powershell","headings":["When to use this","Steps","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Read a password parameter in a PowerShell step without printing it. When to use this. Use it to give a key or token to a tool. Steps. Open Settings › Parameters and click Add parameter. Name it STORE_TOKEN , choose Kind › Environment variable (env.) and Value type › Password. Type the token and click Save. In the PowerShell step write the script below. Do not write %STORE_TOKEN% in the script: Buildspawn refuses to put a secret into a script. A worked example. upload-tool --token $env:STORE_TOKEN. For a credential you share between build configurations, use a credential and the Use secret text(s) or file(s) build feature. What you see when it runs. The console shows dots wherever the value would appear. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The step fails with a secret message. Read the value from the environment variable named in the message. Related pages. Secrets and Vault. Password parameter. Use a secret."},
{"title":"Set the build name from a script","href":"how-to/params-set-build-name","headings":["When to use this","Steps","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Name each build after the version and the changelist. When to use this. Use it so the build page and History show a name that means something, such as 1.4.212-CL48213. Steps. Open the step that knows the version ( Settings › Build steps ). Add a line that prints a buildNumber message (below). Save and run. A worked example. Write-Host \"##buildspawn[buildNumber '1.4.%build.counter%-CL%build.vcs.number%']\". A name can use %build.counter% , a parameter, %build.vcs.number% , {build.number} for the name so far, and %% for a percent sign. It can be 200 characters at most. What you see when it runs. The build page and the History tab show 1.4.212-CL48213. The number and address of the build stay the same. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The name did not change. An unknown parameter or a password in the name is refused. The log says why. Related pages. Service messages."},
{"title":"Use another build's artifacts","href":"how-to/params-use-other-build-artifacts","headings":["When to use this","Steps","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Copy the files of a cook build into your installer build. When to use this. Use it when a build needs files that another build made. Steps. Open the Game/Installer build configuration and go to Settings › Dependencies. Add a snapshot dependency on Game/Cook Content , so both build the same revision. Click Add artifact dependency , choose Game/Cook Content and Get artifacts from › Build from the same chain. In Artifact rules type Cooked/**/* => Content. Tick Clean destination paths before downloading artifacts. Click Save. Make sure the cook build keeps Cooked/** as artifacts. A worked example. A step in the installer reads Content\\. in its workspace. What you see when it runs. The installer's console lists the files copied before the steps begin. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Same chain is refused. Add the snapshot dependency first. No artifacts found. The cook build did not keep them. Check its artifact settings. Related pages. Dependencies. Build chains."},
{"title":"Pass a version number between steps","href":"how-to/pass-values-between-steps","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Next steps"],"text":"Work out a version number in one step and use it in the steps after it, with one ##buildspawn line. A step passes a value on by printing a service message : a line that starts with ##buildspawn. Buildspawn reads it from the step's output and gives the value to every later step. You don't need files or extra scripts. Before you start. A build configuration you can edit. Here it is HT1 Version Pass in the How-to project. A way to work out the version: a file in your workspace, a tag, or a rule such as \"1.4 plus the build number\". Steps. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Build Steps. Click Add build step , pick Command Line and name the step Work out version. Choose Custom script and type the script. It prints the version twice: once as a build parameter, once as an environment variable. @echo off rem Work out the version: 1.4 plus the build number. set V=1.4.%BUILD_NUMBER% echo Version is %V% echo ##buildspawn[setParameter name='version' value='%V%'] echo ##buildspawn[setParameter name='env.GAME_VERSION' value='%V%']. To read the version from a file in your workspace instead: for /f %%v in (Build\\version.txt) do echo ##buildspawn[setParameter name='version' value='%%v']. Click Save. Click Add build step again, pick Command Line and name it Package. Use the value with %version% , or read GAME_VERSION from the environment: @echo off echo Packaging Skyforge-%version%.zip echo GAME_VERSION in the environment: %GAME_VERSION%. Click Save. Click Run. Two steps: the first works out the version, the second uses it. On a Linux or macOS agent, or in a PowerShell step, print the same line: # Shell v=$(cat Build/version.txt) echo \"##buildspawn[setParameter name='version' value='$v']\" # PowerShell $v = (Get-Content Build\\version.txt).Trim() Write-Host \"##buildspawn[setParameter name='version' value='$v']\". Check it worked. Open the build and click Console Output , then Expand all. The first step prints the two ##buildspawn lines. The second step prints the version it received. Step 1 sets the value; step 2 reads 1.4.1 both ways. Troubleshooting. The later step still sees the old value, or %version% as text. The value reaches the steps after the one that printed it, not the rest of the same step. Print the message in an earlier step. The log says the name was refused. Some environment variables can't be set from a step, such as PATH , COMSPEC or any GIT_* name. Pick another name, or use a plain name without env. See Set parameters from a build. The value is cut off or wrong. Quotes and line breaks must be escaped with | : write |' for a quote and |n for a new line. Nothing happens. The message must come from the step's own output, in single quotes, exactly as ##buildspawn[setParameter name='…' value='…']. A batch script that echoes its commands also prints the line with %V% unexpanded; start it with @echo off to keep the log clean. Next steps. Set parameters from a build : the rules, the refused names and step outputs. Service messages : every ##buildspawn message. Parameters : configuration, env. and system. parameters. Share settings between build configurations."},
{"title":"Archive build output","href":"how-to/projects-archive-build-output","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Add the post-build action","Use the files in another build","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Related pages"],"text":"Keep the files of a build so people can download them later, and reuse them in other builds. The Windows Build writes the game to Output/Win64. The studio wants the zip files kept with the build and the symbols left out. Before you start. A build step that writes the files into the workspace. The Configure permission. Steps. Add the post-build action. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Post-build Actions. Click Add post-build action and choose Archive the artifacts. In Files to archive type Output/Win64/**/*.zip. Open More options. In Excludes type **/*.pdb. Tick Archive artifacts only if build is successful. Click Save. Use the files in another build. Open the other build configuration, Settings › More › Dependencies. Add an artifact dependency on Windows Build with the rule Output/Win64/*.zip => packages. See Dependencies. Check it worked. Run the build. The build page has an Artifacts list with the zip files. The log says Archived N artifacts. Download one file and open it. Troubleshooting. Problem. What to do. No files found to archive. The pattern does not match. It is relative to the workspace. Check the step that makes the files. The disk fills up. Keep the artifacts of fewer builds. See Keep the last 30 builds. Related pages. Archive the artifacts. Dependencies. Artifacts on the build page."},
{"title":"Create a build configuration from a template","href":"how-to/projects-from-template","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Create it from the template","Change what differs","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Related pages"],"text":"Make a new build configuration that shares its steps with a template, so a change to the template reaches all of them. The studio has a template Platform build with the compile, cook and package steps. It now needs a Switch Build that does the same for the Nintendo Switch. Before you start. A template in the project, or in a project above it. See Templates. The Create permission on the project. Steps. Create it from the template. Open the project and click New Item. Type Switch Build. Check the ID. Under Start from choose Template. The list shows the templates you can use here, with their number of steps and parameters. Pick Platform build. Leave Keep linked to the template on, so later changes to the template reach this build. Clear it to copy the steps once. Under Type choose Build configuration (the card says linked to the template ). Click Create. The New Item page. With Template chosen, the summary says what is created. Change what differs. On Settings › Parameters , click Override on PLATFORM and type Switch. On General choose the Nintendo Switch icon. Add its own triggers and build features. Templates do not carry them. Click Save. Check it worked. On Settings › Build Steps , the steps carry a chip with the template name. Open More › Templates. It lists Platform build. Open the template. Used by lists Switch Build. Run it. The log shows the template steps with PLATFORM set to Switch. Troubleshooting. Problem. What to do. Template is missing from Start from. No template is within reach. A build configuration can use the templates of its own project and the projects above. I want to stop following the template. Detach it. The build configuration keeps a copy of the steps. See Templates. Related pages. Templates. Copying settings. Share settings with a whole project."},
{"title":"Keep the last 30 builds","href":"how-to/projects-keep-last-30-builds","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Set the rules","Keep one build forever","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Related pages"],"text":"Stop the disk filling up: keep 30 builds of a build configuration, and the artifacts of fewer. The Windows Build makes a 2 GB package per build. The studio wants the log and the change list of the last 30 builds, but the package of only the last 5. Before you start. The Configure permission on the build configuration. Steps. Set the rules. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then General. Go to Clean-up rules and turn on Discard old builds. Leave Strategy on Keep by age and number. Type 30 in Builds to keep. Leave Days to keep builds empty. Type 5 in Builds to keep artifacts of. Read the line In words. Click Save. Keep one build forever. Open a release build. Click Keep this build forever. The rules never delete it. Check it worked. Run a build. After it ends, builds older than the 30th disappear from History. Build 6 and older have no files under Artifacts , but their logs are there. The pinned build is still there. Troubleshooting. Problem. What to do. Nothing is deleted. The rules run after each build. Run one more. I need a build that was deleted. It cannot be restored. Pin builds you may need before the rules reach them. I want the same rule for every build configuration in a project. Set it on the project: Build settings passes steps and parameters. For a server-wide rule ask an administrator. Related pages. Retention and discard old builds. General. Archive build output."},
{"title":"Move a build configuration","href":"how-to/projects-move-build-configuration","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Move it","Fix what changed","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Related pages"],"text":"Move a build configuration to another project and know what will change before it does. The studio moves Windows Build from Skyforge to Skyforge/Main. It keeps its builds, settings and ID. Before you start. The Configure permission on the build configuration and the Create permission on the destination. No build of it is running. Steps. Move it. Open the build configuration and click Move. Under Move to choose Skyforge » Main. Click Move. If the page lists problems, read them. Each one is a setting that will change because the new project passes down other things. Click Move anyway only if that is fine. The Move page. Fix what changed. Open Settings › Parameters and check the Defined in column. Add the parameters it lost to the new project, or to the build configuration. Open More › Dependencies and check that the dependencies still point to the right build configurations. Check it worked. The build configuration shows in the new project in the tree. Its ID is unchanged. The old address no longer works. The address /buildConfiguration/<id> still does. Run it once. Troubleshooting. Problem. What to do. Move is refused: a build is running. Stop it or wait, then move. Move is refused: the destination is archived. Unarchive the destination first. Move is refused: a template is in use. Detach the build configurations from the template first. Related pages. Rename, move and delete. IDs."},
{"title":"Organise projects for a game","href":"how-to/projects-organise-game","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Make the game project","Make the three projects inside","Put shared settings at the right level","Add the build configurations","Order the tree","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Related pages"],"text":"Set up a project tree for one game, with Main, Drops and Release, so each team finds its builds and shared settings sit in one place. The studio Skyforge makes one game. It has day-to-day builds from the Main stream, nightly builds that are given to testers (Drops), and release builds that go to the stores. Each group gets its own project inside the project Skyforge. Before you start. The Create permission on the place where the project goes. A rough list of the build configurations you want. Steps. Make the game project. Open the Projects page and click New Item. Type Skyforge. The ID becomes Skyforge. Under Type click Project and click Create. On its Settings give it a description and an icon, then click Save. Make the three projects inside. Open Skyforge and click New Item. Type Main , choose Project , click Create. Repeat for Drops and Release. Their IDs are Skyforge_Main , Skyforge_Drops and Skyforge_Release. Put shared settings at the right level. On Skyforge open Build settings and add what every build needs, for example the parameter STUDIO_NAME. See Share settings with a whole project. On Skyforge open Perforce roots and add the root of the depot once. Every build configuration below can use it. On Release open Credentials and add the store accounts. Only the release builds can use them. Click Save on each page. Add the build configurations. Open Main , click New Item , and make Windows Build as a Build configuration. For the other platforms use Start from Copy of an existing item , or a template. See Create a build configuration from a template. Give each one its platform icon on General. Order the tree. Open Skyforge › Settings. In Order of items open the Reorder dialog. Put Main , Drops and Release in that order. Add a separator if you want a heading. See Tree order and separators. Click Save. Check it worked. The project tree shows Skyforge with Main , Drops and Release in your order. Open a build configuration in Main. Under Settings › Parameters , STUDIO_NAME shows as defined in Skyforge. The dot after Skyforge shows the worst result inside it. Troubleshooting. Problem. What to do. I cannot see Order of items. Only administrators can reorder the tree. A build in Release does not see the store credentials. Credentials of Release work only for build configurations inside Release. Put the credential higher up only if all builds need it. I want to move a build configuration between the three projects. See Move a build configuration. Related pages. Projects. Tree order and separators. IDs and the parent-ID prefix."},
{"title":"Pause a whole project","href":"how-to/projects-pause-project","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Pause","Resume","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Related pages"],"text":"Stop all automatic builds in a project, for example during a branch freeze, and resume later. Release 1.4 of Skyforge is frozen for two days. The studio pauses the project Skyforge/Main so no trigger starts a build. Testers can still start one by hand. Before you start. The Configure permission on the project. Steps. Pause. Open the project Main and click Pause and archive. In Why it is paused type Release 1.4 freeze until Friday. Click Pause. Resume. Open the project again, click Pause and archive. Click Resume. Check it worked. The page says Paused by. on. with your reason. A build configuration inside shows Paused from above. Triggers and polling do not start builds. Click Run on one: it still starts. Builds that were running finish. Troubleshooting. Problem. What to do. A build configuration inside is still paused after Resume. It also has its own pause. Open its Pause tab. I want nothing to run, not even by hand. Archive the project instead. See Pause and archive. Related pages. Pause and archive. Projects."},
{"title":"Make a Pipeline from a script","href":"how-to/projects-pipeline-script","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Write the script in Buildspawn","Move the script to version control","Handle the sandbox","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Related pages"],"text":"Create a Pipeline build configuration, write the script in Buildspawn or keep it in version control, and handle the sandbox. The studio builds the launcher with a Pipeline. First the script is typed in Buildspawn to try it. Then it moves to a script file in Git. Before you start. The Create permission on the project. For the SCM version: a Git or Perforce repository that holds the file, and a credential that may read it. Steps. Write the script in Buildspawn. Open the project and click New Item. Type Launcher. Under Type choose Pipeline. Click Create. Open the Pipeline tab. Definition is Pipeline script. Type the script into Script. Leave Use Groovy Sandbox on. Click Save and Run. pipeline { agent any stages { stage(\"Build\") { steps { bat \"build.bat\" } } stage(\"Test\") { steps { bat \"test.bat\" } } } }. Move the script to version control. Put the same text in a file such as ci/build.pipeline in the repository and commit it. Open the Pipeline, Settings › Pipeline. Set Definition to Pipeline script from SCM. Choose the SCM and fill in the repository and credential. In Script Path replace the name the field starts with by the path of your file, for example ci/build.pipeline. Leave Lightweight checkout on. Click Save and Run. Handle the sandbox. If a build fails with Scripts not permitted to use method. , ask an administrator to open Script approval in the global settings. The administrator reads the signature and approves it only if it is safe. Run the build again. Check it worked. The build page shows the stages Build and Test as a graph with times. Settings › Build Steps lists the stages read from the script. Edit the file in Git, commit and run again: the build uses the new file. Troubleshooting. Problem. What to do. Script file not found. The Script Path or the branch is wrong. Scripts not permitted to use method. The sandbox blocked it. Get it approved or change the script. The script waits for approval. The sandbox is off and you are not an administrator. Turn it on. Related pages. Pipeline. Build Steps. Canvas and palette."},
{"title":"Make a tab that shows only your team's build configurations","href":"how-to/projects-team-view","headings":["Before you start","Steps","What you see","Make it the first tab people see","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Add a view to the Projects page so the build team sees its own builds first, without the noise of everything else. Before you start. You need the permission to create views. If you do not see a + at the end of the tab bar, ask an administrator. Name your build configurations with a team prefix, for example Engine-Win64 and Engine-PS5. A pattern is then easy to write. See Views for every field. Steps. Open the Projects page. Under the page title, click + at the end of the tab bar. Type the Name Engine team. Under Type choose List View. Click Create. Type a Description , for example Builds owned by the engine team. Under Jobs tick Recurse in subfolders , so builds inside projects are found too. Tick Use a pattern instead. In Pattern type (.*/)?Engine-.*. This matches every build configuration whose name starts with Engine- , at the top or in any project. Under Filters click Add Build Configuration Filter and choose Status Filter. Pick Enabled build configurations only , so old paused ones stay out. Under Columns , check the list. Add Last Failure if the team wants to see when a build last broke. Click Save. What you see. The new tab Engine team appears next to All. Open it: the table lists only the build configurations that match. The Summary on Edit View says how many items it shows now, for example Shows 6 items. Make it the first tab people see. An administrator can open Settings › System and choose Default view Engine team. Everyone then lands on that tab. Or keep a private tab in My Views. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The tab is empty. The pattern must match the whole name. Check the prefix and the path, and end the pattern with.*. A new Engine- build is missing. The pattern finds new builds by name. If you ticked builds by hand instead, tick the new one. You want to delete the tab. Open it, click ⋯ and choose Delete View. The build configurations stay. Related pages. Views. Projects. Organise projects for a game."},
{"title":"Re-run a failed build from the failing step","href":"how-to/rerun-from-failed-step","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Resume from the failed step","Resume from an earlier step","Re-run from the start","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Next steps"],"text":"When a build fails near the end, fix the problem and resume it at the failed step, so the steps that already passed don't run again. Resume works for build configurations that use Perforce from version 1.9.3. In older versions, the resumed build failed at its first step with a NullPointerException. If you see that, upgrade the server. Before you start. You have the Build permission on the build configuration, for example through the Developer role. The build has failed, and you know why. See Find out why a build failed. You have fixed the cause outside the build, for example the file share is back online or the device kit has restarted. Resume runs the same code again; it does not pick up new changes. Pick the right tool: Resume : same agent, same workspace, no new sync, starts at a step you choose. Fastest after a problem with the machine or the network. Re-run : a new build of the same sources and parameters from the start. Use it when the workspace was used by another build since, or to check whether a failure is flaky. Re-run with changes : the same build, but with one thing different, such as a parameter, a changelist or a shelf. Steps. Resume from the failed step. Open the failed build. Look at the Resume from here card on the right. It checks three things and shows a green tick for each: the agent is online; the workspace still exists; no other build has used the workspace since. Click Resume from step N at the top of the page. The same button sits on the failed step in Build steps , and Resume from here in the card does the same. The new build appears in the queue. Click it when it starts. Step 2 and 3: the checks are green, so the build can resume at step 3. Resume from an earlier step. Open the failed build and click the sliders button beside Re-run. Its tooltip says Re-run with changes…. Under Start from step , pick a step. You can pick The beginning , any step that passed, or the step marked Failed here. Later steps can't be start points. Read the blue box under the list. It sums up what will happen: the sources, the parameters, the agent and workspace, and which steps show as done. Click Re-run at the bottom of the dialog. The Re-run dialog of a failed build. Choosing a step other than The beginning makes it a resume. Re-run from the start. Open the build and click Re-run (on a failed build that can't resume, it says Re-run from the start ). To change something first, click the sliders button instead, change a value on the General , Parameters , Changes or Shelved changes tab, and click Re-run. Check it worked. The resumed build ran only step 3. Steps 1 and 2 link to their logs in #1. The new build has a new number. Its Cause says Resumed #1 from step 3. The skipped steps show Done in #1. Click #1 log to read their output. The Resumed from card shows the agent and workspace, which are the same as the original's. The first lines of its console say Same agent and workspace as #1. Workspace not modified since #1 finished. Troubleshooting. Problem. What to do. The Resume button is grey and the main button says Re-run from the start. A check in the card failed: the agent is offline, the workspace was deleted, or another build used it. Bring the agent back online and try again, or re-run from the start. The resumed build of a Perforce build configuration fails at its first step with a NullPointerException. The server is older than 1.9.3. Resume with a Perforce root works from 1.9.3 on. Upgrade the server, then resume again. The resumed build fails the same way. The cause is still there, or it is in the code. Resume does not sync new changes: once you have submitted a fix, use Re-run with changes and choose Latest , or start a new build. You want a step to try again on its own next time. Open the step in Settings › Build Steps and set Retry on failure under Execution. See Resume and retry. The Resume button is missing. The build passed, or you don't have the Build permission. Ask a project administrator for the Developer"},
{"title":"Run a build with different parameters","href":"how-to/run-build-with-different-parameters","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Change the parameters of a build that already ran","Check it worked","Problems and fixes","Next steps"],"text":"Start one build with other values than the defaults, for example a Shipping build instead of Development, without changing the build configuration. Before you start. A build configuration with at least one parameter that people may set. See Parameters. In this guide it is Game / Windows Build , with the parameters CONFIG (default Development ) and CLEAN (default false ). The permission to run the build configuration, for example through the Developer role. Steps. Open the build configuration, for example Game &rsaquo; Windows Build. Click the small button with the sliders, right beside Run. Its tooltip says Run custom build. If a parameter is set to Prompt , click Run… instead. Open the Parameters tab. The number on the tab says how many parameters you can set. Change CONFIG to Shipping. Type it, or pick it from the list if the parameter is a list. Leave the others as they are. To go back to a default, click Reset beside the field. For a password parameter, click Change Password and type the value for this build only. The stored value stays as it is. On the General tab, you can write a Comment , such as Shipping check for the demo. It shows on the build page and in the build list. Click Run Build. The Run custom build dialog. The Parameters tab shows how many values you can set. Change the parameters of a build that already ran. To run an older build again with one value changed, use Re-run with changes. Open the build, click the button with the sliders beside Re-run , open Parameters (filled in from that build), change a value and click Re-run. See Re-run a build. Check it worked. Open the new build and its Summary. The Parameters row lists the values it used, for example CONFIG = Shipping. A password shows as ****. Click the Parameters tab for the full list. In the Console Output , find the line of your step that prints the configuration. Your comment is shown on the build page. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. There is no Parameters tab. The build configuration has no parameters that people may set, or they are all hidden. Add one under Settings &rsaquo; Parameters , and set its display so it is not hidden. A field is read only. The parameter is set to read only. A project administrator can change that on its settings. The dialog refuses my value. The parameter has a rule, for example a list of allowed values or a text that must not be empty. The dialog opens on the Parameters tab and says why. The dialog refuses to change a value and mentions script text or Vault. Only people who can configure the build configuration may change values that end up in script text, and Vault values. The step still used the default. Check that the step reads the value by its exact name, for example %CONFIG%. Names are case sensitive. Next steps. The Run dialog : every option. Parameters : the types and how to add them. Re-run a failed build from the failing step."},
{"title":"Compare two builds","href":"how-to/run-compare-two-builds","headings":["Before you start","Steps","A worked example","What you see","Problems and fixes","Next steps"],"text":"Find out what differs between a build that passed and a build that failed, or between last week's build and today's: the code, the values, the agent and the times. Before you start. You can see the project: at least the Project viewer role. You know the two builds. In this guide they are Game / Windows Build #2, which passed, and #3, which failed. Buildspawn has no single \"compare\" button. You compare the two build pages, which show the same cards in the same places. Open them in two browser windows side by side. Steps. Open the build configuration and click its History tab. Open build #2 in one window and build #3 in another. Info line. Under the title, compare the start time, how long it took, who started it, the agent and the changelist or commit. A different agent or a long gap in time often explains a difference. Summary. Compare Waited in queue , Executed on , Cause and Parameters. For the complete list, click the Parameters tab of both builds. A value that differs, such as CONFIG , is a cause to check first. Changes. Open the Changes tab of #3. It lists the changelists or commits that are new since #2. When the builds are not next to each other, open the build configuration's Change Log tab: it lists the changes of all builds in between. Steps. On the Overview tab, compare each step's duration in Build steps. A step that takes much longer in one build is a lead. Console. Open Console Output in both, switch the Time switch to Elapsed , and compare the blocks. Use Search in log for the same word in both, for example the name of a file. Tests. If the builds have a Test Result tab, compare the counts. The numbers in brackets show the change against the build before. Trend. Open the Statistics tab. Hover over the point of each build in the duration, queue time, test count and artifacts size charts to see both values in one place. Filter by Agent to see whether one machine is slower. See The Statistics tab. A worked example. On the docs server, build #2 of Windows Build took 9 seconds and passed. Build #3 failed after 5.9 seconds. Both ran on win-build-01 with CONFIG = Development , so the machine and the parameters are not the cause. The Overview of #3 says Decided by: Failed: Step \"Compile\" exited with code 1 , and the console shows error C2065: Game/Weather.cpp(88): 'StormIntensity': undeclared identifier. Suppose the Changes tab of #3 lists a changelist by a colleague that touched Game/Weather.cpp. That is the cause. If it says No changes instead, #3 built the same changelist as #2, and you look at the machine, the parameters and the time. What you see. You can say what is different between the two builds: the code, the values, the machine or the time. If nothing differs, the failure is flaky: re-run the build and see. See Re-run a failed build from the failing step. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The Changes tab says No changes. The build used the same changelist or commit as the build before it. Compare the agent, the parameters and the time instead. An old build has no times in the console. Builds from before version 1.1 have no per-line times. I cannot open one of the builds. It may be a personal build of someone else. Ask them to share it. A build is gone. Clean-up rules deleted it. Pin the builds you want to keep with the pin button in the header. Next steps. Find out why a build failed. The build page. History."},
{"title":"Download artifacts","href":"how-to/run-download-artifacts","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Download one file","Download everything as a zip","Download only some files","Download the files of the last good build from a script","Check it worked","Problems and fixes","Next steps"],"text":"Get the files a build kept, such as a game package or symbols: one file, a whole folder as a zip, or the files of the last good build from a script. Before you start. The build kept files. A build configuration keeps files with the Archive the artifacts post-build action and a pattern such as Output/**. Builds that kept files have an Artifacts tab. You can see the project (Project viewer role or more). The build still exists. Clean-up rules delete artifacts together with their build. Pin a build to keep it. Steps. Download one file. Open the build, for example Game / Windows Build #7. On the Overview tab, the Build Artifacts card lists the files. Click the download arrow beside a file. view shows a text file in the browser. Or click the Artifacts tab, click folders to open them, and click a file to download it. The Artifacts tab. Download everything as a zip. On the Artifacts tab, click (all files in zip). Or click it on the Overview card. Your browser saves archive.zip. Download only some files. On the Artifacts tab, type a pattern in the Pattern box, for example **/*.pdb , and press the arrow. Only the matching files are listed. Download them one by one. Download the files of the last good build from a script. Files have a stable address. The words lastSuccessfulBuild in the address always mean the newest build that passed. Use a user name and an API token : # PowerShell $cred = \"docs:YOUR-API-TOKEN\" curl.exe -u $cred -O \"http://buildserver:8080/job/Game/job/Windows%20Build/lastSuccessfulBuild/artifact/Output/Skyforge-Win64-Development.txt\" # The same build as a zip of every file curl.exe -u $cred -o archive.zip \"http://buildserver:8080/job/Game/job/Windows%20Build/lastSuccessfulBuild/artifact/*zip*/archive.zip\". Replace lastSuccessfulBuild with a build number, such as 7 , to get a certain build. Personal builds are never taken as last successful. Spaces in names are written %20. Check it worked. The file opens, and its size matches the size shown beside it. The zip holds the same folders as the Artifacts tab. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. There is no Artifacts tab. The build kept no files. Check the Archive the artifacts post-build action and its pattern on the build configuration, and read the log for the line that says what was archived. A failed build may not get to that step. The files are gone. A clean-up rule deleted the build. Pin the builds you need. The script gets a sign-in page. Send the user name and API token with the request, as in the example. The file is not in the artifacts, but it is in the workspace. The pattern did not match it. Add it to the pattern. A script can also pass a folder to the pattern with setParameter. See Service messages. Next steps. Changes, artifacts and tests. Start builds from a script. The build page."},
{"title":"Follow a build from the terminal","href":"how-to/run-follow-build-from-terminal","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Follow a running build","Stop the build","Without the live screen, for scripts","A worked example","What you see when it works","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Watch a running build's log scroll by in a terminal, find the build that failed last night, and stop a build, without opening a browser. Before you start. You have the terminal program, buildspawn. It comes with every server download. See The terminal program. You can reach the server's address from your computer, for example http://buildserver:8080. To stop a build you need an API token. To only read, you need none. See Watch the server from a terminal for how to make the token file. Steps. Follow a running build. Start the monitor on your server: buildspawn --url http://buildserver:8080 --token-file ~/.buildspawn-token. On Windows, from the program folder, type.\\bin\\buildspawn.exe. Press 2 for the Builds view. It lists every build of the last 24 hours, newest first. Running builds show a progress bar and a percentage. Press f and choose the result running , or press / and type part of the build configuration's name, for example Windows. Select the build with &uarr; and &darr; The card below the list shows its agent, its cause and the result of the last five builds. Press Enter. The Logs view opens with this build's log. Press f to follow the log. New lines appear as the build writes them. Press PgUp to scroll back, and f again to follow again. Press l to change the level and / to filter the lines, for example error. Press s to save the log to a file. Press 2 to go back to the build list, or o to open the build in your browser. Stop the build. In the Builds view, select the running build. Press x. The bottom line says x stops the selected build when your token is in use. See Stop a build. Without the live screen, for scripts. Every view has a text version that prints once and exits: buildspawn builds --running --url http://buildserver:8080 --token-file ~/.buildspawn-token buildspawn builds --failed --limit 5 --json. A worked example. You start the nightly Game / Windows Build before you leave, and want to know at home whether the cook has started. On your laptop: Buildspawn 1.9.3 http://buildserver:8080 Start Job # Result Time Agent ▸ 23:02 Game/Windows Build #8 ▶ ██████ 42% 31:10 win-build-01 22:41 Engine/Unit Tests #4 ✓ success 5:12 win-build-02. You press Enter on the running build, then f , and watch the Cook content step go by. When it says the cook is done, you press q and go to bed. What you see when it works. The Builds view refreshes by itself, every 2 seconds by default, and says LIVE at the top right. The log view shows the build's lines with times, as the browser console does. After x , the build ends as aborted in the list. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. x is refused. The monitor has no valid token. Check the token file: one line, user:token , and a token that was not revoked. Typing buildspawn on Windows opens the app. Type.\\bin\\buildspawn.exe in the program folder, or open a new terminal elsewhere after the Windows setup. The log does not move. The step may not write output. The block in the browser console says Waiting for output then. Press Space to check that the screen is not frozen. The monitor can't connect. Open the same address in a browser. Include the port and http:// or https://. Related pages. Watch the server from a terminal. Terminal program commands. The build console : the browser version."},
{"title":"Run steps in parallel on several agents","href":"how-to/run-steps-in-parallel","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Check it worked","When the sides are the same steps for many platforms","Troubleshooting","Next steps"],"text":"Cut build time by running independent work, such as a content cook and a shader compile, on two agents at the same time. Before you start. You can edit the build configuration (its Settings tab). At least two agents can run this build configuration. They must be in a pool that serves its project (see Pools ). The steps you run side by side don't need each other's output. Steps. Open the build configuration, click the Settings tab, then Build Steps. Click Add Parallel block. A block named Parallel with two empty sides is added. Drag it to where it should run, for example after a Prepare step. Give the block a clear name, for example Cook and shaders. Under How the sides run , choose Separate machines. Each side then runs as a side build on its own agent. Under If one side fails , keep Let the others finish , or choose Stop the others to save agent time. Name the first side, for example Cook Win64 , and click Add step in its header to add its steps. Set the side's Run on : A specific agent , A pool or label , or Any matching machine. Here it is the agent win-build-01. In Files sent back , add a rule for the files that later steps need, for example Saved/Cooked/**. Set up the second side the same way, for example Compile shaders on win-build-02 , sending back Saved/Shaders/**. Click Add side if you need more sides. Under After the block , leave Fallback after at 10 minutes unless your agents are often busy for longer. Save, then click Run. A Parallel block with two sides, each on its own agent. Check it worked. Open the running build. Under Build steps , the block shows Running and the steps after it show Waiting. Open Agents. Both agents show Building , with the same build configuration under Current build. When the build ends, open Console Output and click Expand all. The block has one section per side, with the agent it ran on and its time, then a line for each set of files copied back. The main build waits while its sides run. Both sides run at once, on two agents. Each side has its own section in the console. When the sides are the same steps for many platforms. If every side runs the same steps with a different value, such as platform &times; config , use a Matrix block instead. You write the steps once, give the values, and every combination runs as its own side with %platform% and %config%. You can exclude combinations, choose where each runs, set how many run at once and pick a combination to try first. The build page then shows a grid of the results with a Re-run for each combination. See Side builds. Troubleshooting. Problem. What to do. A side waits and never starts. The agent in Run on is busy, offline, or not in a pool that serves this project. Open the queue to read the reason. Pick a pool instead of one agent, or add the project to the agent's pool. The step after the block can't find a file. Add a Files sent back rule on the side that makes the file. Only files that match a rule come back. A side fails on its second run only. Side builds reuse their agent's workspace. Make scripts safe to run twice, for example create a folder only when it is missing. The build goes on on another agent after the block. The first agent was not free again within Fallback after. Raise the minutes, or tick Never fall back. Next steps. Side builds : how they look in the queue and on the build page. Parallel blocks and overrides : every option, including This machine mode and per-step Run on. Parallel tests and composite builds. Create a pool for console devkits."},
{"title":"Start builds from a script","href":"how-to/script-with-api-token","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Make a token","Start a build with curl","Start a build and wait for the result","Do the same in PowerShell","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Next steps"],"text":"Make an API token, then start a build with parameters from curl or PowerShell, wait for it and read its result. Before you start. You have the Build permission on the build configuration, for example through the Developer role. You know the build configuration's address. Open it in the browser and copy the address, for example http://buildserver:8080/job/How-to/job/HT3%20Scripted%20build/. Each project adds a job/<name>/ part, and spaces become %20. You know its parameter names. They are listed on the build configuration's Settings › Parameters , and in the Run dialog. The examples use PLATFORM and CONFIG. curl comes with Windows 10 and later, macOS and most Linux systems. In Windows PowerShell 5.1, type curl.exe , because plain curl is a different command there. Steps. Make a token. Click your initial at the right of the top bar and choose Security. Under API Token , in Add new token , type a Name that says where the token is used, for example ht3-script. Choose an Expiration , for example 90 Days , and click Generate. Copy the token and store it somewhere safe, such as a password manager or a secret in the tool that runs your script. Then click Done. You can't see the token again. Put it in an environment variable for your script, for example BUILDSPAWN_API_TOKEN. Never write it in a script that is checked in. The token ht3-script in the token table. Last used shows when a script last signed in with it. Start a build with curl. Send a POST to buildWithParameters , with your user name and the token, and one --data-urlencode per parameter: curl -i -X POST -u \"docs:$BUILDSPAWN_API_TOKEN\" \\ \"http://buildserver:8080/job/How-to/job/HT3%20Scripted%20build/buildWithParameters\" \\ --data-urlencode \"PLATFORM=PS5\" \\ --data-urlencode \"CONFIG=Shipping\". Check the answer. 201 Created means the build is in the queue. The Location line is the queue item's address: HTTP/1.1 201 Created Location: http://buildserver:8080/queue/item/54/. Parameters you leave out get their default values. A build configuration without parameters uses /build instead of /buildWithParameters. Start a build and wait for the result. This bash script starts the build, follows the queue item to the build number and waits until the build ends. Its exit code is 0 only when the build passed, so another tool can act on it. #!/bin/bash # Start a build with parameters, wait for it and print its result. SERVER=\"http://buildserver:8080\" JOB=\"job/How-to/job/HT3%20Scripted%20build\" AUTH=\"docs:$BUILDSPAWN_API_TOKEN\" # 1. Start the build. The answer's Location header is the queue item. QUEUE=$(curl -s -i -X POST -u \"$AUTH\" \"$SERVER/$JOB/buildWithParameters\" \\ --data-urlencode \"PLATFORM=Win64\" --data-urlencode \"CONFIG=Shipping\" \\ | tr -d '\\r' | sed -n 's/^Location: //p') echo \"Queued: $QUEUE\" # 2. Wait until the queue item has a build number. until BUILD=$(curl -sg -u \"$AUTH\" \"${QUEUE}api/json?tree=executable[url]\" \\ | grep -o '\"url\":\"[^\"]*\"' | cut -d'\"' -f4) && [ -n \"$BUILD\" ]; do sleep 2; done echo \"Build: $BUILD\" # 3. Wait until the build has a result. until RESULT=$(curl -s -u \"$AUTH\" \"${BUILD}api/json?tree=result\" \\ | grep -o '\"result\":\"[A-Z]*\"' | cut -d'\"' -f4) && [ -n \"$RESULT\" ]; do sleep 5; done echo \"Result: $RESULT\" [ \"$RESULT\" = \"SUCCESS\" ]. $./start-build.sh Queued: http://buildserver:8080/queue/item/59/ Build: http://buildserver:8080/job/How-to/job/HT3%20Scripted%20build/3/ Result: SUCCESS. The -g option stops curl from reading the square brackets in tree=executable[url] as a pattern of its own. Do the same in PowerShell. # Start a build with parameters, wait for it and print its result. $server = \"http://buildserver:8080\" $job = \"job/How-to/job/HT3%20Scripted%20build\" $pair = \"docs:$env:BUILDSPAWN_API_TOKEN\" $auth = [Convert]::ToBase64String([Text.Encoding]::UTF8.GetBytes($pair)) $headers = @{ Authorization = \"Basic $auth\" } # 1. Start the build. The answer's Location header is the queue item. $response = Invoke-WebRequest -Method Post -Headers $headers -UseBasicParsi"},
{"title":"Create a pool for console devkits","href":"how-to/set-up-console-pool","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Next steps"],"text":"Keep console builds on the machines that have the devkits, and keep other builds off them. Before you start. You are an administrator. The devkit machines are already agents (see Add a Windows build agent ). You know which build configurations or project folders build for the console. Steps. Open Agents , click the Pools tab, then Create pool. In Name , type a name that says what the pool builds, for example HT2 Devkits. Under Icon , click Console , or the brand icon: PlayStation , Xbox or Nintendo Switch. Under Icon colour , pick a colour, for example Green. The Sidebar preview shows how the pool will look. Under Agents , tick the devkit machines. The Current pool column says Moves from … for each one: an agent belongs to one pool only. Under Projects , type in Add project and pick each console build configuration or folder, for example How-to » HT2 Devkit Build. A folder adds every build configuration in it. Click Create. Name, icon and colour, agents and projects on one page. A pool decides where a build may run. To also pick a particular machine, label the devkit agents (for example ps5 devkit ) and set a label expression or an agent requirement on the build configuration. See Labels and agent requirements. Set Number of executors to 1 on a devkit (see Builds at a time ). Check it worked. The pool appears in the side panel of the Agents pages with its icon and colour. Click the arrow before it to see its agents. Run one of the pool's build configurations. Its build page says on and the name of a devkit agent, and the Agents page shows that agent Building. To change the pool later, click its name. The Edit pool page has the same settings and Delete pool. The pool in the side panel, and its agent running the console build. The pool page, where you change the pool later. Troubleshooting. Problem. What to do. Console builds wait with Not in a pool assigned to this project. The build configuration is not in the pool's Projects , or it is also in another pool. Add it, or its folder, on the pool page. Other builds no longer run on the devkits. That is the point of the pool: its agents run only the pool's projects. To share a machine at night, use a scheduled move. The pool shows no agents ready. The agents are offline or still in another pool. Open the pool and check Current pool , then the agents' status on the Agents page. Next steps. Pools : how pools work, icons and the sidebar. Move agents to another pool every evening. Give release builds priority on agents. Agent settings."},
{"title":"Share settings between build configurations","href":"how-to/share-settings-with-a-project","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Give every build configuration in a project the same settings","Put the common steps in a template","Attach the template to each build configuration","Change one value for one build configuration","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Next steps"],"text":"Set a step or a parameter once and let many build configurations use it: through their project, or through a template. In this example, a Windows build and a PS5 build both print the build machine, compile and package. The project HT1 Shared gives every build configuration inside it a studio name, a drop folder and one step. The template HT1 Platform build holds the compile and package steps. The PS5 build changes one value: the platform. Before you start. A project to hold the build configurations. See Create a project. The build settings permission on that project, so you can change what it passes down. Without it the Build settings tab is read-only. Two or more build configurations in the project, or the wish to make them. See Create a build configuration. Steps. Give every build configuration in a project the same settings. Open the project and click the Build settings tab. Under Build steps , click Add build step. Pick Command Line , name the step Show build machine and type the script: echo Building %PLATFORM% on %COMPUTERNAME% for %STUDIO_NAME%. Click Save. Under Parameters , click Add parameter. Add STUDIO_NAME as a configuration parameter with the value Skyforge Studio. Click Add parameter again and add env.DROP_SHARE as an environment variable with the value \\\\files.studio.example\\builds. Click Save at the bottom of the page. The project's Build settings: one step and two parameters for every build configuration inside. Put the common steps in a template. In the same project, click New Item. Type HT1 Platform build , choose Build configuration template and click Create. On the template's Build settings , add two Command Line steps: rem Compile echo Compiling Skyforge for %PLATFORM% (%CONFIG%) rem Package echo Packaging Skyforge-%PLATFORM%-%CONFIG%.zip to %DROP_SHARE%. Use the real commands of your game here, for example an Unreal Engine or Unity step. Add two parameters to the template: PLATFORM with the value Win64 and CONFIG with the value Development. Click Save. Attach the template to each build configuration. Open the first build configuration, for example HT1 Windows Build , and click Settings. Open More › Templates. Pick How-to/HT1 Shared/HT1 Platform build in the list and click Attach template. Do the same for the other build configurations, for example HT1 PS5 Build. Pick the template and click Attach template. Only templates of this project and the projects above it are offered. Change one value for one build configuration. Open HT1 PS5 Build and go to Settings › Parameters. On the PLATFORM row, click Override. Type PS5 as the value and save. The template and the Windows build keep Win64. The PS5 build: two parameters from the project, two from the template, and PLATFORM set to PS5. Check it worked. Open a build configuration and go to Settings › Build Steps. The steps carry a chip that says where they come from: From folder How-to/HT1 Shared and From template HT1 Platform build. The project's step runs first, then the template's steps, then the build configuration's own. Open the template. The Used by tab lists both build configurations. Click Run on HT1 Windows Build. The log shows Building Win64 on … for Skyforge Studio , then the compile and package lines. Inherited steps show where they come from. The template's Used by list shows who a change reaches. Troubleshooting. The template is not in the list on the Templates page. A build configuration can only use templates of its own project and the projects above it. Move the template up, or make it in a project the build configuration sits in. The Build settings tab is read-only. You need the build settings permission on that project, not just Configure. Ask an administrator; see Users, groups and permissions. A parameter has the wrong value. The nearer setting wins: the build configuration beats its templates, the templates beat the projects. When two templates define the same parameter, the one higher in the list wins. Look at the Defined in column on th"},
{"title":"Build a .sln with MSBuild","href":"how-to/steps-build-sln-with-msbuild","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Related pages"],"text":"Restore the NuGet packages of a Visual Studio solution and build it with MSBuild on a Windows agent. Before you start. A Windows agent with Visual Studio or the Build Tools. An administrator has added an MSBuild installation under Settings › Tools. See Tools. A solution in your workspace, here Tools/BuildTools.sln. Steps. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Build Steps. Add a NuGet Installer step named Restore packages with Solution Tools/BuildTools.sln. Add an MSBuild step named Build tools. Pick the installation in MSBuild Version. Set Project File to Tools/BuildTools.sln and Command Line Arguments to /p:Configuration=Release /m. Tick Set build to unstable if there are warnings if warnings should be seen. Click Save and run the build. Check it worked. The console shows the restored packages, then the MSBuild output. Compiler errors and warnings are marked in the log. The build is successful when MSBuild ends with no errors. Troubleshooting. No installation to pick. Add one under Settings › Tools , with the folder of MSBuild.exe. Missing packages. Make sure the NuGet Installer step is above the MSBuild step. The agent has no Visual Studio. Run the build configuration on a Windows agent that has it. Related pages. MSBuild. NuGet Installer. Tools."},
{"title":"Always clean up, and collect logs after a failure","href":"how-to/steps-cleanup-after-failure","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Related pages"],"text":"Make a clean-up step run even when the build fails, and a log step run only after a failure. Before you start. A build configuration with a step that can fail, for example a test step with the ID tests. Steps. Open Settings › Build Steps and add a Command Line step named Stop test server. Under Execution set Execute step to Always, even if build stop command was issued. Add a Command Line step named Collect crash dumps and set it to Only if build status is failed. Drag both steps to the end of the list and click Save. Optional: in a script, check BUILD_STEP_STATUS_TESTS to see how the tests ended. Check it worked. On a failed build the Build steps card shows the tests as failed, the dump step as succeeded and the clean-up step as succeeded. On a green build the dump step shows as skipped with the build has not failed. Troubleshooting. The clean-up step is skipped. Check that it is below the step that fails, and that its policy is not the default. Related pages. The steps list and the step editor. Step statuses."},
{"title":"Cook several platforms with a Matrix block","href":"how-to/steps-cook-matrix","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Related pages"],"text":"Write the cook step once and let Buildspawn run it for every platform and configuration. Before you start. A build configuration with a script that takes a platform and a configuration, here Build\\Cook.bat. Agents or a pool for each platform. Steps. Open Settings › Build Steps and click Add Parallel block. Name it Cook. Click Change to Matrix. Add the axis platform with the values Win64, PS5, XboxSeriesX and the axis config with Development, Shipping. Under Exclude add a row for platform = PS5 and config = Development. Add a Command Line step in the block: Build\\Cook.bat %platform% %config%. Under Run on send PS5 and XboxSeriesX to pool:Consoles. Set Max at once to 3 and Files sent back to Saved/StagedBuilds/%platform%/**. Click Save block and run the build. Check it worked. The build page shows a grid with one cell per combination. Re-run in a cell runs that combination again. The console has one lane per combination. Troubleshooting. Too many combinations. The limit is 256. Add exclude rows or remove values. A step does not see the value. Use %platform% in the field, or read the environment variable platform. Related pages. Matrix mode. Run steps in parallel. Parallel and composite builds."},
{"title":"Run NUnit tests and see the results","href":"how-to/steps-nunit-tests-with-results","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Related pages"],"text":"Run your NUnit tests on every build and read the passed and failed tests on the build page. Before you start. A Windows agent with the NUnit 3 console runner ( nunit3-console ) on its PATH. A built test assembly, here Tests/bin/Release/Game.Tests.dll. Build it in an earlier step. Steps. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Build Steps. Add an NUnit step named Unit tests. Put Tests/bin/Release/Game.Tests.dll in Test assemblies. Put Slow in Exclude categories to skip slow tests. Tick Run recently failed tests first , so a test that broke last time is run first. Optional: set Code coverage to Coverlet or dotCover if the tool is on the agent. Click Save and run the build. Check it worked. Open the build. The Build steps card shows the step as succeeded, or unstable when a test failed. Open the Tests tab for each test. With coverage on, the totals show on the build page. See Changes, artifacts and tests. Troubleshooting. nunit3-console not found. Install it, or set Console runner. No tests run. Check the path in Test assemblies. The failed-first pass is skipped. The first build with the option on runs the suite once. Related pages. NUnit. dotCover. Find why a build failed."},
{"title":"Pack and publish a NuGet package","href":"how-to/steps-pack-and-publish-nuget","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Related pages"],"text":"Build a package of a shared game library and push it to the studio feed, with the API key kept secret. Before you start. A.nuspec file, here Packaging/Framework.nuspec. A feed address and an API key. Create a Secret text credential that holds the key, here nuget-feed-key. See Secrets. nuget.exe on the agent's PATH. Steps. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Build Steps. Add a NuGet Pack step named Pack framework. Set Specification to Packaging/Framework.nuspec , Output directory to out and Version to 1.4.%build.number%. Tick Keep the packages as build artifacts and Clean output directory. Add a NuGet Publish step named Publish framework. Set Packages to out/*.nupkg. Set Package source to your feed URL and pick the credential in API key. Put -SkipDuplicate in Arguments. Click Save and run the build. Check it worked. The console shows the package created and then the push, with the key shown as ********. The.nupkg file is under Artifacts. Troubleshooting. The credential is not found. It must be Secret text and this project must be allowed to use it. The feed says the version exists. Raise the version or keep -SkipDuplicate. No package to push. Check that the pack step wrote to out. Related pages. NuGet Pack. NuGet Publish. Use a secret."},
{"title":"Run a PowerShell script with parameters","href":"how-to/steps-powershell-with-parameters","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Related pages"],"text":"Pass the configuration and output folder from your build parameters to a PowerShell script in your repository. Before you start. A Windows agent with PowerShell. Windows PowerShell 5.1 is enough. A script in your repository, here Build/pack.ps1. Build parameters CONFIG and OUT_DIR on the build configuration. See Parameters. Steps. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Steps , then click Add build step. Click the PowerShell tile and name the step Pack build. Set Run to File and Script file to Build/pack.ps1. In Arguments , type one argument per line: -Config %CONFIG% -Out %OUT_DIR%. Build parameters in %name% form are filled in when the step runs. Each line stays one argument, so a value with spaces is safe. Leave Add -NoProfile argument on. If the agent's policy blocks scripts, tick Bypass the execution policy. The script reads its parameters: param([string]$Config, [string]$Out) Write-Host \"Packing $Config into $Out\". Click Save and run the build. Check it worked. Open the build and its console. The step block shows the PowerShell command and Packing Shipping into Builds\\Win64. For a secret, do not use an argument: set a password parameter and read $env:NAME in the script. Troubleshooting. The policy blocks the script. Tick Bypass the execution policy. Arguments are empty in the script. Check that Script execution mode is the file mode. In stdin mode arguments are not passed. The script file was not found. The path is relative to the Working directory. Related pages. PowerShell. The script editor. Use a secret."},
{"title":"Run Python tests and see the results","href":"how-to/steps-python-tests-with-results","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Related pages"],"text":"Run pytest with a Python step and show the results on the build. Before you start. Python 3 on the agent, with pytest installed. Tests in tools/tests. Steps. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Build Steps. Add a Python step named Check assets. Choose Module , type pytest. In Arguments put tools/tests and, on the next line, --junitxml=reports/assets.xml. In Test reports put reports/assets.xml. Click Save and run the build. Check it worked. The build gets a Tests tab. A failed test makes the step unstable and the results are still recorded. Troubleshooting. No results. Check the path in Test reports against the --junitxml path. pytest not found. Use Environment tool › venv and install packages in an earlier step. Related pages. Python. Fields every runner has."},
{"title":"Retry a flaky step","href":"how-to/steps-retry-flaky-step","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Related pages"],"text":"Run a step again when it fails for a reason that comes and goes, such as a dropped network share. Before you start. A step that sometimes fails on a crash or a timeout, and the exit code it ends with. Steps. Open Settings › Build Steps and click Edit on the step. Scroll to Execution. Set Retry on failure to 2 and Wait between attempts to 30 seconds. In Only for exit codes type -1, 3221225477 so only a crash is retried. Click Save. Check it worked. When the step fails with a listed code, the console says Attempt 1 failed with exit code -1, which is in the retry list. Waiting 30 s, then attempt 2 of 3. A step that finally passes says Step passed on attempt 2 of 3. Troubleshooting. A real error is retried. List the crash codes so a normal failure (exit code 1) fails at once. The step is not retried. The exit code is not in the list: Attempt 1 failed with exit code 1, which is not in the retry list. Related pages. Resume and retry. Re-run from the failed step."},
{"title":"Run steps in a Docker container","href":"how-to/steps-run-in-docker-container","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Related pages"],"text":"Run a step inside a container image, so the agent needs Docker but not the tool. Before you start. Docker running on the agent. An image that has the tool, here python:3.12. Steps. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Build Steps. Add a Python step named Check assets. Choose Module and type pytest. Scroll to Container settings. In Run step within container type python:3.12. Open Container options if you need an Image platform , a Pull policy or extra docker run arguments. Click Save and run the build. Check it worked. The step runs in a new container with the workspace mounted, and the build continues on the agent. Files the step writes in the workspace are there afterwards. Troubleshooting. Docker could not be started on this agent. Install Docker there. The daemon did not answer. Start the Docker service. A Windows image on a Linux agent. Run the step on a Windows agent. A JDK or environment tool is refused. The image brings its own tools. Set the JDK to Inherit and the environment tool to None. Related pages. Fields every runner has. Docker. Python."},
{"title":"Use a secret in a build","href":"how-to/use-a-secret","headings":["Before you start","Steps","A password parameter","A credential shared by the project","Read the secrets in a step","A Vault reference","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Next steps"],"text":"Give a build a password, key or token without showing it in the settings or the build log. There are three ways. Pick one: Way. Use it when. Password parameter. One build configuration needs the secret, and people may give another value for one build. Credential. Several build configurations in a project share the secret, or a runner field asks for a credential. Vault reference. Your studio keeps secrets in HashiCorp Vault and they must not be stored in Buildspawn at all. The example build configuration, HT1 Store Upload , uses the first two: a password parameter STEAM_UPLOAD_KEY and a stored token store-upload-token. Before you start. A build configuration you can configure. For a credential: permission to add credentials on the project. For Vault: a Vault server that both the Buildspawn server and the agents can reach, and a way to sign in to it (AppRole, LDAP or GCP IAM). Steps. A password parameter. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Parameters. Click Add parameter. Type the name STEAM_UPLOAD_KEY. Set Value type to Password and type the value. Click Save. The table now shows dots instead of the value. A password parameter. The value is stored encrypted and never shown again. A credential shared by the project. Open the project, here HT1 Secrets , and click the Credentials tab. Click Add Credentials. Choose Secret text , paste the token, type the ID store-upload-token and a description. Click Create. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Build Features. Click Add build feature and pick Bind credentials to variables. Add a Secret text binding: the variable STORE_TOKEN and the credential store-upload-token. Save. The token is stored on the project, so only build configurations inside it can use it. The build feature puts the token in the variable STORE_TOKEN while the build runs. Read the secrets in a step. Go to Settings › Build Steps and add a Command Line step, for example Upload to store. Read the secrets from the environment, never type them into the script: @echo off rem The secrets come from the environment, never from the script text. upload-tool --key \"%STEAM_UPLOAD_KEY%\" --token \"%STORE_TOKEN%\". In PowerShell use $env:STEAM_UPLOAD_KEY ; in a shell script use \"$STEAM_UPLOAD_KEY\". Click Save , then Run. A secret passed as a command-line argument can be seen by other programs on the agent while the step runs. If your tool can read the secret from an environment variable, let it do that. A Vault reference. The docs server has no Vault server, so this part is described from the screens and not run end to end. Open the project's Settings and go to Connections. Click Add connection and choose HashiCorp Vault. Fill in the display name, the connection ID (for example vault ), the Vault URL and, if you use one, the namespace. Choose how to sign in, click Test connection , then Save. On the build configuration, go to Settings › Parameters and click Add parameter. Name it STEAM_UPLOAD_KEY. Set Value type to Remote and Source to HashiCorp Vault. Pick the connection and type the query, a path and a key: secret/data/skyforge/store!/upload_key. Click Test query , then Save. The value is fetched when the build starts and is never saved in Buildspawn. You can also put a reference straight into the value of any parameter: %vault:secret/data/skyforge/store!/upload_key%. Check it worked. Open the build's Console Output and click Expand all. Here the step printed the two secrets on purpose, to check them. Buildspawn replaced both values with stars. The password parameter and the token are masked in the log. In the Run dialog, the password parameter shows Concealed with a Change Password button. People can give another value for one build without seeing the stored one. Troubleshooting. The script gets the text %STEAM_UPLOAD_KEY% , or nothing. Buildspawn never writes a secret into the text of a script. Read it from the environment as shown above. In a batch file %NAME% already does that. The secret shows in the l"},
{"title":"Spot unhealthy agents","href":"how-to/watch-agent-health","headings":["Before you start","Steps","Take an agent offline before it runs out of disk","Check it worked","Troubleshooting","Next steps"],"text":"Find the machines that are short of disk, memory or CPU before their builds fail. Before you start. Telemetry is on (it is by default). Each agent sends CPU, memory, disk, network and GPU values every 30 seconds. To change thresholds you must be an administrator. Anyone who can see agents can look. Steps. Open Agents in the top bar. In the filter bar, tick Warnings only. The list keeps only agents with a warning or critical value. Offline agents count as warnings. Read the coloured bar at the start of each row: yellow is a warning, red is critical. The cell that caused it is coloured too, for example Disk at 99%. Warnings only: every row here needs attention. On this demo machine the disk is nearly full. To set when a value counts as a warning, click Configure monitors. Under Thresholds , set Warning and Critical for CPU use , Memory used , GPU use and Disk free space. For disk, lower is worse: critical is below warning. For example, on machines with big Perforce workspaces, set Disk free space to warn at 25 % and turn critical at 10 %. Click Save. Thresholds decide when a value turns yellow or red. To look at one machine over time, click its name and open the Telemetry tab. Pick a time range: 15 min , 1 h , 6 h or 24 h. The dashed lines on each chart are the Warning and Critical thresholds. Click Pause to freeze the charts while you read a spike. Click it again to go live. One agent's Telemetry tab. The red disk card shows the problem. Take an agent offline before it runs out of disk. Thresholds on the Configure monitors page only colour the pages. To have Buildspawn stop sending builds to a machine that is short of disk, set a threshold on the agent itself: Open the agent, click Configure and open Disk Space Monitoring Thresholds under Node Properties. Set Free Disk Space Warning Threshold to 60GB (a warning on the agent's page) and Free Disk Space Threshold to 30GB (the agent is marked temporarily offline). Click Save. See Agent settings. On the machine itself, the tray app or the terminal program shows the same CPU, memory and disk, and its Work folders view cleans old workspaces. Check it worked. With Warnings only ticked, the list shows only the agents that pass your new thresholds. When none do, the list is empty. The filter is kept in the page address. Bookmark it to open the list filtered each morning. On the Telemetry tab, the dashed lines sit at your new values. Troubleshooting. Problem. What to do. An agent shows \"—\" in every live column. Telemetry is off for it. Open the agent's Configure page and tick Collect telemetry from this agent , or remove it from Agents that don't send telemetry. Freshness says Stale. No sample came in for a while. Check that the agent is still connected; open its Log tab. GPU shows \"—\". The machine has no GPU tool that Buildspawn reads ( nvidia-smi , amd-smi / rocm-smi or xpu-smi ). Install the vendor's tool. Builds still run on an agent in red. Thresholds only colour the pages; they never stop a build. Mark the agent offline from its row menu while you fix it. Next steps. Telemetry : every card, chart and setting. The Agents page : filters, expanded rows and the row menu. Agent settings : disk space thresholds and the telemetry box."},
{"title":"Buildspawn Guide","href":"index","headings":["Topics"],"text":"Everything you need to install Buildspawn, set up your builds and run them on your own machines. Start with What is Buildspawn , or pick a topic below. Press / to search the guide from any page. Topics."},
{"title":"Passing parameters in a build chain","href":"parameters/build-chains","headings":["Down the chain: reverse.dep","Which value wins","Two builds ask for different values","The target has no such parameter","References in a value","Reuse, re-run and secrets","Up the chain: dep","Where you see it","From the API","Things to know"],"text":"A build can tell the builds it depends on what to build, and can read what they used. Both work along a chain of snapshot dependencies. Take a release build that depends on a Windows build and a PS5 build. Both need to be built as Shipping , and each for its own platform. You set that once, in the release build, and the dependencies get it. A chain is made of snapshot dependencies. Artifact dependencies copy files between builds; they are on the same page. Down the chain: reverse.dep. A parameter named reverse.dep.<ID>.<name> sets <name> in the dependency build of the build configuration with that ID. <ID> is the build configuration ID shown under the title of the build configuration. Name. What it does. reverse.dep.Game_Win64Shipping.platform. Sets platform in the dependency build of Game_Win64Shipping. reverse.dep.*.config. Sets config in every dependency of the chain, at any depth. reverse.dep.Game_Cook.env.REGION. The name can be an env. or system. parameter too. You can set them anywhere a build gets parameters: in the build configuration's own parameters, in parameters it inherits from a project or template, in the Run dialog, and from the API or a trigger. They are not passed to the steps of the build that sets them; they mean nothing there. Which value wins. A value typed in the Run dialog beats a configured one. A value for the exact build configuration beats a * value. A value from a closer build beats one from further up the chain. A build in the middle of the chain can send values to its own dependencies in the same way. Two builds ask for different values. A build configuration is built once in a chain. If two builds ask one dependency for different values at the same rank, the chain is not started. The Run dialog says which builds asked for what, and the API answers with the same text. The target has no such parameter. The value is added to that build as a plain build parameter. The Run dialog shows a warning, so a typo is seen before you run. A name that matches no dependency of the chain is ignored, with a warning in the Run dialog and one line in the build log. References in a value. A value may hold %name% references, such as %game.version%-%build.number%. The build that sends the value fills them in from its own parameters, once, before it passes the value on. A value that came from another build is not filled in again. A reference that names nothing stays as written, with a warning. A reference to a secret makes the value a secret. The Run dialog shows the result next to the value, for example → 1.4-57. Reuse, re-run and secrets. A finished dependency build is used again only if it ran with the same values. Otherwise a new build starts. A password parameter can be passed down. It stays masked everywhere and is left out of that comparison, so a changed secret alone does not start a new build. Re-run passes the same values again. Re-run with changes lets you change them in the Run dialog. Up the chain: dep. A build can read what the dependency builds of its own chain used. %dep.<ID>.<name>% is the value used by the dependency build of that build configuration, the reused one or the one started for the chain. Any build of the chain below can be named, not only direct dependencies. Reference. Value. %dep.Game_Win64Shipping.build.number%. The build number. %dep.Game_Win64Shipping.build.id%. The build id. %dep.Game_Win64Shipping.build.vcs.number%. The changelist or commit. %dep.Game_Win64Shipping.build.result%. SUCCESS, FAILURE and so on. %dep.Game_Win64Shipping.platform%. Any parameter of that build, also one the chain sent it. %dep.Game_Win64Shipping.STEAM_BUILD_ID%. An output a step of that build published. A reference that names no dependency or no value stays as written, and the build log says so once. A secret is never written into a script. A value that someone who may not configure the build configuration typed is not written into a script either, as for the build's own parameters. Shell and Batch steps a"},
{"title":"Credentials","href":"parameters/credentials","headings":["When to use credentials","How to add a credential","Fields every kind has","The kinds","Username with password","Use it in a step","Secret text","Use it in a step","Secret file","Use it in a step","SSH Username with private key","Use it in a step","Certificate","Use it in a step","Perforce Password Credential","Use it in a step","Perforce Ticket Credential","Use it in a step","Steam build account","Epic Games Store","Use it in a step","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Stored logins, keys, tokens and certificates that builds use without ever holding the secret. When to use credentials. Use a credential whenever a build needs a password, key, token, certificate or account. A credential is stored encrypted. A step names it by its ID and never holds the secret itself. Buildspawn looks the secret up when the build runs, with the rights of the person or trigger that started it, and masks it in the log. How to add a credential. Open the project and go to its Credentials. For the whole server, open Manage Buildspawn › Credentials. Click Add credentials. Choose the Kind (the sections below) and fill in its fields. Type an ID that you will remember, such as steam-build. Click Save. The Credentials page. Click Add credentials to choose a kind. Fields every kind has. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Kind. The type of credential. Pick one of the types on this page. First in the list. Secret text. Scope. Global (builds and connections can use it) or System (only the server itself). Global. Global. ID. The name that steps use to find the credential. Leave it empty to get a random one. You cannot change it later. A random ID. steam-build. Description. A note for people. Empty. Upload key for the Skyforge store page. The kinds. There are nine kinds: Username with password, Secret text, Secret file, SSH Username with private key, Certificate, Perforce Password Credential, Perforce Ticket Credential, Steam build account and Epic Games Store. Username with password. A user name and a password for a web service, a package feed or a share. Field. What it does. Username. The account name. Treat username as secret. Masks the user name in the log too. Password. The password. Stored encrypted. Use it in a step. Where a runner field asks for a credential, type the ID. For other tools, add the build feature Use secret text(s) or file(s) and a binding: Username and password (separated) sets one variable for the name and one for the password. Username and password (conjoined) sets one variable to name:password. Then read the variables. # PowerShell, after a binding with USER and PASS curl.exe -u \"$($env:USER):$($env:PASS)\" https://packages.example.com/upload. Secret text. One secret string, such as an API key or a token. Field. What it does. Secret. The text. Stored encrypted. Use it in a step. Add a Use secret text(s) or file(s) binding of kind Secret text and name the environment variable. Runner fields that need a key, such as a NuGet API key, take the credential ID. # Batch, after a binding named STORE_TOKEN upload-tool --token \"%STORE_TOKEN%\". Secret file. A file that must stay secret, such as a signing key file or a service account file. Field. What it does. File. Choose the file to upload. Replace. When you edit: tick it to upload another file. The Filename shows the current one. Use it in a step. Bind it with Secret file (a variable holds the temporary path of a copy, which is deleted when the build ends) or Secret ZIP file (the file is unpacked into a temporary folder). $env:SIGNING_KEY_FILE # path of the temporary copy. SSH Username with private key. A user name and private key for SSH or Git over SSH. Field. What it does. Username. The account name. Treat username as secret. Masks the name in the log. Private Key. Choose Enter directly and paste the key. Passphrase. The passphrase of the key, if it has one. Use it in a step. A Git root takes the credential ID. For scripts, use the binding SSH User Private Key : it sets a variable to the temporary key file path, and optionally variables for the user name and passphrase. ssh -i \"$KEY_FILE\" $SSH_USER@build-store.example.com. Certificate. A certificate and its private key, for code signing. Field. What it does. Certificate. Choose Upload PKCS#12 certificate and key (upload the file) or PEM encoded certificate and key (paste the Certificates and the Private key ). Password. The password of the keystore. A short or empty password gets a warning. Use it in a s"},
{"title":"Snapshot and artifact dependencies","href":"parameters/dependencies","headings":["When to use them","How to set them up","Snapshot dependency: every field","What a suitable build is","Artifact dependency: every field","Dependency options","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"How one build configuration needs another: to build first, and to pass files on. When to use them. Use a snapshot dependency when a build needs another build of the same code to run first: the game needs the engine package. Use an artifact dependency when a build needs files that another build made: the installer needs the cooked content. Together they make a build chain. How to set them up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Dependencies. Under Snapshot Dependencies click Add snapshot dependency. Under Artifact Dependencies click Add artifact dependency. Type the build configuration. Start typing a name, such as Engine/Windows Package. It is a path relative to this build configuration's project (. goes up a project, a leading / starts at the top). The suggestions list every build configuration you can see. Set the options below and click Save. Click See the dependency graph to check the chain. Release Candidate depends on Windows Build twice: it runs in the same chain (snapshot) and takes its Win64 files (artifact). Snapshot dependency: every field. Starting this build configuration starts the ones it depends on first, in parallel when they are independent, and waits for them. All builds of a chain use the same revision of the code. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Build configuration. The build configuration this one depends on. Empty. Engine/Windows Package. Do not run new build if there is a suitable one. Reuse a suitable finished build instead of starting a new one. The row says Reuses suitable builds or Always runs a new build. On. On. Only use successful builds from suitable ones. Failed builds are never reused, even if they are otherwise suitable. On. On. On failed dependency. What happens to this build when the dependency fails (finishes worse than successful, is cancelled or fails to start): Run build, but add problem , Run build, but do not add problem , Make build failed to start or Cancel build. Run build, but add problem. Cancel build. What a suitable build is. it succeeded (when the dependency only uses successful builds), and was started after the dependency's configuration was last saved; it used the same parameter values the chain requests (the dependency's default values, with any reverse.dep values applied); it is the dependency's newest build: no newer build is running or queued; the builds of its own snapshot dependencies are reused too. Run… on the build configuration can rebuild failed and incomplete dependencies, or all of them, whatever these options say. Artifact dependency: every field. Before the build steps run, files from builds of other build configurations are copied into the workspace. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Build configuration. Where the files come from. Empty. Game/Cook Content. Get artifacts from. Build from the same chain (needs a snapshot dependency on the same build configuration), Last successful build , Last pinned build , Last finished build or Build number. Last successful build. Build from the same chain. Build number. Only for Build number : the number of the build to use. Empty. 42. Artifact rules. One rule per line: a path or pattern in the build's artifacts ( * and ** wildcards), optionally => a folder in the workspace. The part of the path before the first wildcard is not copied. A line that starts with -: leaves files out. At least one rule is needed, and a target must be relative with no. Empty. dist/**/*.zip => packages. Clean destination paths before downloading artifacts. Deletes the contents of each target folder before copying. Never the workspace root. Off. On. dist/**/*.zip => packages # every zip under dist/ into packages/, keeping the folders below dist/ reports/summary.html # one file into the workspace root -:dist/**/*-debug.zip # leave out the debug zips. A Pipeline copies the files when the script calls resolveArtifactDependencies() inside node { … }. The step fails the build when an artifact cannot be resolved. Dependency opt"},
{"title":"Agent parameters","href":"parameters/param-agent","headings":["When to use them","How to use them","Every built-in agent parameter","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Values that describe the machine a build runs on, such as its name, operating system and free disk space. When to use them. Use them in two places. In Agent requirements , to pick a machine (for example agent.os.family equals windows ). In a step, to read facts about the machine the build runs on, as %agent.name%. How to use them. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Agent requirements to add a rule on an agent parameter. Or type %agent.name% in a step field or a script. The build reads it from the agent that runs the step. To add your own agent parameters, open the agent in Agents and add a parameter. Names that start with agent. are reserved. A name starts with a letter or digit and holds letters, digits,. , _ and - , up to 100 characters. A value is up to 1000 characters. Every built-in agent parameter. Name. What it holds. Example. agent.name. Name of the agent. win-build-01. agent.labels. Labels of the agent, separated by spaces. windows unreal. agent.pool. Pool the agent is in. Windows. agent.type. permanent, cloud or personal. permanent. agent.owner. Owner of a personal machine. dana.k. agent.executors. Number of executors. 2. agent.hostname. Host name of the machine. BUILD01. agent.os.name. Operating system name. Windows 11. agent.os.family. windows, linux or macos. windows. agent.os.version. Operating system version. 10.0. agent.os.arch. Processor architecture. amd64. agent.cpu.count. Number of processors. 16. agent.memory.total.mb. Total memory in MB. 65536. agent.java.version. Java version of the agent. 21. agent.version. Agent program version. 1.9.3. agent.work.dir. Work folder of the agent. D:\\agent. agent.work.dir.free.mb. Free space of the work folder in MB. 120000. agent.home.dir. Folder the agent runs from. C:\\buildspawn. agent.temp.dir. Temporary folder of the agent. C:\\Temp. An administrator can also show chosen environment variables of the agent as env.NAME. Only the ones listed are read, because environment variables can hold secrets. A worked example. A Unreal Engine build needs a Windows machine with 50 GB free. Add two rules in Agent requirements: agent.os.family equals windows and agent.work.dir.free.mb is at least 50000. In a step, print where it ran: echo Built on %agent.name% in pool %agent.pool%. What you see when it runs. The log shows the value in place of the reference, for example Built on win-build-01 in pool Windows. The agent's page lists all its parameters in blocks: agent, system, folders and environment. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The reference stays as written. The build has no agent, or the name is not in the table. Check the spelling. My custom name is refused. Do not start it with agent. Related pages. Build values. The Agents page. Parameters."},
{"title":"Boolean parameter","href":"parameters/param-boolean","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"A checkbox that is on or off. When to use it. Use it for a yes or no choice: clean build, upload to the store, run the slow tests. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Parameters. Click Add parameter. Choose Value type › Checkbox. Set Value to the checked or the unchecked value. Click Save. The Parameters tab saves as you work, so there is no other Save button. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Name. The parameter name. Empty. CLEAN. Set by Default. The box starts on. In the Add parameter form: Value equal to the checked value. Off. On. Checked value. What the parameter is when the box is on. A Checkbox with true and false is saved as a Boolean parameter. true. -clean. Unchecked value. What it is when the box is off. Empty. false. Description. Shown next to the checkbox. Empty. Delete the workspace first. A worked example. Parameter CLEAN , checked value -clean , unchecked value empty. The Unreal Engine step has %CLEAN% in its arguments. A tick adds -clean. What you see when it runs. The Run dialog shows a checkbox. The step reads the checked or unchecked value as text. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The step gets the word true when I wanted a flag. Set the checked value to the flag itself, for example -clean. Related pages. Parameters. Parameter references. The Run dialog."},
{"title":"Choice parameter","href":"parameters/param-choice","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"A list from which people pick one item. When to use it. Use it when only a few values make sense: Development, Shipping or Debug; Win64, PS5 or Switch. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Parameters. Click Add parameter. Choose Value type › Select. Type the Items , one per line. The first line is the default. Click Save. The Parameters tab saves as you work, so there is no other Save button. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Name. The parameter name. Empty. PLATFORM. Choices / Items. One value per line. The first is the default. A line can be label => value. A Select that uses labels, a different default or Allow multiple is saved as a Typed parameter; a plain list is a Choice parameter. Empty. Win64, PS5, Switch. Allow multiple. Several items can be ticked. Off. On. Value separator. Between ticked values. A comma. ,. Description. Shown under the field. Empty. Target platform. A worked example. Parameter PLATFORM with items Win64 , PS5 , Switch. With Allow multiple on, a step receives Win64,PS5 when two are ticked. What you see when it runs. The Run dialog shows a drop-down list. The Parameters tab says Asked when the build runs with the type choice. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Requires Choices. Type at least one item. The default is not the first item. Set Value to the item you want first. Related pages. Parameters. Parameter references. The Run dialog."},
{"title":"Credentials parameter","href":"parameters/param-credentials","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"A pick of one stored credential when a build starts. When to use it. Use it when people choose which account a build uses, for example a test account. The build gets the ID of the credential, not its secret. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Parameters. Add a credentials parameter (from a Pipeline parameters block or the type's own form). Choose the Credential type , tick Required if needed, and pick a Default Value. Click Save. The Parameters tab saves as you work, so there is no other Save button. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Name. The parameter name. Empty. STORE_ACCOUNT. Credential type. The kind of credential people can pick: Username with password, Secret text and so on. First type. Username with password. Required. Removes the empty choice from the list. Off. On. Default Value. The credential that starts selected. None. steam-build. Description. Shown in the Run dialog. Empty. Account to upload with. A worked example. Parameter STORE_ACCOUNT , type Steam build account , default steam-build. A step that takes a credential ID is set to %STORE_ACCOUNT%. At run time the step finds the credential by its ID. What you see when it runs. The Run dialog lists credentials of that type that you may use. The value is the credential ID. The secret is never shown. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Not available to current user. You may not use that credential. Pick another one, or ask for access. Related pages. Parameters. Parameter references. The Run dialog. Credentials."},
{"title":"File parameter","href":"parameters/param-file","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"A file that the person starting the build uploads. When to use it. Use it when a build needs a file you do not keep in version control: a one-off config, a list of test accounts. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Parameters. Add a parameter of the file type. A file parameter is always a Configuration parameter. Type the File location and, if you like, a Description. Click Save. The Parameters tab saves as you work, so there is no other Save button. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. File location. The path in the workspace where the uploaded file is put. This is also the parameter name. Empty. config/overrides.json. Description. Shown in the Run dialog. Empty. Settings for this run. A worked example. A file parameter config/overrides.json. When you run the build, you pick a file in the Run dialog. A step reads config/overrides.json in the workspace. What you see when it runs. The Parameters tab shows Chosen when the build runs instead of a value. The Run dialog shows a file chooser. The file is copied into the workspace before the steps run. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Environment kind is refused. A file parameter is a configuration parameter only. Choose Configuration. The file is missing in the workspace. Nobody chose a file in the Run dialog. A build started by a trigger gets no file. Related pages. Parameters. Parameter references. The Run dialog."},
{"title":"Multi-line string parameter","href":"parameters/param-multiline","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Text with line breaks, such as a list of levels or release notes. When to use it. Use it when one line is not enough: release notes, a list of maps to cook, one file name per line. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Parameters. Add a parameter of the multi-line type (it comes from a Pipeline parameters block or from a configuration that already has one). The table shows it with its value. Edit the Name , Default Value and Description. Click Save. The Parameters tab saves as you work, so there is no other Save button. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Name. The parameter name. Empty. MAPS. Default Value. Many lines of text. Empty. One map per line. Description. Shown under the field in the Run dialog. Empty. Maps to cook, one per line. A worked example. A parameter RELEASE_NOTES holds the notes for the store page. A PowerShell step writes them to a file for the upload step: Set-Content notes.txt $env:RELEASE_NOTES. What you see when it runs. The Run dialog shows a large text area. Line breaks are kept in the value. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. A batch script breaks on the second line. A value with line breaks does not fit on one batch line. Read it as an environment variable in a PowerShell step. Related pages. Parameters. Parameter references. The Run dialog."},
{"title":"Password parameter","href":"parameters/param-password","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"A secret value that Buildspawn stores encrypted and never shows. When to use it. Use it for a key, token or password that a build needs. See Secrets and Vault for how to use it in scripts. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Parameters. Click Add parameter. Choose Value type › Password. Type the Value. Click Save. The Parameters tab saves as you work, so there is no other Save button. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Name. The parameter name. Choose Environment variable as kind so scripts can read it. Empty. STEAM_UPLOAD_KEY. Default Value. The secret. Stored encrypted, never shown again. Editing shows Stored. Leave empty to keep it. Empty. (hidden). Description. Shown in the Run dialog. Empty. Upload key. A worked example. Parameter env.STEAM_UPLOAD_KEY , a Password. A PowerShell step runs upload-tool --key $env:STEAM_UPLOAD_KEY. What you see when it runs. The table shows dots. The Run dialog shows Concealed with a Change Password button. The log shows dots if the value is printed. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The step fails: a secret cannot be written into a script. Read it from the environment: $env:NAME , $NAME or %NAME% in a batch file. Related pages. Parameters. Parameter references. The Run dialog. Use a secret in PowerShell."},
{"title":"Asked parameters (prompt)","href":"parameters/param-prompt","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"How a parameter asks for its value when a build starts, or keeps one fixed. When to use it. Use it to decide what people see in the Run dialog. Some values must be typed every time (the version to release). Others must never change (the SDK path). How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Parameters. Click Edit on a parameter, or Add parameter. Click Customize the run dialog. Choose Display , and fill in Label and Description. Tick Read-only if nobody may change the value. Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Display: Normal. The field is in the Run dialog. A plain Run starts the build at once with the defaults. Normal. Normal. Display: Hidden. The field is left out of the Run dialog. The configured value is a fixed value the build always uses. Hidden is not protection: anyone who can read the build configuration can see the value. -. Hidden. Display: Prompt. The Run button opens the Run dialog every time, so people must look at the value. -. Prompt. Label. Shown in the Run dialog in place of the name. The name. Version to release. Description. Shown under the field. Empty. Like 1.4.0. Read-only. The configured value is always used. A build request cannot change it. Off. On. A worked example. The release build has RELEASE_VERSION , a Text parameter with a Regex rule \\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+ , label Version to release and display Prompt. The nightly schedule starts the same build with a fixed value, so it needs an explicit value too (see Ask for a value when a build starts ). What you see when it runs. The Parameters tab shows Asked when the build runs and the value type for asked parameters, and Fixed value for hidden ones. The Run dialog opens when you click Run and lists the label, description and rules. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. A build started by a trigger has no value. A trigger, the API or the command line uses the default. Give the parameter a safe default, or use a Not empty rule so the build is refused. Someone changed a read-only value. They cannot. The configured value is used. Related pages. Parameters. The Run dialog. Parameter with a value type."},
{"title":"Parameter references","href":"parameters/param-references","headings":["When to use them","How to use them","Every kind of reference","Rules","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Write %name% in a step to put a value there when the build runs. When to use them. Use a reference whenever a runner field or a script needs a value you keep in a parameter, a value the build knows (such as the changelist), or a value from another build in the chain. How to use them. Open the step: Settings › Build steps , then the step. Type % in the script editor. It lists the parameters of the build. Pick one, or type the name. Save. At run time, Buildspawn replaces each reference with its value. Every kind of reference. Reference. What it is. Example. %NAME%. A build parameter (configuration kind). Also a value an earlier step set with setParameter ; the latest value wins. %CONFIG%. %env.NAME%. An environment parameter. In a batch file it becomes %NAME% , which the batch file reads from the environment itself. %env.UE_SDK_ROOT%. %system.name%. A system property. %system.build.flavour%. %build.*%. A value the build knows: number, counter, changelist, branch, who started it, checkout folder. See Build values. %build.vcs.number%. %vcsroot.*%. A property of a version control root: %vcsroot.url% with one root, %vcsroot.<id>.url% with several. %vcsroot.branch%. %agent.*%. A parameter of the agent. See Agent parameters. %agent.name%. %tool.<id>%. The path of a tool on the agent. Not allowed in a container. %tool.msbuild%. %dep.<ID>.<name>%. A value of a dependency build in this chain. See Build chains. %dep.Game_Cook.build.number%. %vault:path!/key%. A secret fetched from HashiCorp Vault when the build starts. See Vault. %vault:secret/data/game!/key%. %reverse.dep.…%. Not a reference but a parameter name that sends a value to a dependency. See Build chains. reverse.dep.*.config. %%. One percent sign, where something else was replaced. 50%%. Rules. One pass from left to right. A value is never read again, so %other% inside a value stays as written. A reference to a name Buildspawn does not know stays as written. A name such as %PATH% is left for the shell. A secret is never written into a script. A reference to a password parameter fails the step, and the message names the environment variable to read instead. In a batch file the reference is left for cmd. A value that someone who may not configure the build configuration typed is not written into code. The step fails and names the parameter. A value at its default is always allowed. In a Windows batch script a value has each percent sign doubled, so cmd reads it once. A worked example. powershell -File Build\\ci.ps1 -Stage package -Version %version% -Changelist %build.vcs.number% -Agent %agent.name%. What you see when it runs. The log shows the command with the values filled in. Secrets show as dots. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The reference stays as written. The name is wrong or the value does not exist yet (for example a %dep.…% of a build that was not in the chain). The step fails with a secret message. Read the secret from the environment variable the message names. The step fails with \"changed value\". Someone without Configure permission typed a value that is not the default. Ask a person who may configure the build configuration to run it. Related pages. Parameters. Build values. The script editor."},
{"title":"Run parameter","href":"parameters/param-run","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"A pick of one finished build of another build configuration. When to use it. Use it when a build works on the result of another one: test the build a person picks, or sign a chosen package. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Parameters. Add a run parameter (from a Pipeline parameters block or the type's own form). Type the Project and choose a Filter. Click Save. The Parameters tab saves as you work, so there is no other Save button. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Name. The parameter name. Empty. PACKAGE_BUILD. Project. The build configuration whose builds can be picked. Empty. Game/Windows Build. Description. Shown in the Run dialog. Empty. The build to test. Filter. Which builds are offered: All Builds , Completed Builds Only , Successful Builds Only or Stable Builds Only. All Builds. Stable Builds Only. A worked example. A run parameter PACKAGE_BUILD for Game/Windows Build , filter Stable Builds Only. A tester picks #212 in the Run dialog. A step reads the build's address from the parameter. What you see when it runs. The Run dialog shows a drop-down list of builds. The build page shows the chosen build as a link. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The list is empty. The other build configuration has no build that matches the filter, or you cannot see it. Related pages. Parameters. Parameter references. The Run dialog. Build chains."},
{"title":"String parameter","href":"parameters/param-string","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"A single line of text with a default value. When to use it. Use it for short free text: a branch name, a version, a platform. A Text parameter with Any and no run dialog settings is saved as this type. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Parameters. Click Add parameter. Choose Value type › Text. Type the Name and the Value. Click Save. The Parameters tab saves as you work, so there is no other Save button. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Name. The parameter name. Empty. BRANCH. Default Value. Used when nobody types another value. Empty. main. Description. Shown under the field in the Run dialog. Empty. Stream to build. Trim the string. Cuts spaces and line breaks from both ends of the value. A String parameter from a Pipeline parameters block has this option. Off. On. A worked example. A parameter BRANCH with default main. A step uses %BRANCH%. For a release, someone types release/1.4 in the Run dialog. What you see when it runs. The Run dialog shows a text box with the default. The step receives the value as %BRANCH% and, for an env. parameter, as an environment variable. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. A space at the end of the value breaks a script. Tick Trim the string , or use a Text parameter with a Regex rule. Related pages. Parameters. Parameter references. The Run dialog."},
{"title":"Parameter with a value type","href":"parameters/param-typed","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"The parameter you create with Add parameter: one name, one kind, and a value type that decides what people can enter. When to use it. Use it for every new parameter. Text, Checkbox, Password, Select and Remote cover the old String, Boolean, Password and Choice parameters, and add rules, labels and values fetched from a vault. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Parameters. Click Add parameter. Type the Name and choose the Kind. Choose the Value type : Text , Checkbox , Password , Select or Remote. The fields below change with it. Fill in the value and the rules. Optional: open Customize the run dialog. Click Save. The Parameters tab saves as you work, so there is no other Save button. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Name. The name, without a prefix. Unique in the build configuration. Empty. BUILD_TARGET. Kind. Configuration, Environment variable ( env. ) or System property ( system. ). See The three kinds. Configuration parameter. Environment variable (env.). Value type. What people can enter. Remote is offered only when a remote source, such as HashiCorp Vault, is installed. Text. Select. Value. The default value (not for Remote). For Select it is the item that starts selected; blank starts with the first item, or with nothing ticked when multiple is allowed. For Checkbox it must be the checked or unchecked value; empty means unchecked. Empty. Shipping. Show allowed value: Any, Not empty, Regex. Text only. Any accepts all text. Not empty refuses an empty value. Regex needs the whole value to match your pattern. Any. Regex \\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+. Message when it does not match. Text shown when a value fails the Regex rule. Empty. Use a version like 1.4.0. Checked value / Unchecked value. Checkbox only: what the parameter is when the box is on or off. true / empty. -clean / empty. Allow multiple. Select only: people may tick several items. Off. On. Value separator. Select with Allow multiple: goes between the ticked values. A comma. ; Items. Select only: one per line, as label => value or just value. Empty. Debug build => Debug. Source, Test query. Remote only: where the value is fetched when the build starts, and a button to check the query. The value found is never shown. -. HashiCorp Vault. Customize the run dialog. Opens Display , Description , Label and Read-only. See Asked parameters. Closed. -. A worked example. The Windows Build asks for the build type. Add a parameter named CONFIG , kind Configuration parameter , value type Select , items Development , Shipping , Debug build => Debug. A step then runs: powershell -File Build\\ci.ps1 -Stage compile -Config %CONFIG%. What you see when it runs. The Parameters tab lists the row with its kind and Asked when the build runs. In the Run dialog people see a list. A value that breaks the rules is refused, so the build does not start. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The name is refused. Names are unique in a build configuration. The Regex message appears in the Run dialog. The value did not match the whole pattern. You do not need anchors: the whole value must match. Remote is missing from Value type. No remote source is installed on this server. See Vault. Related pages. Parameters. Parameter references. The Run dialog. String , Boolean , Choice , Password."},
{"title":"Parameters","href":"parameters/parameters","headings":["The Parameters tab","The three kinds","Configuration parameters","env. parameters","system. parameters","Add or edit a parameter","Value types","Customize the run dialog","One page for every type","Inheritance","Built-in parameters","Tips"],"text":"Parameters are named values your steps can use. You set them once on a build configuration or a project, and you can change them each time you run a build. A parameter has a name , a value , a kind and a value type. The kind decides where the value goes: into %name% references, into the environment of every step, or to your build tools. The value type decides what people can type in the Run dialog : free text, a checkbox, a list or a password. The Parameters tab. Every build configuration has its parameters on Settings › Parameters. Projects have the same page in their settings, and what a project defines passes down to everything inside it. The Parameters tab. Each row shows the value, the kind, where the parameter is defined and when it is set. The table has these columns: Column. What it shows. Name. The name, with its prefix ( env. or system. ) if it has one. Value. The default value. Passwords always show as dots. Kind. Configuration, Environment or System. See The three kinds. Defined in. This job , or the project it comes from. When it is set. Asked when the build runs (people can change it in the Run dialog) or Fixed value , and the value type. Use the chips All , Configuration , Environment and System to filter the list, or type in Search parameters. Press / to jump to the search box. The Parameters tab saves as you work. There is no Save button: when you close the parameter dialog with Save , the change is stored. The three kinds. The kind is part of the name. You pick it in the dialog, and Buildspawn adds the prefix for you. Kind. Name. Where the value goes. Configuration. NAME. Read in steps and runner fields as %NAME%. Environment. env.NAME. Passed to the process of every step as the environment variable NAME. A step can override it for itself. System. system.NAME. Passed to build tools, for example to Maven as -DNAME=value. Not put in the process environment. Configuration parameters. A configuration parameter is the everyday kind. Write %CONFIG% in a runner field or in a script, and Buildspawn puts the value there when the build runs. For example, the Windows Build passes its build type to a script like this: powershell -File Build\\ci.ps1 -Stage compile -Platform Win64 -Config %CONFIG%. The script editor helps you: type % and it lists the parameters of the build. A name it does not know gets a hint. See The script editor. env. parameters. An env. parameter becomes an environment variable. Name it env.UE_SDK_ROOT and every step sees UE_SDK_ROOT. Use this for tools that read their settings from the environment. rem In a batch step echo SDK is in %UE_SDK_ROOT% # In a shell step echo \"SDK is in $UE_SDK_ROOT\". system. parameters. A system. parameter is a property for build tools. Maven steps get it as -D. Other steps don't see it in their environment, but you can still read it as %system.NAME% in a field. Add or edit a parameter. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Parameters. Click Add parameter , or Edit on a row. Type the Name. Don't type the prefix: choose the Kind instead. Names must be unique in a build configuration. Choose the Value type and fill in the value (see below). Optional: open Customize the run dialog to set the label, description and display. Click Save. Value types. Value type. What people can enter. Options. Text. Any text. Any , Not empty , or Regex : the whole value must match the pattern, with your own Message when it does not match. Checkbox. On or off. The Checked value and Unchecked value , for example true / false or -clean / empty. Password. A secret. Stored encrypted and never shown again. See Secrets and Vault. Select. One item from a list, or several. Items , one per line, as label => value or just value. Allow multiple and a Value separator (a comma if empty). Remote. Nothing: the value is fetched when the build starts. A Source , such as HashiCorp Vault, and a query. See Vault. Example items for a Select parameter: Development Shipping Debug build => Debug. Customize th"},
{"title":"Secrets and Vault","href":"parameters/secrets","headings":["Secret parameters","Use a secret in a script","Credentials","Vault references","1. Add a Vault connection","2a. A Remote parameter","2b. A reference inside any value","The query","What happens when a build runs","Credentials by kind","Tips"],"text":"Keep passwords, keys and tokens out of sight: in settings, in the Run dialog and in build logs. Buildspawn has two ways to give a build a secret: a password parameter , stored encrypted on the server; a Vault reference , fetched from HashiCorp Vault when the build starts and never stored at all. Either way, Buildspawn masks the value in the build log, so a script that prints it by accident shows dots instead. Secret parameters. A password parameter is a normal parameter with the value type Password. Open the build configuration or project and go to Settings › Parameters. Click Add parameter. Type a name, for example STEAM_UPLOAD_KEY , and pick a Kind. Pick Environment variable (env.) if your script reads the secret from the environment. Set Value type to Password and type the value. Click Save. After you save: The value is stored encrypted and is never shown again, not even to you. The table shows dots. When you edit the parameter, the field says Stored. Leave empty to keep it. Type a new value only to replace it. In the Run dialog the field shows Concealed with a Change Password button. People can give a different value for one build without seeing the stored one. People who may only view the settings never receive the value. It is masked in the visual editor and in project parameters too. Use a secret in a script. Buildspawn never writes a secret into the text of a script, even if you write %STEAM_UPLOAD_KEY%. Read it from the environment instead: rem Batch upload-tool --key \"%STEAM_UPLOAD_KEY%\" # PowerShell upload-tool --key $env:STEAM_UPLOAD_KEY # Shell upload-tool --key \"$STEAM_UPLOAD_KEY\". In a batch file, %NAME% is the batch file's own syntax for an environment variable, so the value is read when the script runs, not written into it. Masking hides the exact value. It cannot hide a secret that your tool changes before it prints it, for example base64-encoded or split over lines. Don't print secrets on purpose. Credentials. Runner fields that need a secret, such as a NuGet API key, a Unity licence or a Perforce password, take a credential ID , never the value itself. Buildspawn looks up the credential when the build runs, with the rights of the build configuration. Credentials are kept in the project's Credentials page or at server level. Vault references. If your studio keeps secrets in HashiCorp Vault , builds can fetch them when they start. The value is never saved in Buildspawn. 1. Add a Vault connection. A connection tells Buildspawn where Vault is and how to sign in. Add it on a project, and every project below it can use it. Add it on the Root project for the whole server; see Global connections. Open the project's settings and go to Connections. Click Add connection and choose HashiCorp Vault. Fill in the display name, a connection ID (for example vault ), the Vault URL and, if you use one, the namespace. Choose how to sign in: AppRole (role ID and secret ID), LDAP (user name and password) or GCP IAM. Click Test connection , then Save. Both the server and every agent that runs the build must reach Vault. The server signs in; the agent fetches the values. 2a. A Remote parameter. On Settings › Parameters , click Add parameter. Set Value type to Remote and Source to HashiCorp Vault. Pick the connection and type the query, for example secret/data/skyforge/store!/upload_key. Click Test query to check it. The button is shown only to people who can configure the job, and the value found is never displayed. Click Save. 2b. A reference inside any value. You can also put a reference in the value of any parameter: %vault:secret/data/skyforge/store!/upload_key% %vault:studio-vault:secret/data/skyforge/store!/upload_key%. The first form uses the default connection. The second names the connection ID first. The query. A query is path!/key : path is the secret's path in Vault. Both KV version 1 and version 2 work; version 2 data is unwrapped for you. key picks one field of the secret, for example /password. Without a key, a secr"},
{"title":"Set parameters from a build","href":"parameters/set-from-build","headings":["The ##buildspawn message","Rules","Special characters","Names a step may not set","Examples","Pass a version to later steps","Set an environment variable","A value with a quote in it","Step outputs","Other service messages"],"text":"A step can change a parameter while the build runs. It prints one special line, and the steps after it get the new value. This is useful when one step works something out that later steps need: the version number from a file, the name of a package, the folder an engine was unpacked to. You don't need files or scripts to pass it along. You print a service message : a line that starts with ##buildspawn. Buildspawn reads it from the step's output. The ##buildspawn message. The message looks like this: ##buildspawn[setParameter name='NAME' value='VALUE']. The name decides what the value becomes: Name. What the later steps get. version. A build parameter. Later steps read it as %version% in their fields and scripts. It is not put in the environment. env.GAME_VERSION. An environment variable GAME_VERSION in the process of every later step. system.version. A property for build tools, as with system. parameters. If the build already has a parameter with that name, the new value replaces it for the steps after this one. The build log says so, for example setParameter version replaces the build parameter version for the steps after this one. Rules. The name starts with a letter or _ , and holds letters, digits,. , _ and - , up to 100 characters. The value can be up to 8,192 characters. A build keeps at most 200 values set this way. Only the output of a running step's own script counts. A message that comes from elsewhere in the log, or after the step has ended, is refused with a line in the log. If the same name is set twice, the later value wins. The value reaches the steps after the one that printed it, not the rest of the same step. Special characters. Values are in single quotes. To put a quote or a line break in a value, escape it with | : You want. Write. '. |'. |. ||. A new line. |n. A carriage return. |r. Names a step may not set. Some environment variables change which programs run or how they start. To keep builds safe, a step cannot set these as env. names: search paths and loaders: PATH , PATHEXT , LD_PRELOAD , LD_LIBRARY_PATH , LD_AUDIT , and every DYLD_* name; shell start-up: BASH_ENV , ENV , PROMPT_COMMAND , COMSPEC , PSMODULEPATH ; tool options: JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS , _JAVA_OPTIONS , JDK_JAVA_OPTIONS , NODE_OPTIONS , NODE_PATH , PYTHONPATH , PYTHONSTARTUP , PYTHONHOME , PERL5OPT , PERL5LIB , RUBYOPT , RUBYLIB ;NET profilers and start-up hooks: DOTNET_STARTUP_HOOKS and the COR_* and CORECLR_* profiler names; every GIT_* name. Upper and lower case count the same. A refused name gets a line in the build log that says why. Plain names and system. names never touch the environment, so they are never refused. An administrator can lift the block: Settings › System , section Build steps , tick Let build steps set any environment variable. Only lift the block if you trust everyone who can change the scripts your builds run. A step that sets PATH decides which programs all later steps run. Examples. Pass a version to later steps. A first step reads the version from the project and prints it. A batch step: for /f %%v in (Build\\version.txt) do echo ##buildspawn[setParameter name='version' value='%%v']. A PowerShell step does the same: $v = (Get-Content Build\\version.txt).Trim() Write-Host \"##buildspawn[setParameter name='version' value='$v']\". A later step uses it in its command: powershell -File Build\\ci.ps1 -Stage package -Version %version%. Set an environment variable. echo \"##buildspawn[setParameter name='env.ENGINE_DIR' value='$(pwd)/Engine']\". Every later step now has ENGINE_DIR in its environment. A value with a quote in it. echo \"##buildspawn[setParameter name='title' value='Skyforge: the dragon|'s path']\". For how to read the values, see Parameter references. For the other messages, see Service messages. Step outputs. A step can also declare its outputs: a name and a type (Text, Number, True/false or File). The step must then set each output with a setParameter message. A step that ends without setting a declared output fails "},
{"title":"Aggregate downstream test results","href":"post-build/aggregate-tests","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Collects the test results of the builds that follow this one and shows them together on this build. When to use it. Use it when one build compiles and several others run tests, for example a PS5 test build and a Windows test build that start after Windows Build. People then see the whole test result on one page. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Post-build Actions. Click Add post-build action and choose Aggregate downstream test results. Tick Automatically aggregate all downstream tests to take every build configuration that follows. Or type the names in Jobs to aggregate. Tick Include failed builds in results if failed downstream builds count too. Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Automatically aggregate all downstream tests. Take the tests of every build configuration that follows this one, through the whole chain. You do not need to keep a list. Off. On. Jobs to aggregate. A comma separated list of build configuration names, when you do not use automatic mode. Empty. Game/Unit Tests, Game/PS5 Tests. Include failed builds in results. Also count builds that failed. Off. Off. A worked example. Windows Build starts Unit Tests and Smoke Tests when it passes. With automatic aggregation on, the Windows Build page shows the sum of all three. What you see when it runs. The build page shows an Aggregated Test Result with the counts per downstream build configuration. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Nothing is aggregated. The following builds must be started by this build (a trigger or a dependency) and must publish test results themselves. The numbers change later. Results are added as the downstream builds finish. Related pages. After the build: overview. Post-build actions tab. Build other build configurations. Dependencies."},
{"title":"Archive the artifacts","href":"post-build/archive-artifacts","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Keeps files from the workspace with the build, so you can download them from the build page later. When to use it. Use it for anything you want after the agent is cleaned: the game build, installers, crash symbols, logs. Archived files show on the Artifacts tab of the build. Later builds and other build configurations can use them as artifact dependencies. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Post-build Actions. Click Add post-build action and choose Archive the artifacts. In Files to archive type the files, relative to the workspace, for example Builds/Win64/**. Open More options if you need to exclude files or change a switch. Click Save. Every field. The General tab of the build configuration has the same idea under Artifact paths , with the form source => target folder. See General. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Files to archive. Files to keep, as Ant patterns. Comma is the only separator. Only files inside the workspace can be archived. Empty. Builds/Win64/**/*.zip. Excludes. Files that match are not archived, even if they match the first field. Comma separated. Empty. **/*.pdb. Do not fail build if archiving returns nothing. Normally a build fails when no file matches. With this on, the build only gets a warning. Off. Off. Archive artifacts only if build is successful. Failed builds keep no artifacts. Unstable builds still do. Off. On. Fingerprint all archived artifacts. Record a fingerprint of each file, to track where it is used. Off. Off. Use default excludes. Leave out files that version control tools make, such as.git folders. On. On. Treat include and exclude patterns as case sensitive. Off makes **/*.ZIP match.zip files. On. On. Follow symbolic links. Off: symbolic links in the workspace are ignored. Off. Off. A worked example. The Windows Build of Skyforge writes its output to Output/. The studio sets Files to archive to Output/** and Excludes to **/*.pdb. The symbol files stay out; the 2 GB game package stays in. What you see when it runs. The log says how many files were archived. The build page gets an Artifacts list with sizes. Archiving artifacts Archived 412 artifacts. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. No files found to archive. The pattern did not match. Paths are relative to the workspace. Check that an earlier step made the files. Turn on Do not fail build if archiving returns nothing only if empty output is normal. Disk is filling up. Artifacts are kept as long as the build. Use retention to keep artifacts for fewer builds than the builds themselves. Files outside the workspace. They cannot be archived. Copy them into the workspace first. Related pages. After the build: overview. Post-build actions tab. Retention and discard old builds. Archive build output."},
{"title":"Build other build configurations","href":"post-build/build-other","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Starts other build configurations when this build ends. When to use it. Use it for a simple chain: after the code build, start the tests. For chains that must wait for each other and share files, use dependencies instead. They are easier to follow, and a build is not started twice. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Post-build Actions. Click Add post-build action and choose Build other build configurations. In Build configurations to build type the names, separated by commas. Choose when to start them: only if this build is stable, even if unstable, or even if it fails. Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Build configurations to build. Comma separated names (or paths such as Game/Unit Tests ). Empty. Unit Tests, Smoke Tests. Trigger only if build is stable. Start them only when this build succeeded. Selected. Selected. Trigger even if the build is unstable. Start them when this build succeeded or was unstable. Off. -. Trigger even if the build fails. Always start them. Off. -. A worked example. After Windows Build succeeds, Unit Tests and Smoke Tests start. If the build fails, they do not. What you see when it runs. The new builds show Started by upstream build Windows Build #142. The log of this build lists the builds it started. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. A name is not found. Check the spelling and the path. Names are relative to the project. Builds start twice. Another trigger also starts them. Use only one way. Related pages. After the build: overview. Post-build actions tab. Finish Build trigger. Dependencies."},
{"title":"Send build artifacts to a Windows share","href":"post-build/cifs","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Sends files from the build to a server over a Windows file share (SMB), when the build ends. When to use it. Use it to put build output where other people or machines can get it. Use it to copy output to a file server that your artists use, for example \\\\files\\skyforge\\drops. An administrator sets up the servers once in the global settings. The same form is also offered as a build step of a promotion, for build configurations that use promotions. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Post-build Actions. Click Add post-build action and choose Send build artifacts to a Windows share. Click Add under Publishers and choose the server in Name. Add a transfer set: Source files and the other fields. Open Advanced for retries, labels and what to do on an error. Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Name. The server, chosen from the servers set up in the global settings. None. build-share-01. Source files. Files to send, as a comma separated Ant pattern. The base folder is the workspace. Empty. Output/**. Remove prefix. The first part of the path that is not created on the server. Empty. Output. Remote directory. A folder below the one set in the global settings. It is created when missing. Empty. skyforge/nightly. Exclude files. Files that match are not sent. Comma separated Ant patterns. Empty. **/*.pdb. Pattern separator. The regular expression that separates patterns in the two fields above. [, ]+. [,]+. No default excludes. Send files that Ant leaves out by default. Off. Off. Make empty dirs. Also create folders that match, even when empty. Off. Off. Flatten files. Send only files, no folders. File names must be unique, or the transfer stops. Off. Off. Clean remote. Delete the files in the remote directory before sending. Off. Off. Remote directory is a date format. The remote directory is a Java date pattern, filled with the build date. Off. On with yyyy-MM-dd. Retry: Retries. Advanced. How many times to try again after an error. Off. 3. Retry: Delay. Advanced. Milliseconds to wait between tries. Off. 10000. Verbose output in console. Advanced. A lot of detail in the build log, for finding problems. Off. Off. Publish to other shares if an error occurs / Fail the build if an error occurs. Advanced (on the whole list). Keep going with the other servers after an error; mark the build failed instead of unstable. Off / Off. -. Always transfer from master / Give the master a NODE_NAME. Advanced. Send the files through the server, which helps with strict firewalls. Off. -. Use the workspace / Use promotion timestamp. Promotion only. Take files from the workspace instead of the artifacts of the promoted build; use the promotion time for the date format. Off. -. A worked example. After Windows Build , Output/** goes to the share files-drops , into skyforge\\1.4. Artists open the share in Explorer. What you see when it runs. The log shows the share connection and each file copied. An error makes the build unstable, or failed if you chose that. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Logon failure. Check the user name and password of the share in the global settings. The network path was not found. The server name or share name is wrong, or the agent cannot reach it. Related pages. After the build: overview. Post-build actions tab."},
{"title":"Deploy war/ear to a container","href":"post-build/deploy-container","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Deploys a web application (a WAR or EAR file) to an application server when the build ends. When to use it. Use it for web services that you host in a Java application server, such as a launcher back end or an internal tool. Most game builds do not need it. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Post-build Actions. Click Add post-build action and choose Deploy war/ear to a container. In WAR/EAR files type the file or pattern. Fill in the Context path. Under Containers click Add Container , choose the server type, and fill in its address and credentials. Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. WAR/EAR files. The files to deploy, relative to the workspace. Ant patterns such as **/*.war work. Empty. target/*.war. Context path. The path the container publishes the application under. A setting inside the file can override it. Empty. /launcher. Containers. The servers to deploy to. Each one has its own address and credentials. None. Tomcat at http://tools.studio.example:8080. Deploy on failure. Deploy even if the build failed. Off. Off. A worked example. The back end of the launcher builds launcher.war. The post-build action deploys it to the test Tomcat server at /launcher after each good build. What you see when it runs. The log shows the upload and the answer of the server. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Deploy fails with 401. The credentials of the container are wrong. Nothing is deployed. The file pattern did not match. Check that the build produced the file. Related pages. After the build: overview. Post-build actions tab."},
{"title":"E-mail Notification","href":"post-build/email","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Sends an email when a build fails, and when it is fixed. When to use it. It is the same setting as the build feature Notifications. Use it when someone must hear about a broken build without opening Buildspawn. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Post-build Actions. Click Add post-build action and choose E-mail Notification. Type the Recipients , separated by spaces. Choose the two options if you need them. Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Recipients. Whitespace-separated addresses. Build variables may be used. Empty. build-team@studio.example. Send e-mail for every unstable build. On: every unstable build sends mail. On. On. Send separate e-mails to individuals who broke the build. Also mail the people whose changes were in the broken build. Off. On. A worked example. Release Build mails release-team@studio.example. When the build breaks, the team gets one mail. When it is fixed, one more. What you see when it runs. The mail comes from the server mail settings. Its subject says the build is failed, unstable or back to normal. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. No mail arrives. Ask an administrator to check the mail server in the global settings. Too many unstable mails. Turn off Send e-mail for every unstable build. Related pages. After the build: overview. Post-build actions tab. Notifications (build feature)."},
{"title":"Record fingerprints of files to track usage","href":"post-build/fingerprints","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Records a fingerprint (a checksum) of files, so you can find which build made a file and which builds used it. When to use it. Use it when several build configurations pass files to each other, for example the engine DLLs that the game build uses. With fingerprints you can answer: \"this Engine.dll is on my disk. Which build made it?\" and \"which game builds used engine build 87?\". How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Post-build Actions. Click Add post-build action and choose Record fingerprints of files to track usage. In Files to fingerprint type the files, for example Binaries/Win64/*.dll. Click Save. Do the same on the build configuration that uses the files, so the use is recorded there too. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Files to fingerprint. Files to record, as Ant patterns, relative to the workspace. Empty. Binaries/Win64/*.dll. Excludes. Files that match are not recorded. Comma separated. Empty. **/*.pdb. Use default excludes. Leave out the files version control tools make. On. On. Treat include and exclude patterns as case sensitive. Off matches any case. On. On. A worked example. Engine Build fingerprints Binaries/Win64/*.dll. Windows Build copies the DLLs as an artifact dependency. A fingerprint check shows that Windows Build #142 used Engine Build #87. What you see when it runs. The build page has a See fingerprints section with the recorded files. Each file shows where else it was used. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. No fingerprints are shown. The files did not match. Check the pattern. A file has no \"used in\" entry. The other build configuration does not fingerprint it. Archive with Fingerprint all archived artifacts there. Related pages. After the build: overview. Post-build actions tab. Archive the artifacts."},
{"title":"Send build artifacts over FTP","href":"post-build/ftp","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Sends files from the build to a server over FTP, when the build ends. When to use it. Use it to put build output where other people or machines can get it. Use it for servers that only offer FTP. An administrator sets up the servers once in the global settings. The same form is also offered as a build step of a promotion, for build configurations that use promotions. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Post-build Actions. Click Add post-build action and choose Send build artifacts over FTP. Click Add under Publishers and choose the server in Name. Add a transfer set: Source files and the other fields. Open Advanced for retries, labels and what to do on an error. Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Name. The server, chosen from the servers set up in the global settings. None. build-share-01. Source files. Files to send, as a comma separated Ant pattern. The base folder is the workspace. Empty. Output/**. Remove prefix. The first part of the path that is not created on the server. Empty. Output. Remote directory. A folder below the one set in the global settings. It is created when missing. Empty. skyforge/nightly. Exclude files. Files that match are not sent. Comma separated Ant patterns. Empty. **/*.pdb. Pattern separator. The regular expression that separates patterns in the two fields above. [, ]+. [,]+. No default excludes. Send files that Ant leaves out by default. Off. Off. Make empty dirs. Also create folders that match, even when empty. Off. Off. Flatten files. Send only files, no folders. File names must be unique, or the transfer stops. Off. Off. Clean remote. Delete the files in the remote directory before sending. Off. Off. Remote directory is a date format. The remote directory is a Java date pattern, filled with the build date. Off. On with yyyy-MM-dd. Transfer mode. Auto, Binary or ASCII. Auto picks by file type. Use Binary for game builds. Binary (older settings: the ASCII box). Binary. Credentials. Advanced. Other credentials for this server than the ones set globally. Global. -. Retry: Retries. Advanced. How many times to try again after an error. Off. 3. Retry: Delay. Advanced. Milliseconds to wait between tries. Off. 10000. Label. Advanced. A label for this server, used for parameterized publishing. Empty. live. Parameterized publishing: Parameter name. Name of a build parameter that holds a regular expression. Only servers whose label matches are used. Off. PUBLISH_TO. Verbose output in console. Advanced. A lot of detail in the build log, for finding problems. Off. Off. Publish to other FTP servers if an error occurs / Fail the build if an error occurs. Advanced (on the whole list). Keep going with the other servers after an error; mark the build failed instead of unstable. Off / Off. -. Always transfer from master / Give the master a NODE_NAME. Advanced. Send the files through the server, which helps with strict firewalls. Off. -. Use the workspace / Use promotion timestamp. Promotion only. Take files from the workspace instead of the artifacts of the promoted build; use the promotion time for the date format. Off. -. A worked example. The nightly Docs build sends site/** to the web host over FTP with Transfer mode set to Binary, into the remote directory wiki. What you see when it runs. The log shows the FTP connection and each file sent. An error makes the build unstable, or failed if you chose that. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Login incorrect. Check the credentials of the server in the global settings. Files are damaged. Use Binary transfer mode, not ASCII. The server drops the connection. Turn on a retry in Advanced. Related pages. After the build: overview. Post-build actions tab."},
{"title":"Git Publisher","href":"post-build/git-publisher","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Pushes tags, branches or notes to your Git remote when the build ends. When to use it. Use it to tag a release in Git after a good build, or to push a generated file to a branch. It works with the Git root of the build configuration. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Post-build Actions. Click Add post-build action and choose Git Publisher. Tick Push Only If Build Succeeds unless you want to push after a failed build too. Under Tags , Branches or Notes click Add and fill in what to push. Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Push Only If Build Succeeds. Push to the remotes only when the build succeeded. Off. On. Merge Results. If a merge was set up before the build, push the merge result back to the origin. Off. Off. Force Push. Add the force option to git push. Off. Off. Tag to push. The tag name. Build variables may be used. Empty. skyforge-${BUILD_NUMBER}. Tag message. The message of the tag. Empty. Build ${BUILD_NUMBER}. Create new tag. Create the tag now. The push fails if the tag already exists. Off. On. Update new tag. Move the tag if it already exists. Off. Off. Target remote name. The remote to push to. It must be one of the repositories in the Git root. Empty. origin. Branch to push. The remote branch to push the current commit to. Empty. release/1.4. Rebase before push. Rebase your changes before the push. Off. Off. Note to push. The text of a Git note. Empty. Built by Buildspawn. Note's namespace. The notes namespace. master. master. Abort if note exists. Do not replace a note that already exists. Off. Off. A worked example. After Windows Build passes, the studio tags the Git commit of the launcher: Tag to push launcher-${BUILD_NUMBER} , Create new tag on, Target remote name origin. What you see when it runs. The log shows the git tag and git push commands and their result. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Push rejected. The tag exists, or the branch moved. Use Update new tag , or Force Push if you really mean it. Authentication failed. The credential of the Git root must be allowed to push. Related pages. After the build: overview. Post-build actions tab. Git."},
{"title":"After the build","href":"post-build/overview","headings":["How they work","Pick the right one","Related pages"],"text":"Post-build actions run when the build steps have finished. They keep the files, report the tests, tell people and start other work. How they work. They run in the order of the list, after the last build step. A post-build action never changes how the steps ran. Some can change the result: a failed upload can make the build unstable or failed. Open them in Settings › Post-build Actions of a build configuration. See the Post-build Actions tab. A template or a project can pass post-build actions down to many build configurations. Pick the right one. I want to. Use. Keep the files the build made so I can download them. Archive the artifacts. Show test results and trends. Publish JUnit test result report. See the test results of the builds that follow this one. Aggregate downstream test results. Start another build when this one ends. Build other build configurations. Find out where a file came from. Record fingerprints of files to track usage. Email people when a build fails. E-mail Notification. Push a tag or branch to Git. Git Publisher. Label the Perforce revision that was built. Label in Perforce (VCS labeling) or Perforce: Label build. Submit or shelve changed files to Perforce. Perforce: Publish assets. Delete the Perforce workspace after the build. Perforce: Cleanup. Approve or reject a Swarm review. Perforce: ApproveImpl review. Show a badge in Unreal Game Sync. UGS metadata server badge. Copy files to a server over SSH, FTP or a Windows share. SSH , FTP , Windows share. Deploy a web application to a server. Deploy war/ear to a container. Some post-build settings also show as build features , for example Notifications, XML report processing and VCS labeling. They are the same settings. A Pipeline does all this in its script. Related pages. The Post-build Actions tab. The Build Features tab."},
{"title":"Perforce: ApproveImpl review","href":"post-build/perforce-approve-review","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Changes the state of a Helix Swarm review when the build ends, for example to approve it. When to use it. Use it in a build that checks a review. A good build approves it. You can also use a vote or a change of state. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Post-build Actions. Click Add post-build action and choose Perforce: ApproveImpl review. Choose the P4 Credentials. Type the Swarm Review number, usually as a variable such as ${review}. Choose the Approve Status. Type a Description. Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. P4 Credentials. The Perforce credential to use. It must be allowed to change the review. None. p4-swarm-bot. Swarm Review. The review ID. Empty. ${review}. Approve Status. What to set: Needs Review, Needs Revision, Approve, Approve and Commit, Reject, Archive, Vote Up or Vote Down. None. Approve. Description. A comment saved with the change. Empty. Build ${BUILD_NUMBER} passed. A worked example. The shelf check build runs on a Swarm review and, if it passes, uses Vote Up with the description Build ${BUILD_NUMBER} passed. What you see when it runs. The review shows the new state and your description. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Review not found. Check the review ID. Approve and Commit is refused. The user needs permission to commit from Swarm. Related pages. After the build: overview. Post-build actions tab. Check a shelf before submit. Shelves and personal builds."},
{"title":"Perforce: Cleanup","href":"post-build/perforce-cleanup","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Deletes the Perforce workspace (client) after the build. When to use it. Use it with agents that are created and removed often, so the server does not collect old workspaces. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Post-build Actions. Click Add post-build action and choose Perforce: Cleanup. Tick Delete Client. Tick Force Delete Client only if a normal delete fails. Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Delete Client. Delete the workspace spec after the build ( p4 client -d ). Off. On. Force Delete Client. Force the delete ( p4 client -f -d ). Off. Off. A worked example. Cloud build agents that are thrown away each night use Delete Client , so the Perforce server does not keep a workspace per agent. What you see when it runs. The log shows the workspace being deleted. Files on the agent are not touched. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Delete fails: the client has opened files. Use Force Delete Client , or revert the files first. Related pages. After the build: overview. Post-build actions tab. Perforce."},
{"title":"Perforce: Label build","href":"post-build/perforce-label-build","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Creates a Perforce label for the changelist that the build synced, with a name and description you choose. When to use it. This is the label action of the Perforce root settings. Use it for a simple label. If you need a branch filter or a root choice, use Label in Perforce (VCS labeling). How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Post-build Actions. Click Add post-build action and choose Perforce: Label build. Set the Label Name and Label Description. Tick Only tag on build success if failed builds must not be labelled. Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Label Name. The name of the label. Build variables may be used. buildspawn- plus the name of the build configuration. skyforge-${BUILD_NUMBER}. Label Description. The description text. Buildspawn post-build label. Skyforge nightly. Only tag on build success. Label only when the build succeeded. Off. On. A worked example. Release Build labels the synced changelist as skyforge-release-${BUILD_NUMBER} only on success. What you see when it runs. The log says the label was created for the changelist. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Label name is not valid. Perforce label names cannot contain spaces or @#*%. Use letters, digits, - and _. Related pages. After the build: overview. Post-build actions tab. Label in Perforce (VCS labeling). Perforce."},
{"title":"Label in Perforce (VCS labeling)","href":"post-build/perforce-label","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Puts a label on the Perforce revision that the build used. When to use it. It is the same setting as the build feature VCS labeling ; all fields are described there. Use it to find the exact files of a build later. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Post-build Actions. Click Add post-build action and choose Label in Perforce (VCS labeling). Set the Label pattern and Label builds. Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Perforce root. Which root to label when there are several. The only root. Main. Label pattern. The label name. Build variables may be used. build-${BUILD_NUMBER}. skyforge-${BUILD_NUMBER}. Label builds. Successful builds only, or every build. Successful builds only. Successful. Branch filter. Rules +:name and -:name , one per line. Empty. +:main. Label description. Description text for the label. Empty. Skyforge build ${BUILD_NUMBER}. Move a label Buildspawn created. Allow moving a label that Buildspawn made. Off. Off. Email on failure. Who to tell when labelling fails. Empty. p4-admin@studio.example. A worked example. Each good Windows Build gets the label skyforge-142. The artists sync that label to see the build's files. What you see when it runs. The label shows on the Perforce Labels tab. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The label already exists. Put the build number in the pattern. Related pages. After the build: overview. Post-build actions tab. VCS labeling. Perforce: Label build."},
{"title":"Perforce: Publish assets","href":"post-build/perforce-publish","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Submits or shelves the files that the build changed, back to Perforce. When to use it. Use it for generated files that belong in the depot, for example cooked content lists, or version files. Shelve instead of submit when someone must review first. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Post-build Actions. Click Add post-build action and choose Perforce: Publish assets. Choose the P4 Credentials. Under Workspace behaviour choose how the workspace is made. The choices are Manual (custom view) , Spec File (load workspace spec from file in Perforce) , Static (static view, controller only) , Streams (view generated by Perforce for each node) and Template (view generated for each node). Under Publish options choose Submit change or Shelve change , and fill in its fields. Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. P4 Credentials. The Perforce credential to use. None. p4-build-user. Workspace behaviour. How the workspace for the publish is defined. None. Streams. Publish options. Submit change or Shelve change. None. Submit change. Description. The changelist description. Variables are expanded. Submitted by Buildspawn. Build: ${BUILD_TAG} (shelve: Shelved by Buildspawn. ). Update version file. Purge Revisions. Submit only. How many revisions the server keeps: 1 to 10, or 16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512. Empty. 16. Only publish on build success. Publish only when the build succeeded. Off. On. Propagate deletes. Also publish files that were deleted. Off. Off. Use MODTIME for consistency check. Compare file modification times. Off. Off. Reopened files. Submit only. Keep the submitted files open for edit. Off. Off. Revert opened file list. Shelve only. Revert the open files but keep the content in the workspace. Off. Off. Detect filetype changes. Open files whose Perforce file type no longer matches their content. On. On. Publish paths. Advanced. Which paths to publish. The root path. //depot/Main/Version/. A worked example. After Release Build writes Version/build.txt , a post-build action submits it: Submit change , Only publish on build success on, description Release build ${BUILD_NUMBER} version file. What you see when it runs. The log shows the changelist number that was submitted or shelved. It appears in the depot history under the build user. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Nothing to publish. No file changed in the workspace, or the paths do not match. Submit refused by a trigger. A Perforce trigger rejected it. Fix the description or use a shelf. Related pages. After the build: overview. Post-build actions tab. Perforce. Shelves and personal builds."},
{"title":"Send build artifacts over SSH","href":"post-build/ssh","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Sends files from the build to a server over SSH, when the build ends. When to use it. Use it to put build output where other people or machines can get it. You can also run a command on the server after the files arrive. An administrator sets up the servers once in the global settings. The same form is also offered as a build step of a promotion, for build configurations that use promotions. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Post-build Actions. Click Add post-build action and choose Send build artifacts over SSH. Click Add under Publishers and choose the server in Name. Add a transfer set: Source files and the other fields. Open Advanced for retries, labels and what to do on an error. Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Name. The server, chosen from the servers set up in the global settings. None. build-share-01. Source files. Files to send, as a comma separated Ant pattern. The base folder is the workspace. Empty. Output/**. Remove prefix. The first part of the path that is not created on the server. Empty. Output. Remote directory. A folder below the one set in the global settings. It is created when missing. Empty. skyforge/nightly. Exclude files. Files that match are not sent. Comma separated Ant patterns. Empty. **/*.pdb. Exec command. A command to run on the server after the files are sent. A transfer set needs files, a command, or both. Empty./deploy.sh. Pattern separator. The regular expression that separates patterns in the two fields above. [, ]+. [,]+. No default excludes. Send files that Ant leaves out by default. Off. Off. Make empty dirs. Also create folders that match, even when empty. Off. Off. Flatten files. Send only files, no folders. File names must be unique, or the transfer stops. Off. Off. Clean remote. Delete the files in the remote directory before sending. Off. Off. Remote directory is a date format. The remote directory is a Java date pattern, filled with the build date. Off. On with yyyy-MM-dd. Exec timeout (ms). Advanced. How long the command may run. 0 turns it off. 120000. 60000. Exec in pty. Advanced. Run the command in a pseudo terminal, so commands such as sudo that need a terminal work. Off. On. Exec using Agent Forwarding. Advanced. Pass key checks back to the original agent for chained SSH. Off. Off. Keep file permissions. Advanced. Copy the file permissions (POSIX only). Off. Off. Use SFTP for Exec. Advanced. Use SFTP commands ( mkdir , ln , rm , rmdir , cd , get , ls ) instead of a shell. Off. Off. Credentials. Advanced. Other credentials for this server than the ones set globally. Global. -. Retry: Retries. Advanced. How many times to try again after an error. Off. 3. Retry: Delay. Advanced. Milliseconds to wait between tries. Off. 10000. Label. Advanced. A label for this server, used for parameterized publishing. Empty. live. Parameterized publishing: Parameter name. Name of a build parameter that holds a regular expression. Only servers whose label matches are used. Off. PUBLISH_TO. Verbose output in console. Advanced. A lot of detail in the build log, for finding problems. Off. Off. Publish to other SSH servers if an error occurs / Fail the build if an error occurs. Advanced (on the whole list). Keep going with the other servers after an error; mark the build failed instead of unstable. Off / Off. -. Always transfer from master / Give the master a NODE_NAME. Advanced. Send the files through the server, which helps with strict firewalls. Off. -. Use the workspace / Use promotion timestamp. Promotion only. Take files from the workspace instead of the artifacts of the promoted build; use the promotion time for the date format. Off. -. A worked example. The Linux Server Build sends Builds/Linux/** to the studio server build-share-01 , into skyforge/nightly/2026-11-27 (date format yyyy-MM-dd ), then runs./restart-test-server.sh. What you see when it runs. The log shows the connection, each file sent and the command output. An "},
{"title":"Publish JUnit test result report","href":"post-build/test-report","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Reads test report files in JUnit format and shows the results on the build. When to use it. Use it when your tests write XML reports. It is the same setting as the build feature XML report processing. The fields are described there. This page shows the short way. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Post-build Actions. Click Add post-build action and choose Publish JUnit test result report. In Test report XMLs type the files, for example Reports/*.xml. Use Validate Pattern to check. Click Save. Every field. The most used fields. All of them are in XML report processing. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Test report XMLs. The report files as an Ant pattern. Empty. Reports/*.xml. Test output retention. How much test output is kept. Standard. Standard. Health report amplification factor. Effect of failed tests on build health. 1.0. 1.0. Allow empty results. Do not fail the build when no results are found. Off. Off. Skip marking build as unstable on test failure. Keep the build green on test failures. Off. Off. A worked example. The Unit Tests configuration of the Engine project writes Reports/tests.xml. The post-build action reads Reports/*.xml. What you see when it runs. The build page gets a Tests tab. A build with a failed test is Unstable. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. No test report files found. Check the pattern and that the test step ran. Old results are counted. Turn on Skip report files older than build start. Related pages. After the build: overview. Post-build actions tab. XML report processing."},
{"title":"UGS metadata server badge","href":"post-build/ugs-badge","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Sets a badge for a changelist on an Unreal Game Sync metadata server, so developers see in UGS whether the changelist built. When to use it. Use it with an Unreal Engine project in Perforce. The full page, with a worked example, is UGS metadata server badge in the Game builds section. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Post-build Actions. Click Add post-build action and choose UGS metadata server badge. Type the Server URL and use Test connection. Type the Badge name and the Project depot path. Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Server URL. The metadata server, for example http://localhost:3000. Empty. http://ugs.studio.example:3000. Badge. The badge name shown in UGS. Empty. Editor. Project. The depot path of the project folder, not the.uproject file. Empty. //UE5/Main/Samples/Games/Lyra. Credentials. Optional. A Secret text credential sent as a bearer token. Metadata servers in a closed network usually need none. None. -. A worked example. After the Windows Build , badge Editor for //UE5/Main/Skyforge turns green in UGS for the built changelist. What you see when it runs. The log shows the update sent to the server. The badge changes in UGS. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Test connection fails. Check the address and that the metadata server is running. Related pages. After the build: overview. Post-build actions tab. UGS metadata server badge (full page). Unreal Engine step."},
{"title":"Agent Requirements","href":"projects/bc-agent-requirements","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Which agents may run this build configuration, as rules on agent parameters. When to use it. Use it when only some machines can build: an agent with Windows and Visual Studio, or a PS5 devkit agent. Builds wait for an agent that meets every rule. The page also lists the compatible agents. It is under More on the Settings tabs. The Agent Requirements tab. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings. Then click More › Agent Requirements. Click Add requirement. Choose the Parameter (for example agent.os.name ), the Condition and the Value. Check the list of Compatible agents on the right. Click Save requirement. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Parameter name. An agent parameter: agent.name , agent.pool , agent.labels , agent.os.family , agent.os.name , agent.os.arch , agent.java.version , agent.cpu.count , agent.memory.total.mb , or a tool the agent found. An administrator can add custom ones on the agent. -. agent.pool. Condition. exists , does not exist , equals , does not equal , more than , no more than , less than , no less than , starts with , ends with , contains , does not contain , matches , does not match , and the version forms version more than , version no more than , version less than , version no less than. -. equals. Value. What to compare with. Not used for exists and does not exist. A matches value is a regular expression. -. Unreal. Label expression. The older Labels field. It shows as one row of kind label when it has a value, for example windows && gpu. Empty. windows && gpu. From a step. Rows that come from the enabled build steps, for example a tool the Unity step needs. Change the step to change them, or Ignore one when the tool is somewhere the agent cannot see. -. -. A worked example. Windows Build needs agent.os.family equals Windows and agent.pool equals Unreal. The page shows 3 of 8 agents are compatible. What you see when it runs. A queued build that no agent can run says why on the Queue page. The Compatible agents list shows each agent as compatible or incompatible with the reason. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The build waits forever. No agent meets all rules. Open this tab and look at Incompatible and Why. A tool requirement from a step blocks builds. The agent cannot see the tool. Fix the agent, or Ignore the requirement. A name is refused. A parameter name has letters, digits, dots, dashes and underscores. A condition other than exists needs a value. Related pages. Settings homes. Agent pools. Agent parameters. Agents."},
{"title":"Build Features","href":"projects/bc-build-features","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Setup and reporting around the build steps: timestamps, clean workspace, secrets, test reports, notifications and more. When to use it. Use it for settings that act on the whole build, not on one step. A feature that is off stays saved but does not run. The top list shows the features this build configuration uses. Available lists the rest. The Build Features tab. The Add build feature picker. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings. Then click Build Features. Click Add build feature , or click Set up on a card under Available. Fill in the form of the feature and click Save. Use the On switch on a feature to turn it off without deleting it. This page saves each change at once. Every field. The picker lists these features. Each has its own page. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Timestamps in the build log. Shows the time in front of every line of the build log. -. Page. Delete the workspace before the build. Empties the workspace before the sources are fetched. -. Page. Bind credentials to variables. Makes stored secrets available to the steps as variables. -. Page. Parallel tests. Splits the tests into batches that run at the same time. -. Page. XML report processing. Reads test report files (JUnit format) and shows the results. -. Page. Commit status publisher. Reports the build result to Helix Swarm, GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket Cloud or Gitea. -. Page. Notifications. Sends an email when a build finishes or changes result. -. Page. VCS labeling. Labels the built Perforce revision. -. Page. Unity build settings. Activates and returns a Unity license around the Unity steps, and sets the asset cache server. -. Page. Other build setup. Features that wrap a build, each with its own settings: SSH before the build , SSH after the build , Project Properties. -. -. Documentation. Links on the Documentation tab. -. Page. A worked example. Windows Build uses Timestamps in the build log , Bind credentials to variables for the store token, and Notifications to the release team. The build time limit is set under Failure Conditions. What you see when it runs. The list shows a one-line summary for each feature, for example Email to release-team@studio.example when a build fails and when it is fixed. A feature with a problem shows what is missing. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. A feature is greyed out or missing. The build configuration cannot hold it, or the plugin behind it is not installed. An inherited feature. A project passes it down. Use Override , Disable in this job or Reset. Post-build actions are not here. They are on Post-build Actions. Notifications, XML report processing and VCS labeling show in both places. Related pages. Settings homes. Failure conditions. Post-build actions."},
{"title":"Build Steps","href":"projects/bc-build-steps","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"The list of steps a build runs, in order. This page explains how the tab works. Each kind of step has its own page in the Build steps section. When to use it. Use it to add, order, switch off and override steps. Steps run from top to bottom. Steps that a project or a template passes down run first. The Build Steps tab. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings. Then click Build Steps. Click Add build step , pick a runner such as Command Line or Unreal Engine , and fill in its fields. Drag the handle on the left of a row to change the order. Use the Enabled switch to skip a step without losing its settings. The changes on this tab are saved when you finish a step. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Name. What the step is called in the list and in the log. Runner name. Cook content. Runner. The kind of step: Command Line, PowerShell, Unity, Unreal Engine, MSBuild and more. See Runners. -. Unreal Engine. Summary. A short line of what the step runs. -. -. When it runs. The execution rule of the step: only when earlier steps passed, always, and so on. See Common step fields. Only if all previous steps succeeded. Always run. Enabled. Switch a step off to skip it. It stays saved. On. On. Add Parallel block. Steps that run side by side. See Parallel blocks. -. -. Edit and the … menu. Open a step. The menu copies, moves or deletes it. -. -. Override / Disable in this job / Reset. On an inherited step: change it here only, switch it off here only, or go back to what the project or template gives. -. -. A worked example. Windows Build has three steps: Sync and clean (Perforce operations), Cook content (Unreal Engine) and Package (PowerShell). The project Game passes down a first step that prints the build number. It shows with a chip Game ; the studio disables it on the Linux server build only. What you see when it runs. The build page lists each step with its result. The console log shows one block per step. A step that is switched off shows as skipped. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. I cannot edit a step. It is inherited. Use Override , or edit it on the project or template it comes from. A step shows Disabled above. It was switched off on a project higher up. Change it there. A Pipeline shows read-only steps. A Pipeline keeps its steps in the script. See Pipeline. Related pages. Settings homes. Runners. The step list. Parallel blocks. Templates."},
{"title":"Dependencies","href":"projects/bc-dependencies","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","Snapshot dependency","Artifact dependency","Dependency options","When is a build suitable?","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"What a build waits for, which files it takes from other builds, and how it behaves while the builds around it are running. When to use it. Use it to build a chain: the game build needs the engine build first and takes its files. A suitable earlier build is reused, so a chain does not rebuild what did not change. Dependencies is under More on the Settings tabs. The Dependencies tab. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings. Then click More › Dependencies. Under Snapshot Dependencies click Add snapshot dependency. Choose the build configuration this one needs. Choose the options and what happens when the dependency fails. Under Artifact Dependencies click Add artifact dependency to copy files from another build. Click Save. Every field. Snapshot dependency. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Dependency. The build configuration to run first. None. Engine/Linux Server Build. Do not run new build if there is a suitable one. Reuse a suitable existing build instead of starting a new one. On. On. Only use successful builds from suitable ones. Failed builds are never reused, even if they are otherwise suitable. On. On. On failed dependency. What this build does when the dependency fails: Run build, but add problem , Run build, but do not add problem , Make build failed to start or Cancel build. Run build, but add problem. Cancel build. Artifact dependency. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Get artifacts from. Which build to take files from: Build from the same chain (needs a snapshot dependency on the same build configuration), Last successful build , Last pinned build , Last finished build or Build number. Build from the same chain. Last successful build. Artifact rules. One rule per line: a path or pattern in the build's artifacts, optionally => a folder in the workspace. Lines that start with -: exclude files. Empty. dist/**/*.zip => packages. Clean destination paths before downloading artifacts. Delete the contents of each target folder before copying. Never the workspace root. Off. On. Dependency options. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Wait while an upstream build configuration is building. This build configuration does not start while a build configuration it depends on is building or waiting in the queue. Off. On. Wait while a downstream build configuration is building. This one does not start while a build configuration that follows it is building or waiting. Off. Off. Keep the build logs of dependencies. While a build of this build configuration is kept, the builds it used from other build configurations are kept too. Off. On. When is a build suitable? It succeeded (when the dependency only uses successful builds) and was started after the dependency's configuration was last saved. It used the same parameter values the chain asks for. It is the dependency's newest build: no newer build is running or queued. The builds of its own snapshot dependencies are reused too. Run on the build configuration can rebuild failed and incomplete dependencies, or all of them, whatever these options say. A worked example. Windows Build has a snapshot dependency on Engine/Linux Server Build and an artifact dependency that copies dist/**/*.zip => packages. When you run the game build, the engine build runs first if there is no suitable one. What you see when it runs. A chain shows the builds as a graph on the Build Chains tab. The log says which builds were reused. A build started this way says Started as a snapshot dependency of Windows Build. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The dependency is rebuilt every time. Its parameters differ from the chain's, or a newer build is queued. See the list of what makes a build suitable. An artifact rule fails. Enter at least one rule. A target path must be inside the workspace and must not contain. Related pages. Settings homes. Snapshot and artifact dependencies. Build chains. Use another build's artifacts."},
{"title":"Failure Conditions","href":"projects/bc-failure-conditions","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Says when a build counts as failed or unstable, besides the exit code of a step. When to use it. Use it to catch problems that a step does not report: a build that takes too long, a log line such as an out-of-memory error, or a sudden drop in the number of tests. For each condition you choose the Result : Failed or Unstable. A result can only get worse, never better. The Failure Conditions tab. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings. Then click Failure Conditions. Switch on the conditions you need and choose the Result of each. Fill in the minutes, the percentages or the log rules. Click Save. The card Summary says in words when a build fails. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Non-zero exit code. Fail the build if a build step exits with a non-zero code. The step policy still decides what runs after a failure. Does not apply to a Pipeline. On, Failed. On. Build time: Fixed limit. Fail if the build runs longer than the number of minutes. Counted from the start of the first step; time in the queue is not counted. Off. 90 minutes. Build time: Compared with earlier builds. Limit = the average of recent successful builds plus a margin. Hanging build detection: three times the average, and at least 5 minutes over it. Off. Margin 150 %. Build time: No new log output. Fail when the log stays silent for the number of minutes you give. Off. 20 minutes. Test failures. Fail the build if some tests failed. Off adds nothing: the step that reads the tests can still mark the build unstable. Off. Unstable. Text in the build log. Add log condition: a text or pattern that matches, or does not match, a line in the log. Several rules are allowed. Add out-of-memory rule adds a ready-made rule. None. Out of memory fails. Metric change: Test count drops by more than. Compared with the last successful build. Percent. Empty is off. Empty. 10 %. Metric change: Artifact size drops by more than. Compared with the last successful build. Percent. Empty is off. Empty. 20 %. A worked example. The PS5 Build uses Compared with earlier builds with a 150% margin, a log rule that fails the build on LogOutOfMemory , and Artifact size drops by more than 20 % as Unstable , to notice a package that lost content. What you see when it runs. When a limit is reached the build is stopped and marked as you set. A line in the log says which condition fired. The limits are checked every 10 seconds, so a build can stop up to 10 seconds late. Build stopped: it ran longer than 90 minutes (failure condition Build time). Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The minutes are refused. Use a whole number of minutes in the allowed range. A timeout shows that I did not add. It was set earlier as a build timeout. It is kept and shown here. Changing it here moves it into the failure conditions. The Exit code line is missing. A Pipeline decides what a failed step does, so that condition does not apply. Related pages. Settings homes. Terminate a build if it is stuck. XML report processing."},
{"title":"General","href":"projects/bc-general","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","Build configuration","Build number","Artifacts","Build options","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"The name, ID, icon, build number and artifact rules of a build configuration, plus how builds are queued, limited, cleaned up and checked out. When to use it. Open General first when you make a build configuration. It says what the build is called, which icon shows in the tree, how builds are numbered, and how many builds may run at once. Everything else on this page is optional. The General tab. The index on the left jumps to a card; the summary on the right shows the next build number. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings. Then click General. Check the Name and the Build configuration ID. Choose an Icon for the platform. Set the Build number format if you want names such as 1.4.2. Open the other cards (Artifacts, Build options, Clean-up rules, Checkout options) if you need them. Click Save , or Apply to save and stay on the page. Every field. Build configuration. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Name. What the build configuration is called. It is part of its address. To rename, use Rename in the page header. The name you gave. Windows Build. Build configuration ID. Stable and unique on the server. Dependencies and links use it. Letters, digits and underscores, starting with a letter. Changing it keeps the old ID as an alias. Regenerate makes a new one from the name. See IDs. Parent project ID, an underscore and the name. Game_WindowsBuild. Description. Free text shown under the name. Empty. Compiles, cooks and packages Skyforge for Windows. Icon and Icon colour. The platform the build makes. Shown in the tree, in tables and in the Run dialog. See Project icons. Generic. Windows. Build number. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Build number format. Text with %build.counter% and other parameters. It becomes the display name of each build. The plain number stays the build number. Empty means the usual #N. Empty. 1.4.%build.counter%. Build counter. The number the next build gets. It cannot go below the last build. Last number plus 1. 500. Artifacts. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Artifact paths. One rule per line: source => target folder. Files kept with each build when it ends. Build configurations with the Archive the artifacts action keep it in that action instead. Empty. Output/** => game. Publish artifacts only if the build succeeds. Off: artifacts are kept when the build fails too. On: a failed build keeps none, an unstable one still does. Off. On. Build options. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Display name. Shown in lists and in the page title instead of the name. Optional. Empty. Win64 Dev. Build priority. Decides the order of builds that wait for an agent. Builds with the same priority go first come, first served. A whole number from -100 to 100. 0. 10. Quiet period. Seconds a new build waits in the queue, so several triggers can join one build. Empty uses the server default. Server default. 60. JDK. Shown only when the server has a JDK set up. The build runs with this JDK on the agent. System. -. Concurrent builds. Allow concurrent builds (a new build starts at once when an agent is free), Limit the number of builds at once (Up to N builds at once; the rest wait), or Run one build at a time (a new build waits until the running one ends). Run one build at a time for a new build configuration. Limit, up to 2. Throttle builds. Limits how often builds start, however they are triggered: Allow N builds per time period. A tick lets builds that a person starts ignore the limit. Off. 1 build per hour. The rest of the page, Clean-up rules and Checkout options , is described on Retention and discard old builds and Workspace options. A worked example. The Skyforge Windows Build uses the ID Game_WindowsBuild and the Windows icon. The Build number format is 1.4.%build.counter% , so the first build of the day shows as 1.4.142. Build priority is 10 so it goes before the nightly Linux builds when agents are scarce. What you see when it runs. The build page and the History list show "},
{"title":"Other settings","href":"projects/bc-other-settings","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Settings that add-ons put on a build configuration and that have no page of their own. When to use it. Use it to find a setting that you cannot see on the other tabs. Triggers are on the Triggers tab and build setup and reporting on Build Features. Any other setting is listed here, in a group named after the add-on that adds it. It is under More. The Other settings tab. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings. Then click More › Other settings. Find the group of the add-on. The header says From N plugins. Change the fields and click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Group per add-on. Each group shows the fields of the setting, for example Refresh Perforce lists on a custom interval. -. -. (no settings). When no add-on adds anything the page says so. -. -. A worked example. The Perforce add-on adds Refresh Perforce lists on a custom interval. On Windows Build the studio sets Interval to 300 seconds. See Job settings. What you see when it runs. The settings are saved with the build configuration. They take effect on the next build. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The page is empty. No add-on has a setting for this build configuration. Related pages. Settings homes. Job settings."},
{"title":"Parameters","href":"projects/bc-parameters","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Every value a build passes to its steps, in one table. This page explains how the tab works. When to use it. Use it to define values that the build asks for or passes on. Parameters can come from the build configuration, a template or a project. The kinds and types have their own pages in the Parameters section. The Parameters tab. Password values are always masked. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings. Then click Parameters. Use the filters Configuration , Environment and System to find a parameter. Click Add parameter. Type the name and choose the type. Set the default value. Choose whether the build asks for it when it starts. Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Configuration parameters. Read as %NAME% in steps. They are also in every step's environment. -. PLATFORM. Environment variables ( env.NAME ). Every step's process gets them. -. env.BUILD_CONFIG. System properties ( system.NAME ). Go to build tools. -. system.version. Defined in. Whether the value is this build configuration's own or comes from a project or template. -. Game. When it is set. Whether it is asked when the build runs, and its type: text, choice, boolean, password or file. -. Asked, choice. Add parameter. Adds one. Edit on a row changes it. -. -. A worked example. Windows Build has the parameter PLATFORM with the choices Win64 and Win32 , asked at run time, default Win64. The Unreal step uses %PLATFORM%. What you see when it runs. When you press Run the dialog asks for the asked parameters. The build page lists the values that were used. Passwords show as dots. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. A value is read-only. It comes from a project or template. Use Override to change it here only. A password is empty in the step. Use a password or credentials parameter and read it as an environment variable. Related pages. Settings homes. Parameters. Parameter references. Secrets."},
{"title":"Post-build Actions","href":"projects/bc-post-build-actions","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"What happens after a build completes: keep files, report tests, send mail, start other builds. When to use it. Use it for work that comes after the last step. Post-build actions run in the order of the list. Each one is a card that folds. See After the build for a page on each action. The Post-build Actions tab. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings. Then click Post-build Actions. Click Add post-build action and choose one from the picker. Use the search box to filter. Fill in the card. More options shows how many options are set. Drag the handle to change the order. The bin removes an action. Click Save. Every field. The picker offers these actions. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Archive the artifacts. Keeps files with the build. -. Page. Publish JUnit test result report. Shows test results. -. Page. Aggregate downstream test results. Sums the tests of following builds. -. Page. Build other build configurations. Starts other builds. -. Page. Record fingerprints of files to track usage. Tracks where files are used. -. Page. E-mail Notification. Mails people. -. Page. Git Publisher. Pushes tags and branches. -. Page. Perforce actions. Label, publish, clean up, approve a review. -. Pages. Send build artifacts over SSH, FTP or to a Windows share. Copies files to a server. -. Pages. Deploy war/ear to a container. Deploys a web application. -. Page. UGS metadata server badge. Sets a badge in Unreal Game Sync. -. Page. A worked example. Windows Build archives Output/** , mails the release team, and starts Unit Tests. What you see when it runs. The end of the console log lists what each action did. A failed action can make the build unstable or failed, as its page says. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. An action is missing from the picker. The build configuration type does not support it. A Pipeline uses steps in its script. An action shows as inherited. A project or template passes it down. Override or disable it here. Related pages. Settings homes. After the build. Build features."},
{"title":"Retention and discard old builds","href":"projects/bc-retention","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Rules that delete old builds and their files after each build, so the disk does not fill up. When to use it. Use it when builds and artifacts take much space. A build of a game can have gigabytes of artifacts. Keep the last 30 builds, and keep the artifacts of fewer. The rules run after every build, not at once when you save. The card is on General under Clean-up rules. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings. Then click General. Go to Clean-up rules and turn on Discard old builds. Choose the Strategy : Keep by age and number. Set Days to keep builds or Builds to keep , or both. Set Days to keep artifacts or Builds to keep artifacts of to remove artifacts sooner than the builds. Click Save. Every field. Keep rules are whole numbers from 1. An empty field means no limit. The card shows a line In words that says what the rules do. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Discard old builds. Master switch. When off, nothing is deleted and no limit is set. Off. On. Strategy. The clean-up strategy. Keep by age and number is built in. Other strategies show their own settings here. Keep by age and number. Keep by age and number. Days to keep builds. Builds (with their logs) older than this are deleted. Empty (no limit). 14. Builds to keep. Only this many of the newest builds are kept. Empty (no limit). 30. Days to keep artifacts. Artifacts older than this are deleted. The builds stay. Empty. 7. Builds to keep artifacts of. Only the newest builds keep artifacts. Empty. 5. Remove the last build too when it is old enough. Also apply the rules to the newest build. Off. Off. Builds that you pin with Keep this build forever , and the last stable and last successful build, are never deleted by these rules. If both a number and an age are set, a build is deleted when either limit is passed. A worked example. The studio keeps the last 30 builds of Windows Build and the artifacts of the last 5. The game package of 2 GB is then kept for 5 builds only. The log and the change list stay for 30. See Keep the last 30 builds. What you see when it runs. After each build, builds and artifacts outside the rules disappear from the History list. The summary says Nothing is deleted when no limit is set. An administrator can also set a server-wide rule in the global settings. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Old builds are still there. The rules run after a build finishes. Run a build, or wait for the next one. I need one old build. Open it and use Keep this build forever. A number is refused. Keep rules are whole numbers from 1, or empty for no limit. Related pages. Settings homes. General. Keep the last 30 builds. Archive the artifacts."},
{"title":"Triggers","href":"projects/bc-triggers","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"What starts a build by itself: a new change, a schedule, another build, or a web request. This page explains how the tab works. When to use it. Use it to make builds start without a person. Each trigger is one entry that you can switch off without deleting it. Every kind of trigger has its own page in the Running builds section. The Triggers tab. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings. Then click Triggers. Click Add new trigger and choose the kind. Fill in its fields and save the trigger. Use the switch on a trigger to turn it off. For the Remote trigger, copy the address and token shown once. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Add new trigger. Opens a picker of the trigger kinds: VCS, Schedule, Finish Build, Retry build, Perforce and Remote. -. -. On/Off switch. A disabled trigger stays saved but never starts a build. On. On. Remote trigger. Lets a script or another system start a build with a web request. Anyone with the token can start a build without logging in. Regenerate makes a new token, shows it once, and the old one stops at once. Off. On. A worked example. Windows Build has a VCS trigger on the Perforce stream and a Schedule trigger at 02:00 for a full build. What you see when it runs. A started build says why it started, for example Started by a VCS trigger or Started by timer. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Nothing starts. Check that the trigger is on, that polling is on for the root and that the build configuration is not paused. Related pages. Settings homes. VCS trigger. Schedule trigger. Finish Build trigger. Remote trigger."},
{"title":"Version Control","href":"projects/bc-version-control","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Where a build configuration gets its sources: Git, Perforce, a Perforce root that a project shares, or several roots. When to use it. Use this tab to say where the sources are. The page is titled Source Code Management. The card Checkout options below the form is described in Workspace options. The number beside the tab counts the roots when there are several. The details of each choice are in the Version control section. The Version Control tab. A red dot on the tab means a root has a problem. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings. Then click Version Control. Click a card: None , Git , Perforce Software , Perforce root or Several roots. Fill in the form that opens under the cards (see Git and Perforce ). With Several roots each root is a card with its own checkout folder and its own polling switch. Use the arrows to change their order. Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. None. The build configuration fetches no sources. Use it for builds that only run scripts. None. -. Git. A Git repository, with its credential and branches. -. https://git.studio.example/skyforge/launcher.git. Perforce Software. A Perforce server with its own workspace in this build configuration: P4 Credentials , Workspace behaviour (Manual, Spec File, Static, Streams or Template), Workspace name and Workspace options. -. Workspace name skyforge-win64-${NODE_NAME}. Perforce root. A root that a project shares (see Perforce roots ). Server, credential and workspace come from the root. -. Main. Several roots. More than one root, Git or Perforce, each with a checkout folder and a polling switch. -. Perforce game and Git scripts. A worked example. Windows Build uses Perforce Software with the credential Studio Helix Core (buildbot) , a manual workspace view of the //Skyforge/Main stream and the workspace name skyforge-win64-${NODE_NAME}. Each agent fills in its own name. What you see when it runs. The build log starts with the checkout of each root in order. The build page lists the changes of every root. A Perforce connection tab appears next to this tab when the build configuration uses Perforce. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. A red dot on the tab. A root has a problem, for example no connection. Open the tab to see what. Files land in the wrong folder. Set the checkout folder of each root when there are several. The workspace name shows Unknown client. A name with variables such as ${NODE_NAME} is filled in for each agent when the build runs. The page says so. Related pages. Settings homes. Version control overview. Several roots. Git. Perforce."},
{"title":"Workspace options","href":"projects/bc-workspace-options","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","Checkout options","Run behaviour (Pipeline)","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"What happens around getting the sources onto the agent: retries, a custom workspace folder, and how a Pipeline run is saved and resumed. When to use it. Use them when a checkout is unreliable, when you want the sources in a fixed folder, or when a Pipeline must not resume after a restart. They are cards on General , with the Dependency options on the Dependencies tab. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and click Settings. Then click General. Open Checkout options. Set Retry count , or turn on Use a custom workspace and type the Directory. For a Pipeline, open Run behaviour and choose the options below. Click Save. Every field. Checkout options. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Retry count. How often a failed checkout is tried again before the build fails. 0 tries once. Empty uses the server setting. Server setting. 3. Use a custom workspace. Checks the sources out into a folder you choose, not the default one for this build configuration. Off. On. Directory. The custom folder. A relative path is read from the agent's work directory. Builds on the same agent share it, so run them one at a time. Empty. E:\\ws\\skyforge. Run behaviour (Pipeline). Field. What it does. Default. Example. Resume after a controller restart. A running build carries on after the server restarts. Off: a build that is running when the server restarts ends and is not resumed. On. Off. Speed and durability. How often the running Pipeline saves its state. Server default ; Performance-optimized is fastest but a running build is lost if the server stops without a clean shutdown; Less durable, a bit faster ; Maximum survivability saves every step. Server default. Maximum survivability. Keep stashes of completed builds. Stashes are normally deleted when a build ends. Keep them to restart a later stage from a finished build. Number of completed builds says how many. Off. 3. To remove a workspace before a build, use the build feature Delete the workspace before the build. A worked example. The Unity project Unity Windows Build keeps its sources in E:\\ws\\skyforge-unity so the Unity Library folder survives. Retry count is 2 because the Perforce server sometimes drops the first connection. What you see when it runs. The log shows each retry of the checkout. With a custom workspace the log shows that folder as the workspace. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Two builds break each other. They share the custom folder. Use Run one build at a time in Build options. The folder is refused. Enter the directory, or turn the option off. The retry count is refused. It must be 0 or a whole number above it. Related pages. Settings homes. General. Delete the workspace before the build. Pipeline."},
{"title":"Create a build configuration","href":"projects/create-build-configuration","headings":["Create from scratch","The other item types","Fill in the settings","Copy an existing configuration"],"text":"A build configuration says what to build, where and when. You create one inside a project, then fill in its settings one section at a time. A build configuration holds the version control roots to fetch, the build steps to run, the triggers that start it, the parameters it asks for, and the rules that decide if a build passed. Each build it runs keeps its own log, changes, artifacts and test results. Create from scratch. Open the project the build configuration belongs to, for example Game. Click New Item in the page header. Under Item name , type a name, for example Switch Build. Under Type , click the card Build configuration. This is the build configuration with build steps, the visual editor and all the settings homes. Click Create. The build configuration opens on its Settings tab. The New Item page. The ID is made from the project ID and the name. Created in is where the new item goes. The other item types. The New Item page has one card for each type. Each type has its own page: see Item types. Type. Use it for. Build configuration. Steps that run one after another. Use this one in most cases. Page. Pipeline. A build written as a script file. Its steps show read-only on the Build Steps page; the visual editor can edit declarative Pipelines. Page. Build configuration template. Steps and parameters that several build configurations share. Page. Project. A folder that groups build configurations and passes settings down. Page. Multibranch Pipeline. One Pipeline per branch of a repository, under More types. Page. Multi-configuration projects and Organization Folders are no longer offered for new items. Ones that already exist keep working. Fill in the settings. A new build configuration does nothing until you give it steps. Work through the settings tabs from left to right: General: check the name, the Build configuration ID and pick an Icon for the platform it builds. Version Control: add the Git or Perforce roots to fetch. See Git and Perforce. Build Steps: click Add build step and pick a runner, for example Command Line or Unreal Engine. See Runners. Triggers: start builds on a new change or on a schedule. See Triggers. Parameters: add values the build asks for or passes to its steps. Each of these is described on Settings homes and has its own page: General , Version Control , Build Steps , Triggers and Parameters. When you are done, click Run in the header to start the first build. Prefer to draw it? Open Visual editor on the Settings tab and drag steps onto the canvas. The form and the visual editor edit the same settings. See Canvas and palette. Copy an existing configuration. The fastest way to make a PS5 Build when you already have a Windows Build is to copy it. Open the project where the copy should go and click New Item. Type the new name. Under Start from choose Copy of an existing item. In Copy from , start typing the name of the build configuration to copy and pick it from the list. Under Settings of the copy , choose what happens to inherited settings: Keep inheriting : the copy uses the settings of the project it is created in. Pick this when the copy should follow its new project. Make inherited settings its own : the copy keeps running the same steps and parameters, whatever the project above holds. Pick this when you copy into another project and want the build to stay the same. Click Create , then change what differs, such as the platform in the steps. Overrides are copied either way. The copy gets a new ID, and it starts with no build history. More on copies and templates: Copying settings. If many build configurations share most of their steps, a template is better than copies: you change the template once and every build configuration follows."},
{"title":"Documentation links","href":"projects/documentation-links","headings":["Add a link","On a build configuration","On a project","Where links show"],"text":"Link a build configuration to the pages your team needs: the runbook, the release checklist, the SDK versions. When a build fails at night, the answer is one click away. Documentation links are web addresses with a label and a short description. You can add them to a build configuration, to a project, or to both. They show on the build configuration's Documentation tab. The Documentation tab, right after Overview. The build feature has its own page: Documentation. Project links are in Project settings. Add a link. On a build configuration. Open the build configuration and click Settings › Build Features. Find Documentation and click Set up (or Edit if it is already on). You can also click Edit documentation on the Documentation tab. Give the section a title and a one-line summary , for example Windows build runbook and How the Windows build works, who owns it and what to do when it fails. Add a link for each page: a label , the address and a short description. Save. Make sure the On switch of the Documentation card is on. On a project. Open the project and click Settings. Scroll to Documentation under General. Add a summary and the links, the same way, and click Save. Every build configuration in the project and its subprojects shows these links, under the project's name. Only http and https addresses are accepted. Links always open in a new browser tab. Where links show. The Documentation tab of a build configuration appears after Overview as soon as there is at least one link. Without links, there is no tab. Each link is a card with its label, its description and the site it points to. The project's links come first, then the build configuration's own links. Links of an outer project come before those of an inner one. Each group is headed with where it comes from, for example This job. Don't want a project's links on one build configuration? Untick Show the project’s links on this build configuration in its Documentation card. Its own links still show. Put the links everyone needs, such as the studio's build wiki, on the top project once. Put links for one platform, such as its SDK page, on that platform's build configuration."},
{"title":"Terminate a build if it is stuck","href":"projects/feature-build-timeout","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Stops a build that runs too long or goes quiet. In Buildspawn this lives under Failure conditions. In the list of build features this is called Terminate a build if it's stuck. When to use it. Use it for builds that can hang, such as a cook that waits for a window to close. You do not add it as a build feature. The time limits are in Failure conditions under Build time. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Failure Conditions. Under Build time choose how the limit is set: Fixed limit , Compared with earlier builds or No new log output. Set the minutes, and choose the Result (Failed or Unstable). Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Fixed limit. Fail if the build runs longer than the number of minutes you give. Off. 90. Compared with earlier builds. Limit = the average of recent successful builds plus a margin in percent. Off. Margin 150 %. No new log output. Fail when the log stays silent for the number of minutes you give. Off. 20. Result. What the build becomes when the limit is reached. Failed. Failed. A worked example. The PS5 Build normally takes 40 minutes. The studio picks Compared with earlier builds with a margin of 150%. A build that runs longer than about an hour is stopped and marked failed. What you see when it runs. The build is stopped. A line in the log says which limit was hit. Time in the queue is not counted. The limit is checked every 10 seconds, so a build can stop up to 10 seconds late. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. A timeout I set earlier shows on the Failure Conditions tab. It is shown there on purpose. Changing it there moves it into the failure conditions. This limit uses a setting this card cannot show. It is kept as it is. It still works. Related pages. Failure conditions. The Build Features tab."},
{"title":"Commit status publisher","href":"projects/feature-commit-status","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","Git hosts: GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket Cloud, Gitea","Helix Swarm","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Tells your code host or review tool whether a build passed, so people see the result next to the commit or review. In the list of build features this is called Commit status publisher for Git hosts. When to use it. Use it when people review changes before they merge, and you want a green or red mark on the change itself. It supports Helix Swarm (for Perforce) and GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket Cloud and Gitea (for Git). The Commit status publisher form. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Build Features. Click Add build feature and choose Commit status publisher. At the top, choose the publisher: Helix Swarm , GitHub , GitLab , Bitbucket Cloud or Gitea. Fill in the fields for that publisher (see the tables). Click Save. Every field. Git hosts: GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket Cloud, Gitea. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Git host. Which host receives the status. The one you picked. GitHub. Server URL. The address of the REST API. Fill it only for GitHub Enterprise, or a self-hosted GitLab or Gitea. Empty uses the default of the host: https://api.github.com , https://gitlab.com/api/v4 , https://api.bitbucket.org/2.0 or https://gitea.com/api/v1. Host default. https://git.studio.example/api/v1. Access token. A Secret text credential that holds the access token. It is read when the build runs and is sent in a request header. It is never shown or written to the log. None (required). git-status-token. Repository filter. Optional. A status is sent only for checkouts whose repository URL contains this text. Empty sends it for every version control root. Empty. skyforge-game. Test connection. A button that checks the host, the address and the token. -. -. Helix Swarm. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Swarm server. The Swarm server to report to. None (required). swarm.studio.example. Code review comments. Also post the result as a comment on the review, besides its Tests section. Off. On. Create Swarm test. A button that makes the test definition in Swarm. It needs a Swarm administrator. It adds the test to the Global Workflow, On Update, blocking nothing. Without an administrator, make the test in Swarm by hand. -. -. The same Swarm setting is also available as Publish build status to Helix Swarm in the job settings. A worked example. The Skyforge launcher is in Git on a self-hosted Gitea. The studio picks Gitea , sets Server URL to https://git.studio.example/api/v1 , and chooses the credential gitea-status-token. Every pull request now shows a Buildspawn status: build passed. What you see when it runs. When the build starts, the status is pending. When it ends it becomes success , failure or error , with a link back to the build. The summary on the Build Features tab shows Build status on Gitea. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Choose an access token. The token field is empty. Add a Secret text credential and pick it. Choose a Swarm server. Pick the server from the list. An administrator adds Swarm servers in the global settings. Test connection fails. Check the address of the API (it must end in the API path), and that the token may write commit statuses. No status appears. The status is sent for each checkout of a version control root. Check the Repository filter. Related pages. The Build Features tab. Git. Shelves and personal builds. Job settings."},
{"title":"Bind credentials to variables","href":"projects/feature-credential-bindings","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Makes stored secrets available to your build steps as variables. The values are hidden in the log. When to use it. Use it when a step needs a password, a token or a key file, for example the Steam account password or a signing certificate. You keep the secret in Credentials once. The build configuration only says \"put this credential in that variable\". Nobody types a secret into a script. The Bind credentials to variables form. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Build Features. Click Add build feature and choose Bind credentials to variables. Under Bindings click Add and pick the kind of binding (see the table below). Type the Variable name your step will read, for example STEAM_PASSWORD. Choose the credential from the list. Add more bindings if you need them. Click Save. Every field. Every binding has a Variable name and a Credentials choice. The kinds you can add are: Field. What it does. Default. Example. Secret text. One secret in one variable. -. Variable API_TOKEN. Secret file. The secret is a file. The variable holds the path of a temporary copy, which is deleted after the build. -. Variable SIGN_KEY_FILE. Username and password (two variables). The user name goes in one variable and the password in another. -. P4_USER and P4_PASS. Username and password (one variable). The variable holds user:password. -. FTP_LOGIN. SSH user private key. A key file path variable, plus optional user name and passphrase variables. -. SSH_KEY. Certificate. A keystore file path, and optional password and alias variables. -. CERT_FILE. Zip file. A zip is unpacked into a temporary folder. The variable holds the folder. -. SDK_DIR. A worked example. A PowerShell step uploads the build to a store. The credential store-upload-token is a Secret text. The studio adds a binding with Variable STORE_TOKEN. The script reads $env:STORE_TOKEN. See also Use a secret. What you see when it runs. The step can read the variable. If the value ever appears in the log, it is replaced with ****. Secret files are removed when the build ends. Upload started with token ****. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The variable is empty in my step. Check the name. Variables are case sensitive on Linux and macOS. On Windows read it as %STORE_TOKEN% or $env:STORE_TOKEN. The credential is not in the list. It must be visible to this project. Credentials of a project can only be used inside that project. The value shows in the log. Only the exact value is hidden. If a script changes it, for example encodes it, the changed text is not hidden. Related pages. The Build Features tab. Credentials. Secrets. Use a secret."},
{"title":"Delete the workspace before the build","href":"projects/feature-delete-workspace","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Empties the workspace folder on the agent before the sources are fetched, so every build starts clean. When to use it. Use it when old files cause strange results: stale cooked content, a half-finished Unity Library folder, or files that were deleted in version control but are still on disk. The price is time: every build fetches and rebuilds everything. If only some folders are slow to rebuild, keep them with the Keep field. The Delete the workspace before the build form. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Build Features. Click Add build feature and choose Delete the workspace before the build. In Keep , type one pattern per line for the files you want to keep, for example Library/**. Leave it empty to delete everything. Tick Also delete directories that match only if you want the matching folders themselves removed too. Click Save. Every field. An Ant pattern is a path with wildcards: * matches any text in one folder name, ** matches any number of folders. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Keep. Files that stay. One Ant pattern per line, relative to the workspace. Empty keeps nothing. Empty. Library/**. Also delete directories that match. Remove the directories that the patterns match, not only the files. Off. On. A worked example. The Unity Windows Build keeps its Library folder, because importing the project takes 25 minutes. It still needs a clean output folder. The studio sets Keep to Library/**. Everything else in the workspace, including old Builds/ output, is deleted before each build. What you see when it runs. Deleting the workspace before the build, keeping Library/** Cleaning E:\\ws\\Game_UnityWindows. Checking out from Perforce. When Keep is empty the line says Deleting the workspace before the build. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The build is much slower. The workspace is fetched in full each time. Use Keep for folders that are expensive to make again. A file I wanted to keep is gone. Check the pattern. It is relative to the workspace root and case matters on Linux and macOS. Two builds fail on the same agent. Builds that share a workspace folder cannot run at the same time. Run them one at a time, or give each its own workspace. Related pages. The Build Features tab. Workspace options."},
{"title":"Documentation","href":"projects/feature-documentation","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Adds links to the Documentation tab of the build configuration. When to use it. Use it for the runbook, the release checklist or the SDK page. The full description is on Documentation links. The Documentation form. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Build Features. Click Add build feature and choose Documentation. Give the section a Title and a one-line summary. Add a link: label, address and short description. Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Title. The heading of the section. Empty. Windows build runbook. Summary. One line under the title. Empty. Who owns it and what to do when it fails. Link: label, address, description. The page to link. Only http and https addresses are accepted. Empty. https://wiki.studio.example/skyforge-build. Show the project’s links on this build configuration. Show the links of the projects above, too. On. On. A worked example. The Windows Build shows a link to the studio wiki page How the Windows build works. People find it on the Documentation tab when a night build fails. What you see when it runs. The Documentation tab appears after Overview as soon as there is at least one link. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. No Documentation tab. The tab only shows when a link exists, and the feature must be on. Related pages. The Build Features tab. Documentation links."},
{"title":"Notifications","href":"projects/feature-notifications","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Sends an email when a build fails, and when it is fixed. When to use it. Use it when someone must hear about a broken build without opening Buildspawn. It sends an email for every failed build, for the first successful build after a failure, and for an unstable build after a successful one. An administrator sets the mail server once in the global settings. The Notifications form. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Build Features. Click Add build feature and choose Notifications. In Recipients type the email addresses, separated by spaces. Tick Send separate e-mails to individuals who broke the build if the people who changed the code should hear too. Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Recipients. Whitespace-separated email addresses. You can use build variables. Empty. build-team@studio.example. Send e-mail for every unstable build. On: every unstable build sends an email. Off: only the first one after a successful build. On. On. Send separate e-mails to individuals who broke the build. Email the people whose changes were in the broken build. With no recipients, only they get the mail. Off. On. A worked example. The nightly Release Build notifies release-team@studio.example and the people who submitted since the last good build. On a Monday the build fails; the team gets one email. After the fix the team gets one email that the build is good again. What you see when it runs. Emails come from the mail server in the global settings. The Build Features card says, for example, Email to release-team@studio.example when a build fails and when it is fixed. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Give at least one recipient, or send to who broke the build. Both are empty. Fill one. No email arrives. Ask an administrator to check the mail server settings and to send a test email. Check the spam folder. Individuals do not get email. Their user must have an email address in their profile, or the server must have a default suffix set. Related pages. The Build Features tab. E-mail Notification (post-build action)."},
{"title":"Parallel tests","href":"projects/feature-parallel-tests","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","How a parallel build runs","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Splits the tests of one build into batches that run at the same time, each on its own agent, and merges the results. When to use it. Use it when your test step is slow and you have free agents. The test run of Unit Tests takes 40 minutes on one agent. With 4 batches it takes about 10, plus the checkout time. It works best when you have hundreds of test classes of about the same size. The Parallel tests form. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Build Features. Click Add build feature and choose Parallel tests. Type the Number of batches , from 2 to 20. Click Save. Make sure your test step can take its share (see the table of runners). Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Number of batches. How many batch builds one build starts. Each batch runs all the build steps with its share of the test classes. Shares follow the durations of the last successful build. New tests are spread evenly. Between 2 and 20. 4. 4. Runners. Says which steps take their share by themselves: Maven, Gradle and NUnit. Other steps read two environment variables: BUILDSPAWN_PARALLEL_INCLUDE_FILE (the classes of this batch) and BUILDSPAWN_PARALLEL_EXCLUDE_FILE (all the others). -. -. How a parallel build runs. You start the build. It becomes the parent. It does not fetch sources and runs no steps. It holds no agent while it waits. The parent starts one batch build per batch. Each batch fetches the sources on its own agent, works out its share of the test classes and runs your steps. The parent waits, adds up the test counts and the durations, and ends with the worst result of the batches. Notifications, such as email, run once on the parent with the merged result. A worked example. Unit Tests has a Maven step. The studio sets Number of batches to 4. Maven gets -Dtest= with the classes of its batch. For a step that runs a game test runner, the script reads the include file: $classes = Get-Content $env:BUILDSPAWN_PARALLEL_INCLUDE_FILE.\\RunTests.exe --classes ($classes -join \",\"). What you see when it runs. The parent build page has a Parallel tests block with one row per batch: its number, a link to the batch build, its agent, the duration and the result, and the merged test summary. A batch shows Batch 2 of 4 with a link to the parent. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The number is refused. It must be a whole number from 2 to 20. One batch takes much longer. Shares follow the last successful build. The first run is spread by name. It evens out after one good run. A step runs every test in every batch. That step does not read the share. Use the include file, or give it the filter arguments of your test tool. Batches wait in the queue. You need as many free agents as batches. Lower the number, or add agents. Related pages. The Build Features tab. Parallel blocks. Matrix mode."},
{"title":"Project Properties","href":"projects/feature-project-properties","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Gives the build configuration the properties set on the projects above it. When to use it. Use it when a project holds a list of properties (name and value pairs, set in the project settings under List of project properties ) and you want builds in it to see them as environment variables. Examples are the studio name, a server address or a share path. The Project Properties form. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Build Features. Click Add build feature and choose Project Properties. There is nothing to fill in. Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. (no fields). The feature inherits every property defined in the project settings of the parent project, or of any project above it. Off. -. A worked example. The Game project lists the property DROP_SHARE with the value \\\\files\\skyforge\\drops. A build configuration with this feature can read %DROP_SHARE% in a step. What you see when it runs. The properties are in the environment of every step. A build configuration in a project with no properties gets nothing extra. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The variable is not there. Check the List of project properties on the project, and that this feature is on. Related pages. The Build Features tab. Project settings: List of project properties."},
{"title":"Send files or run commands over SSH after the build","href":"projects/feature-ssh-after","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Copies files to a server, or runs commands on it, when the build steps have finished. When to use it. Use it to stop or restart something at the end of a build, or to move files that the build left in the workspace. The form is the same as before the build. It runs after the build steps but before the post-build actions. The SSH after form. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Build Features. Click Add build feature and choose Send files or execute commands over SSH after the build runs. Choose the server and add transfer sets, as on the before-the-build page. Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Name. The SSH server, chosen from the servers set up in the global settings. None. build-share-01. Source files. Files to send, as a comma separated Ant pattern. The base folder is the workspace. Empty. Tools/**. Remove prefix. The first part of the path that is not created on the server. Empty. Tools. Remote directory. A folder below the one set in the global settings. It is created if it does not exist. Empty. skyforge/tools. Exec command. A command to run on the server after the files are sent. Empty. sudo systemctl stop skyforge-cache. A worked example. After the Linux Server Build , a transfer set starts the cache service again with sudo systemctl start skyforge-cache. What you see when it runs. The log shows the SSH connection and the commands after your last build step. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. It runs even when the build failed. Wrappers run around the build. Use the post-build action if you only want to send files of a good build. The command needs a terminal. Tick Exec in pty in Advanced. Related pages. The Build Features tab. SSH before the build. Send build artifacts over SSH."},
{"title":"Send files or run commands over SSH before the build","href":"projects/feature-ssh-before","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Copies files to a server, or runs commands on it, after the sources are fetched and before the build steps start. When to use it. Use it to prepare a remote machine, for example to stop a service or to upload tool files before a build starts. The after-the-build version is the same form and runs when the build steps end. To send build output, use the post-build action Send build artifacts over SSH. The SSH before form. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Build Features. Click Add build feature and choose Send files or execute commands over SSH before the build starts. Under Publishers click Add , and choose the server in Name. An administrator sets up SSH servers in the global settings. Add one or more transfer sets: Source files , Remove prefix , Remote directory and Exec command. Open Advanced if you need retries, other credentials or a fail rule. Click Save. Every field. The full list of transfer options is on Send build artifacts over SSH. These are the common ones. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Name. The SSH server, chosen from the servers set up in the global settings. None. build-share-01. Source files. Files to send, as a comma separated Ant pattern. The base folder is the workspace. Empty. Tools/**. Remove prefix. The first part of the path that is not created on the server. Empty. Tools. Remote directory. A folder below the one set in the global settings. It is created if it does not exist. Empty. skyforge/tools. Exec command. A command to run on the server after the files are sent. Empty. sudo systemctl stop skyforge-cache. Fail the build if an error occurs. Advanced. Off: a failed transfer only makes the build unstable and the build goes on. On: the build fails and the post-build steps do not run. Off. On. Exec timeout (ms). Advanced. How long the command may run. 0 turns the timeout off. 120000. 60000. A worked example. Before the Linux Server Build , the studio stops the cache service on the server: a transfer set with no files and Exec command sudo systemctl stop skyforge-cache. What you see when it runs. The log shows the connection and each transfer and command before your first build step. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The build is unstable after the transfer. The publish failed. Read the log. Tick Fail the build if an error occurs if you want the build to stop. No server in the list. An administrator must add an SSH server in the global settings first. Related pages. The Build Features tab. SSH after the build. Send build artifacts over SSH."},
{"title":"Timestamps in the build log","href":"projects/feature-timestamps","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Puts the time, as HH:mm:ss, in front of every line of the console output. In the list of build features this is called Add timestamps to the Console Output. When to use it. Turn it on when you need to see where a build spends its time. A game build can run for an hour. With times in the log you can see that the cook took 22 minutes and the shader compile took 9. It has no settings and costs almost nothing. The Timestamps form. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Build Features. Click Add build feature and choose Timestamps in the build log. There is nothing to fill in. Click Save. Make sure the On switch of the feature is on. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. (no fields). The feature only has an on and off switch. Off. -. A worked example. The Windows Build configuration of the Skyforge game takes 48 minutes and nobody knows why. The team adds Timestamps in the build log. The next build shows that the step Cook content starts at 00:07:12 and ends at 00:31:40. What you see when it runs. Every line of the console log starts with the time: 12:04:31 Started by user dana.k 12:04:33 Running as agent WIN-BUILD-02 12:04:40 Cook content: start 12:29:02 Cook content: done. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. No times show in the log. Check that the On switch of the feature is on. The times only show for builds that start after you save. The feature is not in the list. The build configuration type cannot hold build features. A Pipeline does this in its script. Related pages. The Build Features tab."},
{"title":"Unity build settings","href":"projects/feature-unity","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Activates a Unity license around your Unity steps and sets the asset cache server. When to use it. Use it when your agents have no signed-in, licensed Unity Editor. The full description of every field, with a worked example, is on Unity build settings in the Game builds section. The Unity build settings form. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Build Features. Click Add build feature and choose Unity build settings. Choose the License : None, Plus or Pro, or Personal. Choose the credentials, and the Unity version or folder. Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. License. None, Unity Plus or Pro (serial number and account), or Unity Personal (a license file). None. Unity Plus or Pro. License scope. Activate for each build step, or once for the whole build configuration. Build step. Build configuration. Serial number. Secret text credential with the serial number. None. unity-serial. Unity account. Username with password credential. None. unity-build-account. License file. Secret file or Secret text credential with the.ulf file. None. unity-personal-ulf. Detection mode. Auto picks an Editor the agent found. Manual uses the folder. Auto. Auto. Unity version. Auto mode. Empty takes the latest Editor. Empty. 6000.0. Unity folder. Manual mode: the folder with the Editor. Empty. C:\\Program Files\\Unity\\Hub\\Editor\\6000.0.23f1. Cache server address. A Unity Accelerator or cache server as host:port. Empty. unity-cache.studio.example:10080. A worked example. One Unity Pro seat serves two Windows agents. License scope is set to the whole build configuration, so both Unity steps share one activation, and the seat is returned at the end. See the full example on the Unity build settings page. What you see when it runs. The log has a block Activate Unity license before your Unity step, and Return Unity license after it. Secrets show as ********. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The Unity Editor could not be started on this agent. Install Unity there, or choose Manual detection and name the folder. Manual detection needs the folder. Fill Unity folder. Related pages. The Build Features tab. Unity build settings (full page). Unity step."},
{"title":"VCS labeling","href":"projects/feature-vcs-labeling","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Puts a label on the Perforce revision that a build used, so you can find the exact files again. When to use it. Use it when you need to rebuild or compare a release later. The label is named from the build, for example build-1.4.2. Anyone can then sync that label in P4V. The VCS labeling form. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Build Features. Click Add build feature and choose VCS labeling. If the build configuration has several Perforce roots, choose the Perforce root. Set the Label pattern. Choose Label builds : only successful builds, or every build. Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Perforce root. Which root to label when the build configuration has several. A build that did not check out this root gets a warning. The only root. Main. Label pattern. The name of the label. You can use build variables. build-${BUILD_NUMBER}. skyforge-${BUILD_NUMBER}. Label builds. Which builds get a label: successful builds only, or every build. Successful builds only. Successful. Branch filter. Which branches are labelled. One rule per line: +:name or -:name. * matches any text, <default> is the stream of the root. The last matching rule decides. Empty labels every branch. Empty. +:main. Label description. Text for the description of the label. You can use build variables. Empty. Skyforge build ${BUILD_NUMBER}. Move a label Buildspawn created. Off: a label that already exists is refused. On: move a label that Buildspawn made. A label that Buildspawn did not make is never changed. Off. Off. Email on failure. Who to tell when labelling fails, separated by spaces or commas. Uses the mail server of the global settings. Empty. p4-admin@studio.example. A worked example. Windows Build uses the label pattern skyforge-${BUILD_NUMBER} with Label builds set to successful builds. Build 142 creates the label skyforge-142 on the changelist it synced. What you see when it runs. The build log shows the label being created. The Perforce Labels tab of the build configuration lists it. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The label already exists. Use a pattern with the build number, or turn on Move a label Buildspawn created if the label was made by Buildspawn. Warning: root not checked out. The build did not use the root you chose. Choose the root the build syncs. No label on a failed build. That is the default. Set Label builds to every build. Related pages. The Build Features tab. Label in Perforce (post-build action). Perforce."},
{"title":"XML report processing","href":"projects/feature-xml-report","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Reads test report files in JUnit format and shows the results on the build page. When to use it. Use it when your test tool writes XML reports and does not already report to Buildspawn. Many tools can write this format: Unreal Automation, NUnit and C++ test libraries. You then get pass and fail counts, a trend, and the list of failed tests. The XML report processing form. How to set it up. Open the build configuration, click Settings , then Build Features. Click Add build feature and choose XML report processing. In Test report XMLs type the files to read, relative to the workspace, for example Saved/Automation/*.xml. Use Validate Pattern to check it. Choose the other options you need (see the table). Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Test report XMLs. The report files, as an Ant pattern. Several patterns are separated by commas. Empty (a feature with no files shows a problem). Saved/Automation/*.xml. Test output retention. How much standard output and error of the tests is kept. Long output of passing tests is cut by default to save disk space. Standard. All. keep all the properties. Keep the properties of each test case. Off. Off. Archive files attached to tests in Open Test Reporting reports. Archive files that tests attach, so they open from the page of each test. Off. Off. Keep original test names, even when reporting from multiple stages. Do not add the stage name to the test names. Off. Off. Health report amplification factor. How much failed tests lower the health of the build configuration. 0 turns it off; 1 means 10% failing gives 90% health. 1.0. 1.0. Additional test report features. Extra publishers for the test data. None. -. Do not fail the build on empty test results. Normally a build fails when no report file is found. Turn this on to allow it. Off. Off. Skip publishing checks. Do not send the results to the checks of the code host. Off. On. Checks name. The name used when results are sent to the checks of a code host. Tests. Unit tests. Skip marking build as unstable on test failure. Report the failures but keep the build successful. Off. Off. Skip report files older than build start. Ignore report files that existed before the build started. Off. On. A worked example. The Unit Tests build configuration of the Engine project runs RunTests.exe --report-xml Reports/tests.xml. The studio sets Test report XMLs to Reports/*.xml and turns on Skip report files older than build start , so an old report on a reused workspace never counts. What you see when it runs. The build gets a Tests tab with the counts and the failed tests. A build with a failed test is Unstable (yellow), unless you skip that. Failure conditions can turn it into Failed. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. No test report files found. The pattern did not match. It is relative to the workspace. Check the folder and use Validate Pattern. Results are counted from an old build. Turn on Skip report files older than build start. The build is yellow, I want red. On Failure Conditions set Test failures to Failed. Related pages. The Build Features tab. Publish JUnit test result report. Failure conditions. Tests on the build page."},
{"title":"Project settings","href":"projects/folder-settings","headings":["The project settings page","General","Project name","Credentials","Perforce roots","List of project properties","Pipeline libraries (FolderLibraries)","Project-based security (AuthorizationMatrixProperty, and the Entra ID matrix)","Child Health metrics","Child Orphaned Item Strategy","Child Scan Triggers","Suppress automatic SCM triggering","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Every setting of a project, what it holds and where to find it. The project settings page. Open Settings on a project. It has sub-tabs. General has the name, description, documentation, icon, views, order of items and health metrics. The properties below are on the full form. ID , Versioned Settings and Connections follow. Build settings and Templates pass steps, parameters and templates down. Perforce roots and Credentials are shared with the build configurations below. The project settings. General. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Name. Read-only here. Change it with Rename in the page header, which also moves the address. The name you gave. Game. Configuration ID. Stable and unique. See IDs. Parent ID plus name. Game. Display name. Shown in lists, the tree and the page title instead of the name. Optional. Empty. Skyforge. Description. Text under the name. Used as the summary of build configurations in this project that have none of their own. Empty. Skyforge, the game. Documentation. Links for every build configuration in this project, up to 50. See Documentation links. No links. -. Icon and Icon colour. The same picker and colour as build configurations. See Project icons. Project. Tools. Views. Appearance: the views of the project. Edit views opens them. All. -. Order of items. Alphabetical or Custom. Reorder. opens the dialog. See Tree order and separators. Alphabetical. -. Health metrics. What decides the weather icon of the project: Child item with the given name , Child item with worst health. Add metric adds one; Remove removes one. Worst child health. -. Open the full form. Administrators only. Every setting of the project on one long page, including Properties such as Pipeline libraries. -. -. Project name. The name of a project made by a scan of a code host (Multibranch or Organization Folder). It keeps the name of the repository. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Project name. The name shown for the project. The repository name. -. Credentials. Secrets that only build configurations in this project may use. On the Credentials tab of the project. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Credentials. Add a password, token or key. See Credentials. None. steam-build-account. Perforce roots. Perforce connections that the build configurations below can share. On the Perforce roots tab. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Perforce roots. Server, credential, workspace and view. See Perforce roots. None. Main. List of project properties. Name and value pairs for every build configuration below. They are read when the build feature Project Properties is on. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Name. The property name. Empty. DROP_SHARE. Value. The property value. Empty. \\\\files\\skyforge\\drops. Pipeline libraries (FolderLibraries). Shared libraries for Pipelines in this project. They are untrusted: their code runs in the Groovy sandbox. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Name. The name used in @Library. Empty. skyforge-lib. Default version. The branch, tag or commit used when a script names none. Empty. main. Load implicitly. Every Pipeline gets the library without @Library. Off. Off. Allow default version to be overridden. A script may name another version. Off. On. Include @Library changes in job recent changes. Show changes of the library in recent changes. Off. Off. Cache fetched versions on controller for quick retrieval. Keep fetched versions on the server. Off. Off. Refresh time in minutes. How long a cached version is kept. 0. 30. Versions to exclude / include. Patterns that limit the versions a script may use. Empty. release/*. Retrieval method. Modern SCM or Legacy SCM. Modern SCM. Modern SCM. Project-based security (AuthorizationMatrixProperty, and the Entra ID matrix). Who may do what in this project and below. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Users and groups. One card per user or group with every permission grouped inside. See Users, groups and permissions. None. art-team : Read, Build"},
{"title":"Item types","href":"projects/item-types","headings":["Create an item","The types","Types that are hidden","Related pages"],"text":"An item is anything in the project tree: a project, a build configuration, a Pipeline or a template. You pick the type when you create it on the New Item page. Create an item. The New Item page of the Game project. Open the project the new item goes in. Click New Item in the page header, or + above the project tree. Type the Item name. The page checks it as you type. These characters are not allowed: ? * / \\ % ! @ # $ ^ & | < > [ ] : ; The name must be unique in the project. Check the ID. It is made from the ID of the project and the name, and you may edit it. See IDs. Check Created in. Click Change to pick another project. Choose Start from : Empty , Copy of an existing item or Template. Under Type click a card. Click Create. The button stays off until everything is valid and says why. The summary card on the right says what will be created. The types. Type. What it is. Page. Build configuration. Steps run one after another, with the step editor, templates and Perforce roots. Use it for most builds. Build configuration. Pipeline. A script file that defines stages, parallel work and approvals. Pipeline. Build configuration template. Steps and parameters that several build configurations share. Build configuration template. Project. Groups projects. Holds shared settings, credentials, Perforce roots and permissions. Project. Multibranch Pipeline. Creates a set of Pipeline build configurations, one per branch of a repository. Under More types. Multibranch Pipeline. The cards are grouped as Builds (Build configuration, Pipeline, Template), Organise (Project) and More types. Types that are hidden. Two types are not offered on the New Item page. Items of these types that already exist keep working. You can open, build, configure and copy them. Only the choice of the type for a new item is gone. Type. Why it is hidden. Page. Multi-configuration project. The Matrix mode of Parallel blocks does the same job inside a normal build configuration. Multi-configuration project. Organization Folder. It is not created by hand on this page. It scans an account of a code host and makes a Multibranch Pipeline for each repository it finds. Organization Folder. A Start from choice of Copy of an existing item shows only when the server has items. Template shows only when a template is within reach of the project. Related pages. Create a build configuration. Projects. Templates. Copying settings."},
{"title":"Job settings","href":"projects/job-settings","headings":["What a job setting is","Platform icon","Build priority","Dependencies","Discard old builds","Do not allow concurrent builds","Do not allow the pipeline to resume if the controller restarts","Documentation","Parallel tests","Perforce data refresh","Pipeline speed/durability override","Preserve stashes from completed builds","Publish build status to Helix Swarm","This project is parameterised","Throttle builds","Build triggers (Pipeline)","Perforce root: build when the Perforce server triggers it","Project-based security (AuthorizationMatrixProperty, and the Entra ID matrix)","Override multibranch or organization branch indexing triggers","Settings you do not edit","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Every setting that is stored with a build configuration, what it holds and where to find it. What a job setting is. A job setting is a property stored with one build configuration. Most have a place on the tabs of the build configuration. This page lists every one, what it holds and where to find it. The server calls them job properties; in the user interface a job is a build configuration. Platform icon. Choose the platform icon of a build configuration. On General. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Icon. The platform. See Project icons. Generic. Windows. Icon colour. The colour of the icon. Theme colour. -. Build priority. The order of builds that wait for an agent. On General under Build options. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Build priority. A whole number from -100 to 100. Higher goes first. Same priority: first come, first served. 0. 10. Dependencies. Snapshot and artifact dependencies. On the Dependencies tab. The full form shows a summary with a link to it. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Snapshot and artifact dependencies. See Dependencies. None. -. Discard old builds. Delete old builds and artifacts. On General under Clean-up rules. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Strategy and keep rules. See Retention and discard old builds. Off. -. Do not allow concurrent builds. A Pipeline runs one build at a time. On General , Run one build at a time and Abort the running build when a newer one starts. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Abort previous builds. A new build immediately aborts the running one, instead of waiting in the queue. Useful for expensive tests of an outdated commit. Off. On. Do not allow the pipeline to resume if the controller restarts. A running Pipeline ends when the server restarts. On Workspace options , Resume after a controller restart. Field. What it does. Default. Example. (no fields). Turning it on has no extra fields. Off. -. Documentation. Links on the Documentation tab. As the build feature Documentation. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Title, Summary, links. See Documentation links. Empty. -. Parallel tests. Split tests into batches. As the build feature Parallel tests. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Number of batches. 2 to 20. 4. 4. Perforce data refresh. How often the Perforce lists of the build configuration are read again. On Other settings. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Refresh Perforce lists on a custom interval. Turn on to set your own interval. Off. On. Interval. Seconds. How often the lists (submitted changes, shelves, pending changes, labels, branch streams) are read in the background while someone has viewed the build configuration in the last 30 minutes, or while it has a polling, commit or shelve trigger. The interval is shorter for ten minutes after a change and grows while nothing changes, up to 15 minutes. 120. 300. Pipeline speed/durability override. How often a running Pipeline saves its state. On Workspace options , Speed and durability. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Custom Pipeline Speed/Durability Level. Server default, Performance-optimized, Less durable (a bit faster) or Maximum survivability. Server default. Maximum survivability. Preserve stashes from completed builds. Keep stashes so a later stage can restart from a finished build. On Workspace options. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Number of completed builds. How many builds keep their stashes. 1. 3. Publish build status to Helix Swarm. Reports the build to a Swarm review. As the Helix Swarm choice of Commit status publisher. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Swarm server. The server to report to. None. swarm.studio.example. Code review comments. Also post a comment on the review. Off. On. This project is parameterised. The parameters of the build configuration. On the Parameters tab. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Parameters. See Parameters. None. -. Throttle builds. Limit how often builds start. On General under Build options. Fiel"},
{"title":"Copying settings","href":"projects/project-copy-settings","headings":["Copy an existing item","Start from a template","Share settings with a project","A worked example","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Make a new build configuration from an existing one, from a template, or copy settings from a project, and choose what happens to the settings it inherits. Copy an existing item. Open the project where the copy should go and click New Item. Type the new name. Check the ID. Under Start from choose Copy of an existing item. In Copy from type part of a name and pick the item. Every item you can read is listed. Under Settings of the copy choose one of the two cards (below). Click Create. Choice. What it does. Keep inheriting. The copy uses the settings of the project it is created in. Pick this when the copy should follow its new project. Make inherited settings its own. The copy keeps running the same steps and parameters, whatever the project holds. Pick this when you copy into another project and want the build to stay the same. Overrides are kept either way. The copy gets a new ID and starts with no build history. Start from a template. Under Start from choose Template. The list shows the templates you can use in this project, with how many steps and parameters each has. Pick one. Leave Keep linked to the template on if the new item should follow later changes to the template. Clear it to copy the steps once. Choose the type and click Create. Share settings with a project. To give many build configurations the same steps or parameters, put them on the project instead of copying. See Share settings with a whole project. A worked example. The studio copies Windows Build into Game as Switch Build with Keep inheriting. The copy picks up the steps of the project Game. A copy of Windows Build into the project Tools with Make inherited settings its own keeps the Game steps as its own. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Copy of an existing item is missing. The server has no items that you can read. Template is missing. No template is within reach of this project. Templates of a project can be used in it and in the projects below. The copy builds differently. You chose Keep inheriting and the new project passes other steps. Choose Make inherited settings its own. Related pages. Projects. Create a build configuration. Templates."},
{"title":"Project icons","href":"projects/project-icons","headings":["Why use them","Set an icon","The icons","Icon colour","What is inherited","A worked example","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Show the platform of a project or build configuration with an icon in the tree, in tables and in the Run dialog. Why use them. A tree with PS5 Build , Xbox Series Build and Windows Build is easier to scan when each has its platform icon. The icon shows in the project tree, in tables, in the page header and in the Run dialog. The build result is shown separately, as a dot. Set an icon. Open the project or build configuration and click Settings. For a build configuration open General. Under Icon click the platform. The Sidebar preview shows how it looks. Choose an Icon colour if you like (see below). Click Save. The platform icons on the General tab of a build configuration. The icons. Icon. Use for. Project. The default for a project. PlayStation. PS4, PS5 and dev kit builds. Xbox. Xbox builds. Windows. Windows desktop builds. Steam. Steam uploads and Steam Deck. Nintendo Switch. Switch builds. Apple. macOS builds. iOS. iOS builds. Android. Android builds. Linux. Linux and dedicated server builds. Software. Generic software. Tools. Tools and pipelines. Tests. Test builds. Custom. Any icon from the icon library. Custom. or Browse the icon library. opens the library. Search it for any other icon. Icon colour. Choice. What it does. Default. The theme's normal icon colour. Favourites. Colours your team keeps for reuse. Manage edits the list. They are shared with everyone. Colours. A set chosen to read on both the light and the dark theme. Custom. A colour as RRGGBB , one for the light theme and one for the dark theme. Clear removes it. What is inherited. A project without a choice keeps the project icon. A build configuration without a choice shows the generic icon. The icon is saved with the item and kept when you rename, move or copy it. A worked example. In Game , PS5 Build gets PlayStation, Xbox Series Build gets Xbox and Windows Build gets Windows. The project Tools gets the Tools icon. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The icon does not show. Save the page. The tree updates on the next page load. The icon is hard to read in the dark theme. Pick a colour from Colours , which reads on both themes, or set a separate dark colour. Related pages. Projects. General. Project settings."},
{"title":"IDs and the parent-ID prefix","href":"projects/project-ids","headings":["What an ID is","How it is made","Change an ID","Where it shows","A worked example","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Every project, build configuration and template has an ID that stays the same when you rename or move it. What an ID is. The ID is a name that is unique on the server. Dependencies, parameters such as dep.<id>.<name> , links and settings refer to it. A rename or a move never breaks them. How it is made. On the New Item page the ID is made from the ID of the project the item goes in, an underscore and the name, until you edit it. A build configuration Windows Build in the project with the ID Game gets Game_WindowsBuild. This is the parent-ID prefix. Rule. Detail. Characters. Letters, digits and underscores only. Start. A letter. Length. Up to 225 characters. Unique. Another item may not use it. The page checks as you type and says is free. Edited by hand. If you change it, it no longer follows the name. Change an ID. The ID tab, with its links. Open the build configuration, then Settings › More › ID. For a project open Settings › ID. Type the new ID, or click Regenerate from name. Click Save. The old ID keeps working as an alias. Aliases are listed on the page. Remove one to let another item take that ID. Links that used the alias then stop working. Where it shows. In the header of the page, as a chip next to the type. In the address /buildConfiguration/<id> , which goes to the item whatever its place. In dependency settings and in dep. and reverse.dep. parameters. A worked example. The studio moves Windows Build from Game to Game/Main. Its ID stays Game_WindowsBuild , so the dependency in Release Build keeps working. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The ID is not valid. Remove spaces and symbols. Start with a letter. The ID is not free. Choose another. Or remove the alias that holds it. I changed the ID and a link broke. Use the new ID. The old one works as an alias unless you removed it. Related pages. Projects. Moving and deleting. General."},
{"title":"Rename, move and delete","href":"projects/project-move-delete","headings":["Rename","Move","Delete","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Change where an item lives, what it is called, or remove it. Each action has its own page with a clear summary of what will happen. Rename. Open the item and click Rename in the page header. Type the New Name. The page says: the name and the address change, and links to the old address stop working. Click Rename. The ID does not change. The item keeps its place in the tree order. Move. The Move page. Open the item and click Move (a tab for a build configuration, or the. menu for a project). Under Move to choose the project. Click Move. If the move can break something, the page lists it and offers Move anyway. Check. What it means. A build of it is running. Refused. Stop it or wait, then move. Nothing is changed. The destination is archived. Refused. Nothing in an archived project can run. A template is in use. Refused. Detach the build configurations first. It uses a template the destination cannot reach. Refused. Detach the template first. It loses inherited settings. Warning: steps and parameters inherited from the old project stop applying, including the parameters named on the page. A dependency changes. Warning: the dependency leads to another build configuration, or none, after the move. A move keeps builds, settings, ID and history. Links to the old address stop working. Use the ID address /buildConfiguration/<id> for links that must survive a move. Delete. Open the item, open the. menu and choose Delete. Read what will be removed. For a project, type its name to confirm. Click Delete. Deleting removes the item, its builds and its settings. For a project it removes everything inside. This cannot be undone. A template that is in use is detached first: each build configuration keeps a copy of the template's settings, and the confirmation says how many. To take something out of use without losing it, archive it. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Move says a build is running. Wait for it, or stop it. I cannot delete a template. Delete detaches the build configurations. If you may not change one of them, nothing happens. Ask someone who may. I need the old name back. Rename it again. The ID did not change. Related pages. Projects. IDs. Templates. Move a build configuration."},
{"title":"Pause and archive","href":"projects/project-pause-archive","headings":["Pause","Archive","What is inherited","What you see","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Stop a whole project from starting builds, or take it out of use without deleting anything. The Pause and archive tab. Pause. Pause stops triggers and polling for everything in the project. A person can still press Run. Use it for a branch freeze, a Perforce maintenance window or a license problem. Open the project and click Pause and archive. Under Pause type the reason in Why it is paused. Click Pause. The page says Paused by Dana on 27 Nov 2026. To end it click Resume. Builds that already run finish. Everything below the project is paused too. A build configuration shows Paused from above , with the name of the project and who paused it. Resume it on the project where the pause was set. Archive. Archive takes a project and everything below it out of use. It cannot be run by triggers, polling, the REST API or the command line. Builds that wait for it are removed from the queue. Nothing is deleted: every build, log and setting stays. Unarchive brings it back. Open the project and click Pause and archive. Under Archive click Archive and confirm. Archiving needs the Configure permission on the project. When project roles are in use, you also need the right to manage its roles. What is inherited. State. What the build configurations inside get. Not paused. They run normally. Paused here or above. No triggers and no polling. Manual runs still work. The state says where it was set. Archived here or above. Cannot be run in any way. The state says Archived because Game is archived. A single build configuration has its own Pause tab with the same pause. Moving something into an archived project is refused. What you see. A banner at the top of the paused or archived project, and a state chip in lists. The state page shows the current state: Not paused , Paused , Paused from above , Archived. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. A build configuration is paused and I cannot resume it. The pause was set higher up. Resume on the project named on the page. Resume does nothing for a build that was paused. It may be paused on its own tab as well. Check both. I cannot archive. You need Configure on the project and, with project roles, the right to manage roles. Related pages. Projects. Pause a whole project. Moving and deleting."},
{"title":"Tree order and separators","href":"projects/project-tree-order","headings":["How the tree is sorted","Who can change it","Reorder with the dialog","Reorder with the tree","Separators","Back to alphabetical","What happens when things change","A worked example","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Put the projects and build configurations of the tree in the order your team wants, and group them with named separators. How the tree is sorted. Every project has two groups: projects first , then build configurations. Nothing crosses the line between the groups. The default order is alphabetical. A project can switch to a custom order. Then each group keeps the order you set. Everyone sees the same order. It also applies to the Projects page and the dashboard list. Who can change it. Only administrators (the Administer permission) can change the order and the separators. For the whole server, use the root project settings. Reorder with the dialog. The Reorder dialog, opened from Order of items. Open the project and click Settings. On General , in the card Order of items , click Reorder. The card says how the items are sorted now. The dialog is titled Reorder items in Game. Drag an item by its handle. Or use Move to top , Move up , Move down and Move to bottom. With the keyboard press Space to pick up, the arrow keys to move and Space to drop. Click Save. The x button, Cancel and Esc leave without saving. The footer says No changes until you move something. Clicking outside the dialog does not close it. Reorder with the tree. In the header of the project tree switch on Reorder mode. It shows only if you may reorder something. Handles appear on the rows you may move. Drag a row by its handle, only among its own siblings. Every drop saves that project and shows an Undo toast. Press Esc to cancel a drag. Separators. A separator is a named line in the tree, for example Main , Drops or Release. It groups the items under it. Add one with Add separator in the dialog, give it a name, and drag it to its place. You can rename and delete separators there. A separator belongs to one group: the projects or the build configurations. Back to alphabetical. Use Sort alphabetically in the dialog (it asks first), or click Alphabetical in the card Order of items. The custom list is dropped. The card has two choices, Alphabetical and Custom. What happens when things change. Change. What happens. A new item in a custom order. It is added at the end of its group. In alphabetical mode nothing changes. Rename. The item keeps its place. Move to another project. It leaves the old order and goes to the end of the group in the new project. Copy. The copy is a new item. Delete. The entry is dropped. Items you cannot see. They are not shown but keep their place. A save only orders the visible items among themselves. A worked example. The Game project lists Windows Build , PS5 Build and Xbox Series Build. The studio wants the dev kits together. It sets a custom order, adds the separator Consoles above PS5 and Xbox, and Desktop above Windows. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. I cannot change the order. Only administrators can. The dialog says so at the top. The order changed back. The project is in alphabetical mode. Save a custom order again. A separator is not shown on the dashboard. The dashboard list applies the order but not the separators. The tree and the Projects page show them. Related pages. Projects. Settings homes. Users, groups and permissions."},
{"title":"Projects","href":"projects/projects","headings":["What a project holds","Project settings","What a project passes down","Change what a build configuration inherits","Create a project","More about projects","Related pages"],"text":"Projects are folders that group build configurations and pass settings down to them. Set something once on a project and every build configuration inside gets it. A studio usually has one project per game or tool, for example Game , Engine and Tools. Inside a project you keep the build configurations (the things that run, such as Windows Build or PS5 Build ) and, if you like, more projects inside it (subprojects). The Game project. The table lists its build configurations with their latest result. What a project holds. Open a project from the project tree on the left, or from the Projects page. The header shows its name, how many build configurations fail, and the New Item button. The tabs are: Tab. What you find there. Overview. The build configurations and subprojects inside, with result, latest build, duration and health. Use Filter to find one by name. The play button starts a build; the … menu holds more actions. Build History. Every build of every build configuration in this project, newest first. Perforce roots. Perforce connections that the build configurations below can share. See Perforce. Build settings. Build steps and parameters that every build configuration below gets. See What a project passes down. Pause and archive. Stop every build configuration inside from starting builds, or archive the whole project. Credentials. Passwords, keys and tokens that only the build configurations in this project can use. Settings. The project's own settings: name, ID, description, documentation links, versioned settings, connections and templates. Build Chains. Builds that start other builds, drawn as chains. Inside the project, the tabs of a project are described on this page. The coloured dot after a project in the tree is the worst result inside it: if one build configuration fails, the project shows red. Project settings. Open Settings on the project. The side menu groups the settings: Project: General (name, display name, description, documentation links), ID , Versioned Settings (keep the project's settings in Git or Perforce, see Versioned settings ) and Connections. Build: Build settings and Templates. The number beside each shows how many items it holds. Shared with the jobs below: Perforce roots and Credentials. The project's General settings. The ID is used by other settings to refer to the project. The Display name is what people see in lists and in the tree. Leave it empty to show the name. To change the name itself, and the address of the project, use Rename in the page header. What a project passes down. A project can hand settings to everything below it. A build configuration then runs with its own settings plus what its projects give it. The chain goes from the Root project (the top of the server, under Settings ), through each project and subproject, to the build configuration. A project passes down: Build steps , which run in every build configuration below, before the configuration's own steps. Parameters : configuration parameters, env. environment variables and system. properties. See Parameters. Post-build actions and build features that wrap the build. Perforce roots , credentials and documentation links. The project's Build settings: steps and parameters for every build configuration below. Change what a build configuration inherits. On a build configuration's Build Steps and Parameters pages, an inherited item has a chip that says where it comes from. For each inherited item you can: Override it: change it for this build configuration only. The project's item stays as it is. Disable in this job : switch it off for this build configuration only. Reset : drop your override or your disable and go back to what the project gives. A subproject can do the same with what its parent project gives. If something was switched off higher up, the page shows Disabled above : change it on the project that switched it off. Changing what a project passes down needs the build settings permission on that project, not just"},
{"title":"Settings homes","href":"projects/settings-homes","headings":["The homes","Saving","Inherited settings","Who can change what","Related pages"],"text":"Each build configuration keeps its settings in a few clear sections, called settings homes. Every setting has one place, so you always know where to look. Open a build configuration and click the Settings tab. A second row of tabs shows the settings homes. The ones that do not fit sit under More. A number beside a tab counts its items, for example Build Steps 3. The settings homes of a build configuration, open on General. The homes. Home. What it holds. Page. General. Name, ID, icon, build number, artifact rules, build options, clean-up rules and checkout options. General. Version Control. The Git and Perforce roots to fetch. Version Control. Perforce connection. Server, workspace and sync settings. Shown when the build configuration uses Perforce. Perforce. Build Steps. The steps in the order they run. Build Steps. Triggers. What starts a build. Triggers. Failure Conditions. When a build counts as failed or unstable. Failure Conditions. Build Features. Setup and reporting around the steps. Build Features. Parameters. Every value the build passes to its steps. Parameters. Post-build Actions. What happens after the build. Post-build Actions. Visual editor. The same settings drawn as a graph. The visual editor. More › Templates. The templates this build configuration is based on. Templates. More › Dependencies. What this build waits for, and what waits for it. Dependencies. More › Agent Requirements. Which agents may run it. Agent Requirements. More › Other settings. Settings of add-ons with no page of their own. Other settings. More › ID. The stable ID, its aliases and links. IDs. Saving. Some homes have a Save bar at the bottom of the window. It is as wide as the page and never covers anything. Nothing changes until you click Save (or Apply to save and stay on the page). The bar says No unsaved changes when there is nothing to save, and Cancel leaves without saving. Other homes, such as Build Features, save each change at once. Their bar says Changes on this page are saved as you make them. Inherited settings. On Build Steps, Parameters, Build Features and Post-build Actions an item can come from a project or a template. It has a chip with the name of its source. You can Override it here, Disable in this job , or Reset it. See What a project passes down. Who can change what. Changing settings needs the Configure permission on the build configuration. Without it the homes are read-only and say so. Do you prefer a picture? Open Visual editor on the Settings tab. The form and the visual editor edit the same settings. Related pages. Create a build configuration. Job settings. Project settings. Canvas and palette."},
{"title":"Templates","href":"projects/templates","headings":["Create a template","Attach a configuration to a template","The order rules","A worked example: Stream copy","Override inherited settings","Detach a template"],"text":"Share steps and settings between many build configurations. Change the template once and every build configuration based on it follows. A template holds build steps, parameters, post-build actions and version control roots, just like a build configuration, but it never runs by itself. A build configuration attaches one or more templates and inherits what they hold. It can still add its own steps and override what it inherits. Use a template when several build configurations do almost the same thing. For example, Windows Build , PS5 Build and Xbox Series Build can share one Platform build template with the compile, cook and package steps, and each passes its own PLATFORM parameter. Templates or projects? A project passes its settings to everything inside it. A template reaches only the build configurations that attach it, wherever they sit below its project. Create a template. See also Build configuration template as an item type. Open the project that should own the template. Build configurations in this project and its subprojects can use it. Click New Item. Type a name, for example Platform build. Choose Build configuration template and click OK. Add the build steps, parameters and version control roots the build configurations should share, the same way as on a build configuration. Click Save. The project's Settings › Templates lists its templates, with a count. A template's page has a Used by section that lists the build configurations attached to it, so you can see who a change reaches. A template that is in use cannot be moved. Detach it from every build configuration first. Delete does the detaching for you (see below). Attach a configuration to a template. Open the build configuration and click Settings. Open More › Templates. Pick the template to attach. Only templates of this project and the projects above it are offered. To attach more than one, add them in turn and use the up and down buttons to set their order. The Templates home of a build configuration that is not based on a template yet. The order rules. Steps from the templates run first, template by template in the listed order, then the build configuration's own steps. Parameters: when two templates define the same parameter, the template higher in the list wins. The page shows which one lost. Version control roots come in the same order, before the build configuration's own roots. The whole chain is: Root project, then each project, then the templates, then the build configuration itself. The nearer one wins. Templates carry steps, parameters, post-build actions and roots. Triggers and build features are not part of a template: set them on each build configuration. A Pipeline build configuration takes only parameters from a template. A worked example: Stream copy. A Perforce studio copies the Main stream to the Drop streams every night, and the Drop streams to Live. Twenty build configurations do the same job with different streams. The studio makes one template Stream copy : One Perforce root, with polling off. The parameter env.StreamMapping , read-only, empty. Each build configuration overrides it. The parameter env.CopySourceCL , asked when the build runs, with the description Source CL to use on the copy. Leave empty to use latest. One PowerShell step Copy to Stream that runs the copy script. Each build configuration, for example Copy Main to Drop9 , is made with Start from Template › Stream copy. It overrides env.StreamMapping with main->drop9 and has its own Schedule trigger at 02:00. When the script changes, the studio changes the template once. See Copy a stream (Main to Drop) and Create a build configuration from a template. Override inherited settings. On the Build Steps and Parameters homes, an item from a template has a chip with the template's name. For each one you can: Override : change it for this build configuration only, for example a different command or default value. Disable in this job : skip it here only. Reset : go back to what the "},
{"title":"Build configuration","href":"projects/type-build-configuration","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"A build configuration says what to build, where and when. It is the item you use for most builds. On the New Item page the card carries a small label, Freestyle. That is only its technical name. Everything in the guide calls it a build configuration. When to use it. Use it for one kind of build: Windows Build , PS5 Build , Unit Tests. It runs steps one after another, gets its sources from one or more version control roots, and keeps its own history, logs, artifacts and test results. It can inherit settings from its projects and templates. The New Item page of the Game project. How to set it up. Open the project the build belongs to and click New Item. Type the name, for example Switch Build. Check the ID. Under Type choose Build configuration. Click Create. The new build configuration opens on its Settings tab. Work through the settings tabs from left to right. Click Run in the header to start the first build. Every field. These are the fields of the New Item page. The settings of the build configuration itself are on the tab pages: General , Version Control , Build Steps , Triggers , Parameters , Dependencies , Agent Requirements , Build Features , Post-build Actions , Failure Conditions. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Item name. The name people see. Not allowed: ? * / \\ % ! @ # $ ^ & | < > [ ] : ; Empty. Switch Build. ID. Used in links, parameters and settings. Letters, digits and _ only; it must start with a letter. Project ID, _ , name. Game_SwitchBuild. Created in. The project the item goes in. The current project. Game. Start from. Empty, Copy of an existing item, or Template. Empty. Copy. Type. The card Build configuration. -. -. A worked example. In the project Game the studio makes Switch Build by copying PS5 Build ( Start from Copy of an existing item , Keep inheriting ). It then changes the platform parameter and the icon to Nintendo Switch. What you see when it runs. The item appears in the project tree with its icon and a grey dot (never ran). After the first run, the dot shows the result. The page header shows the name, last result, the type chip Build configuration and the ID. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Create stays off. Read the line next to it. Usually the name is empty, the name is already used in the project, or the ID is not valid. The ID is not free. Another item uses it. Edit the ID, or choose another name. I cannot see New Item. You need the Create permission on the project. Related pages. Item types. Create a build configuration. Settings homes. Templates."},
{"title":"Multi-configuration project","href":"projects/type-multi-configuration","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"One project that runs the same steps for every combination of values, such as each platform and each build type. It is no longer offered for new items. When to use it. New work should use the Matrix mode of Parallel blocks instead. You write the steps once, give the values, and every combination runs as its own side. A Multi-configuration project that already exists keeps working: you can open, build, configure and copy it. How to set it up. Open the existing Multi-configuration project and click Settings. Under Configuration Matrix add or change the axes. Set the Combination Filter and the Execution Strategy if needed. Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Axes. The values to combine: user-defined values, label expressions, agents or JDKs. Every combination is one configuration. None. PLATFORM : Win64, PS5. Combination Filter. An expression that removes combinations you do not want. Off. !(PLATFORM==\"PS5\" && CONFIG==\"Debug\"). Execution Strategy. How the configurations run: all at once, or sequentially; touchstone builds first. Classic. -. Use custom child workspace. Override the workspace folder of each configuration. Off. -. Display Name. A name shown instead of the name. Empty. -. A worked example. An old Tools/Asset Pipeline is a Multi-configuration project with the axes PLATFORM and CONFIG. The studio leaves it as it is and builds new tools with a Matrix block. What you see when it runs. The project page shows a grid of the configurations with their latest result. Each cell links to its build. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. I cannot create one. The type is hidden. Use a build configuration with a Matrix block in a Parallel block. Related pages. Item types. Matrix mode. Parallel blocks."},
{"title":"Multibranch Pipeline","href":"projects/type-multibranch","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"A Multibranch Pipeline makes one Pipeline build configuration for each branch of a repository that has a pipeline script file. When to use it. Use it when every branch must be built by the same script, for example each feature branch of the launcher. New branches are found by a scan, and removed branches are cleaned up. It is under More types on the New Item page. How to set it up. Open the project and click New Item. Type a name. Under More types choose Multibranch Pipeline. Click Create. Under Branch Sources click Add source and fill in the repository. Under Build Configuration , Mode is the choice that reads the pipeline script file. Set the Script Path : replace the name the field starts with by the path of your file. Set the scan trigger and the orphaned item strategy. Click Save. The first scan starts. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Branch Sources. The repositories to scan for branches. None. A Git repository. Mode. How the build is defined: by the pipeline script file. The script file mode. -. Script Path. The path of the script file in each branch. The field starts with a default file name: replace it with the path of your file. A default file name. ci/build.pipeline. Property strategy. Properties given to every branch project. All branches get the same properties. -. Build strategies. When no strategy is added, everything except tags is built when found or changed. Adding one turns the defaults off. Empty. -. Scan triggers and orphaned items. Scan on a timer, and what to do with branches that no longer exist: discard them after some days. -. -. A worked example. The launcher repository has branches main , release/1.4 and feature/login. The Multibranch Pipeline Launcher shows three Pipelines, one per branch. What you see when it runs. The page shows the branch projects with their results. The Scan log says which branches were found. Orphaned branches are removed by the strategy. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. No branches are found. The scan found no script file at the Script Path , or the credential cannot read the repository. Open the scan log. Too many builds start on a scan. Set Build strategies or the branch name rule for automatic triggers. Related pages. Item types. Pipeline. Project settings."},
{"title":"Organization Folder","href":"projects/type-organization-folder","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"An Organization Folder scans an account or organization of a code host and makes a Multibranch Pipeline for each repository it finds. It is no longer offered for new items. When to use it. It can no longer be created by hand on the New Item page. One that already exists keeps working. Make a Multibranch Pipeline for each repository instead. How to set it up. Open the existing Organization Folder and click Settings. Under Projects change the Repository Sources and the Project Recognizers. Set the Property strategy and the Build strategies. Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Repository Sources. Which accounts or organizations to scan. None. -. Project Recognizers. What counts as a project in a repository, for example a pipeline script file. Pipeline script file. -. Property strategy. Properties for the branch projects. All branches get the same properties. -. Build strategies. By default everything except tags is built. Adding a strategy turns the defaults off. Empty. -. Child scan triggers, health metrics and orphaned items. Settings passed down to the repositories. See Project settings. -. -. A worked example. An old Platform project scans one account and has a Multibranch Pipeline for each repository. The studio keeps it and makes new ones by hand. What you see when it runs. The folder lists the repositories it found. A scan log shows what changed. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. I cannot create one. The type is hidden. Related pages. Item types. Multibranch Pipeline. Project settings."},
{"title":"Pipeline","href":"projects/type-pipeline","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","Pipeline script","Pipeline script from SCM","Other tabs of a Pipeline","The sandbox and script approval","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"A Pipeline is a build written as a script. The script, typed in Buildspawn or kept as a file in version control, defines stages, parallel work and approvals. When to use it. Use it when your team already writes build logic in code, or when the build needs branching logic that is hard to draw as a list of steps. For most game builds a build configuration is easier to read and edit. A Pipeline needs someone who can write Groovy. How to set it up. Open the project and click New Item. Type a name. Under Type choose Pipeline. Click Create. On Settings open the Pipeline tab. Under Definition choose Pipeline script (the script is written here) or Pipeline script from SCM (the script is a file in version control). Fill in the fields of the definition (see the tables). Leave Use Groovy Sandbox on if you can. Click Save , then Run. Every field. Pipeline script. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Script. The Groovy script that defines the Pipeline. Use the Pipeline Syntax link for help with the steps. Empty. pipeline { agent any. }. Use Groovy Sandbox. Run the script in a sandbox with limited abilities. Off: if you are not an administrator, the script waits until an administrator approves it. On. On. Pipeline script from SCM. Field. What it does. Default. Example. SCM. Where the repository with your pipeline script file is: Git or Perforce. It is checked out on the server to read the script. None. Git. Script Path. The path of the script file in the repository, separated by / on every platform. The field starts with a default file name: replace it with the path of your file. The script always runs in the Groovy sandbox. A default file name. ci/build.pipeline. Lightweight checkout. Read only the script from the SCM, without a full checkout. Faster, but there is no change log or polling from this checkout, and build parameters are not put into the SCM settings. Use checkout scm in the script to get changes and polling. On. On. Other tabs of a Pipeline. General , Build Triggers and Parameters work as on a build configuration. Concurrent builds can also abort the running build when a newer one starts. Build Steps shows the stages read from the text of the script. It does not edit them and it does not run the script. A Pipeline from SCM shows only where the file is, because it is read when a build starts. Run behaviour in Workspace options : resume after a restart, durability and stashes. The Visual editor can edit declarative Pipelines. See Canvas and palette. A template gives a Pipeline only its parameters. The sandbox and script approval. The Groovy sandbox lets a script call only methods that an administrator has approved. If the script calls something else, the build fails with Scripts not permitted to use method. An administrator opens Script approval in the global settings, reads the pending signature and approves it, or removes it. If you turn the sandbox off, the whole script needs approval by an administrator, unless you are one. Keep the sandbox on, and keep the script small. Never approve a signature you do not understand. An approved signature is allowed for every sandboxed script on the server, not only this Pipeline. A worked example. The studio keeps ci/build.pipeline in the Git repository of the launcher. Definition is Pipeline script from SCM , SCM is Git with the repository address and the credential, Script Path is ci/build.pipeline. The file has three stages: Build , Test and Package. See the how-to Make a Pipeline from a script. pipeline { agent any stages { stage(\"Build\") { steps { bat \"build.bat\" } } stage(\"Test\") { steps { bat \"test.bat\" } } stage(\"Package\") { steps { bat \"package.bat\" } } } }. What you see when it runs. The build page shows the stages as a graph, with the result and time of each. The console log is one block per step. The History tab lists the builds, as for a build configuration. Failed builds can be replayed or restarted from a stage. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Scripts not permitt"},
{"title":"Project","href":"projects/type-project","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"A project is a folder. It groups build configurations and other projects, and passes settings down to everything inside. When to use it. Make one project per game or tool: Game , Engine , Tools. Inside you can make a project per kind of build, such as Main , Drops and Release. A project holds shared steps, parameters, credentials, Perforce roots, templates, documentation links and permissions. The page Projects has the full description. How to set it up. Open the project the new one sits in, or the Projects page for a top-level project. Click New Item. Type the name, for example Engine. Under Type choose Project. Click Create. On its Settings , add a description, an icon and a colour. Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Display Name. Shown in lists and the tree. Empty shows the name. Empty. Skyforge Engine. Description. Free text. Empty. Engine builds and tests. Icon and Icon colour. See Project icons. Project. Tools. Health metrics. How the health of the project is worked out from what is inside. Worst child health. -. Properties. Settings the build configurations inside get: Pipeline libraries, credentials, Perforce roots. See Project settings. -. -. A worked example. The studio makes Game with the subprojects Main , Drops and Release. See Organise projects for a game. What you see when it runs. The project appears in the tree. The dot after its name is the worst result inside it. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. A build configuration does not get a setting of the project. Check the order of inheritance, and the Disabled above note. See What a project passes down. Related pages. Item types. Projects. Project settings. Tree order and separators."},
{"title":"Build configuration template","href":"projects/type-template","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"A template holds steps, parameters, post-build actions and roots that several build configurations share. It never runs by itself. When to use it. Use it when Windows Build , PS5 Build and Xbox Series Build do almost the same. Change the template once and every build configuration that uses it follows. The page Templates explains how to create, attach and detach. How to set it up. Open the project that should own the template and click New Item. Type a name, for example Platform build. Under Type choose Build configuration template. Click Create. Add the steps, parameters and roots to share. Click Save. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Name and ID. As for any item. A template has an ID like Game_PlatformBuild. -. Platform build. Build steps, Parameters, Version Control, Post-build Actions. The same tabs as a build configuration. -. -. Used by. A list of the build configurations attached to the template. -. -. A worked example. The project Game has the template Platform build with Sync , Cook and Package steps and the parameter PLATFORM. Each platform configuration attaches it and sets its own value. What you see when it runs. A template has no Run button. Build configurations that use it show its steps with a chip carrying the template name. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. I cannot delete or move the template. Move is refused while build configurations use it. Detach them first. Delete detaches them for you and asks first. Related pages. Item types. Templates. Create a build configuration from a template."},
{"title":"Views","href":"projects/views","headings":["When to use it","Where the tabs are","How to set it up","View types","List View: every field","General","Jobs","Filters","Columns","Widgets","Include a global view","Automatic views of multibranch projects and organization folders","My Views","System settings for views","A worked example","What you see","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"A view is a tab that shows a chosen set of build configurations, so each team sees only what it cares about. When to use it. Use views when a project or the Projects page holds more build configurations than one person needs. For example, make a tab Release for the builds that ship, a tab Nightly for the scheduled ones and a tab Failing for the ones you must look at. A view only changes what you see. It never moves, copies or changes a build configuration, and one build configuration can be in many views. Where the tabs are. The tab bar sits under the page title, on the Projects page and on the Overview of a project. Every place that can hold views starts with one tab called All. It shows everything. It is the default view at first, and the default view cannot be deleted. The tab bar of views. The tabs are sorted by name, not by the order you created them. + at the end of the tab bar (its tooltip is New View ) adds a view. It shows only if you have the View create permission. Rename : open the tab, click the ⋯ menu in the page header, choose Edit View and change Name. Delete : open the tab, click ⋯ and choose Delete View. Buildspawn asks Delete the view ‘Release’? Deleting a view never deletes the build configurations in it. The default view cannot be deleted, and neither can the last view. The same ⋯ menu also holds the Atom feeds of the view ( All , Failures , Just latest builds ). How to set it up. Open the Projects page, or the project that should hold the view. Click + in the tab bar. Type a Name , for example Release. Under Type choose List View , My View or, where offered, Include a global view. Click Create. On Edit View , fill in the fields in the tables below. The top of the page shows a Summary : how many items the view shows now, its type and the pattern if there is one. Click Save. The view opens. If a view with that name already exists, Buildspawn says A view already exists with the name “Release”. View types. Type. What it shows. List View. Items in a simple list. You choose which build configurations show. Details below. My View. Automatically all the build configurations that the current user has access to. It has no settings of its own. Include a global view. Shows the content of another view that lives at the top of the server. See Include a global view. List View: every field. These are the sections of Edit View for a List View. General. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Name. The text on the tab. Change it to rename the view. If another view already has the same display name, Buildspawn warns you. What you typed in +. Release. Description. Describe the purpose of the view. It shows at the top of the view. Empty. Builds that go to players. Jobs. The section title is Jobs and its help line says Select the build configurations to show in this view. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Recurse in subfolders. Off: the list holds only the items directly in the project that owns the view. On: Buildspawn also looks inside every project below, and the tick list shows the nested items too. Names are then written with the path, such as Game/Win64. Off. On, for a view on the Projects page that should reach into every game. The list of projects (tick boxes). Tick the build configurations to show. Tick a project to show the project itself. None ticked. Tick Win64 Build and PS5 Build. Use a pattern instead. A tick box. When it is on, the field Pattern shows. Off. On. Pattern. A regular expression, a text pattern that is tried on the name of every item. Every item whose whole name matches is added, on top of the ticked ones. The pattern has to match the whole name, so Win64 alone matches nothing; write Win64.*. To leave something out, use a negative lookahead: (?!test.*).* shows everything not starting with test. With Recurse in subfolders on, the name includes the path. Empty. (Win64|PS5)-.*. Filters. This section only shows when a filter type is installed. Click Add Build Configuration Filter and choose a type. A"},
{"title":"API tokens","href":"reference/api-tokens","headings":["Create a token","Use a token","In the terminal program","In scripts","See and rename tokens","Revoke a token","Tips"],"text":"An API token lets a script, a tool or the terminal program sign in as you, without your password. An API token is a long random code that stands in for your password. Use one whenever something other than your browser talks to Buildspawn: the terminal program , to stop builds, cancel queue items or take agents offline; scripts that read the server's data, for example the status of the last build; scripts or other tools that start builds. A token has the same permissions as the user who made it. You can have several tokens, one for each script, and revoke one without touching the others. Create a token. Click your initial at the right of the top bar and choose Security. Under API Token , find Add new token. Type a Name that says where the token is used, for example build-scripts or terminal on studio-pc. Choose an Expiration : 7, 30, 60 or 90 days, Custom to pick a date, or No Expiration. Click Generate. Click the copy button beside the token and store it somewhere safe, such as a password manager. Then click Done. The new token is shown only once. Copy it before you click Done. Buildspawn shows the token only once and can't show it again later. If you lose it, revoke it and make a new one. The copy button works only over HTTPS, or when you open Buildspawn on the same computer. On a plain HTTP address, select the token and copy it by hand. Use a token. Sign in with your user name and the token in place of the password. Most tools call this basic authentication. In the terminal program. Save a text file with one line: your user name, a colon and the token. sam.lee:11f3c9a0e5b27d48c6a1f09e3b7d5c2a84. Make the file private, so only you can read it. On Linux and macOS: chmod 600 ~/.buildspawn-token. Start the program with the file: buildspawn --token-file ~/.buildspawn-token. Or set BUILDSPAWN_TOKEN_FILE to the file's path once, in your shell profile. In scripts. Add api/json to the address of most pages to get their data as JSON. Pass the user name and token with the request: # The result of the last build of a build configuration curl -u sam.lee:TOKEN \"http://buildserver:8080/job/Game/job/Windows%20Build/lastBuild/api/json?tree=number,result\" # Start a build curl -X POST -u sam.lee:TOKEN \"http://buildserver:8080/job/Game/job/Windows%20Build/build\" # Start a build with parameters curl -X POST -u sam.lee:TOKEN \"http://buildserver:8080/job/Game/job/Windows%20Build/buildWithParameters\" \\ --data-urlencode \"platform=Win64\". # PowerShell $pair = \"sam.lee:$env:BUILDSPAWN_API_TOKEN\" $auth = [Convert]::ToBase64String([Text.Encoding]::UTF8.GetBytes($pair)) Invoke-RestMethod -Headers @{ Authorization = \"Basic $auth\" } ` -Uri \"http://buildserver:8080/job/Game/job/Windows%20Build/lastBuild/api/json\". Requests made with a token don't need any other security header, so a POST works as shown. Keep tokens out of scripts that are checked in to version control. Store the token as a secret parameter or in Vault, and read it from an environment variable. See Secrets and Vault. See and rename tokens. The Current token(s) table on the Security page lists your tokens. For each one it shows the name, the expiry date, when it was created and when it was last used. Your tokens, with when each one was last used. Click the pencil to rename a token. A token that says Never used for a long time is probably not needed. Revoke it. Revoke a token. Open Security from your account menu. In the Current token(s) table, click the red bin icon beside the token. Confirm with Revoke. The token stops working at once. Anything that still uses it can no longer connect, so give those scripts a new token first. When a token expires, it stops working in the same way. Make a new one and put it in place of the old one. Tips. Use one token per script or machine. Then you can revoke one without breaking the rest. For scripts that run on their own, consider a separate user with only the permissions the script needs. See Users and permissions. The administrator controls tokens on Set"},
{"title":"Build values","href":"reference/build-values","headings":["When to use this page","How to use a value","References","Environment variables of every step","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Every value a build step can read: references like %build.number% and the environment variables every step gets. When to use this page. Use it when you wonder what a step can read. Write a %…% reference in a runner field or a script, or read an environment variable in the process. How to use a value. In a runner field or a script of Command Line, PowerShell, Python, Node.js, Kotlin Script and C# Script steps, type the reference, for example %build.number%. In a Shell or Batch step, or in any program, read the environment variable (see the second table). See Parameter references for the rules. References. Reference. What it holds. Example. %build.number% , %build.counter%. The build number. Not the display name, which a format can fill with parameter values. 212. %build.vcs.number%. The changelist or commit. With several roots: %build.vcs.number.<id>% for each. 48213. %build.branch%. The branch or stream without its prefix, of the first root that records one. release/1.4. %build.branch.isDefault%. true when the branch is the root's default branch. false. %build.vcs.branch.<id>%. The full branch of a root. refs/heads/main. %build.changedFiles.file%. The path of a text file that lists the changed files (a name and a status, ADDED, REMOVED or CHANGED). C:\\.\\changedFiles.txt. %build.checkoutDir%. The checkout folder on the agent. D:\\agent\\ws\\Game. %build.is.personal%. true in a personal build; not set otherwise. true. %build.triggeredBy.username%. The user ID of whoever started the build. For a build another build started, the user who started the first one, when known. Empty for a schedule or a version control trigger. dana.k. %vcsroot.url% , %vcsroot.branch% , %vcsroot.name% , %vcsroot.type%. Properties of the root, with one root. Names that look like secrets are left out, and user:password@ is cut from addresses. With several roots: %vcsroot.<id>.url%. //depot/game/main. %agent.…%. The agent the step runs on. See Agent parameters. %agent.name%. %tool.<id>%. The path of the tool on this agent. C:\\Tools\\msbuild. %dep.<ID>.build.number% ,.build.id ,.build.vcs.number ,.build.result ,.<name>. What the dependency build of this chain used or produced. See Build chains. %dep.Game_Cook.build.result%. %NAME% , %env.NAME% , %system.name%. Your own parameters. %CONFIG%. Environment variables of every step. Variable. What it holds. Example. BUILD_NUMBER. The build number. 212. BUILD_ID. The build ID. 212. BUILD_DISPLAY_NAME. The build name shown on its page, which a buildNumber message can change. 1.4.212-CL48213. BUILD_URL. The address of the build page. Set when the server address is known. http://buildspawn/job/Game/job/Win/212/. JOB_NAME. The full name of the build configuration, with its projects. Game/Windows Build. JOB_BASE_NAME. The short name. Windows Build. JOB_URL. The address of the build configuration. -. WORKSPACE. The checkout folder on the agent. D:\\agent\\ws\\Game. WORKSPACE_TMP. A temporary folder next to the workspace. -. NODE_NAME. The name of the agent. win-build-01. NODE_LABELS. The labels of the agent. windows unreal. EXECUTOR_NUMBER. The number of the executor of the agent. 0. CI. Always true. true. BUILD_VCS_NUMBER. The changelist or commit, with one root. 48213. BUILD_VCS_NUMBER_<id>. The same for each root, with the id written with underscores. -. BUILD_IS_PERSONAL. true in a personal build. true. BUILDSPAWN_TRIGGERED_BY. Who started the build. dana.k. P4_PORT, P4_USER, P4_CLIENT, P4_TICKET. In a build with a Perforce root: its server, user, workspace and a login ticket. -. Your env. parameters and the values that setParameter set with an env. name are environment variables too. The Perforce variables and the values of version control are described in Version control parameters. A worked example. Write-Host \"Build %build.number% of changelist %build.vcs.number% on $env:NODE_NAME\" Write-Host \"Started by $env:BUILDSPAWN_TRIGGERED_BY\". What you see when it runs. Build 212 of changelist 48213 on win-build-01 Started by d"},
{"title":"Release notes","href":"reference/release-notes","headings":["1.9.3","Fixes","Checked","1.9.2","Security","Agents","Checked","1.9.1","1.9","Trusted certificates","Build scripts","Look and feel","Fixes","1.8","Store uploads","Build chains","Projects tree","Perforce","Agents and fixes","1.7","Sign in and words","New Item","Build configurations","Agents","Forms and pages","1.6","Personal machines: run builds on your own computer","Agent app and terminal view","Forms and pages","Permissions and logs","1.5","Builds and steps","Parameters, dialogs and pages","Agents, pools and Perforce","1.4","Agents page","Telemetry","Pools and the Pool Scheduler","Other changes","1.3","1.2","The visual editor","Builds and steps","Parameters and settings","Top bar","Terminal program","Security and fixes","1.1","New look","The build console","Linux and macOS","Other changes","1.0","Main features","Added during the release candidates"],"text":"What each version of Buildspawn added or fixed, from 1.0 to 1.9.3, newest first. Installing a newer version over an older one always keeps your settings and data. Back up the data folder first. See Backups and upgrades. Every release includes everything in the older ones. Java 21 (Eclipse Temurin 21.0.13.1) is included in every server package and agent download since 1.4. Version. Build. Main theme. 1.9.3. 2.589-buildspawn.21. Fix: Resume from a step works with Perforce. 1.9.2. 2.589-buildspawn.20. Two security fixes, agent tray app and terminal screen with a Certificates view, Windows service files. 1.9.1. 2.589-buildspawn.19. Statistics charts fit their cards and redraw. 1.9. 2.589-buildspawn.18. Trusted certificates, build name and status text, resizable sidebar. 1.8. 2.589-buildspawn.17. Steam and Epic Games Store steps, values passed along build chains, project order, view mappings as a table. 1.7. 2.589-buildspawn.16. Matrix blocks, Perforce operations step, agent parameters, new New Item page, new sign-in page. 1.6. 2.589-buildspawn.15. Personal machines, agent terminal screen and tray app, new form layout on every settings page. 1.5. 2.589-buildspawn.14. Fixes: parallel sides, Unstable steps, dialogs, Add parameter, Perforce checks, clearer wording. 1.4. 2.589-buildspawn.13. Agents page, telemetry, pool icons and the Pool Scheduler, agents with Java included. 1.3. 2.589-buildspawn.10 to.12. Test builds only; their changes are in 1.4. 1.2. 2.589-buildspawn.9. The visual editor, parallel blocks, resume and retry, one Parameters tab, the terminal program. 1.1. 2.589-buildspawn.8. New look, the build console with blocks and times, Linux and macOS packages. 1.0. 2.589-buildspawn.5 to.7. The first release: projects, build configurations, Perforce, agents, Windows setup. 1.9.3. Build 2.589-buildspawn.21. Install it over 1.9.2, 1.9.1, 1.9 or 1.8. Fixes. Resume from a step works with Perforce. Before, resuming a build of a build configuration with a Perforce root failed at its first step with a NullPointerException. The source records taken over from the failed build (the changelist, the credential and the login ticket) now belong to the resumed build. Git roots that tag builds are fixed the same way. See Resume a build. Checked. 12 resume tests, including a new one for this case. The server starts with no SEVERE lines in its log. Every agent download was compared with the tools built for it. The Linux and macOS packages passed their package test with 0 failures. 1.9.2. Build 2.589-buildspawn.20. Security. Script Security 1436.1438.v3dc5ef7d16f2-buildspawn fixes a sandbox bypass through a partly written @Builder annotation name (SECURITY-4278). Pipeline: Groovy 4403.4405.va_b_cdb_b_d0b_89f-buildspawn fixes a sandbox bypass through field values in interfaces (SECURITY-4152). The notice Warnings have been published for these two goes away. Agents. Download the agent again from the server ( New agent page) or copy the new files over the agent folder. The agent's settings and registration ( conf\\buildspawn-agent.properties ) do not change. Windows tray app ( bin\\Buildspawn Agent.exe ): the Certificates tab is there. In 1.9 and 1.9.1 the agent download still carried the old tray app without it, although the agent did receive the server's certificates. The old tray app could also fail to start on some machines; this one starts. When the tray starts the agent, what the agent prints goes to logs\\run-agent.log , and unexpected errors of the tray app go to logs\\tray.log. Terminal screen ( buildspawn-agent-tui , every system): a new view 8, Certificates , shows the certificates from the server and the ones added on that machine. You can add a certificate file, remove one, see its details, switch the Windows certificate store and restart the agent. A new command buildspawn-agent-tui certs list | add FILE | remove FINGERPRINT-OR-NAME | windows-store on|off is for machines without a screen. Windows service: the download now includes the serv"},
{"title":"Service messages","href":"reference/service-messages","headings":["Buildspawn messages","Message format","Escaping special characters","setParameter","buildStatisticValue","buildNumber","buildStatus","Keeping files a script names","Log markers","Values a script can read","Tips"],"text":"Special lines a build prints to talk to Buildspawn: set a parameter, record a number, name the build, set its status, or group the log into blocks. A service message is a normal line of output. Your script prints it with echo , Write-Host , print or any other command. Buildspawn spots the line while the build runs and acts on it. Any tool that can print text can use them. There are two families: Family. Looks like. Used for. Buildspawn messages. ##buildspawn[name attribute='value']. Setting parameters, recording statistics, naming the build and setting its status. Log markers. ::group::Name. Blocks, warnings and errors in the build console. Buildspawn messages. Message format. ##buildspawn[messageName attribute='value' attribute='value']. A message with one value can leave out the attribute name: ##buildspawn[messageName 'value']. The line contains ##buildspawn[ , the message name, then attributes, then ]. Each attribute is name='value' , in single quotes, with a space before it. Spell it exactly like this. Other spellings are ignored. Only the processes a build step starts, while the step runs, can send messages: its script or its tool. The same line anywhere else in the log is refused, with a line that says so. Message. What it does. setParameter. Sets a value for the later steps. buildStatisticValue. Records a number to chart across builds. buildNumber. Gives the build its name, such as 1.4.212-CL48213. buildStatus. Sets the build's status text, and can make its result worse. Escaping special characters. Inside a value, put a vertical bar | in front of special characters: Write. To get. |'. a single quote '. ||. a vertical bar |. |[ and |]. square brackets [ and ]. |n. a new line. |r. a carriage return. setParameter. Sets a value for the build steps that come after this one. ##buildspawn[setParameter name='version' value='1.4.212']. Name you use. What it sets. Later steps read it as. version. A build parameter. It is not put into the environment. %version%. env.GAME_VERSION. The environment variable GAME_VERSION. %env.GAME_VERSION% , or $GAME_VERSION / %GAME_VERSION% in a script. system.build.flavour. A system property, kept as it is. It is not an environment variable. %system.build.flavour%. From a few common shells: # Bash echo \"##buildspawn[setParameter name='env.GAME_VERSION' value='1.4.212']\" # PowerShell Write-Host \"##buildspawn[setParameter name='env.GAME_VERSION' value='1.4.212']\" Write-Host \"##buildspawn[setParameter name='package' value='$($zip.FullName)']\" # Windows batch echo ##buildspawn[setParameter name='env.GAME_VERSION' value='1.4.212'] # Python print(\"##buildspawn[setParameter name='env.GAME_VERSION' value='1.4.212']\"). The rules: Rule. Detail. Who can set values. Only the processes a build step starts, while the step runs: its script or its tool. The same line anywhere else in the log is refused, for example in a commit message or after the step ended. Names. Start with a letter or _. Then letters, digits,. , _ and -. 100 characters at most. Values. 8,192 characters at most. How many. 200 values per build at most. Same name as a build parameter. The new value replaces it for the later steps. The log says so once for each name. Blocked environment names. Names that change which programs run or how they start are refused, for example PATH , LD_LIBRARY_PATH , PYTHONPATH , NODE_OPTIONS , JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS , and every DYLD_* and GIT_* name. An administrator can allow them with Let build steps set any environment variable in the system settings. Refusals. Each refused message gets a line in the build log that says why. Resumed builds. A build that resumes from a later step keeps the values the earlier steps set. A step can also declare outputs in the step editor. Each output must then be set with setParameter before the step ends. See Set parameters from a build for worked examples. buildStatisticValue. Records a number for this build, such as code coverage, a package size or a load time. Buildspawn charts it a"},
{"title":"Settings files","href":"reference/settings-files","headings":["Where the file is","buildspawn.cfg (Windows)","buildspawn.conf (Linux and macOS)","The recommended Java options","macOS","The Linux packages","Portable markers on Windows","Inside the data folder","Agent files","Tips"],"text":"One small text file holds how the server starts: its port, memory, folders and Java options. Windows uses buildspawn.cfg. Linux and macOS use buildspawn.conf. They hold the same settings with slightly different names. Everything else, such as projects, users and agents, lives in the home folder and is set in the web interface. You rarely need to open the file by hand. These all edit it for you and keep your comments: the Settings tab of the Windows app (see The Windows app ); the Settings view ( 7 ) of the terminal program; buildspawn set key=value (see Terminal program commands ; its key for the data folder is home ). Restart Buildspawn after you change the file. The server reads it only when it starts. Where the file is. Download. Settings file. Home and logs by default. Windows setup. C:\\ProgramData\\Buildspawn\\buildspawn.cfg. C:\\ProgramData\\Buildspawn\\data and \\logs. Windows zip (portable). buildspawn.cfg next to Buildspawn.exe. data and logs in the same folder. Debian, Ubuntu package. /etc/default/buildspawn. /var/lib/buildspawn and /var/log/buildspawn. RHEL, Rocky, Fedora package. /etc/sysconfig/buildspawn. /var/lib/buildspawn and /var/log/buildspawn. Linux and macOS folders (tar.gz). buildspawn.conf in the folder. data/ and logs/ in the same folder. The Windows app, the Windows service and the terminal program all read the same file, so they never disagree. The last line of buildspawn help shows which file this copy uses, and buildspawn check prints the full Java command made from it. buildspawn.cfg (Windows). One key=value per line. Lines that start with # are comments. Key. Default. What it does. port. 8080. The web port. listen. all. all : every network interface. localhost : this computer only. memory. a quarter of the computer's memory, between 2g and 16g. The Java heap, used as both the start and the largest size, for example 4g. java-options. the recommended set (below). Extra Java options. data. see Where the file is. The home folder: projects, build history and settings. logs. see Where the file is. buildspawn.log , gc.log and heap dumps. arguments. empty. Extra Buildspawn arguments, for example --sessionTimeout=480. In java-options , data and logs you can use placeholders: Placeholder. Stands for. {install}. The program folder. {data}. The home folder. {logs}. The logs folder. An example: # Buildspawn settings port=8080 listen=all memory=8g data={install}\\data logs={install}\\logs arguments=--sessionTimeout=480. Folders named in the Java options are created before Java starts. Keys that this version doesn't know are kept as they are. An installed copy keeps its file in C:\\ProgramData , which only administrators can change. The Windows app asks for permission when you save. buildspawn.conf (Linux and macOS). The file uses shell syntax: KEY=value , or KEY=\"value with spaces\". Lines that start with # are comments. Key. Default. What it does. BUILDSPAWN_PORT. 8080. The web port. BUILDSPAWN_LISTEN. all. all : every network interface. localhost : this computer only. BUILDSPAWN_MEMORY. empty. The Java heap, for example 4g. Empty means a quarter of the computer's memory, between 2 GB and 16 GB. BUILDSPAWN_HOME. \"{install}/data\". The home folder: projects, build history and settings. The packages use /var/lib/buildspawn. BUILDSPAWN_LOGS. \"{install}/logs\". gc.log , heap dumps, and buildspawn.log when the server runs in the background. The packages use /var/log/buildspawn. BUILDSPAWN_JAVA_OPTIONS. the recommended set (below). Java options. BUILDSPAWN_ARGUMENTS. empty. Extra Buildspawn arguments, for example --sessionTimeout=480. {install} is the program folder. In the Java options and arguments, {home} and {logs} stand for those folders. An example: # Buildspawn settings BUILDSPAWN_PORT=8080 BUILDSPAWN_LISTEN=all BUILDSPAWN_MEMORY=8g BUILDSPAWN_HOME=\"/srv/buildspawn\" BUILDSPAWN_LOGS=\"{install}/logs\" BUILDSPAWN_ARGUMENTS=--sessionTimeout=480. To use another file, set BUILDSPAWN_CONFIG=/path/to/file before you run bin/builds"},
{"title":"Terminal program commands","href":"reference/terminal-commands","headings":["How to call it","Commands","Set up","Run, start and stop","The service","Watch the server","Trusted certificates","Change settings","Exit codes","Options","Environment variables","Keys in the live monitor","Anywhere","In each view","In the launch flow","Where presets are saved"],"text":"Every command, option and key of the buildspawn terminal program, in one place. The terminal program comes with every server download. On Windows it is bin\\buildspawn.exe ; on Linux and macOS it is bin/buildspawn. The Linux packages and Windows setup put it on the PATH, so there you just type buildspawn. For a walk-through of the screens, see The terminal program , and for how it behaves, The server terminal program. How to call it. buildspawn [command] [options]. With no command it opens the terminal interface: the launch flow on a first run, otherwise the live monitor. Without a terminal, for example in a scheduled task, it prints the status instead. Commands. Set up. Command. What it does. buildspawn. Opens the launch flow on a first run, otherwise the live monitor. launch [--preset NAME] [--yes]. Opens only the launch flow. --preset fills it from a saved preset and skips the start screen. --yes accepts every default. preset list. Lists the saved launch presets. preset save NAME. Saves the current settings as a preset. preset delete NAME. Deletes a preset. admin-password [--copy]. Prints the initial admin password while it exists. --copy puts it on the clipboard instead. check. Checks the settings file and Java, and prints the full Java command. version. Prints the version and build (also --version and -v ). help. Lists every command and option, and ends with the settings file this copy uses (also --help and -h ). Run, start and stop. Command. What it does. run. Runs the server in this terminal until you press Ctrl + C. The services use this too. start. Starts the service when one is installed for this copy. Otherwise starts the server in the background. stop. Stops the service, or the background server. restart. Stops it and starts it again. The service. The service is a Windows service, a systemd service on Linux, or a LaunchAgent on macOS. Command. What it does. service status. Shows whether a service is installed, whether it runs and whether it starts at boot. service install. Installs the service and starts it. service remove. Removes the service. The home, the logs and the settings stay. service enable / disable. Turns start at boot on or off. install-service. The same as service install. Options: --user NAME (Linux: the system user to run as, buildspawn by default), --no-start (install but don't start), --dry-run (only say what would be done). uninstall-service. The same as service remove. --remove-user (Linux) also deletes the system user, but not its files. Every service command takes --dry-run. Actions that need administrator or root rights ask first: Windows shows its permission prompt, and on Linux the program runs sudo only after you answer y. Watch the server. These are the text twins of the monitor's views, for use in scripts. The table commands print plain text, or JSON with --json. Command. What it prints. status [--json]. Where this copy is, whether it runs, the URL, the home folder, and counts of builds, queue and agents. builds [--running | --failed] [--limit N] [--json]. The Builds table: only running builds, only failed builds, or the last N builds. queue [--json]. The Queue table, with the reason each item waits. agents [--json]. The Agents table. logs [-f] [--level warn] [--filter TEXT] [--source gc]. The server log. With no option it follows the log. --level hides lines below that level, --filter keeps only lines with the text, and --source gc shows the Java memory (GC) log. Trusted certificates. Manages the trust folder in the home: company root certificates that Java must trust. It works on this computer's home and does not need the server to run. Changes take effect when the server starts again. See Trusted certificates. Command. What it does. certs list. Shows the trust folder, the Windows store setting, the added certificates and whether a restart is needed. certs add FILE. Adds the certificates in a.pem ,.crt ,.cer ,.der ,.p7b or.p7c file. Shows each one and asks before adding. Use - as the fi"},
{"title":"Parallel tests and composite builds","href":"running-builds/parallel-and-composite","headings":["Parallel tests","Turn it on","How the tests are split","The results","Composite builds","Parallel blocks and Matrix blocks","Related"],"text":"Split a long test run over several agents so it finishes sooner, and see the results as one build. Parallel tests. Parallel tests is a build feature. When it is on, one build of the build configuration becomes a parent that starts 2 to 20 batch builds at the same time. Each batch runs its share of the test classes. The parent waits for them and merges the results. Turn it on. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Build Features. Add the feature Parallel tests. Set the Number of batches , from 2 to 20. Save. Each batch needs a free agent, so choose a number your agents can serve. How the tests are split. Maven , Gradle and NUnit steps get their share of the test classes on their own. Buildspawn uses the time each class took in earlier runs to give every batch about the same amount of work. New classes are spread evenly. Any other step can read its share from environment variables: Variable. What it holds. BUILDSPAWN_PARALLEL_BATCH. The number of this batch, starting at 1. BUILDSPAWN_PARALLEL_BATCHES. How many batches there are. BUILDSPAWN_PARALLEL_INCLUDE_FILE. A file with the test classes of this batch, one per line. BUILDSPAWN_PARALLEL_EXCLUDE_FILE. A file with all the other known classes, for tools that only take an exclude list. For example, a Unity or Unreal test script can pass the include file to its own test filter: python Build/run_tests.py --classes-from \"$BUILDSPAWN_PARALLEL_INCLUDE_FILE\". The results. The parent's build page has a Parallel tests block: one row per batch with a link, the agent, the duration and the result, and the merged test counts. A batch build says Batch 2 of 4, part of #12 with a link to the parent. The parent ends with the worst batch result. Each batch keeps its own artifacts and test reports. Mail goes out once, from the parent. Stopping the parent stops its batches. Pin the revision in the build configuration, or use one Perforce changelist, so every batch builds the same code. Each batch prints its share size in its log, so you can check. Composite builds. A composite build is a build that only starts and waits for other builds. It uses no agent : it runs on the server, starts at once and finishes when its parts finish. Because it holds no executor while it waits, it cannot take the agent its own parts need. You meet composite builds in two places: the parent build of Parallel tests ; a distributed Unreal Engine BuildGraph build: a hidden setup build exports the graph, the groups run on agents of the matching type, and the parent collects their artifacts. A composite build shows No agent (composite) in the executor lists, and is not counted as a busy executor. Its build page and log say it runs without an agent. The hidden builds made for it are not listed as build configurations and are cleaned up with the build. Parallel blocks and Matrix blocks. Parallel tests splits one test run. A Parallel block instead runs steps of one build side by side, on this machine or on other agents. The parts that run on other agents are side builds. A Matrix block runs the same steps for every combination of values, such as platform &times; config. See Side builds for how they look in the queue and on the build page. Related. Parallel blocks : run steps side by side inside one build. Side builds : the parts of a Parallel block. The queue : where batches wait for agents."},
{"title":"Personal builds and shelves","href":"running-builds/personal-builds","headings":["When to use it","What makes a build personal","Run a personal build of a shelf","Run on my machine","Add your machine","Run a build on it","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"A personal build tries your own change, not yet submitted, on the real build steps. It never changes the status of the build configuration, so nobody sees a red build because of your experiment. When to use it. Use a personal build before you submit a change to Perforce: shelve it, run a personal build of the shelf and submit only when it passes. You can also run a build on your own computer when a build farm slot is far away or you need your own tools. What makes a build personal. A build is personal when you tick Personal build in the Run dialog , when you run shelved changes, or when you pick My machine. A shelve trigger on a build configuration starts a personal build for each new shelf. See Triggers. Only you and administrators see the build. Other people get a page-not-found answer for it and do not find it in build lists, unless you share it. The build configuration's status and its last successful build, for dependencies on that, come from regular builds only. A personal build also does not start other build configurations. A personal build is marked Personal in the build list and on the build page. Its Summary has a Personal build row, such as Personal build of shelf 260 by dana.k , that links to the shelf. Run a personal build of a shelf. Shelve your change in Perforce, for example p4 shelve -c 260. Open the build configuration and click the button with the sliders beside Run. Open the Shelved changes tab. Tick your shelf, or type its number in Shelved changelist IDs. Separate several numbers with commas. Click Run personal build. The build syncs the code, then unshelves each change in the order you gave ( p4 unshelve ) and merges what is older than the head ( p4 resolve -am ). If two changes conflict, the build fails and names the files. Then it runs the steps. Step by step instructions with a worked example are in Test a shelved change before you submit. Run on my machine. With My machine you run the build on your own computer, as a personal agent. Only you can build on it, and only your own builds run there. It is hidden from everyone else. Add your machine. Open your account menu and choose My machines. You see it only when an administrator has turned on Personal machines under Settings &rsaquo; Security , and you have the Use a personal machine permission (under Agents in the permission table). Add a machine and give it a name. Buildspawn shows a secret once and an agent download set up for it. Unzip it on your computer and start the agent. It connects by itself, and the page says Waiting for name to connect until then. Choose how builds use your workspace (see the table). Workspace choice. What it means. Own copy (A). The build uses its own copy of the workspace. The first sync is full. My workspace (B). The build uses a folder you have already synced, with its Perforce client. The agent unshelves into it and reverts the shelved files after the build, and only when the client has no other open files. A synced folder is never wiped or cleaned up. Run a build on it. Open the Run dialog and choose My machine · name in Agent , or pick your shelf on the Shelved changes tab and click Run on my machine. The dialog shows Personal: only you see this build and how many builds the machine runs at a time. When your machine is offline, the build waits for it, up to the time an administrator set under Settings &rsaquo; Security &rsaquo; Personal machines ( Offline machine: cancel waiting builds after ). Then it is cancelled and says why. An administrator chooses which credentials personal machines may use. A build configuration that uses another credential stops before it starts, and the dialog says so. If an administrator turns personal machines off, your machines stay listed but take no builds. What you see when it runs. The build has a Personal chip. The Parameters tab shows p4.status = shelved and p4.review with your changelist number for a Perforce shelf. The Changes tab lists your shelved changelist. The console says w"},
{"title":"The queue","href":"running-builds/queue","headings":["Read the queue","The order","Reorder and remove builds","Cancel a queued build","Builds that never wait for an agent"],"text":"Builds wait in the queue until an agent that can run them is free. The Queue page shows what waits, why, and in which order. Click Queue in the top bar. The number beside it shows how many builds wait now. Read the queue. The Build Queue. The build waits for an executor on a Linux agent. Each row is one waiting build: Column. What it shows. #. Its place in the queue. Number 1 starts first. Build configuration. What will run, with its project. Triggered by. Who or what started it: a user, a trigger, another build. Time in queue. How long it has waited. Wait reason. Why it cannot start yet, for example Not in a pool assigned to this project , an agent that is offline, or a missing agent requirement. Click Why is it waiting? for the full story. Agents. How many agents could run it. Estimated start. When it should start, from the builds running now. Unknown when no agent can take it. The top right of the list shows how many builds run and how many executors are free. Use All and Mine at the top to see every waiting build or only yours. The card title shows how many builds wait. On its right it shows how many builds run and how many executors are free, for example No builds running · 3 of 3 executors free. When the queue is empty, the page says No builds in the queue and shows the free capacity. When the server is shutting down, it says no further builds will be performed. A build whose position you set by hand shows a marker: Placed by hand: keeps this position until it starts. A build configuration with a build priority shows Priority N. Side builds of a Parallel block wait in the queue like any other build, and show as Side build of #N under their main build. The order. Builds start in this order: Builds someone moved by hand come first. Then builds of build configurations with a higher build priority. Priority runs from −100 to 100 and is set on Settings › General of the build configuration. Then the builds that were queued first. Reorder and remove builds. Move to top : the build becomes number 1. ⋯ menu : move the build up or down one place, or cancel it. Drag : grab the handle at the left of a row and drop it where you want. You can move builds of build configurations you are allowed to run. Administrators can move any build. To put a build at the top when you start it, tick Put the build to the queue top in the Run dialog. If a build waits a long time, read its wait reason first. Often an agent is offline or in the wrong pool. See Pools and The Agents page. Cancel a queued build. Use the … menu of the row and choose Cancel. Buildspawn asks Cancel the queued build of.? before it does it. Someone who may stop builds of that build configuration can cancel it. In the terminal program's Queue view, press c (it needs an API token). See Stop a build. Builds that never wait for an agent. Some builds only coordinate other builds, such as the parent of parallel tests. These composite builds start at once and use no agent, so they never block the queue."},
{"title":"Re-run a build","href":"running-builds/rerun","headings":["Re-run","Re-run with changes","Resume a failed build","Re-run a Matrix combination","Re-run a shared personal build","Problems and fixes","Tips"],"text":"Run a finished build again with exactly the same code and parameters, or change some of them first. Every finished build can be re-run, whatever its result: successful, failed, unstable, stopped or personal. A re-run is a new build with a new number. You need the permission to run the build configuration. Re-run. Open the build, for example Windows Build #6. Click Re-run , the main button at the top right. You can also use the ⋯ menu of a row in the build history: it has Re-run and Re-run with changes. A re-run builds the same sources as the original: the same Git commit and the same Perforce changelist, for every root; the same shelves, for a personal build; the same parameters, passwords included (reused, never shown); by default, the same dependency builds. If a revision no longer exists, for example a deleted branch, the re-run stops with a clear message instead of building something else. The new build says Re-run of #6 by <you> with a link to the original and lists its sources. The original lists its re-runs. A re-run uses the build configuration's current settings and steps, not the ones from the time of the original. File parameters are not reused. Re-run with changes. Use this when you want the same build, but with one thing different: another changelist, another value, another shelf. Open the build. Click the button with the sliders beside Re-run. Its tooltip says Re-run with changes…. The Re-run dialog opens, filled in from the original. Change what you need (see below). Click Re-run. Click Cancel to close without starting anything. The Re-run dialog has the same tabs as the Run dialog , filled in from build #6. Tab. What you can change. General. The agent, queue top, clean checkout and comment, as in the Run dialog. For each Git root a Source row: Same as #6 , Latest , or Other commit… (a commit, tag or branch such as main ). For dependencies: Reuse the dependency builds of #6 , or rebuild them. Parameters. The values of the original. Change any of them. Changes. For Perforce: the original changelist, the latest, or another changelist. Shelved changes. Pick other shelves, or remove the original's shelf. The agent, queue position, clean checkout and comment are not taken from the original. Choose them again if you need them. Resume a failed build. A failed or stopped build can also be resumed : the new build starts at the failed step and reuses the sources, agent and workspace of the original, instead of running every step again. That is much faster for a long build that failed near the end. When resume is not possible, the main button changes to Re-run from the start. In the Re-run dialog of such a build, Start from step lets you choose The beginning (a re-run) or a step (a resume). See Resume a build and Resume and retry. The Re-run dialog of a failed build. Here the agent is offline, so only The beginning can be chosen. Re-run a Matrix combination. For a build with a Matrix block, the build page shows a grid of the results. Each combination has its own Re-run. See Parallel and composite builds. Re-run a shared personal build. Someone who views a personal build only through a share link cannot re-run it. See Shared builds. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The re-run stops with a message about a revision. The revision no longer exists, for example a deleted branch. Re-run with changes and pick Latest or another commit. There is no Re-run button. You may not run this build configuration, or you only view a shared personal build. The re-run behaves differently from the original. It uses the build configuration's current steps and settings. File parameters are not reused. Tips. Re-run a failed build to check whether a failure is flaky: same code, same values. Use Latest on the Source row to build today's code with last week's parameters. To retry failed builds on their own, add a Retry build trigger. See Triggers."},
{"title":"Resume a build","href":"running-builds/resume","headings":["When to use it","Resume, re-run or retry","Resume a failed build","Resume from an earlier step","When resume is not available","What you see when it runs","Resume with Perforce","Parallel blocks and chains","Retry","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Pick up a failed or stopped build at the step that failed, on the same agent and in the same workspace, so the steps that already passed do not run again. When to use it. Use it when a long build fails near the end: a two-hour cook, then a package step that fails because a file share was offline. You fix the cause outside the build, then resume. Resume runs the same code again. It does not pick up new changes. To build new changes, use Re-run with changes and choose Latest. Resume, re-run or retry. Tool. What it does. Use it when. Resume. A new build on the same agent and workspace, with no new sync, starting at a step you choose. The revision and parameters are copied from the failed build. The problem was the machine or the network, and the work done so far is still good. Re-run. A new build of the same sources and parameters from the start. The workspace was used by another build, or you want to see whether a failure is flaky. Retry. A step, or the whole build, runs again on its own after a failure. A failure is flaky and you want Buildspawn to try again without you. Resume a failed build. Open the failed build. Its page has a Resume from here card on the right. It checks three things and shows a tick for each: the agent is online, the workspace still exists, and no other build used the workspace since. Click Resume from step N at the top of the page. It is next to Re-run. The failed step in Build steps has the same button. A message says Resume queued. The new build starts, on the same agent, at step N. Resume from an earlier step. Click the button with the sliders beside Re-run. Its tooltip says Re-run with changes…. Under Start from step , pick The beginning (a re-run), any step that passed, or the step marked Failed here. Later steps are not offered. Read the blue box. It sums up what will happen: the sources, the parameters, the agent and workspace, and which steps show as done. The button now says Resume from step N. Click it. In the dialog, the sources, the agent and the clean checkout are locked for a resume, because it uses the sources, agent and workspace of the original. A failed build whose agent is offline. The card says why resume is not available, and Re-run from the start is the main button. A failed build that can be resumed. The card lists what Buildspawn checked. When resume is not available. The Resume button is greyed out, the main button says Re-run from the start , and the card names the reason: Reason. What to do. Agent is offline , or The agent no longer exists. Bring the agent back online and try again, or re-run from the start. Agent no longer matches the build configuration. The settings no longer let that agent run it, for example a changed label. Re-run from the start. The workspace is gone : wiped, or the folder no longer exists. Someone used Wipe Out Current Workspace. The files the earlier steps made are gone. Re-run from the start. Another build used the workspace. Its files no longer match what the failed build made, so the step could work on the wrong files. Re-run from the start. The build passed, or has no failed step. Only a failed or stopped build with a failed step can be resumed. Resume needs the same permission as Re-run. It works for build configurations with build steps. Pipelines cannot be resumed. What you see when it runs. A resumed build. Steps 1 and 2 show Done in #1 , with a link to their logs. Step 3 runs. The new build has a new number. Its Cause says Resumed #1 from step 3. The first lines of the console say Resumed #1 from step 3 and Same agent and workspace as #1. Workspace not modified since #1 finished. Skipped steps show Done in #1 with Open in #1 log. The sources step says it was skipped and where the revision came from: Skipped: revision info copied from #1. The steps from N on run, and the post-build actions run again. The values that earlier steps set with ##buildspawn[setParameter] are kept, and so are the earlier test results. The Overview has a Resumed from c"},
{"title":"The Run dialog","href":"running-builds/run-dialog","headings":["Run, or Run custom build","The tabs","General: every option","The Agent list","Dependencies","Parameters","Changes and Shelved changes","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Start a build by hand and choose its options: the agent, the parameters, the changes and the shelves to build. Run, or Run custom build. The Run button on a build configuration starts a build at once with the default values. A message says Build scheduled , and the build joins the queue. If the build configuration depends on other builds, Run starts the whole chain. When you want something different for one build, use the Run dialog. Its title is Run custom build. Open the build configuration, for example Game / Windows Build. Click the small button with the sliders, right beside Run. Its tooltip says Run custom build. Choose your options on the tabs. Click Run Build. Click Cancel , or press Esc , to close without starting anything. Clicking outside the dialog does not close it. The Run custom build dialog, on the General tab. The other tabs fill in while you look. If a parameter's display is set to Prompt , the main button is called Run… and opens this dialog every time. See Parameters. The tabs. Tab. When you see it. General. Always. Dependencies. When the build configuration depends on other builds. The number is how many. Parameters. When the build configuration has parameters people may set. The number is how many. Changes and Shelved changes. When the build configuration has a Perforce root. They load in the background and show their counts when ready. General: every option. Option. What it does. Default. Agent. Which machine runs the build. See the list below. <default: any compatible agent>. Personal build. Marks the build as personal: only you and administrators see it, and it never changes the status of the build configuration. See Personal builds and shelves. It is always on when you pick My machine. Off. Put the build to the queue top. The build goes ahead of the builds already waiting. See The queue. Off. Delete all files in the checkout directory before the build. A clean checkout: the agent deletes the old files and gets all the code again. Use it when a build fails because of leftover files. A resumed build never does this, because it continues in the workspace as it was. Off. Comment. Why you run this build, for example Release candidate for QA. It is shown on the build page and in the build list. Empty. The Agent list. Choice. What happens. <default: any compatible agent>. Buildspawn picks a free agent that fits the build configuration's requirements. A note under the list tells you the label the build configuration runs on, or that it can run on any agent that is not reserved for other build configurations. Run on all enabled compatible agents (N). Starts one build on every enabled agent that can run it. Use it to try a change on every machine type. It is greyed out when no compatible agent is connected. My machine · name. One of your own computers. Only you see this choice. See Personal builds and shelves. An agent name, under Compatible agents. Runs the build on that agent. Each line shows the agent, whether it can start now (for example Can start now (2 idle) ) and its pool. An agent under Incompatible agents. Greyed out. The line says why it cannot run the build, for example Not in a pool assigned to this project. The link Compatible Agents under the list opens a page that explains which agents can run this build configuration and why. A Pipeline picks its agents in its own script, so the Agent list is greyed out and the clean-checkout box is off. Use deleteDir() or cleanWs() in the script instead. Dependencies. This tab is for build configurations that depend on others (snapshot dependencies). Under Rebuild snapshot dependencies choose one: Choice. What happens. Default: reuse suitable builds. Each dependency is rebuilt only when it has no suitable build. Rebuild failed and incomplete dependencies. Successful builds are reused. Dependencies whose last build failed or did not finish are built again. Rebuild all dependencies. Every dependency in the chain is built again. Below that, This build configurati"},
{"title":"Shared builds","href":"running-builds/shared-builds","headings":["When to use it","Share a build","What the other person sees","Stop sharing","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Share a personal build with a link, so a colleague can look at its result and log without getting any other access. When to use it. A personal build is private: only you and administrators see it. Share it when someone else should read its log, for example a programmer who helps you with a failing shelf, or a lead who wants to see a build before you submit. Share a build. Open the personal build. The Share button is in the header, next to the other build actions. Only the person who started the build, and administrators, see it. Click Share. Buildspawn makes a link and copies it. A message says Link copied. If your browser refuses to copy, the link is shown in a box so you can copy it by hand. Send the link to your colleague. The link ends in ?share= and a long random code. Clicking Share again gives the same link. What the other person sees. They must be signed in and may read Buildspawn. The link then lets them view that one build, and only for their browser session. They can read the build page, console, changes, parameters and artifacts. They do not see Re-run , and they do not see your other personal builds. Stop sharing. Open the build and click Stop sharing. The button appears while the build is shared. A message says Sharing stopped. The link no longer works. The link and every session that used it lose access at once. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. There is no Share button. The build is not personal, or you did not start it. Only personal builds can be shared. The colleague gets page not found. They may not be signed in, or you stopped sharing. Share again and send the new link. The browser could not copy the link. Select the link in the box that opens and copy it yourself. Related pages. Personal builds and shelves. The build page."},
{"title":"Side builds","href":"running-builds/side-builds","headings":["When to use it","How a main build and its sides run","Where you see side builds","If a side fails or you stop the build","Matrix blocks","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"A side build is one part of a Parallel block that runs on its own machine. Buildspawn starts it for you, and shows it under the main build. When to use it. You meet side builds when a build configuration has a Parallel block set to Separate machines , or a Matrix block. For example, one side cooks for PS5 on a console agent, and another cooks for Xbox on another agent. This page explains what you see in the queue, the build list and the build page. How to set the block up is on Parallel blocks and overrides. How a main build and its sides run. The main build runs its steps up to the Parallel block on its agent. At the block it starts one side build for each side. Each one needs an agent that fits the side's Run on choice, and fetches the same revision as the main build. While the sides run, the main build gives up its executor slot on its agent and keeps its workspace. The log says This build gives up its slot on agent while its 2 sides run on their own machines. Its workspace stays reserved for it. When all sides are done, the main build comes back to the same agent, copies in the files the sides sent back and goes on. If that agent is not free again in time, or went offline, the Fallback of the block lets the build go on on any matching agent. A main build that started before its block was set to run on separate machines keeps its slot: Starting 2 side builds. This build keeps its slot. Where you see side builds. Place. What you see. The build list and History of the build configuration. Side builds are nested under their main build. They read Side build of #12. They are never listed as build configurations of their own. The queue. Each side waits like any build, and counts in agent usage. Last successful build and other permalinks. They skip side builds. The main build's console. Messages such as Queued the side build of PS5 , Started PS5 on ps5-devkit-01 , PS5 passed in 4 min 10 s on ps5-devkit-01 and Sending back 120 files. A side build's own name. #12 · side 1 (PS5) , with the cause Side 1 (PS5) of #12. If a side fails or you stop the build. With Let the others finish (the default) every side runs to its end. With Stop the others , a side that fails stops the rest: Stopped: another side of. failed, and the block stops the others. Either way the main build fails after the block. Stopping the main build stops its side builds, and takes the queued ones out of the queue. To run a failed build again without redoing the sides that passed, use Resume : the sides that passed are not run again, and only the failed side restarts, from its failed step. Matrix blocks. A Matrix block writes the steps once and runs them for every combination of values, for example platform &times; config. Each combination runs as its own side and can use %platform% and %config%. A block has at most 4 axes and 256 combinations. On the build page you see: a Matrix card with a grid of the results: Success , Failed , Unstable , Running , Stopped , Did not start or Excluded , with counts such as 3 passed , 1 failed ; for each combination a Log link and a Re-run button. Run it again. The combinations that passed are not run again. A message says Re-run queued ; in the console, a lane for each combination. Click the lane chip to show only that combination; on the build configuration's Overview, a trend of the last builds. How the block decides what happens when a combination fails ( Let the others finish , No new combination starts or Stop the others ) and how many run at once are set on the block. See Parallel blocks and overrides. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. A side waits in the queue for a long time. Click Why is it waiting? Often no agent of the pool in Run on is free or online. See The queue. The build configuration no longer has the side. The side was removed from the Parallel block after the main build started. Re-run the main build. No files match the files sent back. The side made no files that match its Files sent back rules. Check the "},
{"title":"Stop a build","href":"running-builds/stop-a-build","headings":["When to use it","Stop a running build","What happens to the steps","Take a build out of the queue","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Stop a build that runs, or take a build out of the queue before it starts. When to use it. Stop a build when you started it by mistake, when a newer change makes it useless, or when it hangs. A stopped build ends with the result Aborted. It keeps its log and what it did so far. Stop a running build. Open the running build. Click its number in the History tab, or in the Queue and Agents pages. Click Stop in the header. It replaces Re-run while the build runs. You need the permission to cancel builds of this build configuration. The build ends with the result Aborted. You can also stop a build from the Agents page. Expand an agent's row and click the stop button beside the build it runs. In the terminal program , select the build in the Builds view and press x. That needs an API token. A running build on the build configuration page. The red button on the row stops it. What happens to the steps. The step that runs is stopped. The steps after it do not run, except steps set to Always, even if build stop command was issued. Use that for clean-up steps. See Resume and retry. For a build with a Parallel block , the log says The build was stopped: stopping its side builds. Each side build stops too, with Stopped with. For a parent of Parallel tests , stopping the parent stops its batches. A stopped build can be resumed or re-run. See Resume a build. Take a build out of the queue. Open Queue in the top bar. Click the … menu of the row and choose Cancel. Confirm Cancel the queued build of.? A build that waits for a personal machine that stays offline is cancelled for you after the time an administrator set, and its page says Cancelled: your machine 'name' was offline for N min. See Personal builds and shelves. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. There is no Stop button. The build is finished, or you may not cancel builds of this build configuration. Ask a project administrator. The build does not stop at once. The tool a step started may need a moment to end. A step set to run always still runs after the stop. The agent is gone and the build still shows as running. Open the Agents page, check the agent, and stop the build from its row. Related pages. The queue. Resume a build. The build page."},
{"title":"Finish Build trigger","href":"running-builds/trigger-finish-build","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Starts a build when builds of other build configurations finish. In the trigger picker this is called Build after other build configurations are built. When to use it. Use it to run tests after a build, or to package after the asset pipeline. If the second build must use the same code as the first, add a snapshot dependency as well: this trigger only starts a build and does not pin the revision. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Triggers. Click Add new trigger and click Add next to Finish Build Trigger. Type the build configurations in Build configurations to watch , separated by commas. Choose the result in Start a build when the watched build is. Click Save. The Finish Build Trigger form. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Build configurations to watch. Names of build configurations, separated by commas. A name is checked as you save. Use a path from the project, such as Game/Windows Build. Empty. Game/Windows Build, Game/Linux Server Build. Start a build when the watched build is. Stable (successful only), Unstable or better , Failed or better or Any result. Stable. Unstable or better. A worked example. The Unit Tests build watches Game/Linux Server Build with Stable. Each good server build starts a test build. What you see when it runs. The row says After the watched builds finish, at least SUCCESS and lists the names. The build's cause says it was started by an upstream build, and the log of the watched build says Triggering the new build. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. No such build configuration. Check the name and path. You need permission to see it. The build did not start although the other finished. The result was worse than the level you chose, or the trigger is disabled. The new build used other code. Add a snapshot dependency for the same revision. See Build chains. Related pages. Triggers. Run B when A succeeds. Build chains."},
{"title":"Perforce root trigger","href":"running-builds/trigger-p4-root","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Starts a build when the Perforce server calls Buildspawn, for a build configuration that uses a shared Perforce root. When to use it. Use it instead of the P4 trigger when the build configuration is on a Perforce root (a shared connection to a depot). The picker offers it only for those build configurations. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Triggers. Click Add new trigger and click Add next to Perforce root: build when the Perforce server triggers it. The trigger has no settings. Click Save. Set up the same change-commit line on the Perforce server as for the P4 trigger. The Perforce root trigger form. Every field. There are no fields. The trigger works like the P4 trigger, with these differences: It checks the build configuration's root, with the rights of the system, and starts a build when it finds changes. While the trigger is active, the root polls at most once in a set time, even if something else asks. The log says Polling skipped: a Perforce trigger is active on root …. The Perforce pages show the new change soon after. A worked example. Skyforge's build configurations share the root skyforge-main. Add this trigger to the Windows Build. A submit starts one build. What you see when it runs. The root's page says Perforce trigger active: last call … ago. The log of the trigger says P4: Changes found or P4: No changes. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The trigger is not in the list. The build configuration is not on a Perforce root. No build after a submit. The Perforce server's trigger line is missing, or the call is refused. See the P4 trigger page. Related pages. Perforce trigger (P4). Perforce. Triggers."},
{"title":"Perforce shelve trigger","href":"running-builds/trigger-p4-shelve","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Starts a personal build for each new shelf that matches your rules. When to use it. Use it to test a change before it is submitted. A developer shelves a change with a keyword in its description, and Buildspawn builds the shelf. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Triggers. Click Add new trigger and click Add next to Perforce shelve trigger. Check the Schedule and the Keyword. Add a Description pattern or Users if you need them. Optional: open Build customization to tick Enforce clean checkout and add Parameters. Click Save. The Perforce shelve trigger form. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Schedule. How often to look for new shelves, in cron syntax. Empty: only when a Perforce trigger calls Buildspawn. H/5 * * * *. H/2 * * * *. Keyword. Only changelists whose description contains this text. Required unless a description pattern or users are set. #buildspawn. #build. Description pattern. A regular expression the description must match. Empty: any description. Empty. ^QA: Users. Perforce users whose shelves count, separated by spaces or commas. Empty: everyone. Empty. dana.k sam.lee. Build customization › Enforce clean checkout. Every build this trigger starts begins with an empty workspace. Off. On. Build customization › Parameters. Build parameters for these builds, one name=value per line. They win over the defaults. Names that start with p4. belong to Buildspawn. Empty. CONFIG=Development. A worked example. Add the trigger with keyword #buildspawn. A developer shelves changelist 51234 with the description Fix crate collision #buildspawn. Within five minutes a personal build starts with that shelf. What you see when it runs. Each new revision of a matching shelf starts one personal build. The cause says Started by the Perforce shelve trigger: shelf 51234 of dana.k. The Shelves tab lists them. Only files in the build configuration's workspace view count. Shelves that exist when the trigger starts, or when the filters change, wait for their next revision. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The keyword is required. Type a keyword, a pattern or a user. A parameter name is refused. Names are letters, digits, _ and. , and cannot start with p4. Each line must be name=value. No build for an old shelf. Only new revisions start a build. Related pages. Triggers. Shelves. Check a shelf before you submit."},
{"title":"Perforce trigger (P4)","href":"running-builds/trigger-p4","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Starts a build when the Perforce server tells Buildspawn about a new change. When to use it. Use it for the fastest start after a submit: no polling, no wait. The Perforce server calls Buildspawn on every submit, and Buildspawn checks the build configuration's roots for new changes. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Triggers. Click Add new trigger and click Add next to P4 triggered build. The trigger has no settings. The page shows a line to set up on the Perforce server. Click the copy button. Click Save. On the Perforce server run p4 triggers and add the line to the list of triggers. The page shows it with a user and an API token. Create the token in API tokens. The user needs the Build permission. The Perforce trigger form. Every field. The trigger has no fields. What it needs is on the Perforce side: Part. What it does. Example. Trigger line. A change-commit trigger that posts the change number and server address to /p4/change of Buildspawn. buildspawn change-commit //depot/. \"curl -s -u USER:API_TOKEN -X POST -d payload={\\\"change\\\":%change%,\\\"p4port\\\":\\\"%serverport%\\\"} http://buildspawn/p4/change\". Credential match. Buildspawn starts only the build configurations whose Perforce credential has the same server address. perforce:1666. A worked example. Skyforge has one Perforce server, perforce:1666. The Windows Build has a Perforce root with a credential for that server. After the trigger line is added, a submit to //depot/game/main starts a build within seconds. What you see when it runs. The build configuration gets a P4 Trigger Log page. A line says P4: Changes found and the build starts, or P4: No changes. The build's cause is the version control trigger. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Nothing happens on submit. Check the trigger line with p4 triggers -o , and that the server can reach Buildspawn. Open the P4 Trigger Log page. The call is refused. The user needs the Build permission, and the API token must be valid. The wrong server. The port in the call must be contained in the full port of the credential. Related pages. Triggers. Perforce root trigger. Perforce. Run on every Perforce submit."},
{"title":"Remote trigger","href":"running-builds/trigger-remote","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Lets a script or another system start a build with a web request and a token. When to use it. Use it when something outside Buildspawn must start a build: a store webhook, a nightly script of another team, a chat command. It is off by default. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Triggers. Turn on the Remote trigger switch at the bottom. Copy the Token. It is shown once and hidden after you save. Use the example curl line. The Remote trigger section, with its token and an example request. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Remote trigger (switch). On or off. Shown when security is on or the build configuration already has a token. Off. On. Token. The secret in the request. Copy and Regenerate buttons. Regenerate makes a new token, shows it once, and the old one stops working at once. Generated. (hidden). Use my own token. Type a token of 8 to 200 letters, digits or. _ ~ - and click Save token. Empty. skyforge-nightly-2026. A worked example. A store webhook starts the Release build: curl -X POST \"http://buildspawn/job/Game/job/Release/build?token=TOKEN\" curl -X POST \"http://buildspawn/job/Game/job/Release/buildWithParameters?token=TOKEN&CONFIG=Shipping\". What you see when it runs. The call answers with a status and the build is queued. The build's cause says it was started by a remote request. A value that someone typed with a token is not written into scripts (see Parameter references ). Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. The switch is missing. Security is off and the build configuration has no token. A 403 answer. The token is wrong or was regenerated. The token is refused when I set my own. It must be 8 to 200 letters, digits or the characters. _ ~ -. Related pages. Triggers. API tokens."},
{"title":"Retry build trigger","href":"running-builds/trigger-retry","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Starts a failed build again after a delay. When to use it. Use it for builds that sometimes fail for reasons outside your code, such as a busy network share or a license server that is slow. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Triggers. Click Add new trigger and click Add next to Retry build trigger. Set Retry delay and Maximum retries. Tick or untick Retry with the same parameters. Choose Retry from : The beginning or The failed step. Click Save. The Retry build trigger form. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Retry delay. Seconds to wait after a failed build before the retry starts. 0 or more. 10. 60. Maximum retries. How many times a failed build is retried. A retry that fails again counts against the same limit. 1 or more. 3. 2. Retry with the same parameters. The retry gets the parameter values of the failed build. Otherwise it uses the defaults. On. On. Retry from: The beginning. A new build that syncs the sources again and runs every step. The beginning. -. Retry from: The failed step. A resumed build: same sources, parameters, agent and workspace. Steps before the failed one show as done in the failed build. If it cannot be resumed (the agent is offline, the workspace is gone or another build used it), the retry starts from the beginning and its cause says why. -. The failed step. A worked example. The Windows Build sometimes fails while it copies files from the share. Set delay 60, maximum retries 2 and The failed step. The cook step is not run again if it was done. What you see when it runs. The row reads Up to 2 times · 60 s apart · from the failed step. The console of the failed build says Retry 1 of 2 scheduled in 60 seconds: it resumes this build at the failed step. The retry's cause says Retry 1 of 2 after failure of #12. When the limit is reached the log says Not retrying: the build was already retried 2 times. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. No retry happens. Only a result of Failure is retried (not Unstable or Aborted). A personal build and a side build of a Parallel block are not retried by themselves. The build configuration must be able to build (not disabled). The retry starts from the beginning. The console says why: agent offline, workspace gone or used by another build. Related pages. Triggers. Resume and retry."},
{"title":"Schedule trigger","href":"running-builds/trigger-schedule","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","Cron syntax","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Starts a build at set times, such as a nightly build of every platform. In the trigger picker this is called Build periodically. When to use it. Use it for work that does not depend on a change: a nightly full build, a weekly cook, a morning smoke test. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Triggers. Click Add new trigger and click Add next to Schedule Trigger. Choose a Frequency : Every hour , Every day , Every week or Custom. Fill in the Hour , Minute and (weekly) the Day. Check the Cron line. It is the line that is saved, and it is checked as you type. Click Save. The Schedule Trigger form. The cron line at the bottom is what is saved. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Frequency. Every hour, Every day, Every week, or Custom. A choice other than Custom writes the cron line for you. Every day. Every week. Day. Weekly only. Sunday to Saturday. Monday. Monday. Hour. A whole number 0 to 23, or H. Empty means H. H (2 for a new daily schedule). 2. Minute. A whole number 0 to 59, or H. Empty means H. H. 0. Cron line. The schedule. Edit it directly when Custom is chosen. H 2 * * * for a new trigger. 0 2 * * 1-5. Cron syntax. A cron line has five fields, separated by spaces: minute (0-59), hour (0-23), day of month (1-31), month (1-12) and day of week (0-7; 0 and 7 are Sunday). Part. Means. Example. *. Every value. * * * * * every minute. a,b. A list. H H 1,15 * *. a-b. A range. 0 9 * * 1-5 weekdays at 09:00. */n or a-b/n. Every n steps. */10 * * * *. H. A fixed value picked from a hash of the build configuration name, so not every build starts at the same second. It can have a range: H(0-7). H H(0-7) * * * some time between midnight and 07:59. @hourly , @daily , @midnight , @weekly , @monthly , @yearly. Short names that use H. @midnight means some time from 00:00 to 02:59. @daily. TZ=Zone. First line only. Uses another time zone instead of the server's. The IDs are the Java time zone IDs. TZ=Europe/London. Empty lines and lines that start with # are comments. You can write several lines. # Every weekday at 02:00 0 2 * * 1-5 # Every 4 hours H */4 * * * # London time, once in the morning TZ=Europe/London H 8 * * *. */3 and H/3 in the day of month field do not repeat evenly near the end of a month, because months have different lengths. Use days of the week instead. A worked example. The Skyforge nightly builds every platform at 02:00 on weekdays. Choose Custom and type 0 2 * * 1-5. See Run nightly at 02:00. What you see when it runs. The row on the Triggers page reads Daily at 02:00 , Every Monday at 03:30 or On a cron schedule. The build's cause says it was started by the timer. %build.triggeredBy.username% is empty. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. A minute or hour is refused. A minute is a whole number from 0 to 59, or H. An hour is 0 to 23, or H. Pick a day of the week. Choose a day for a weekly schedule. A schedule is needed. The cron line is empty. The build starts at the wrong time. The server's time zone is used. Add TZ=. as the first line. The build starts at a minute I did not choose. H picks a minute for you. Type a number to fix it. Related pages. Triggers. Run nightly at 02:00. VCS trigger."},
{"title":"VCS trigger","href":"running-builds/trigger-vcs","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Starts a build when someone commits or submits a change to a version control root of the build configuration. When to use it. Use it so every change is built. It is one trigger over every version control system the build configuration uses (Git, Perforce and others). It checks on a schedule, and a hook from the server can start a check sooner. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Triggers. Click Add new trigger and click Add next to VCS Trigger. Check the Poll schedule (a cron line). Optional: set the Quiet period and tick Ignore post-commit hooks. Click Save. The VCS Trigger form. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Poll schedule. A cron line that says how often to look for changes. It uses the same syntax as the Schedule trigger , with H and an optional first line TZ=Europe/London. H/15 * * * * (about every 15 minutes). H/5 * * * *. Quiet period (seconds). How long to wait after a change before the build is queued. Changes that arrive in that time go into one build. Empty uses the quiet period of the build configuration. A whole number, 0 or more. Empty. 60. Ignore post-commit hooks. Do not start a check when the version control system sends a hook. Only the schedule checks. The version control integration must support this. Off. On. A worked example. The Linux Server Build is on a Git root. The trigger polls every five minutes with a quiet period of 60 seconds. Three commits in two minutes give one build with all three changes. What you see when it runs. The trigger's row on the Triggers page reads Quiet period: 60 s and shows the schedule. The build's cause says it was started by the version control trigger. The Change Log lists the changes. Problems and fixes. Problem. What to do. Builds start late. The poll schedule is long, or the quiet period is. Shorten them, or set up a hook so the server tells Buildspawn at once. No build after a commit. The change is outside the root's view or path filter, or the trigger is disabled. The quiet period is refused. Use a whole number of seconds. Related pages. Triggers. Git. Perforce. Perforce trigger (P4)."},
{"title":"Triggers","href":"running-builds/triggers","headings":["Add a trigger","The trigger types","Parameters and triggers","Triggers from projects and templates","How-to pages"],"text":"Triggers start builds on their own: when code changes, on a schedule, or when another build finishes. Each build configuration has its triggers on Settings › Triggers. A build configuration can have several triggers. Each is a separate entry that you can edit, disable, enable or delete. A disabled trigger stays saved but never starts a build. The Triggers page. Click Add new trigger to add one. Add a trigger. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Triggers. Click Add new trigger. The list shows only the kinds that work for this build configuration. A kind that is already on it says so, and you edit it instead of adding it twice. Click Add next to the kind and fill in its settings. Click Save. On the Triggers page each row has Edit , Disable or Enable , and Delete. Changing triggers needs the Configure permission. The summary on the right says in one sentence what starts a build, for example Runs every day at 02:00 and after Game/Windows Build finishes. When a build configuration or its project is paused , no trigger starts a build. People can still start builds by hand. The trigger types. Trigger. Starts a build when. VCS Trigger. Someone commits or submits a change to a root. Schedule Trigger. The time matches a schedule. Finish Build Trigger. A watched build configuration finishes a build. Retry build trigger. A build of this configuration fails. P4 triggered build. The Perforce server calls Buildspawn after a submit. Perforce root: build when the Perforce server triggers it. The same, for a build configuration on a Perforce root. Perforce shelve trigger. A new shelf matches your keyword, pattern or users. Remote trigger. A script sends a web request with a token. Core triggers show a Core chip and those that come from a plugin show Plugin. The VCS, Schedule and Finish Build triggers have a plain form. The others bring their own form, or say This trigger has no settings and show what to set up on the other side. A project that indexes branches has one more trigger, Periodically if not otherwise run , with an Interval (default 1d ; also 5m , 2h , 7d ). It runs an index if none ran within that time. It belongs to the project, not to a build configuration. Parameters and triggers. A trigger starts a build with the default values of its parameters. The rules of each parameter still count: a value that breaks them is refused. To start with other values, use the remote trigger with buildWithParameters , the shelve trigger's Parameters , or reverse.dep values. Triggers from projects and templates. Triggers can come from a template or a project. Inherited triggers show where they come from, and you can override, disable or reset them on the build configuration, as with parameters. How-to pages. Run nightly at 02:00. Run on every Perforce submit. Run B when A succeeds."},
{"title":"Git","href":"version-control/git","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Several roots","Branches","Build another branch once","Templates and shared roots","Every field","Additional Behaviours","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Tips","Related pages"],"text":"Connect a Git repository to a build configuration, so each build checks out the code and lists what changed. A root (or VCS root) is one repository a build configuration checks out. Git support is built into Buildspawn: you don't need to install anything on the server. Agents need the git command. When to use it. Use Git when the code of the build configuration lives in a Git repository. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Version Control. Choose Git. Type the Repository URL , for example https://git.studio.example/skyforge/engine.git or git@git.studio.example:skyforge/engine.git. Pick the Credentials to use, or add new ones. Public repositories need none. Under Branches to build , type the branch, for example */main. Click Save. The Unit Tests build configuration builds the main branch of a Git repository. The next build checks out the code into its checkout directory on the agent. The build page shows the commit it built, and the Change Log tab lists the new commits since the last build. To build when someone pushes, add a version control trigger. Several roots. A build configuration can check out more than one root, for example a game repository and a tools repository, or Git and Perforce together. See Several roots. Branches. Branches to build decides what a build checks out: You type. What it builds. */main. The main branch. */release/*. Any branch under release/. A trigger builds the one that changed. refs/tags/v1.4. One tag. The branch a build used is in %build.branch% and in the build's info line. Build another branch once. You don't need to change the settings to build a different commit, tag or branch once. Use Re-run with changes and choose the revision in the Source row of the root. Templates and shared roots. A template can hold roots too. Build configurations that use the template get its roots. Template roots always check out into a directory of their own, so they never clash with the build configuration's roots. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Repositories › Repository URL. Where the code is. Add more with Add Repository. git@git.studio.example:skyforge/engine.git. Credentials. An SSH key or access token. Public repositories need none. None. git-buildbot. Name and Refspec (advanced). The remote's name, and which refs to fetch. origin. +refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*. Branches to build › Branch Specifier. What to build. Add more with Add Branch. One branch per build configuration works best: with many, the changes between branches can be wrong. */master. */main. Git executable. Which Git to use, when more than one is set up under Tools. Default. Additional Behaviours. Extra options, listed below. None. Additional Behaviours. Click Add under Additional Behaviours and choose: Behaviour. What it does. Clean before checkout. Deletes untracked files (also those in.gitignore ) and resets tracked files before every checkout. Clean after checkout. The same, after the checkout. Wipe out repository & force clone. Empties the workspace before building. Advanced clone behaviours. Shallow clone depth, tags, timeouts and reference repositories. A shallow clone saves time but lists every file as changed in the first changes. Advanced checkout behaviours. The checkout timeout in minutes (default 10). Advanced sub-modules behaviours. Update submodules, recursive, shallow, with parent credentials. Git LFS pull after checkout. Pulls large files after the checkout. Needs git lfs on the server and every agent. Good for game assets. Check out to a sub-directory. A folder under the workspace for the repository. Check out to specific local branch. Checks out the revision as a local branch of this name. Empty or ** uses the remote branch name. Sparse checkout paths. Only these paths are checked out. Merge before build. Merges a branch into the build's branch first, for example an integration branch into master. Calculate changelog against a specific branch. Lists changes against th"},
{"title":"Version control overview","href":"version-control/overview","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","The choices","More about version control","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Choose where a build configuration gets its code: nothing, Git, Perforce or several places at once. When to use it. Every build configuration has a Version Control setting. It decides what a build checks out, how changes are found, and which builds start when someone submits. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Version Control. Choose one of the options below. Fill in its fields and click Save. The choices. Choice. What it is. Page. None. The build checks out nothing. It has no fields. Use it for builds that fetch their own files in a step. Git. One Git repository, with branches and extra behaviours. Git. Perforce root. A shared Perforce connection and workspace, defined once. Perforce roots. Perforce Software. A Perforce workspace defined in this build configuration. Perforce Software. Several roots. More than one of the above, each in its own folder. Several roots. More about version control. Perforce : the hub for everything Perforce. Version control parameters : the commit, changelist and branch of a build. Versioned settings : keep settings in your repository. A worked example. Skyforge uses Perforce for the game ( Perforce root skyforge-main ) and Git for small tools ( Git ). The Tools Unit Tests build configuration uses Git only. Windows Build uses the Perforce root. The Release build configuration uses Several roots for both. What you see when it runs. The log starts with the checkout. The build page shows the revision, and the Changes tab lists what is new since the last build. Problems and fixes. You see. What to do. Builds do not start on a submit. Add a trigger. See Triggers. Problems with the connection show on the build configuration page. Open Version Control and read the message. Related pages. Triggers , Templates."},
{"title":"Perforce connections","href":"version-control/perforce-connections","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","Administrator connections","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"A connection is a stored Perforce credential: the server address, the user and a password or ticket, kept encrypted and never shown. When to use it. Every Perforce root, Perforce Software setting and Perforce operations step signs in through a credential. Make one credential per build user. The same page also covers Administrator connections , which let a few steps do admin work, and the Perforce variables a build gets ( P4_PORT , P4_USER , P4_CLIENT , P4_TICKET ). See Perforce variables for those. How to set it up. Open the credentials page where the credential should be visible: the project's, or the server's under Manage Buildspawn › Credentials. Click Add credentials and choose Perforce Password Credential or Perforce Ticket Credential. Fill in the fields below. Click Test Connection (administrators only). Fix any error. Click Create. The Perforce password credential form. You can also add a credential from the root form: the Credential picker has an Add button. The connection page of a build configuration ( Perforce connection tab) shows the server and user in use and checks that they work. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. ID , Description. The name you pick this credential by. Generated. helix-buildbot. P4Port. Host and port of the server. Do not type ssl: ; use the checkbox. perforce.studio.example:1666. SSL connection. Use SSL. Off. On. Trust. With SSL: the fingerprint of the server's certificate. Optional if the server has a CA certificate. Test Connection shows the fingerprint the first time. 7A:B3: Username. The Perforce user for builds. It needs at least the open protection level. buildbot. Password (password credential). The user's password. Login with ticket value / Ticket (ticket credential). A valid session ticket. Make one with p4 -u buildbot -p perforce:1666 login -ap. The -a lets it work from any machine. Use P4TICKETS file / P4TICKETS (ticket credential). The full path of a tickets file on the agent. C:\\Users\\build\\p4tickets.txt. All Hosts (advanced, password). Ask for a ticket that works on all hosts. Off. Retry (advanced). How many times to retry a connection or task. The wait grows with each try (n squared seconds). 0. 3. Tick interval (ms) (advanced). A waiting message goes to the log after this long, so long commands do not look stuck. 0 turns it off. 0. 30000. RPC_SOCKET_SO_TIMEOUT_NICK (advanced). Read and write timeout of the connection in milliseconds. 0 turns timeouts off. 0. P4HOST (advanced). Host name to pretend to be. Enable user session , Minimum session life (advanced). Caches the user and ticket expiry. While the ticket has at least this much life left, no login check is done. Off, 0. Administrator connections. Some work needs more rights than a build user has: removing a workspace another user owns, or a custom command. An Administrator connection is a connection with admin or super access, defined with the root. On the roots page, Administrator Access opens the list. An administrator must also turn on Allow Administrator connection in the Perforce operations settings, and the person who sets up a step needs the Perforce permission Use administrator connection. See Perforce operations. A worked example. Skyforge has one build user buildbot. An administrator adds a Perforce Ticket Credential with ID helix-buildbot , P4Port perforce.studio.example:1666 , SSL on, Trust set, the ticket from p4 login -ap , and a Tick interval of 30000. All roots of the Game project use it. What you see when it runs. The log of a Perforce operation starts with P4: buildbot@ssl:perforce.studio.example:1666 (.). The password and the ticket never appear. If a secret would be printed, it is replaced by ********. Problems and fixes. You see. What to do. The password or ticket is rejected. Buildspawn shows the problem on the build configuration page and in its settings. Update the credential. Tickets expire: make one with -a and a long-lived user. SSL trust error. Click Test Connection to get the fi"},
{"title":"Perforce operations","href":"version-control/perforce-operations","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Connection","Every operation","Files","Changelists","Branching and labels","Anything else","Options (all operations)","Rules about dangerous commands","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"A build step that runs Perforce commands for you: sync, clean, submit, shelve, copy a stream and more, without a password in any script. When to use it. Use it when a build must do more than the first sync. Typical jobs: clean the workspace before a build, submit a version file after the build, shelve generated files, copy one stream to another, or label what you built. The step uses the Perforce root of the build configuration, so the server, user and workspace are already set. The password stays in the connection. It is never typed into the step, put in a script or shown in the log. The step does not start the p4 program. Buildspawn sends the commands to the server itself, so the agent does not need p4 installed. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Build steps. Click Add build step , type Perforce in Filter step types and click Perforce operations. Under Connection , leave Use the project's Perforce root. The card below shows the root, server, user and workspace the step will use. Click Test connection to check them. Under Operation , click the card of the operation you want. The fields under Settings change to fit it. Fill in the fields. The Command box at the bottom shows the Perforce command the step will run. Under Options , choose what counts as a failure and what the step hands to the next steps. Click Save. The Perforce operations step. The Connection card shows the root the step uses. The build configuration needs a Perforce root as its version control. Without one the step stops with This job has no Perforce root. Connection. Field. What it does. Default. Connection. Use the project's Perforce root runs the commands with the root's connection and workspace. Administrator connection runs them as an administrator, without the workspace. It is shown only when an administrator has allowed it (see below), you have the Perforce permission to use it, and one exists. Use the project's Perforce root. Administrator connection. Which administrator connection to use. It must be defined where the Perforce root is, or above. None. Test connection. Signs in and shows Connected as user@server. An administrator connection has no workspace, so the operations that work on workspace files (sync, clean, reconcile, submit and the like) are not offered with it. Every operation. The operations are in four groups. Each one lists the Perforce command it runs. Build parameters such as ${BUILD_NUMBER} can be used in the text fields. Files. Sync: get files from the depot. Runs p4 sync. Use it to get a different revision, or more paths, after the first sync. Field. What it does. Files. One path per line, a depot path or a path in the workspace. Empty means the whole workspace. Revision. A changelist, label or date, for example @48200 or @release-1.3. It is added to each path. Force sync. Gets every file again, even those the workspace already has ( -f ). Preview only. Lists what would change and changes nothing ( -n ). Clean: put the workspace back to what the depot has. Runs p4 clean. It removes files the depot does not have (build output, for example), restores files you changed on disk and restores files that were removed. Before it changes anything it runs a preview and counts the files, so it can stop before it deletes too many. Field. What it does. Files. Which paths to clean. Empty means the whole workspace, and the form shows a warning: This touches the whole workspace. Added files. Delete local files the depot does not have ( -a ). Edited files. Restore files changed on disk to the depot version ( -e ). Deleted files. Restore files removed from disk ( -d ). Ignore.p4ignore. Off keeps the files your.p4ignore lists, such as Intermediate/ , Saved/ and DerivedDataCache in an Unreal project. On cleans those too ( -I ). Fail if more files would be deleted than. A safety limit. If the check finds more files to delete, nothing is deleted and the step fails. 0 means no limit. Preview only. Lists what would change "},
{"title":"Perforce polling and triggers","href":"version-control/perforce-polling","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Poll on a schedule","Build when the Perforce server calls","Every setting","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Start a build when someone submits to Perforce, either by asking the server on a timer or by letting a Perforce trigger call Buildspawn. When to use it. Polling means Buildspawn asks Perforce on a schedule whether there is a new changelist. A Perforce trigger is a script on the Perforce server that calls Buildspawn after each submit. Polling is simple. A trigger starts builds at once and puts less load on the server. You can use both: polling is the safety net. How to set it up. Poll on a schedule. Open the build configuration, then Settings › Triggers. Click Add new trigger and add Poll SCM (or, with Perforce Software, P4 triggered build. ). Type a schedule such as H/5 * * * *. Click Save. See Triggers for the schedule syntax. Build when the Perforce server calls. Add the trigger Perforce root: build when the Perforce server triggers it to the build configuration. It needs the build configuration to use a Perforce root. On the Perforce server, an administrator adds a trigger line with p4 triggers that calls Buildspawn after each submit. Use a Buildspawn user that can only build these build configurations, and its API token. The root shows Commit hook is active or Commit hook is inactive , so you can see whether the server has called. A Pipeline job reads the note build when the Perforce server triggers it and takes the same trigger. Every setting. Where. Setting. What it does. Perforce root, Advanced settings. Minimum polling interval. At least this many seconds between two polls of a build configuration on the root. Kept across restarts. While a Perforce trigger is active, the interval doubles with each trigger call, from 15 minutes up to 4 hours, and goes back when a poll finds a change the trigger missed. Then the log says Polling skipped: a Perforce trigger is active on root. Build configuration, Perforce root. Checkout rules. Excluded paths do not start polling. Perforce Software. Polling build filters. See Perforce Software. Job property Refresh Perforce lists on a custom interval. Interval in seconds. How often the Perforce lists of the build configuration (submitted changes, shelves, pending changes, labels, branch streams) are read again in the background. It runs while someone has viewed the build configuration in the last 30 minutes, or while it has a polling, commit or shelve trigger. Pages show the stored lists at once, with their age. The default is 120 seconds, at least 15. A worked example. The Windows Build of Skyforge uses root skyforge-main with Minimum polling interval 60. It has the trigger Perforce root: build when the Perforce server triggers it and a Poll SCM trigger H/30 * * * * as backup. A submit at 14:02 starts a build within seconds. What you see when it runs. The build lists the changelists since the last build. For builds started by a trigger, the cause says run by a Perforce trigger. The build configuration page shows a polling log. Problems and fixes. You see. What to do. No build after a submit. The change may be outside the view or the checkout rules, or removed by a filter. Look at the polling log. The commit hook stays inactive. The server has not called yet. Check the p4 triggers line, the user and the API token. The build triggers itself. Its own submit starts it. Use the filter Exclude changes from user for the build user. Related pages. Triggers , Build every Perforce submit , Shelves."},
{"title":"Perforce root: unshelve","href":"version-control/perforce-root-unshelve","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"A build step that unshelves a shelved changelist into the workspace of a build configuration that uses a Perforce root. When to use it. Use it when the build configuration gets its code from a Perforce root and a step must put a named shelf on top of the synced files. It does the same as Perforce: Unshelve , which does not work with a Perforce root. For one-off tests of a shelf, use the Shelved changes tab of the Run dialog instead (see Shelves ). How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Build steps. Click Add build step , type unshelve in Filter step types and click Perforce root: unshelve. Fill in the fields below. Click Save. The Perforce root: unshelve step. Every field. The form is the same as for Perforce: Unshelve. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Unshelve Changelist. The number of the shelved changelist. Build parameters can be used. Empty. ${SHELF}. Resolve Options. None, Safe ( -as ), Merge ( -am ), Force Merge ( -af ), Yours ( -ay , keep your edits) or Theirs ( -at , keep the shelf content). The first in the list. Resolve: Safe (-as). Clean up Review actions. Removes review actions the unshelve left behind. Off. Off. Ignore this step if ChangeList value not set. An empty changelist makes the step do nothing and succeed. Off. On. The step uses the root's connection and its workspace. With several roots , it uses the Perforce root's own checkout folder. A worked example. On Windows Build , a parameter SHELF is empty for normal builds. The unshelve step has Unshelve Changelist ${SHELF} and Ignore this step if ChangeList value not set on. A build started with SHELF=48790 tests that shelf on top of the latest code. What you see when it runs. The log shows the unshelve and resolve. The build's Changes list the shelved changelist first, then the changelists since the last build. Problems and fixes. You see. What to do. The step fails with a number error. The field is empty or not a number. Fill it in, or tick Ignore this step if ChangeList value not set. A credential error. The root's connection is not usable here. Check it on the connection page. Related pages. Perforce: Unshelve. Perforce operations. Shelves."},
{"title":"Perforce roots","href":"version-control/perforce-roots","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Create a root","Use a root in a build configuration","Every field of a root","Name and scope","Connection","What to check out","Sync and cleanup","Advanced settings","Other actions on a root","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"A Perforce root is a Perforce connection plus a workspace definition, set up once and shared by many build configurations. When to use it. Use a Perforce root when several build configurations sync the same depot or stream. You define the server, user, stream or view and sync options in one place. A change to the root reaches every build configuration that uses it. For a build configuration that needs its own workspace settings, use Perforce Software instead. Roots live on the global list or on a project. A root on a project is seen by the build configurations in that project and the projects below it. A build configuration sees the roots of its projects, nearest first, then the global ones. If two roots have the same ID, the nearer one wins. How to set it up. Create a root. Open the project, then click Perforce roots. For a root every build configuration may use, open Manage Buildspawn › Perforce roots. Click Create root. Fill in the form (every field is listed below). Click Test connection to check that Buildspawn can sign in and see the stream or client. Click Create root. The Perforce roots tab of a project. Roots from higher up are listed as read-only. Use a root in a build configuration. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Version Control. Choose Perforce root. Pick the root in Perforce root. The line under the picker shows what the root checks out and the workspace name for this build configuration. Optionally fill in Checkout rules. Click Save. Version Control with Perforce root chosen. Field. What it does. Perforce root. Roots of this build configuration's projects, nearest first, then the global ones. Polling. Shown when polling was skipped because a Perforce trigger is active on the root (see Polling and triggers ). Checkout rules. One rule per line: +:path includes, -:path excludes, path=>dir and.=>dir put files into a folder. Paths are relative to the workspace side of the root's mapping and name directories. The most specific rule wins. Without a +: rule everything else is included. On a stream only +: and +:=>dir work, unless the root has Create non-stream workspace. Excluded paths are not synced, do not start polling and do not show in changes. Every field of a root. Name and scope. Field. What it does. Example. Root name. Unique in this scope. Shown in the picker. Skyforge Main. Root ID. Build configurations refer to the root by this ID. Letters, digits, _ ,. and - , starting with a letter or digit. It is offered from the name. After the root is made, Change ID renames it and updates the build configurations that use it. Their next build starts from an empty workspace once. skyforge-main. Description. Optional text. Main line of the game. Belongs to. Shows where the root is defined and who sees it. Connection. Field. What it does. Credential. A Perforce password or ticket credential. The port and user come from it. The password never leaves it. See Perforce connections. Test connection. Checks the credential, mode, stream and client you typed. What to check out. Choose one of three cards. Card. Field. What it does. Stream : work on a stream; the mapping comes from the stream. Stream. An existing stream, such as //skyforge/main. It may use parameters: //skyforge/${BRANCH}. Enable feature branches and Branch streams. One stream per line. * is a wildcard, -: leaves a stream out, and the last matching line decides. Each stream gets its own builds and workspace. See Streams. Create non-stream workspace. Allows every checkout rule, but the build cannot submit to the stream. Client : use an existing client as it is. Client name. An existing client. Only its mapping is used. Builds run in the build configuration's own client, so the client's name is ignored. Example: build-${NODE_NAME}. Client mapping : write the depot to workspace lines here. Client mapping. Depot to workspace lines in the View Mappings editor. Use //${P4_CLIENT}/ in place of a client name. ChangeView. Limits paths to a revision or labe"},
{"title":"Perforce shelves and personal builds","href":"version-control/perforce-shelves","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Build a shelf by hand","Build every new shelf","The Shelves tab","Review status with Swarm","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Build a shelved change on top of the latest code before it reaches the depot. When to use it. A shelf (shelved changelist) holds files someone has not submitted. A personal build syncs the latest code, puts the shelf on top and runs the steps. It tells the author whether the change works before it breaks the main line. It does not change the status of the build configuration or its last successful build. How to set it up. Build a shelf by hand. Open the build configuration and click the arrow beside Run to open the Run dialog. Open the Shelved changes tab. Tick one or more shelves, or type the numbers in Shelved changelist IDs , separated by commas, for example 16, 17. Empty means a regular build. Click Run Build. The Shelves tab. Here nobody has shelved files in the view. The build unshelves each change in the order given after the sync ( p4 unshelve ) and merges what is older than the head ( p4 resolve -am ). A conflict fails the build and names the files. The build is marked Personal. Build every new shelf. Open Settings › Triggers , click Add new trigger and add Perforce shelve trigger. Fill in the fields below and click Save. Field. What it does. Schedule. How often to look for new shelves, in cron syntax, for example H/2 * * * *. Empty means only when a Perforce trigger calls Buildspawn. Keyword. Only changelists whose description contains this text, for example #buildspawn. Required unless a description pattern or users are set. Description pattern. A regular expression the description must match. Empty means any. Users. Perforce users whose shelves count, separated by spaces or commas. Empty means everyone. Build customization. Start every build clean, and give name=value parameters, one per line. Names that start with p4. belong to Buildspawn. Only files in the build configuration's workspace view count. Each new revision of a matching shelf starts one personal build. Shelves that exist when the trigger starts, or when you change its filters, wait for their next revision. The Shelves tab. The Shelves tab lists shelved changelists in the view, with a switch between Shelved and Pending (not shelved) , a user filter, and the columns Change, Description, User, Workspace, Files, Date and Personal build. Search by number, user, workspace or description. A shelf page shows its files and diffs. Lists show their age and have a Refresh button. If the list is cut, it says Showing N of M shelved changes ; type any other number in the field. Review status with Swarm. Changelist and shelf pages show the review a change belongs to. The job property Publish build status to Helix Swarm posts the result to the review as a test, and optionally also as a comment. Create Swarm test needs a Swarm administrator; otherwise you make the test in Swarm by hand, with the Base64 of a Buildspawn user and API token that may build the configuration. Add Swarm servers in the system settings. The Build Review tab starts a build for a review by hand, with the review, change, status and the pass, fail and update addresses. Swarm usually does this for you. The build then has P4_REVIEW and P4_REVIEW_TYPE. A worked example. An artist changes a material, runs p4 shelve -c 48790 and writes #buildspawn in the description. The shelve trigger on Windows Build sees it within two minutes and starts personal build #212. The artist reads the result and submits. What you see when it runs. The build has a Personal chip. Summary shows p4.status = shelved , p4.review with the changelist, and the shelf and owner. Changes lists the shelved changelist. The shelf's Personal build column links back. Problems and fixes. You see. What to do. My shelf is not listed. Only shelves with files in the view are shown. Check the view, then click Refresh. The build fails before the steps and names files. The shelf conflicts with newer submits. Update, resolve, shelve again and run a new build. The trigger did not build. Check the keyword and users. An older shelf needs a new revision."},
{"title":"Perforce Software","href":"version-control/perforce-software","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Workspace behaviour","Workspace spec fields (Manual)","Populate options","Polling build filters","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"The version control choice that defines a Perforce workspace inside one build configuration: credential, workspace type, sync options and polling filters. When to use it. Choose Perforce Software when one build configuration needs its own workspace settings that no other build configuration shares. If several build configurations use the same depot, use a Perforce root instead: you change it in one place. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Version Control. Choose Perforce Software. Under P4 Credentials , pick a connection , or click Add. Choose Workspace behaviour and fill in its fields. Choose Populate options. Optionally add Polling build filters ( Add new build filter ). Click Save. Version Control with Perforce Software chosen. Workspace behaviour. Choice. Use it when. Manual (custom view). You write the view yourself. An existing workspace is updated, or a new one made. Spec File (load workspace spec from file in Perforce). A workspace spec is kept as a file in the depot. Static (static view, controller only). You use a workspace that already exists and has a valid view. Streams (view generated by Perforce for each node). Your depot uses streams. Buildspawn makes or updates the workspace from the stream. Template (view generated for each node). You copy the view of an existing workspace for each agent. All five have Character Set (use none unless the server is in Unicode mode) and Pin the workspace to the build host (the workspace is tied to the machine the build runs on). Choice. Fields. Manual. Workspace name (a name with ${NODE_NAME} in it, so each agent has its own), then the workspace spec fields below. Spec File. Workspace name and Depot path for workspace spec , for example //spec/clients/skyforge-ws.p4s#3. The spec must match the workspace name. Static. Workspace name of the existing workspace. Streams. Stream Codeline (for example //skyforge/main ), Stream at change (builds the view from the stream spec at or before this changelist, optional) and Workspace Name Format. Template. Template workspace (the existing workspace to copy) and Workspace Name Format. Workspace Name Format must hold at least one variable, such as ${NODE_NAME} or ${JOB_NAME} , because every agent needs its own workspace. Workspace spec fields (Manual). Field. What it does. Default. Workspace options. Switches: Make all files writable , Overwrite writable files , Compress data on transfer , Lock the workspace to its owner , Keep file times from the depot , Remove empty folders. Overwrite writable files on. Line Endings. Default, Unix, Windows, Mac or Shared. Default. Type. Normal (writable) , Graph depot , Read only or Partitioned. Read-only workspaces have their own have-list table, which keeps long-lived build workspaces fast, but cannot edit or submit. Normal. Stream , Stream at change. Dedicate the workspace to a stream. The view is then replaced by the stream's view. View Mappings. The View Mappings editor. Use ${P4_CLIENT} for the workspace name. Change View. Restricts depot paths to a point in time, such as //depot/path/.@1000. Limit View. Restricts the paths of the view, useful for streams. Exclusions start with -. Server ID. Restricts use to the named server. Empty allows the main server and replicas other than edge servers. Backup Client. Whether the workspace is part of the server's backup. On. Custom Polling Path. Extra depot paths outside the view to poll, separated by commas, at most 10. A change there starts a build. Build once after saving. Off. Populate options. These decide how the workspace is cleaned and filled at the start of a build. Choice. What it does. Auto cleanup and sync. Removes files that are not in the depot, restores missing and changed files, then syncs. Best for clean builds. Sync only. Syncs without cleaning. Files are overwritten because of clobber. Best for incremental builds. Forced clean and sync. Removes all files under the workspace root, then force syncs. Slow, and not "},
{"title":"Perforce streams","href":"version-control/perforce-streams","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","One stream","Many streams in one build configuration","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Build one stream, or every stream that matches a pattern, without writing view lines. When to use it. Use streams when your depot uses Perforce streams, such as //skyforge/main , //skyforge/dev-ai and //skyforge/release-1.4. A stream defines its own view, so Buildspawn makes the workspace for you. How to set it up. One stream. Open a Perforce root and, under What to check out , choose the Stream card. Type the stream in Stream , for example //skyforge/main. A parameter works: //skyforge/${BRANCH}. Click Test connection , then Create root or Save. With Perforce Software , choose Streams (view generated by Perforce for each node) and type the Stream Codeline. Many streams in one build configuration. On the root, tick Enable feature branches. In Branch streams , type one pattern per line, for example: //skyforge/dev-* -://skyforge/dev-sandbox. * is a wildcard. A line starting with -: leaves a stream out. The last matching line decides. Click Save. The Stream card and Branch streams. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Stream. An existing stream. May use parameters. Empty. Enable feature branches. Builds the streams you list. Off. Branch streams. One stream or pattern per line. Each stream gets its own builds and workspace. Empty. Create non-stream workspace. Allows every checkout rule. The build cannot submit to the stream. Off. Stream at change (Perforce Software). Builds the view from the stream spec at or before this changelist. Empty. A worked example. Skyforge has //skyforge/main and one stream per feature. The Game root has Stream //skyforge/main , Enable feature branches on, and Branch streams //skyforge/feat-*. When a programmer makes //skyforge/feat-rain , the next poll starts a build for it. The build shows the stream as a branch chip. What you see when it runs. Builds carry a branch chip with the stream. The build configuration page has a Builds / Branches toggle. Overview, History and Changes can be filtered by branch. The stream of a build is in %vcsroot.stream% and P4ROOT_<ID>_STREAM. The list of branch streams is read in the background and shown with its age. Problems and fixes. You see. What to do. A checkout rule is refused on a stream. On a stream only +: and +:=>dir work. Tick Create non-stream workspace for other rules. A stream you want is missing from the branches. Check the order of the lines: the last matching line decides, so a later -: line may remove it. Related pages. Perforce roots , View Mappings , Copy a stream."},
{"title":"Perforce: Unshelve","href":"version-control/perforce-unshelve","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"A build step that puts a shelved Perforce changelist into the build's workspace, so the build tests a change that is not submitted yet. When to use it. Use it when a build configuration uses the Perforce Software version control and a build must test a shelf you name, for example from a build parameter. For a build configuration that uses a Perforce root , use Perforce root: unshelve instead. To test a shelf by hand, the Shelved changes tab of the Run dialog is easier. See Shelves. A shelf is a set of files stored on the Perforce server but not submitted. Unshelve copies them into your workspace. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Build steps. Click Add build step , type Unshelve in Filter step types and click Perforce: Unshelve. Fill in the fields below. Make sure the step comes after the sync. The sync happens before all steps, so any position works. Put it before the compile step. Click Save. The Perforce: Unshelve step. Every field. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Unshelve Changelist. The number of the shelved changelist. Build parameters can be used. Empty. ${SHELF}. Resolve Options. What to do when a shelved file also changed in the workspace. Resolve: None does not resolve. Safe (-as) resolves only files with no conflict. Merge (-am) merges what it can. Force Merge (-af) merges and leaves conflict marks. Yours (-ay) keeps your edits. Theirs (-at) keeps the shelf content. The first in the list. Resolve: Safe (-as). Clean up Review actions. Removes review actions the unshelve left behind. Off. Off. Ignore this step if ChangeList value not set. If the changelist field is empty after parameters are filled in, the step does nothing and succeeds. Without it, an empty value fails the step. Off. On. A worked example. The Skyforge team has a build parameter SHELF on Windows Build. Artists leave it empty for normal builds. A programmer who wants to test a shelf types its number. Unshelve Changelist. ${SHELF}. Resolve Options. Resolve: Safe (-as). Ignore this step if ChangeList value not set. On. What you see when it runs. The log shows the Perforce unshelve and the resolve. The changes of the build then list the shelved changelist first, followed by the changelists since the last build. Problems and fixes. You see. What to do. The step fails at once, with a number error. The field is empty or not a number, and Ignore this step if ChangeList value not set is off. Fill it in or tick the box. The step fails on a build configuration with a Perforce root. Use Perforce root: unshelve. Files stay in conflict. Choose a different Resolve Options , or bring the shelf up to date and shelve it again. Related pages. Perforce root: unshelve. Perforce operations (Unshelve operation). Shelves , Test a shelved change before you submit."},
{"title":"Perforce variables","href":"version-control/perforce-variables","headings":["When to use it","The variables","Variables of a Perforce root","Hide the ticket","The changelist in the build name","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Every build with a Perforce checkout gets variables for the server, user, workspace, changelist and a login ticket, so your scripts can talk to Perforce. When to use it. Use them in a script step that runs p4 commands, to stamp the changelist into a package, or to name a build after its code. You do not define them. Buildspawn fills them in when the build starts. For the full list of version control values, see Version control parameters. The variables. Variable. What it holds. Example. P4_PORT. The Perforce server connection. ssl:perforce.studio.example:1666. P4_USER. The Perforce user of the connection. buildbot. P4_CLIENT. The name of the workspace of this build. buildspawn-win-build-01-Game-Windows-Build-0. P4_ROOT. The folder of the workspace on the agent. C:\\ws\\skyforge. P4_CHANGELIST. The last changelist in the synced workspace. If none is found, the latest changelist on the server. 48512. P4_TICKET. A valid login ticket for the connection. Shown only if the connection could log in, and only when Hide TICKET is off (see below). P4_REVIEW , P4_REVIEW_TYPE. The Swarm review number and its type, shelved or committed , for a build of a review. 1024 , shelved. P4_STREAM_AT_CHANGE. The Stream at change value of a stream workspace. P4_INCREMENTAL. With the Polling per change filter: set it to false to build all changelists, not only the oldest. Variables of a Perforce root. With a Perforce root , a build also gets variables named after the root. Replace <ID> by the root ID in capitals, with letters other than A to Z and 0 to 9 turned into _. A root skyforge-main becomes SKYFORGE_MAIN. Variable. What it holds. P4ROOT_<ID>_PORT , P4ROOT_<ID>_USER. The server and user of the root. P4ROOT_<ID>_CLIENT. The workspace of this build. P4ROOT_<ID>_CHANGELIST. The changelist that was synced. P4ROOT_<ID>_STREAM. The stream of this build. The first root of the build configuration also sets P4PORT , P4USER and P4CLIENT (no underscore), the names the p4 program reads. A plain p4 changes in a script then works without options. These never hold a password or ticket. With several roots , each variable also exists with the root number at the end, such as P4_CHANGELIST_2. Hide the ticket. In the system settings, the Perforce section P4: Secure P4_TICKET has the switch Hide TICKET. Off (the default) puts P4_TICKET into the environment of every build step. On removes it. Turn it on if people who can edit build steps should not be able to read the ticket. The value is a secret: scripts must not print it. See Use P4 commands in a script with the P4 ticket. The changelist in the build name. A build is numbered before the checkout, so the number format on Settings › General cannot hold the changelist. Name the build from a step instead. Print a buildNumber message: echo ##buildspawn[buildNumber '1.4.%build.counter%-CL%build.vcs.number%']. In a PowerShell step write: Write-Host \"##buildspawn[buildNumber '1.4.%build.counter%-CL$env:P4_CHANGELIST']\". The last name printed wins. See Service messages. A worked example. A Skyforge packaging script writes the changelist into the version file and names the build: Set-Content Config\\Version.txt \"CL $env:P4_CHANGELIST\" Write-Host \"##buildspawn[buildNumber '2.3.%build.counter%-CL$env:P4_CHANGELIST']\". What you see when it runs. On the build page, the build is called 2.3.57-CL48512. The Parameters list shows P4_CHANGELIST and the other values, but not the ticket. Problems and fixes. You see. What to do. P4_TICKET is empty. Check that Hide TICKET is off and that the credential can log in. P4_CHANGELIST is empty. The step ran before a checkout, or the build configuration has no Perforce version control. A plain p4 asks for a password. The standard variables carry no password. Log in with the ticket: p4 -P %P4_TICKET%. Related pages. Version control parameters , Perforce connections."},
{"title":"View Mappings","href":"version-control/perforce-view-mappings","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","The table","Quick add","Text view and pasting","Preview","Streams, Limit View and Change View","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"The View Mappings editor says which depot files a Perforce workspace gets and where they go on the agent. When to use it. You use it whenever a workspace is not a plain stream: the Client mapping card of a Perforce root , and View Mappings in a manual workspace of Perforce Software. A view is a list of lines. Each line maps a depot path to a path in the workspace. Game depots are big, so a good view saves sync time: a cook job only needs Content and Config. How to set it up. Open the root or the manual workspace settings and find View Mappings (or Client mapping ). Click Add mapping , or use Quick add for a ready-made set of lines. For each row, choose the Mode , type the Depot path , or click the folder button to browse the depot. The Workspace path fills in from the depot path and the workspace name. You can change it. Drag the handle of a row to move it. Later lines win. Open Preview what the workspace gets to check the result. Click Save on the page. The view mapping table. The table. Column. What it does. Drag handle and line number. Drag to reorder. A later line wins over an earlier one. Mode. Include maps the path. Exclude leaves it out (a - in front of the line). Overlay adds the files on top of earlier lines, in the same place (a + in front). Depot path. Starts with //. It has a folder button to browse the depot and suggests paths as you type. Workspace path. Starts with //<workspace name>/. In a root, write //${P4_CLIENT}/ , which stands for the workspace name of each build. Wildcards. , * and %%1 must match on both sides. Paths with spaces get quotes for you. The editor checks each row and says what is wrong, for example a depot path that does not start with // , or a workspace path of another workspace. Quick add. Button. What it adds. Skip Saved, Intermediate, DerivedDataCache. Exclude rows after your includes, so they win. Content only (cook job). Only Content and Config come down. Source only. Code and project files, no Content. Skip Binaries and Platforms. Leaves out built output and platforms you do not build. Engine and game. Game, Engine and Shared into the workspace root. Whole depot, one folder. Every folder of the depot, under its own name. Type the Game folder in the depot , for example //depot/Skyforge , so the rows use it. Text view and pasting. The Text view shows the same lines as plain text, one mapping per line: depot path, a space, workspace path. The table and the text stay in step. Lines that start with # are comments and are kept. Lines the table cannot show stay as text until you fix them. Paste from a client spec takes the View: lines of a workspace spec. Preview. Preview what the workspace gets has two parts. Check paths: type a depot path and the editor shows which line decides about it and where the file lands. This runs in Buildspawn, with no call to the server. Count from the server: asks the server for the size and number of files of each line, with a sample of files. Use Count again after a change. The server cannot subtract excludes from includes, so the counts of lines are shown side by side. Streams, Limit View and Change View. On a stream workspace the view comes from the stream and is shown read only under From the stream , with the share, import and exclude tags. Limit View (manual workspaces) restricts the paths of the view. It is a list of single paths. Use exclusion lines starting with - to leave out paths or file types. It is useful for streams, where the view cannot be edited. Change View restricts depot paths to a point in time, for example //depot/path/.@1000. Files that match cannot be submitted. A worked example. A cook build for Skyforge only needs content. Workspace name skyforge-cook-${NODE_NAME} : //depot/Skyforge/Content/. //skyforge-cook-${NODE_NAME}/Content/. //depot/Skyforge/Config/. //skyforge-cook-${NODE_NAME}/Config/. -//depot/Skyforge/Content/Movies/. //skyforge-cook-${NODE_NAME}/Content/Movies/. The last line leaves out the cinematics folder. Because later l"},
{"title":"Perforce","href":"version-control/perforce","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","The parts","Connections","Perforce roots","Perforce Software","View Mappings","Streams","Shelves and personal builds","Polling and triggers","Perforce variables","Perforce operations","Perforce root: unshelve","Perforce: Unshelve","Several roots","The workbench pages","Run a build at a revision, or from an empty workspace","More Perforce features","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Build from Perforce depots, streams and shelves, and browse changes, shelves and reviews without leaving Buildspawn. When to use it. Perforce (Helix Core) support is built in. Use it when your game lives in a Perforce depot. A build configuration with Perforce syncs a workspace on the agent, lists the changelists since the last build, and gets extra tabs for shelves, pending changes, the depot and reviews. This page is the map: each part has its own page. How to set it up. Add a connection : the server, the user and a password or ticket. Define a Perforce root once, on the project. Choose a stream or write View Mappings. Open the build configuration, go to Settings › Version Control , choose Perforce root and pick the root. (For a workspace that only one build configuration uses, choose Perforce Software.). Add a trigger so a submit starts a build. Click Save. A build configuration that uses Perforce Software with a manual workspace named after the agent it runs on. Put ${NODE_NAME} in the workspace name so each agent gets its own workspace. Two agents must never share one. The parts. Connections. Password and ticket credentials, SSL trust, administrator connections. Perforce roots. Shared connection and workspace, every field, checkout rules. Perforce Software. Workspace types, populate options and polling filters. View Mappings. The table editor, quick add and preview. Streams. One stream or many feature branches. Shelves and personal builds. Test a shelf before submit; Swarm reviews. Polling and triggers. Poll, Perforce trigger, refresh interval. Perforce variables. P4_PORT, P4_USER, P4_CLIENT, P4_TICKET and the changelist in the build name. Perforce operations. Build step: sync, clean, submit, copy and more. Perforce root: unshelve. Build step for roots. Perforce: Unshelve. Build step for Perforce Software. Several roots. Perforce and Git in one build. The workbench pages. A build configuration with Perforce gets tabs to work with the depot from the browser. Some are under More. The Shelves tab lists the shelved changelists in the build configuration's view. Tab. What it shows. Change Log and Submitted changes. Submitted changelists with their files and diffs, and the first build that contains each one. Pending Changes. Changelists people are still working on. Mine shows only yours. Shelves. Shelved changelists, ready to build, filtered by user. Depot. A depot browser: folders, file history, compare, diff, annotate, time-lapse, the revision graph, labels and branches. Labels. Perforce labels, and branches. Perforce Jobs. Perforce jobs and the changelists linked to them. Build Review. Start a build for a Swarm review. Perforce connection. The server and user in use, with a connection check. These pages open at once. Their Perforce data fills in a moment later with a spinner. Lists are kept and refreshed in the background, so they show Updated N ago with a Refresh button. If the server cannot be reached, you get a message with Retry instead of a page that hangs. Run a build at a revision, or from an empty workspace. In the Run dialog , a revision tab builds at a chosen changelist or label. The build page says which one it was built at. Latest syncs to the newest changelist, as a regular build does. The build configuration action to enforce a clean checkout makes the next build start with an empty workspace and check everything out again. More Perforce features. VCS labeling (a build feature, Label in Perforce ): creates an automatic Perforce label on what the build synced. You set the label name (variables work), a description, whether an existing label may be moved, which branches are labelled ( +:name / -:name , <default> is the root's own stream), the root to label when there are several, and who gets an email if labeling fails. A failure shows a warning and does not change the build result. Personal builds are not labelled. Workspace cleanup: remove the workspace after each build, remove inactive workspaces, or delete wo"},
{"title":"Several roots","href":"version-control/several-roots","headings":["When to use it","How to set it up","Every field of a root card","Variables of several roots","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages"],"text":"Check out more than one repository or depot in one build, each into its own folder. When to use it. Use it when a build needs code from more than one place: the game in Perforce and a tools repository in Git, or two Perforce roots such as game and engine. A root is one repository or depot a build checks out. How to set it up. Open the build configuration and go to Settings › Version Control. Choose Several roots. Your present root moves into a card named root1. A yellow note reminds you that build steps now find its files in root1/ , for example root1/build.sh. Files already at the top of the workspace are not deleted. Click Add root to add a card. On each card click Edit root and choose the Version control ( Git , Perforce Software or Perforce root ), then fill in its settings. Set the Checkout directory of each card. Use Move up and Move down to set the order. Click Save. Several roots with two root cards. To go back to one root, remove cards until one is left. The build configuration then keeps that root at the top of the workspace and the directory is dropped. Every field of a root card. Field. What it does. Default. Example. Name. The name of the root in the lists. Under Edit root. The version control type, or the directory. Engine. Version control. The kind of root. Under Edit root. Git. Checkout directory. Relative to the workspace. Each root needs its own folder, not inside another root. root1 for the moved root. tools. Poll this root for changes. Whether a change in this root starts a build. On. On. Move up , Move down , Edit root , Remove. Order, edit the root's own settings, or delete the card. Each root keeps the rules of its own version control. Perforce roots take view lines or checkout rules, so only those files are synced. Git roots check out the whole repository: path filters only limit which commits count as changes, unless you add sparse checkout. Variables of several roots. Each root adds its variables with its number at the end: GIT_COMMIT_1 , P4_CHANGELIST_2. The first root that sets a variable also sets the plain name, such as GIT_COMMIT , so steps that read the plain name still work. VCS_ROOT_DIR_1 , VCS_ROOT_DIR_2 , … say where each root is. Put the root you want to be \"first\" at the top. With two Perforce roots, each gets its own workspace. Add something to the name, for example -root2. Changes are grouped by root, and the build page shows all roots together. You can view changes by time or by root. A change in one root starts a build without noise from the others. Two branches of the same Git repository as two roots of one build configuration are not supported. Use one root and fetch the second branch in a step, or use two build configurations. With several Perforce roots, unshelving uses the first Perforce root and the label feature labels the last one. Choose the root in the VCS labeling feature. A worked example. The Skyforge build checks out the game from Perforce into game and the shared tools from Git into tools. Root 1, Perforce root skyforge-main. Checkout directory game. Root 2, Git https://git.studio.example/skyforge/tools.git , */main. Checkout directory tools. A PowerShell step runs tools\\pack.ps1 -Source game -Changelist $env:P4_CHANGELIST. What you see when it runs. The log shows each root being checked out in turn. The build page lists the changes of both roots, with the root name on each change. Problems and fixes. You see. What to do. Steps cannot find files after adding a second root. The files moved to the root's directory. Change the step paths, for example root1/build.sh. A message that a directory is not allowed. A directory must be relative, and not inside another root's directory. Both Perforce roots fight over a workspace. Each root needs its own client; add something to one of the names. Related pages. Git , Perforce roots , Version control parameters , Templates."},
{"title":"Version control parameters","href":"version-control/vcs-parameters","headings":["When to use it","Available parameters","About the build","About each root","Environment variables","Use them in steps","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related"],"text":"Every build knows which code it checked out. Steps can read the commit, changelist, branch and folder as parameters. You don't define these parameters: Buildspawn fills them in when the build starts. Use them to stamp a version into your game, to name a package after the changelist, or to find a second root on disk. When to use it. Use these values to stamp a version into your game, name a package after the changelist, or find the folder of a second root. Available parameters. About the build. Parameter. What it holds. %build.vcs.number%. The revision the build used: the Git commit or the Perforce changelist. %build.branch%. The branch or stream the build used. %build.branch.isDefault%. true when that is the default branch. %build.vcs.branch.<root>%. The branch of one root. %build.checkoutDir%. The folder on the agent where the code was checked out. %build.changedFiles.file%. The path of a file that lists the files changed since the last build, one per line. %build.is.personal%. true for a personal build, for example of a Perforce shelf. %build.triggeredBy.username%. The user ID of whoever started the build, or of the build chain. Empty for a schedule or a version control trigger. Also in the environment variable BUILDSPAWN_TRIGGERED_BY. About each root. Replace <root> with the root's name. With only one root, you can leave it out: %vcsroot.url% works too. Parameter. What it holds. %vcsroot.<root>.url%. The Git repository URL. %vcsroot.<root>.branch%. The branch the root is set to build. %vcsroot.<root>.port%. The Perforce server address. %vcsroot.<root>.user%. The user the root signs in as. %vcsroot.<root>.changelist%. The Perforce changelist that was synced. %vcsroot.<root>.stream%. The Perforce stream. %vcsroot.<root>.shelvedChangelist%. The shelf that was unshelved, in a personal build. No password or token ever appears in these parameters. Environment variables. The same values also reach every step as environment variables, for scripts that read the environment: Variable. What it holds. BUILD_VCS_NUMBER. The same as %build.vcs.number%. VCS_ROOT_DIR_1 , VCS_ROOT_DIR_2 , …. The folder of each root, when the build configuration has several. P4_CHANGELIST. The Perforce changelist that was synced. GIT_COMMIT , GIT_BRANCH. The Git commit and branch. P4_PORT , P4_USER , P4_CLIENT , P4_ROOT , P4_TICKET. The Perforce connection, workspace and login ticket. See Perforce variables. P4ROOT_<ID>_CHANGELIST and others. Values of one Perforce root, named after the root ID. GIT_COMMIT_1 , P4_CHANGELIST_2 , …. With several roots , each variable also exists with the root number at the end. The first root that sets a variable also sets the plain name. Use them in steps. Type % in the script editor of a step to see the list. For example, put the changelist into the package name: powershell -File Build\\ci.ps1 -Stage package -Platform Win64 -Changelist %build.vcs.number%. Name each build after its code with a buildNumber message from a step. The build number format on Settings › General is applied before the checkout, so it cannot use these values: Write-Host \"##buildspawn[buildNumber '1.4.%build.counter%-CL%build.vcs.number%']\". Or only run a slow step for real code changes, not personal builds: if \"%build.is.personal%\"==\"true\" ( echo Personal build: skipping upload ) else ( upload-build --changelist %build.vcs.number% ). Branch names and other values from version control are put into script text only when they hold safe characters. A branch with odd characters is not expanded. In a script, prefer the environment variable. What you see when it runs. On the build page, Parameters lists the values the build used. In the console, a step that prints %build.vcs.number% shows the changelist or commit. Problems and fixes. You see. What to do. The value stays as %build.vcs.number%. The build configuration has no version control, or the name is misspelled. A branch name is not filled in inside a script. It has unsafe characters. Read the environment "},
{"title":"Versioned settings","href":"version-control/versioned-settings","headings":["When to use it","Turn on versioned settings","What goes into the repository","Edit settings in the repository","Changes in the browser","Conflicts","Permissions","A worked example","What you see when it runs","Problems and fixes","Related pages","Tips"],"text":"Keep a project's settings in your repository next to the code, with a history of every change. With versioned settings on, Buildspawn writes the settings of a project and of everything inside it to a folder in a Git or Perforce repository. Every change you make in the browser becomes a commit. A change pushed to the repository is loaded back into Buildspawn. You can see who changed what, and roll back with your normal tools. When to use it. Use it when you want to review settings changes as commits or pull requests, undo a bad change, or keep the settings of many build configurations next to the game code. Turn on versioned settings. Open the project and go to its settings. Choose Versioned Settings. Under Synchronization , tick Synchronization enabled. More settings appear. Under Settings storage , choose the version control (Git, or Perforce when it is offered) and fill in the repository URL or Perforce root, the branch, and the folder in the repository (empty means the top). Give a credential ID if the repository needs one. Under Behaviour , choose: Allow editing via UI : on, or off to make the repository the only place to change settings; When a build starts : use the current settings, or the settings from the repository; the check interval : how often Buildspawn looks for new commits (30 seconds or more). Click Save , then Commit current project settings to write the first commit. The Versioned Settings page of the Game project, before synchronization is turned on. It is off by default. The status card shows the state: In sync with VCS , or Synchronization stopped with the error. It also shows the last commit and the last load, with the revision, user and time. The Git storage runs the git command on the Buildspawn server, so git must be installed there. Each command has a 120-second limit. A project inside a project with versioned settings is covered by it. A project with its own versioned settings is stored on its own. What goes into the repository. settings-format.txt the format: buildspawn-xml 1 config.xml the project's own settings jobs/Windows Build/config.xml each build configuration, project or template inside jobs/Tools/jobs/Asset Pipeline/config.xml. Builds, workspaces and build numbers are never committed. Secrets stay on the server. Passwords and other encrypted values are emptied in the files and filled back in from the server when settings are loaded. A secret set only in the repository cannot be loaded: set it in the browser. The versioned-settings setup itself is not committed, so a commit cannot switch it off. Edit settings in the repository. Clone the repository and change a config.xml , for example to add a step. Commit and push. Within the check interval, Buildspawn sees the new commit and loads it. Each file is checked before anything changes; a broken file stops the load and the status names it. You can also load at once: click Load project settings from VCS. A dialog warns you that changes not yet committed are lost. Changes in the browser. Every save in the browser is committed for you. Saves within 5 seconds become one commit, with the message Change settings of <items> (by <user>) and the user as author. Conflicts. Buildspawn does not merge. If someone pushed while you edited in the browser, the automatic commit stops and the status asks you to choose: Commit current project settings : the settings in Buildspawn win. Load project settings from VCS : the repository wins. Permissions. Anyone who can read the project can see the page. Saving, committing and loading need the right to configure the project. Loading also needs the right to configure every item it changes; otherwise nothing is loaded. A worked example. The Skyforge Game project stores its settings in the Git repository git.studio.example/skyforge/ci-settings.git , branch main , folder buildspawn. Allow editing via UI is on. A programmer adds a step in the browser. A minute later the repository has the commit Change settings of Windows B"},
{"title":"Canvas and palette","href":"visual-editor/canvas","headings":["Canvas","Palette","Wiring","Problems and Compile preview","Properties","Save"],"text":"Draw a build by dragging blocks onto a canvas and wiring them up. The visual editor shows the same settings as the form, as a graph. Open a build configuration, click Settings , then Visual editor. The editor and the form edit the same settings: a change in one shows in the other. People who may only view the settings see the graph read-only. The visual editor: palette on the left, canvas on the right. The visual editor is marked Beta on the Settings row. It works on Build configurations and on declarative Pipelines. For a Pipeline the graph is made from the pipeline { } script and written back to it. See Pipeline. Canvas. The canvas is a grid you can pan and zoom: Drag an empty part of the canvas to move around. Use the mouse wheel, or − and + in the toolbar, to zoom. Fit (the square button beside the zoom) shows the whole graph. The minimap in the corner shows where you are. Auto-layout tidies the blocks into neat rows. Search nodes (press Space ) finds a block by name. Drag a block to move it. The layout is saved with the settings, but moving blocks never starts a build. The legend above the canvas names the wire colours: Run order , Text , Number , Boolean and File. Palette. The palette on the left lists everything you can add, in groups: Build configuration : the one job-wide block. See The build configuration block. Parameters : configuration parameters, environment variables, system properties, and parameters that ask for text, a choice, yes or no, a password or a file. Build steps : the common runners, such as Command Line, PowerShell, Unity, Unreal and MSBuild. More steps holds every other runner. Flow : Sequence, Branch on condition, Parallel and Fail if. Also sources, dependencies and outputs such as artifacts and notifications. Drag an item onto the canvas to add it. Type in Search nodes to filter the palette. Wiring. Blocks connect with wires. Drag from a block's output dot to another block's input dot. Run-order wires (grey) set the order the steps run in: from the build configuration block to the first step, then step to step. Data wires (coloured by type) carry values: a parameter into a step that uses it, or a step's output into a later step. Wires are type-checked when you save. To run steps side by side, wire them through a Parallel block. See Parallel blocks. Flow blocks shape the build. Sequence runs steps one after another. Branch on condition runs a branch only when a condition is true. Parallel runs sides at the same time; with a Matrix it shows as one card with its axes, and an include row can set where it runs. Fail if fails the build on a condition. See Parallel blocks and Matrix mode. Problems and Compile preview. Under the canvas two tabs help you check your work before you save: Problems lists what is wrong with the graph, for example a wire of the wrong type, a loop or a missing value. No problems found means it can be saved. A line at the right says when the last check ran and that there are no cycles. Compile preview shows what Save will write, in the words of the forms and in the order of the Build Steps list. Each item is marked new , changed or unchanged since the last save. It also shows the failure conditions, the declared outputs and who reads them, and anything else that changes. Properties. To change a block's settings: Right-click the block and choose Open properties , or select it and press Enter. A large dialog opens with the block's own form: for a step, the same fields as on the Build Steps home, plus its execution settings, retry, outputs and Run on. Click Save in the dialog. The change is kept in the editor until you save the page. The side panel button in the toolbar opens a read-only panel beside the graph that shows the selected block. You can resize it; it remembers its width. Save. The Save button in the toolbar has a small arrow beside it for the other ways to save. Validate checks the graph without saving and lists any problems. Save writes the graph back to the build conf"},
{"title":"The build configuration block","href":"visual-editor/configuration-block","headings":["The build configuration block","Parameter groups"],"text":"The block that holds settings for the whole build. Every graph has exactly one, and the steps start from it. The Build configuration block (top left) and the Parameters group wired into it. The build configuration block. The block is marked job-wide. Its sections sum up the settings that are not steps: Section. What it shows. Parameters. How many parameters the build has. The Parameters group is wired here. Triggers. What starts a build, or None. Source. The version control roots, such as a Perforce depot path or a Git repository. + root adds one. Every step inherits. Settings every step gets, such as Runs on pool Windows. Rules for the build. How many build features are on, and how many failure conditions are set. The Run order dot on the right of the block starts the chain of steps. Open the block's properties (right-click, Open properties ) to change any of these settings. They are the same settings as on the settings homes ( Triggers , Version Control , Build Features , Failure Conditions ). Parameter groups. All parameters sit in one Parameters group, wired into the build configuration block. Each parameter is a small block with its name, its value, its type and whether it is asked at run time, for example choice · asked at run. The chips at the top of the group filter by kind: All , Configuration , Environment and System , with a count each. + Add parameter adds one. You can also drag a parameter type from the palette into the group. A system. property shows a system tag. Password values always show as dots. Each parameter has a coloured dot on the right. Wire it to a step to show that the step uses it. The parameter's properties list the steps that use it under Used by. People who may only view the settings never see secret values: they are masked in the visual editor too. See Parameters for what each kind does."},
{"title":"Run view","href":"visual-editor/run-view","headings":["Open the run view","Read the run view"],"text":"Watch a running build move through the blocks you drew. The run view lays a build's results over the graph, live. Build #7 drawn over the graph. Each step shows its result and time. Open the run view. There are three ways in: In the visual editor, open the build menu in the toolbar (it says Last build #7 ) and pick a build. On a build's page, click Visual in the header. On the History tab, use the visual link on a build's row. The toolbar then says Viewing build #N. Choose the build menu again to switch builds or to go back to editing. The run view needs only permission to see the build configuration. An administrator can switch off the visual links on the History and build pages in the system settings. Matrix blocks show one cell per combination. The console has a lane chip for each combination, and the build page lists the results in a grid with Re-run for each combination. Read the run view. A bar above the canvas gives the build's result and time, for example Build #7 passed in 0:54 , and the legend. Each block is lit with its state: State. Meaning. Passed. The step finished well. A green tick and its time. Running. The step runs now. Its time counts up live. Failed. The step failed. It shows the exit code, and Attempt 2 of 4 when it was retried. Skipped. The step did not run because of its execution rule or conditions. Done earlier. On a resumed build: the step passed in an earlier build, which is named. Not reached. The build stopped before this step. Open a step to jump to its block in the build console log. On a failed step, Resume from here starts a resumed build at that step. It needs the Build permission. See Resume and retry. For a Parallel block on separate machines, each side shows its agent and status, and the main build shows when it waits for the sides or for a slot on its agent. Keep the run view open on a big screen during a release build: it updates every few seconds while it is visible."}];
